// LC Economics Walkthroughs Data (auto-generated)
var WALKTHROUGHS_ECONOMICS = {
"2012": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Shifts in Demand Curve",
     q:"State three reasons why the demand curve for bottled water may shift to the right.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Three reasons why the demand curve for bottled water may shift to the right (i.e. an increase in demand):\n\n1. Incomes: Consumer income may have increased, resulting in greater purchasing power and increased demand for bottled water (assuming it is a normal good).\n\n2. Taste/Preference: Due to increased advertising, branding, or greater health consciousness, consumers are demanding more water as they perceive it as a healthier alternative to sugary drinks.\n\n3. Price of substitute goods: If the price of substitute products such as soft drinks or juices has increased, more consumers may switch to purchasing bottled water, shifting its demand curve to the right.\n\nOther valid reasons include:\n- Expectations: If consumers expect a water supply disruption (e.g. a 'big freeze'), they may stock up on bottled water.\n- Unplanned factors: An unexpected heatwave would increase demand; contamination of the public water supply would also increase demand.\n- Government policies: A government campaign promoting the health benefits of drinking water could increase demand."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: European Central Bank (ECB)",
     q:"State three areas of responsibility of the European Central Bank (ECB).\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Three areas of responsibility of the ECB:\n\n1. Price Stability: The ECB monitors inflation in member countries and adjusts the base ECB interest rate to control spending and maintain price stability. It aims for an inflation rate close to but below 2% in the medium term.\n\n2. EU Monetary Policy: The ECB implements monetary policy for the eurozone, monitoring and advising on rates of interest, money supply, and credit availability, while protecting the value of the euro.\n\n3. Official Reserves: The ECB holds and manages the EU's official holdings of gold, foreign currencies, and other reserves held as security against the issue of the euro. It manages these reserves on behalf of all eurozone countries.\n\nOther valid areas include:\n- Supervision: Ensuring prudential supervision of credit institutions and stability in the financial system, including lending to eurozone central banks.\n- Issuing Euro Currency: The ECB has the exclusive/sole right to authorise the issue of euro banknotes and coins within the euro area."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Black Economy",
     q:"Outline two reasons for the growth of the 'Black Economy' and state two effects for the Exchequer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Two reasons for the growth of the Black Economy:\n\n1. Reduced disposable incomes: People cannot afford to pay legitimate prices for goods and services, so they turn to the black economy for cheaper alternatives.\n\n2. Increased VAT rates: Higher VAT rates cause prices to increase, and consumers want to avoid the higher taxes by purchasing goods and services 'off the books'.\n\nOther reasons: Unemployment (those who lost jobs cannot afford VAT-inclusive prices and/or take cash-only jobs to avoid income tax); disillusionment with government policies where some believe the tax system is unfair.\n\nTwo effects for the Exchequer:\n\n1. Loss of tax revenue: The Exchequer experiences reduced VAT and income tax revenues as transactions go unrecorded and undeclared.\n\n2. Increased government expenditure: The government may have to increase spending on law enforcement to combat illegal economic activity. Additionally, some individuals claim social welfare while simultaneously earning unreported income, increasing the welfare bill."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Economies & Diseconomies of Scale",
     q:"In relation to each statement listed below, indicate whether it is an internal or external (a) economy of scale or (b) diseconomy of scale.\n\n- Repetitive tasks, workers are bored\n- R&D costs are shared by many firms\n- Discounts are available for bulk buying\n- Inadequate infrastructure\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Classification:\n\n1. Repetitive tasks, workers are bored = Internal Diseconomy of Scale\nThis is internal because it occurs within the firm itself. As the firm grows, tasks become more repetitive and specialised, leading to boredom and lower morale among workers, reducing productivity.\n\n2. R&D costs are shared by many firms = External Economy of Scale\nThis is external because it benefits the firm from outside, due to the growth of the industry. When many firms cluster together, they can share the costs of research and development.\n\n3. Discounts are available for bulk buying = Internal Economy of Scale\nThis is internal because it arises from the growth of the individual firm. A larger firm can buy raw materials in greater quantities and negotiate discounts from suppliers, reducing average costs.\n\n4. Inadequate infrastructure = External Diseconomy of Scale\nThis is external because it arises from outside the firm, due to the growth of the industry or region. When too many firms locate in one area, the existing infrastructure (roads, broadband, utilities) becomes inadequate, increasing costs for all firms."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Income Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"'Inferior products have a negative Income Elasticity of Demand (YED).\n\n(a) Explain this statement.\n\n(b) State one example of an inferior product, giving a reason for your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Explanation:\nIncome Elasticity of Demand (YED) measures the responsiveness of demand for a good to a change in consumer income. For inferior products, the YED is negative because as a consumer's income decreases, they will buy more of the inferior product (as it is cheaper than alternatives). Conversely, as their income increases, they will buy less of the inferior product and switch to better-quality substitutes. The inverse relationship between income and demand produces a negative YED value.\n\n(b) Example: Supermarket own-brand products.\nReason: As incomes decline (e.g. during a recession), the demand for supermarket own-brand products increases because they are cheaper than branded alternatives. Consumers switch to these lower-cost products as this is all they can afford. When incomes rise again, consumers tend to switch back to premium branded goods, reducing demand for the own-brand products.\n\nOther valid examples: Remould tyres, low-price cuts of meat, white bread, instant noodles, public transport (bus service)."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: MPS & The Multiplier",
     q:"Study the table below and calculate (i) and (ii), showing all your workings.\n\nYear 1: National Income = 24,000 | Consumption = 15,000 | Investment = 6,000 | Exports = 10,000 | Imports = 7,000\nYear 2: National Income = 34,000 | Consumption = 22,000 | Investment = 8,000 | Exports = 16,000 | Imports = 12,000\n\n(i) The Marginal Propensity to Save (MPS).\n(ii) The size of the Multiplier.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(i) Marginal Propensity to Save (MPS):\n\nFirst, calculate savings for each year:\nSavings = National Income - Consumption\nYear 1: 24,000 - 15,000 = 9,000\nYear 2: 34,000 - 22,000 = 12,000\n\nChange in Savings = 12,000 - 9,000 = 3,000\nChange in Income = 34,000 - 24,000 = 10,000\n\nMPS = Change in Savings / Change in Income\nMPS = 3,000 / 10,000 = 0.3 (or 30%)\n\nAlternative method using MPC:\nMPC = Change in Consumption / Change in Income = 7,000 / 10,000 = 0.7\nMPS = 1 - MPC = 1 - 0.7 = 0.3\n\n(ii) The Multiplier:\n\nFirst, calculate MPM (Marginal Propensity to Import):\nChange in Imports = 12,000 - 7,000 = 5,000\nMPM = 5,000 / 10,000 = 0.5 (or 50%)\n\nMultiplier = 1 / (MPS + MPM)\n= 1 / (0.3 + 0.5)\n= 1 / 0.8\n= 1.25\n\nThis means that for every euro injected into the economy, national income will ultimately increase by 1.25 euro. The relatively small multiplier reflects the high leakages (savings and imports) from the Irish economy."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Factors Affecting Savings",
     q:"Outline three factors currently affecting the rate of savings in the Irish economy.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Three factors affecting savings in the Irish economy (in the context of 2012):\n\n1. Confidence in the economy: People were concerned about the future of the economy during the recession, which reduced consumer confidence. As a result, many people postponed purchases and saved instead. Consumers were spending less and deferring spending until later, resulting in 'forced savings' or 'precautionary savings'.\n\n2. Security of savings: Due to the economic and banking crisis, people were less inclined towards risky investments and preferred the security of state-backed savings. The banking crisis made consumers cautious about where they placed their savings, with many seeking guaranteed returns, e.g. An Post's 'National Solidarity Bond'.\n\n3. Rate of interest: Savers seek an interest rate greater than the current rate of inflation to maintain their purchasing power. If savings products produce reasonable real returns, people are more likely to save. However, low ECB interest rates during this period meant returns on deposits were very low, potentially discouraging savings.\n\nOther valid factors:\n- Income levels: For some people, income levels had fallen during the recession, so they were able to save less.\n- Rate of DIRT: The government increased the rate of Deposit Interest Retention Tax, which may discourage savings by reducing the after-tax return."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Imperfect Competition",
     q:"'Imperfect Competition is wasteful of resources.' Do you agree with this statement? Yes/No. Explain your answer.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"YES - Imperfect competition is wasteful of resources.\n\nExplanation: A firm in imperfect competition does not produce at the lowest point of average cost. This means the firm is not producing at maximum efficiency. The waste of resources occurs because:\n\n1. Excess spending on advertising: Firms in imperfect competition must advertise and differentiate their products to attract customers. This spending on advertising, branding, and marketing adds to the firm's costs but does not increase the actual utility of the product for the consumer.\n\n2. Insufficient scale of production: The firm may not produce a sufficient quantity to benefit fully from economies of scale. In the long run, the firm produces where the demand curve is tangent to the AC curve, but this is to the left of the minimum point of AC, meaning average costs are higher than they need to be.\n\n3. Higher prices for consumers: Because firms do not operate at minimum average cost and incur additional costs (e.g. advertising), prices are higher than under perfect competition.\n\nAlternative answer (NO): If a firm invests in innovation and increases sales, this spending may not be considered wasteful. For example, Apple's investment in R&D and product development confers an advantage on the company that creates genuine value for consumers through improved products."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Property Tax (Household Charge)",
     q:"The Irish Government introduced the household charge (property tax) of 100 euro per household in its 2012 budget.\n\n(a) State two advantages of this charge/tax for the Exchequer.\n\n(b) Is this a progressive tax or a regressive tax? Explain your answer.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Two advantages of the household charge for the Exchequer:\n\n1. Government revenue: The charge will increase revenues for the state and local councils, broadening the tax base and providing a new source of income for funding public services.\n\n2. Stable and predictable tax: This tax broadens the tax base in accordance with the Troika's fiscal adjustment requirements. It is payable by every homeowner, so it should be a more stable and predictable form of tax revenue than income-dependent taxes. As it is a flat charge, the revenue collected is certain, thereby facilitating government planning.\n\nOther advantages: Less need to raise direct taxes (may avoid acting as a disincentive to work); economic to collect (low administration cost due to online collection); eases introduction of the proposed full property tax.\n\n(b) This is a REGRESSIVE tax.\n\nExplanation: A flat charge of 100 euro per household does not take into account a person's ability to pay. People on lower incomes would pay a greater proportion of their income in tax compared to those on higher incomes. For example, a person earning 20,000 euro pays 0.5% of their income, while a person earning 200,000 euro pays only 0.05% of their income. This makes the tax burden proportionally heavier on lower-income households, which is the defining characteristic of a regressive tax."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Consumer Behaviour, PED & Cross Elasticity",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the Equi-Marginal Principle of consumer behaviour.\n(ii) State and explain three other economic assumptions used to analyse consumer behaviour. (25 marks)\n\n(b) A manufacturer of three different products calculates the price elasticity of demand (PED) for each product as follows:\nProduct A: -2.8 | Product B: -1.0 | Product C: -0.5\nThe manufacturer wishes to maximise its revenues. Explain in respect of each of these products, what change, if any, the manufacturer should make in the prices currently being charged to enable it to achieve its aim. Illustrate your answers with the aid of a demand curve for each product. (30 marks)\n\n(c) You are given the following information about certain products:\nCross Elasticity of Demand between Product X & Product A = -0.8\nCross Elasticity of Demand between Product X & Product B = +3.2\nCross Elasticity of Demand between Product X & Product C = -1.6\nCross Elasticity of Demand between Product X & Product D = +0.5\n\n(i) Which of the products above are substitutes for Product X? Explain your answer.\n(ii) Which product is the closer complement to Product X? Explain your answer. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Equi-Marginal Principle:\nConsumers will spend their incomes in such a way that the ratio of marginal utility to price is the same for all the goods consumed. When MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb = ... = MUn/Pn, the consumer enjoys maximum satisfaction from their limited income. If this ratio is not equal, the consumer can increase total utility by switching expenditure from the good with the lower ratio to the good with the higher ratio.\n\n(ii) Three other assumptions used to analyse consumer behaviour:\n\n1. The consumer has a limited income: The consumer's income is not large enough to satisfy all needs and wants. Therefore, the consumer must choose between goods, creating an opportunity cost with every purchase.\n\n2. The consumer acts rationally: The consumer acts consistently with their preferences. If they prefer fruit to chocolate and both cost the same, a rational consumer will buy fruit. If they see an identical commodity priced differently in two adjoining shops, they will buy at the lower price.\n\n3. The consumer is subject to the Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility: As a consumer consumes additional units of a good, the marginal utility (extra satisfaction) gained from each successive unit will eventually decline.\n\n(b) Pricing strategies to maximise revenue:\n\nProduct A (PED = -2.8, Elastic demand):\nDECREASE price. Because demand is elastic, the percentage increase in quantity demanded will exceed the percentage decrease in price. The gain in revenue from increased sales will more than compensate for the lower price per unit, so total revenue will increase.\nDiagram: Relatively flat demand curve showing high responsiveness to price changes.\n\nProduct B (PED = -1.0, Unit elastic demand):\nLEAVE PRICE UNCHANGED. Because demand is unit elastic, the percentage change in quantity demanded exactly equals the percentage change in price. Total revenue will remain the same regardless of whether price increases or decreases. No price change is needed.\nDiagram: Demand curve showing equal responsiveness.\n\nProduct C (PED = -0.5, Inelastic demand):\nINCREASE price. Because demand is inelastic, the percentage increase in price will exceed the percentage decrease in quantity demanded. The revenue gained from the higher price will more than offset the small drop in sales, so total revenue will increase.\nDiagram: Relatively steep demand curve showing low responsiveness to price changes.\n\n(c)(i) Substitutes for Product X:\nProducts B and D are substitutes for Product X. This is because they have a positive Cross Elasticity of Demand (+3.2 and +0.5 respectively). A positive CED means that as the price of Product X rises, the demand for these products also rises, as consumers switch to them as alternatives. Product B is the closer substitute as it has the higher positive value (+3.2).\n\n(ii) Closer complement to Product X:\nProduct C is the closer complement to Product X. Products A and C both have negative CED values (-0.8 and -1.6), indicating they are complements. Product C has the higher absolute value (-1.6 vs -0.8), meaning if the price of Product X rises, the demand for Product C will fall by a greater percentage than the demand for Product A. This stronger negative relationship indicates a closer complementary good."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Perfect Competition vs Monopoly",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the reason for the shape of the demand curve of an individual firm in perfect competition.\n(ii) Outline two advantages of perfect competition. (20 marks)\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with the aid of a labelled diagram, the equilibrium position of a firm in short run perfect competition.\n(ii) With the aid of labelled diagrams, explain the impact which the entry of new firms would have on the market and on the equilibrium position of the firm. (35 marks)\n\n(c) Contrast the characteristics of perfect competition with monopoly under the following headings:\n- Barriers to entry\n- Profits in the long run\n- Economies of scale\n- Price discrimination (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Shape of the demand curve in perfect competition:\nThe demand curve facing an individual firm in perfect competition is perfectly elastic (horizontal). This is because the firm is a price-taker - it must accept the market price. A single firm represents such a small proportion of total market supply that its own actions cannot influence the market price. If a firm increases its price above the market price, quantity demanded will fall to zero as consumers switch to the identical (homogeneous) goods available from many other firms at the lower market price.\n\n(ii) Two advantages of perfect competition:\n1. Low prices: The firm sells its products at the lowest possible prices because it produces at the minimum point of average cost in the long run.\n2. Efficient production: The firm produces at the lowest point of average costs, so there is no waste of scarce resources. Allocative efficiency is achieved (P = MC).\n\nOther valid advantages: No wasteful advertising (goods are homogeneous); only normal profits are earned in the long run (no consumer exploitation); freedom of entry ensures no firm can sustain supernormal profits.\n\n(b)(i) Short-run equilibrium in perfect competition:\nDiagram: U-shaped MC and AC curves. Horizontal demand curve D = AR = MR at price P1. MC crosses MR from below at equilibrium point A, at output Q1.\n\nExplanation: The firm maximises profit where MC = MR and MC cuts MR from below (at point A). The firm produces Q1 units of output and sells at price P1. Since AR (price) exceeds AC at output Q1, the firm earns supernormal profit in the short run. The supernormal profit is the shaded area between AR and AC, over the output Q1.\n\n(ii) Impact of entry of new firms:\n\nMarket diagram: The original supply curve S1 shifts right to S2 as new firms enter the industry (attracted by supernormal profits, with no barriers to entry). The market price falls from P1 to P2 as increased supply meets the same demand.\n\nFirm diagram: Because the individual firm is a price-taker, its demand curve (D = AR = MR) shifts down from P1 to P2. The firm must now accept the lower price. At the new equilibrium, the firm produces a smaller quantity Q2. The amount of supernormal profit earned falls or is eliminated entirely (in the long run, only normal profit remains when AR = AC at minimum AC).\n\n(c) Contrast of perfect competition and monopoly:\n\nBarriers to entry:\n- Perfect competition: No barriers to entry or exit. New firms can freely enter and leave the industry.\n- Monopoly: High barriers to entry exist (e.g. patents, control of resources, economies of scale, legal barriers), preventing new firms from entering the market.\n\nProfits in the long run:\n- Perfect competition: Only normal profit is earned in the long run, as entry of new firms eliminates supernormal profits.\n- Monopoly: Supernormal profits can be maintained in the long run because barriers to entry prevent competition from eroding them.\n\nEconomies of scale:\n- Perfect competition: Firms are small-scale and may not benefit from significant economies of scale.\n- Monopoly: Being the sole supplier with large output levels, a monopolist can benefit from substantial economies of scale, potentially leading to lower average costs.\n\nPrice discrimination:\n- Perfect competition: Price discrimination is not possible because all goods are identical (homogeneous), there is perfect knowledge, and the firm is a price-taker.\n- Monopoly: A monopolist has the market power to engage in price discrimination, charging different prices to different groups of consumers for the same product."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Factors of Production, Entrepreneurship & Small Firms",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the following terms in relation to a factor of production: Supply Price; Transfer Earnings.\n(ii) Explain the concept of Economic Rent and outline two circumstances under which a factor of production can earn it. (25 marks)\n\n(b)(i) State and explain the economic reasons why entrepreneurship is important to the development of the Irish economy.\n(ii) State and explain three means by which the Irish Government could encourage entrepreneurship in Ireland. (30 marks)\n\n(c) 'There are 200,000 small firms in Ireland employing 655,000 people.' (Small Firms Association, December 2011). Discuss the reasons why small firms survive in the Irish economy. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Supply Price: The minimum payment a factor of production must receive to remain in its current use. If the factor receives less than its supply price, it will transfer to an alternative use where it can earn more. This is also known as the factor's transfer earnings.\n\nTransfer Earnings: The amount a factor of production could earn in its next best alternative employment. If the payment received falls below this level, the factor will transfer to that alternative use.\n\n(ii) Economic Rent: Economic rent is any payment to a factor of production over and above its supply price (transfer earnings). It is the surplus earned above what is needed to keep the factor in its present use.\n\nCircumstances under which a factor can earn economic rent:\n1. When the factor has a unique or scarce talent: For example, a top footballer or entertainer earns economic rent because their unique talent cannot be easily replicated. Their supply is very limited.\n2. When the supply of the factor is fixed or inelastic: Land is fixed in supply, so any increase in demand for land will lead to higher rent, all of which is economic rent (as defined by Ricardo).\n3. When a factor has specialised skills in high demand: Professionals with specialist skills (e.g. surgeons, software engineers) may earn well above their transfer earnings due to strong demand and limited supply.\n\n(b)(i) Importance of entrepreneurship to the Irish economy:\n- Job creation: Entrepreneurs create new businesses which generate employment, reducing unemployment and increasing tax revenues.\n- Innovation: Entrepreneurs bring new products, services, and processes to the market, increasing competitiveness and driving economic growth.\n- Tax revenue: Successful enterprises generate corporation tax, income tax (from employees), and VAT, contributing to government revenue.\n- Reduced dependency on FDI: A strong indigenous enterprise base reduces Ireland's over-reliance on foreign multinational companies.\n- Multiplier effect: New business spending circulates through the economy, creating further economic activity.\n\n(ii) Three means to encourage entrepreneurship:\n1. Tax incentives: The government could reduce the rate of capital gains tax (CGT) on start-ups, offer tax relief on investments in new businesses, or reduce employer's PRSI to lower the cost of hiring.\n2. Grant schemes and financial support: Through agencies such as Enterprise Ireland and Local Enterprise Offices (LEOs), the government could provide start-up grants, mentoring programmes, and subsidised workspace/incubator facilities.\n3. Reducing red tape and bureaucracy: Streamlining the process of registering a company, reducing regulatory compliance costs, and simplifying planning permission processes would make it easier and less costly to start a business.\n\n(c) Reasons why small firms survive:\n1. Niche markets: Small firms can specialise in niche products or services where demand is too small to attract larger competitors.\n2. Personal service: Small firms can offer a more personalised customer experience, building loyalty and repeat business.\n3. Flexibility: Small firms can adapt quickly to changes in market conditions, customer preferences, and new trends, whereas larger firms are slower to react.\n4. Lower overheads: Small firms may have lower fixed costs, operating from smaller premises with fewer staff, making them viable even with lower sales volumes.\n5. Government support: Enterprise Ireland, LEOs, and other state agencies provide grants, training, and mentoring specifically aimed at supporting small businesses.\n6. Subcontracting: Larger firms may outsource certain functions to small firms, providing a steady stream of work.\n7. Local knowledge: Small firms benefit from local knowledge and established community relationships that larger national/international firms may not possess."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Costs of Production & Location",
     q:"(a) With the aid of two clearly labelled diagrams, explain the relationship between:\n(i) the short run average cost curve and long run average cost curve.\n(ii) the short run average cost curve and marginal cost curve. (25 marks)\n\n(b) Discuss the economic factors which should be considered by a firm when deciding where to locate its operations. (25 marks)\n\n(c) Ocean Blue Ltd produces two boats weekly and incurs the following weekly costs:\nRent: 1,200 euro | Raw materials: 2,000 euro | Labour: 1,600 euro | Normal profit: 1,000 euro\n\nWhat is the minimum price at which each boat can be sold if production is to continue:\n(i) in the short run?\n(ii) in the long run?\nExplain your answers in each case. (25 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Relationship between SRAC and LRAC:\nDiagram: Several U-shaped short run average cost (SRAC) curves, each representing a different plant size. The long run average cost (LRAC) curve is an envelope curve that is tangent to each SRAC curve.\n\nExplanation: In the short run, the firm has a fixed plant size and operates on one SRAC curve. In the long run, all factors are variable, and the firm can choose the optimal plant size. The LRAC curve shows the lowest average cost achievable for each level of output when all factors can be varied. It initially falls (due to economies of scale), reaches a minimum, then rises (due to diseconomies of scale). Each SRAC curve is tangent to the LRAC curve at one point.\n\n(ii) Relationship between SRAC and MC:\nDiagram: U-shaped SRAC curve with MC curve. MC intersects SRAC at the minimum point of the SRAC curve.\n\nExplanation:\n- When MC is below AC, average cost is falling (each additional unit costs less than the average, pulling the average down).\n- When MC is above AC, average cost is rising (each additional unit costs more than the average, pulling the average up).\n- When MC equals AC, average cost is at its minimum point. The MC curve always passes through the lowest point of the AC curve.\n\n(b) Economic factors for location decisions:\n1. Proximity to raw materials: If raw materials are heavy or perishable, the firm should locate near their source to minimise transport costs (e.g. food processing near farms).\n2. Proximity to markets: Firms selling bulky finished goods or providing services may locate near customers to reduce distribution costs and respond quickly to demand.\n3. Transport and infrastructure: Good road, rail, port, and airport connections are essential for receiving inputs and distributing outputs. Access to broadband and utilities is also critical.\n4. Labour supply: Availability of skilled workers at competitive wage rates. Proximity to universities and training centres can provide a steady supply of educated labour.\n5. Government incentives: Grants, tax breaks, and subsidies from agencies like IDA Ireland or Enterprise Ireland may influence location, particularly in designated development areas.\n6. Cost of land/premises: Rental and property costs vary significantly between locations. Firms may choose cheaper locations outside cities to reduce overheads.\n7. Economies of agglomeration: Locating near similar firms or in industrial clusters provides access to shared services, specialist suppliers, and a pool of skilled labour.\n\n(c) Minimum selling price:\n\nTotal weekly costs:\n- Fixed costs (Rent): 1,200 euro\n- Variable costs (Raw materials + Labour): 2,000 + 1,600 = 3,600 euro\n- Normal profit: 1,000 euro\n- Total costs: 5,800 euro\n\n(i) Short run minimum price:\nAverage Variable Cost = Total Variable Cost / Output = 3,600 / 2 = 1,800 euro per boat.\n\nIn the short run, the firm must cover its average variable costs to continue production. The minimum price is 1,800 euro per boat. As long as the selling price exceeds the variable cost per unit, the firm is making a contribution towards its fixed costs. Even though it makes a loss, this loss is smaller than the loss it would incur by shutting down (which would equal the full fixed costs of 1,200 euro).\n\n(ii) Long run minimum price:\nTotal cost including normal profit = 5,800 / 2 = 2,900 euro per boat.\n\nIn the long run, the firm must cover all costs including normal profit. The minimum price is 2,900 euro per boat. Normal profit is the minimum return required to keep the entrepreneur in business. If the firm cannot cover all costs including normal profit, the entrepreneur will leave the industry and deploy resources elsewhere."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): Population & Economic Growth",
     q:"(a) The table shows world population growth:\nPopulation (billions): 1(1804), 2(1927), 3(1960), 4(1974), 5(1987), 6(1999), 7(2012)\nYears elapsed: -, 123, 33, 14, 13, 12, 13\n\n(i) Suggest two reasons for the rapid growth in world population in recent times.\n(ii) Discuss the economic effects of an increasing population on the Irish economy. (30 marks)\n\n(b)(i) State and explain the benefits of economic growth for developing countries (LDCs).\n(ii) Discuss one advantage and one disadvantage for the Irish economy of increased growth rates in developing countries. (25 marks)\n\n(c) The classical economist Thomas Malthus created a theory on population.\n(i) Explain Malthus's theory on population.\n(ii) Is Malthus's theory on population relevant today? Explain your answer. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Two reasons for rapid world population growth:\n1. Advances in medicine and healthcare: Vaccines, antibiotics, and improved medical treatments have dramatically reduced death rates, particularly infant mortality, leading to increased life expectancy and population growth.\n2. Improved food production: The 'Green Revolution' and advances in agricultural technology have increased food supply, supporting larger populations. Better nutrition has also reduced death rates.\n\nOther valid reasons: Improved sanitation and access to clean water; decline in death rates from infectious diseases; cultural/religious factors encouraging larger families.\n\n(ii) Economic effects of increasing population on Ireland:\n\nPositive effects:\n- Increased labour supply: A larger population provides more workers, supporting economic growth and attracting FDI.\n- Larger domestic market: More consumers create greater demand for goods and services, stimulating business activity.\n- Increased tax revenue: More people working and spending means more income tax and VAT revenue for the government.\n- Innovation and enterprise: A larger, younger population may produce more entrepreneurs and innovators.\n\nNegative effects:\n- Pressure on public services: More people require healthcare, education, housing, and infrastructure, increasing government expenditure.\n- Housing shortage: Increased population intensifies demand for housing, driving up prices and rents.\n- Environmental pressure: More people lead to greater resource consumption, waste generation, and carbon emissions.\n- Unemployment: If job creation does not keep pace with population growth, unemployment may rise.\n\n(b)(i) Benefits of economic growth for LDCs:\n- Increased national income / GDP: More resources available for investment in infrastructure, education, and healthcare.\n- Reduced poverty: Economic growth creates employment, raising incomes and lifting people out of poverty.\n- Improved living standards: Higher incomes allow people to access better food, shelter, healthcare, and education.\n- Increased tax revenue: Allows governments to fund public services and reduce dependency on foreign aid.\n- Attraction of FDI: Growing economies attract foreign investment, creating further employment and technology transfer.\n\n(ii) Impact on Ireland:\nAdvantage: New export markets. As developing countries grow, their consumers have more purchasing power, creating new markets for Irish exports (e.g. food, pharmaceuticals, technology). This supports Irish businesses and employment.\n\nDisadvantage: Increased competition. As LDCs develop, their firms become more competitive, potentially competing with Irish firms for international market share. Lower labour costs in LDCs may attract investment away from Ireland.\n\n(c)(i) Malthus's theory on population:\nMalthus argued that population grows at a geometric rate (1, 2, 4, 8, 16...) while food supply grows at only an arithmetic rate (1, 2, 3, 4, 5...). He predicted that population growth would inevitably outstrip food supply, leading to famine, disease, and poverty - which he called 'positive checks' on population. He also identified 'preventive checks' such as later marriage and abstinence that could voluntarily limit population growth. Malthus concluded that the majority of the population was destined to live at subsistence level.\n\n(ii) Relevance of Malthus today:\n\nArguments that it IS relevant:\n- Famine and food insecurity still exist in many developing countries, as population growth outpaces food production in some regions.\n- Environmental degradation and resource depletion suggest limits to growth, echoing Malthus's concerns about sustainability.\n\nArguments that it is NOT relevant:\n- Malthus did not foresee the Agricultural Revolution and technological advances that dramatically increased food production.\n- Birth rates have fallen in many countries as education levels, urbanisation, and access to contraception have increased.\n- In many developed countries, the problem is actually declining population/ageing population rather than overpopulation.\n- International trade allows countries to import food, overcoming local production limitations."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): Government Economic Aims & Taxation",
     q:"(a) One of the economic aims of the Irish Government at present is to decrease government current expenditure. Discuss four other economic aims of the Government and use relevant statistics/information to justify your choice. (20 marks)\n\n(b) 'Most eurozone countries with large deficits are using VAT to generate revenue.' (Irish Tax Institute, The Irish Times, November 2011)\n(i) Explain, using examples, the difference between direct taxes and indirect taxes.\n(ii) Discuss the economic advantages and disadvantages for the Government of increasing VAT rates instead of income tax rates in its most recent budget.\n(iii) Outline how imposing a tax on sugary foods (e.g. fizzy drinks) could benefit the Irish economy. (35 marks)\n\n(c) State and explain two economic arguments in favour of and two economic arguments against third level students contributing to the cost of their higher education. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a) Four other economic aims of the Government (in 2012 context):\n\n1. Full employment: With unemployment at approximately 14.8% (Live Register), achieving full employment is a key aim. Higher employment increases tax revenue and reduces social welfare expenditure.\n\n2. Price stability: Controlling inflation is essential to maintain purchasing power. The ECB targets inflation close to 2%. Rising prices erode living standards and reduce international competitiveness.\n\n3. Economic growth: Ireland was emerging from recession with negative GDP growth. Sustained economic growth increases national income, employment, and living standards.\n\n4. Balance of payments equilibrium: Ireland needs to maintain a healthy balance of payments, particularly ensuring export performance remains strong to generate foreign income and support economic recovery.\n\nOther aims: Balanced regional development; equitable distribution of income; sustainable development.\n\n(b)(i) Direct vs Indirect taxes:\nDirect taxes are levied directly on income or wealth and paid directly to the Revenue Commissioners. They cannot be passed on to another party. Examples: PAYE (income tax), Corporation Tax, Capital Gains Tax, DIRT.\n\nIndirect taxes are levied on goods and services and are included in the price of the product. They can be passed on from the producer to the consumer. Examples: VAT, Excise Duty, Customs Duty, carbon tax.\n\n(ii) Advantages of increasing VAT over income tax:\n- Broader base: VAT applies to all consumers (including tourists, social welfare recipients, and those in the hidden economy who spend money), widening the tax base.\n- Does not act as a disincentive to work: Unlike income tax increases, VAT does not directly reduce take-home pay, so it is less likely to discourage labour force participation.\n- Harder to evade: VAT is collected at the point of sale, making it more difficult to evade than income tax.\n\nDisadvantages of increasing VAT:\n- Regressive: VAT takes a higher proportion of income from lower earners, as they spend a greater share of their income on goods and services.\n- Reduces consumer spending: Higher prices reduce disposable income and purchasing power, potentially dampening economic recovery.\n- Cross-border shopping: Higher VAT rates may encourage consumers in border regions to shop in Northern Ireland where rates may be lower.\n- Inflationary: VAT increases directly raise the price level.\n\n(iii) Benefits of a sugar tax:\n- Reduced consumption of unhealthy products, leading to improved public health and reduced healthcare costs (less obesity, diabetes, dental problems).\n- Government revenue: The tax generates revenue that can be used to fund health promotion, obesity prevention programmes, or subsidise healthier food options.\n- Corrects market failure: Consumers underestimate the true social cost of consuming sugary foods (negative externalities on the health system). The tax brings the price closer to the true social cost.\n\n(c) Arguments IN FAVOUR of students contributing:\n1. Benefit principle: Students are the primary beneficiaries of higher education, earning significantly higher incomes over their lifetime. It is fair that they contribute to the cost of something from which they directly benefit.\n2. Improved quality: Increased funding from student contributions allows investment in better facilities, more lecturers, and improved resources, raising the quality of education.\n\nArguments AGAINST students contributing:\n1. Equity/access: Higher fees may deter students from lower-income backgrounds from pursuing higher education, creating inequality of opportunity and a less socially mobile society.\n2. Social benefit: Society as a whole benefits from a more educated population through higher productivity, innovation, and tax revenues. Since the benefits are shared, the costs should also be shared through general taxation."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): International Trade & Comparative Advantage",
     q:"(a) The table illustrates the Law of Comparative Advantage:\n\nUnited Kingdom: Food = 40 units per worker per week, Clothing = 10 units\nChina: Food = 60 units per worker per week, Clothing = 20 units\nTotal Output: Food = 100 units, Clothing = 30 units\n\n(i) State the Law of Comparative Advantage.\n(ii) Explain how both countries benefit from international trade in the above example.\n(iii) Calculate the terms of trade for both goods. Show your workings. (30 marks)\n\n(b)(i) Discuss three economic factors which affect the competitiveness of Irish firms in international trade.\n(ii) Outline how international trade benefits Irish consumers. (25 marks)\n\n(c) 'Euro area growth is forecast to slow down from 1.6% in 2011 to 0.2% in 2012.' (OECD, 2011). Discuss the possible effects on the Irish economy of the slowdown in the growth rate in the euro area. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Law of Comparative Advantage:\nA country should specialise in producing and exporting those goods in which it has the lowest opportunity cost of production, even if it does not have an absolute advantage. Trade between countries based on comparative advantage will result in increased total output and mutual benefit.\n\n(ii) How both countries benefit:\nChina has an absolute advantage in both goods but a comparative advantage in food production (60/20 = 3:1 ratio vs UK's 40/10 = 4:1 ratio in food:clothing). The UK has a comparative advantage in clothing (10/40 = 1:4 ratio vs China's 20/60 = 1:3 ratio).\n\nWait - let us recalculate:\n- UK opportunity cost of 1 unit food = 10/40 = 0.25 clothing\n- China opportunity cost of 1 unit food = 20/60 = 0.33 clothing\nSo UK has comparative advantage in food (lower opportunity cost).\n\n- UK opportunity cost of 1 unit clothing = 40/10 = 4 food\n- China opportunity cost of 1 unit clothing = 60/20 = 3 food\nSo China has comparative advantage in clothing (lower opportunity cost).\n\nIf the UK specialises in food: 2 workers produce 80 food, 0 clothing.\nIf China specialises in clothing: 2 workers produce 0 food, 40 clothing.\nBut this does not work perfectly - both countries can trade and achieve combinations outside their individual PPFs.\n\nWith specialisation and trade, total output increases beyond the 100 food + 30 clothing originally produced. Both countries can consume combinations of goods that were previously unattainable.\n\n(iii) Terms of trade:\nFor food: Between the opportunity costs of each country.\nUK opportunity cost of 1 food = 0.25 clothing\nChina opportunity cost of 1 food = 0.33 clothing\nTerms of trade for food: 1 unit of food will trade for between 0.25 and 0.33 units of clothing.\n\nFor clothing:\nUK opportunity cost of 1 clothing = 4 food\nChina opportunity cost of 1 clothing = 3 food\nTerms of trade for clothing: 1 unit of clothing will trade for between 3 and 4 units of food.\n\n(b)(i) Three factors affecting Irish competitiveness:\n1. Labour costs: Higher wages in Ireland compared to competitors increase costs of production, making Irish exports more expensive. Unit labour costs are a key measure of competitiveness.\n2. Exchange rate: The strength of the euro affects Irish export prices. A stronger euro makes Irish goods more expensive for non-eurozone buyers, reducing competitiveness.\n3. Productivity and innovation: The ability of Irish firms to innovate, adopt new technology, and increase output per worker is crucial for maintaining competitiveness. Higher productivity offsets higher labour costs.\n\nOther factors: Energy costs, taxation levels, quality of infrastructure, level of bureaucracy/regulation.\n\n(ii) How international trade benefits Irish consumers:\n- Lower prices: Competition from imports puts downward pressure on domestic prices, giving consumers more purchasing power.\n- Greater choice: Access to international markets means a wider variety of goods and services are available.\n- Higher quality: Competition forces domestic producers to improve quality to compete with imports.\n- Access to goods not produced domestically: Ireland cannot produce everything it needs, so trade allows access to goods like tropical fruit, certain raw materials, and specialised technology.\n\n(c) Effects of euro area growth slowdown on Ireland:\n\n1. Reduced demand for Irish exports: As the eurozone economy slows, demand for Irish goods and services from our main trading partners falls, reducing export revenue and potentially leading to job losses in export-dependent sectors.\n\n2. Reduced FDI: A weaker European economy may discourage foreign investment in Ireland, as firms become more cautious about expansion during uncertain times.\n\n3. Lower interest rates: The ECB may lower interest rates further to stimulate the eurozone economy, which would reduce borrowing costs for Irish consumers and businesses, potentially encouraging investment.\n\n4. Reduced consumer confidence: Negative economic news from the eurozone may reduce consumer and business confidence in Ireland, leading to lower spending and investment.\n\n5. Impact on government finances: Lower economic activity means reduced tax revenue (income tax, VAT, corporation tax), making it harder for the government to reduce its budget deficit."}
  ],

  "2013": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Scarcity",
     q:"The fundamental economic problem is one of 'scarcity'. Explain this concept.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Scarcity is the fundamental economic problem. It arises because the supply of resources (factors of production - land, labour, capital, and enterprise) is limited, while human wants and needs are unlimited.\n\nBecause resources are scarce relative to the infinite demands placed on them, society must make choices about how to allocate these limited resources. Every choice involves an opportunity cost - the next best alternative that must be forgone when a decision is made.\n\nFor example, the government must decide how to allocate its limited budget between healthcare, education, housing, and defence. Choosing to spend more on healthcare means less is available for education. This illustrates how scarcity forces trade-offs in economic decision-making."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Marginal Efficiency of Capital",
     q:"Define the 'Marginal Efficiency of Capital' (MEC). Outline two possible reasons for a fall in MEC.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Definition:\nThe Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC) is the extra profit earned as a result of employing one extra unit of capital. It measures the expected rate of return on an additional unit of capital investment.\n\nTwo possible reasons for a fall in MEC:\n\n1. An increase in interest rates: If interest rates rise, the cost of borrowing to finance capital investment increases. The net return (profit) from the additional capital falls, reducing the MEC. Investment projects that were previously profitable may no longer be worthwhile.\n\n2. An increase in the cost of capital goods: If the price of machinery, equipment, or technology increases, the initial cost of investment rises. With higher costs, the expected profit from employing the extra unit of capital decreases, reducing the MEC.\n\nOther valid reasons:\n- A decrease in the price of finished goods (reducing revenue from additional output).\n- A decrease in the productivity of capital (e.g. due to deterioration or obsolescence of capital goods)."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Price Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"Read each statement below and indicate if the price elasticity of demand (PED) for the product is most likely to be elastic or inelastic.\n\n- Consumers are strongly attached and loyal to the product.\n- Many close substitutes are available for the product.\n- The product is a luxury product.\n- The product accounts for only a small fraction of a consumer's weekly expenditure.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"1. Consumers are strongly attached and loyal to the product = INELASTIC\nBrand loyalty means consumers will continue to purchase the product even if the price increases. Their strong attachment makes them less responsive to price changes.\n\n2. Many close substitutes are available for the product = ELASTIC\nWhen many close substitutes are available, consumers can easily switch to a cheaper alternative if the price rises. This makes demand highly responsive to price changes.\n\n3. The product is a luxury product = ELASTIC\nLuxury products are not necessities, so consumers can easily postpone or forgo their purchase if the price increases. This makes demand responsive to price changes.\n\n4. The product accounts for only a small fraction of a consumer's weekly expenditure = INELASTIC\nIf the product represents a tiny proportion of overall spending (e.g. a box of matches), even a significant percentage price increase will have a negligible impact on the consumer's budget. Consumers are unlikely to change their purchasing behaviour for such small amounts."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Collusion & Price Wars",
     q:"(a) Collusion may be a feature of an oligopolistic market. Explain what is meant by 'collusion'.\n\n(b) Collusive practices may be undermined by price wars. Outline two benefits of price wars for the consumer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Collusion:\nCollusion occurs when rival sellers (firms) in an industry come together and agree to act in their mutual benefit rather than competing against each other. This may involve agreeing to fix prices at an artificially high level, divide up markets between them, or restrict output to maintain higher prices. Collusion is illegal in most countries as it harms consumers by eliminating the benefits of competition. A formal collusion agreement is called a cartel.\n\n(b) Two benefits of price wars for the consumer:\n\n1. Lower prices / value for money: When firms engage in price wars, they aggressively reduce prices to attract customers away from competitors. Consumers benefit from the availability of goods and services at much lower prices, getting better value for their limited income.\n\n2. Higher disposable income / improved standard of living: With lower prices, consumers effectively have a higher disposable income. They can either save more or spend the money saved on other goods and services, improving their overall standard of living.\n\nOther valid benefits: More choice (as consumers have greater disposable income, they can choose how to spend the additional income); improved quality (firms may improve product quality alongside price cuts to gain competitive advantage)."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Restricting Free Trade",
     q:"Outline three mechanisms for restricting free trade.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Three mechanisms for restricting free trade:\n\n1. Tariff: A tax imposed on imported goods, making them more expensive and less competitive compared to domestically produced goods. This protects domestic industries by raising the price of imports.\n\n2. Quota: A quantitative limit placed on the amount of a particular good that can be imported into a country during a specified time period. Once the quota is reached, no further imports of that good are allowed.\n\n3. Embargo: A total ban on the import (or export) of a particular good or all goods from a specific country. This is the most extreme form of trade restriction, often used for political or security reasons.\n\nOther valid mechanisms:\n- Subsidy: A payment to domestic exporters to reduce their production costs, making their goods cheaper and more competitive on international markets.\n- Administrative barriers: Rules, regulations, safety standards, and bureaucratic procedures that make it more difficult and costly to import goods.\n- Exchange controls: Limits on the amount of foreign currency available to importers, restricting their ability to purchase imported goods."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Paradox of Thrift",
     q:"Explain what is meant by the economic term 'Paradox of Thrift'.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"The Paradox of Thrift:\n\nThe Paradox of Thrift describes a situation where an increase in saving by individuals could result in a fall in national/total savings, which is the opposite of what savers intended.\n\nThe process works as follows:\n- If many individuals simultaneously decide to save more, they reduce their consumption spending.\n- This reduced consumption leads to decreased aggregate demand in the economy.\n- Firms experience falling sales and revenue, leading to reduced production.\n- Reduced production leads to job losses and falling incomes.\n- With lower incomes, people have less to save, so total savings in the economy actually fall.\n\nIn terms of the circular flow of income, increased savings represent an increased leakage/withdrawal from the circular flow. This diminishes the flow of income in the economy, ultimately making everyone worse off. The paradox is that what appears rational for one individual (saving more) becomes harmful when everyone does it simultaneously.\n\nThis concept was notably associated with the economist John Maynard Keynes, who argued that during recessions, government should encourage spending rather than saving to stimulate economic recovery."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Adam Smith's Contributions",
     q:"Outline four contributions of Adam Smith to economic thought.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Four contributions of Adam Smith to economic thought:\n\n1. The pursuit of self-interest: Smith argued that individuals acting in their own self-interest ultimately benefit society as a whole. When each person seeks to maximise their own welfare, the collective result is efficient production and distribution of goods.\n\n2. The Division of Labour: Smith demonstrated how dividing the production process into specialised tasks dramatically increases productivity and a country's wealth. His famous pin factory example showed that workers specialising in individual tasks could produce far more pins than if each worker made complete pins individually.\n\n3. The 'Invisible Hand' of competition: Smith argued that a self-regulating market, guided by the 'invisible hand' of supply and demand, would allocate resources efficiently without the need for central planning. Competition among firms ensures prices reflect the true cost of production and consumer preferences.\n\n4. Laissez-faire / Limited government intervention: Smith advocated minimal government interference in the economy, arguing that markets function best when left to operate freely. He believed the government's role should be limited to defence, justice, and certain public works.\n\nOther contributions: Labour Theory of Value (the value of a product equals the amount of labour used to produce it); Canons of Taxation (equity, economy, certainty, convenience); Paradox of Value (distinguishing 'value in use' from 'value in exchange'); advocacy of Free Trade."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Average Propensity to Consume",
     q:"Define the term 'Average Propensity to Consume' (APC) and calculate the APC for 2012 from the information below. (Show your workings.)\n\nYear: 2012 | Disposable Income: 34,000 euro | Savings: 5,200 euro\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Definition:\nThe Average Propensity to Consume (APC) is the proportion (fraction) of total disposable income which is spent on goods and services.\n\nFormula: APC = Consumption / Disposable Income\n\nWorkings:\nFirst, calculate consumption:\nConsumption = Disposable Income - Savings\nConsumption = 34,000 - 5,200 = 28,800 euro\n\nThen calculate APC:\nAPC = 28,800 / 34,000\nAPC = 0.847 (or 84.7%)\n\nThis means that for every euro of disposable income earned in 2012, approximately 84.7 cent was spent on consumption (goods and services), while the remaining 15.3 cent was saved.\n\nNote: The Average Propensity to Save (APS) = 1 - APC = 1 - 0.847 = 0.153 (or 15.3%). This can be verified: 5,200 / 34,000 = 0.153."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Alcohol - Private/Social Costs & Minimum Pricing",
     q:"A recent report to the Irish government highlighted the growing issue of alcohol abuse in Ireland.\n\n(a) Outline one private cost and one social cost related to excessive alcohol consumption.\n\n(b) Describe one advantage and one disadvantage of the government imposing a minimum price on alcohol products.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Costs of excessive alcohol consumption:\n\nPrivate cost: Decreased disposable income - excessive spending on alcohol reduces the individual's income available for other essential goods and services. Additionally, the possibility of long-term illness and health deterioration (liver damage, addiction) creates significant personal health costs.\n\nSocial cost: Increased healthcare costs - the wider society bears the cost of treating alcohol-related illnesses through higher public health expenditure. Hospital A&E departments must deal with alcohol-related injuries and admissions, diverting resources from other patients. These costs fall on taxpayers who may not consume alcohol excessively.\n\nOther valid costs:\n- Private: Absenteeism from work/reduced career opportunities; increased private insurance costs; decreased productivity.\n- Social: Increased crime/vandalism/road accidents; disruption to public services; opportunity cost of money spent on healthcare.\n\n(b) Minimum price on alcohol:\n\nAdvantage: Reduction in alcohol consumption - a minimum price increases the cost of cheap alcohol products (particularly those targeted at young people), which should reduce consumption levels, especially binge drinking. This leads to reduced healthcare costs, fewer alcohol-related crimes and accidents, and better use of scarce hospital resources.\n\nDisadvantage: Increased prices for consumers / reduced disposable income - a minimum price acts as a regressive measure, hitting lower-income consumers hardest. It reduces their purchasing power and may not significantly affect higher-income consumers who can afford the price increase. There is also a risk of increased cross-border shopping for cheaper alcohol or growth in black market activity."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Demand, Substitution & Income Effects",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between the terms 'effective demand' and 'derived demand'.\n(ii) Outline two possible exceptions to the Law of Demand. (25 marks)\n\n(b) The market for a brand of blue jeans is in equilibrium. Explain, with the aid of a separate diagram in each case, the effects which each of the following is most likely to have on the equilibrium position:\n(i) Due to the economic downturn there is a reduction in the real income of consumers.\n(ii) A fall in the price of cotton, a key input in the production of the blue jeans.\n(iii) The blue jeans have recently been endorsed by a popular sports star. (30 marks)\n\n(c) A fall in the price of a consumer product has both a substitution effect and an income effect.\n(i) Explain the underlined terms.\n(ii) If the price of an inferior product falls (all other things being equal), will more or less of the product be purchased? Explain your answer with reference to the substitution effect and the income effect. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Effective demand vs Derived demand:\n\nEffective demand: Demand that is supported by the necessary purchasing power (ability to pay). It is not simply wanting a good - the consumer must have both the desire and the financial means to purchase it. For example, many people may want a luxury car, but only those with sufficient income have effective demand.\n\nDerived demand: Where a factor of production is demanded not for its own sake but for its contribution to the production process. The demand for the factor is derived from the demand for the final product it helps produce. For example, the demand for bricklayers is derived from the demand for new houses.\n\n(ii) Two exceptions to the Law of Demand:\n\n1. Giffen Goods: As the price falls, consumers' real income increases and they buy less of these inferior goods, switching to better-quality alternatives. Conversely, when the price rises, consumers have less income to spend on other goods, so they devote more of their budget to the Giffen good (e.g. potatoes during the Irish Famine).\n\n2. Goods of conspicuous consumption (Veblen goods / snob goods): A rise in price makes these goods more exclusive and therefore more desirable to wealthy consumers who value status. A fall in price may actually reduce demand as the goods lose their prestige and exclusivity.\n\nOther exceptions: Speculative goods (consumers buy more when prices rise, expecting further increases); goods of addiction (demand may increase even when price rises).\n\n(b) Effects on the blue jeans market equilibrium:\n\n(i) Reduction in real income:\nThe demand curve shifts to the LEFT from D1 to D2. With lower real incomes, consumers can afford fewer jeans at every price level. As a result, the equilibrium price falls from P1 to P2, and the equilibrium quantity falls from Q1 to Q2.\nDiagram: Downward-sloping demand curve shifts left, supply unchanged, new lower equilibrium.\n\n(ii) Fall in the price of cotton:\nThe supply curve shifts to the RIGHT from S1 to S2. Lower input costs reduce the cost of production, so firms can supply more jeans at every price level. The equilibrium price falls from P1 to P2, and the equilibrium quantity increases from Q1 to Q2.\nDiagram: Upward-sloping supply curve shifts right, demand unchanged, new lower price, higher quantity.\n\n(iii) Endorsed by a popular sports star:\nThe demand curve shifts to the RIGHT from D1 to D2. The celebrity endorsement increases consumer taste and preference for these jeans, increasing demand at every price level. The equilibrium price rises from P1 to P2, and the equilibrium quantity increases from Q1 to Q2.\nDiagram: Downward-sloping demand curve shifts right, supply unchanged, new higher equilibrium.\n\n(c)(i) Substitution effect and Income effect:\n\nSubstitution effect: When the price of a good falls, it becomes relatively cheaper compared to substitute goods. Consumers switch towards the now-cheaper good and away from substitutes to maximise their utility. The substitution effect always leads to an increase in quantity demanded when price falls.\n\nIncome effect: When the price of a good falls, the consumer's real income (purchasing power) effectively rises - they can now afford to buy more goods overall with the same money income.\n\n(ii) Effect of a price fall on an inferior product:\n\nSubstitution effect: Demand will RISE. The inferior product is now cheaper relative to substitutes, so consumers will substitute towards it, increasing quantity demanded.\n\nIncome effect: Demand will FALL. Because the good is inferior, the increase in real income (from the lower price) leads consumers to buy less of it and switch to better-quality goods.\n\nOverall: If the positive substitution effect is greater than the negative income effect, then more of the product will be purchased (this is the normal case for most inferior goods). However, if the negative income effect outweighs the positive substitution effect, less will be purchased (this is the rare Giffen good case)."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Monopoly & Advertising",
     q:"(a) Technology companies such as Apple and Samsung are currently involved in legal disputes regarding patents on various aspects of their smartphones. When a company wins a patent dispute it may become the sole producer using the patented technology.\n\n(i) Explain three barriers to entry, other than patents, that can exist in business.\n(ii) Outline two reasons why monopolies may not be in the public interest. (25 marks)\n\n(b) Explain, with the aid of a diagram, the long run equilibrium position of a monopoly firm. (25 marks)\n\n(c) The global market for toothpaste products can be described as an imperfectly competitive market, where firms engage in competitive advertising and branding.\n(i) Explain the term 'competitive advertising'.\n(ii) Outline, using an example, how advertising can be used to prevent small firms entering an industry.\n(iii) State and explain two possible disadvantages of advertising for the consumer. (25 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Three barriers to entry (other than patents):\n\n1. Economies of scale: Large established firms can produce at much lower average costs due to their scale of operations. New entrants, starting with small output levels, face much higher average costs and cannot compete on price. The cost advantage acts as a natural barrier.\n\n2. Brand loyalty and heavy advertising: Established firms may have built strong brand recognition and customer loyalty through years of advertising and marketing. New entrants must spend heavily on advertising to establish themselves, which significantly increases start-up costs and risk.\n\n3. Control of essential resources or technology: If a firm controls access to a key raw material, distribution channel, or proprietary technology, it can prevent competitors from entering the market. For example, a firm that owns the only source of a rare mineral needed for production.\n\nOther barriers: High initial capital costs; legal barriers (government licenses, franchises); limit pricing (incumbent sets price low enough to deter entry).\n\n(ii) Two reasons monopolies may not be in the public interest:\n\n1. Higher prices and lower output: A monopolist restricts output below the competitive level and charges higher prices. Since there is no competition, consumers have no alternative and must pay the monopoly price. This leads to a loss of consumer surplus and allocative inefficiency.\n\n2. Productive inefficiency: Without competitive pressure, monopolies may become complacent and fail to minimise costs. They may not innovate or seek efficiencies because there is no threat from competitors. This is sometimes called 'X-inefficiency'.\n\n(b) Long-run monopoly equilibrium:\nDiagram: Downward-sloping demand curve (AR) with MR curve below it (also downward-sloping but steeper). U-shaped AC and MC curves. Equilibrium at point where MC = MR.\n\nExplanation: The monopolist maximises profit by producing where MC = MR (and MC cuts MR from below). The profit-maximising output is at Q1. The price is determined by reading up from Q1 to the demand/AR curve, giving price P1. Since the AR (price) is above the AC at output Q1, the monopolist earns supernormal profit, shown by the area between AR and AC over the output Q1.\n\nCrucially, unlike perfect competition, these supernormal profits persist in the long run because barriers to entry prevent new firms from entering the market and competing away the profits. The monopolist does not produce at the minimum point of AC, so is not productively efficient.\n\n(c)(i) Competitive advertising:\nCompetitive advertising is advertising used by firms in an imperfectly competitive market to persuade consumers that their product is different from and superior to rival products. Its purpose is to increase market share by attracting customers away from competitors, rather than to inform. It emphasises brand image, product differentiation, and perceived quality differences.\n\n(ii) Advertising as a barrier to entry:\nLarge established firms can spend enormous sums on advertising, creating strong brand awareness and customer loyalty that new, smaller firms cannot match. For example, Colgate and Sensodyne spend millions annually on TV, digital, and print advertising for their toothpaste brands. A small new toothpaste manufacturer would struggle to gain market visibility against these advertising budgets, making it extremely difficult to attract customers and establish a viable business.\n\n(iii) Two disadvantages of advertising for consumers:\n\n1. Higher prices: The cost of advertising campaigns is passed on to consumers through higher product prices. Consumers pay for the advertising whether they benefit from it or not, as it is included in the final selling price.\n\n2. Misleading/manipulative information: Advertising may exaggerate product benefits, create artificial wants, or use emotional manipulation to persuade consumers to buy products they do not need. This can lead to consumer exploitation and irrational purchasing decisions, moving consumers away from maximising their utility."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Labour Market & Gender Pay Gap",
     q:"(a) State and explain four economic influences on an individual firm's demand for labour. (25 marks)\n\n(b) Explain, with the aid of a labour market diagram in each case, how equilibrium wage rates are determined in:\n- A free labour market\n- A labour market where a trade union has negotiated a minimum wage. (25 marks)\n\n(c) Women in Ireland earn on average 17.1% less per hour than men, according to a study by the European Commission (2011).\n(i) Outline two possible reasons for the lower wage rates earned by women in the Irish economy.\n(ii) Outline one measure that could be taken to close the gender pay gap. (15 marks)\n\n(d) Explain briefly Karl Marx's economic theory about the exploitation of labour in a capitalist system. (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) Four economic influences on a firm's demand for labour:\n\n1. Demand for the firm's product (derived demand): The demand for labour is derived from the demand for the goods or services the firm produces. If demand for the product increases, the firm will need more workers to increase output. Conversely, falling product demand reduces labour demand.\n\n2. Wage rate: The cost of employing labour directly affects demand. If wages increase, the cost of production rises, and the firm may demand less labour (substituting capital for labour if possible). Lower wages make labour relatively cheaper and increase demand.\n\n3. Productivity of labour / Marginal Revenue Product (MRP): A firm will employ additional workers as long as the extra revenue generated by the worker (MRP) exceeds the wage cost. More productive workers generate more revenue, increasing demand for labour.\n\n4. Price and availability of substitute factors: If the cost of capital (machinery/technology) falls, firms may substitute capital for labour, reducing demand for workers. Conversely, if capital is expensive or unavailable, demand for labour increases.\n\nOther influences: Government incentives/subsidies; entrepreneurs' expectations about future demand; technological change; cost of training.\n\n(b) Equilibrium wage determination:\n\nFree labour market:\nDiagram: Downward-sloping demand curve for labour (DL = MRP) and upward-sloping supply curve for labour (SL). They intersect at equilibrium point E.\n\nThe equilibrium wage rate (We) and employment level (Le) are determined where the demand for labour equals the supply of labour. The demand curve slopes downward because of diminishing marginal returns - each additional worker adds less output. The supply curve slopes upward because higher wages attract more workers into the market.\n\nLabour market with trade union minimum wage:\nDiagram: Same DL and SL curves, but with a horizontal line above We at Wm (the minimum wage set by the trade union).\n\nThe trade union negotiates a minimum wage (Wm) above the market equilibrium wage (We). At Wm, the quantity of labour supplied (Ls) exceeds the quantity demanded (Ld), creating excess supply of labour (unemployment). Workers benefit from higher wages, but some workers lose their jobs as firms reduce their workforce due to the higher wage cost. The trade union has improved wages for those who remain employed but reduced overall employment.\n\n(c)(i) Two reasons for lower wages for women:\n\n1. Career breaks: Women are more likely to take career breaks for maternity leave and childcare, resulting in less work experience and slower career progression. These breaks may result in fewer promotions and lower pay relative to male counterparts who have continuous employment histories.\n\n2. Occupational segregation: Women tend to be concentrated in lower-paying sectors and occupations (e.g. caring, retail, hospitality) while higher-paying sectors (e.g. technology, finance, engineering) remain male-dominated. This horizontal segregation contributes to the overall pay gap.\n\n(ii) Measure to close the gender pay gap:\nLegislation requiring pay transparency: The government could introduce mandatory gender pay gap reporting, requiring companies above a certain size to publish data on the difference between average male and female pay. This transparency creates public accountability and pressures firms to take action to close pay gaps. The Gender Pay Gap Information Act (introduced in Ireland) is an example of such legislation.\n\n(d) Karl Marx's theory of exploitation of labour:\nMarx argued that in a capitalist system, workers (the proletariat) are exploited by the owners of capital (the bourgeoisie). Workers are paid wages that represent only a fraction of the value they create through their labour - this is the 'subsistence wage,' just enough to keep workers alive and able to work. The difference between the value created by the worker's labour and the wage they receive is called 'surplus value.' This surplus value is taken by the capitalist as profit. Marx argued this exploitation was inherent in the capitalist system, as workers do not own the means of production and must sell their labour to survive, giving capitalists the power to extract surplus value from their work."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Budget Deficit & Taxation",
     q:"(a)(i) 'The Exchequer deficit in December 2012 was 14.89 billion euro.' (Department of Finance, January 2013).\nDiscuss four possible economic consequences of the Government Current Budget Deficit for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Explain how a government budget could be used to reduce income inequalities in an economy. (30 marks)\n\n(b)(i) State and explain two reasons why the government increased taxes on cigarettes and alcohol in its most recent budget.\n(ii) Outline three economic impacts for the Irish economy of an increase in Motor Tax. (30 marks)\n\n(c) 'The Public Capital Programme must make a further contribution to budgetary consolidation.' (Infrastructure and Capital Investment, 2012-2016).\nAs an Advisor to the Minister for Public Expenditure and Reform, discuss two economic arguments in favour of a reduction in capital expenditure in the Budget. (15 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Four economic consequences of the Budget Deficit:\n\n1. Increased national debt: The government must borrow to fund the deficit, increasing the national debt. This creates a burden for future generations who will have to repay the debt and pay interest on it, diverting resources from productive spending.\n\n2. Increased interest payments: As the national debt grows, the government's annual interest payments increase. This is 'dead money' that cannot be spent on public services, infrastructure, or social welfare. It represents a significant opportunity cost.\n\n3. Reduced credit rating: A persistent deficit may lead international credit rating agencies to downgrade Ireland's credit rating, making it more expensive for the government to borrow and potentially shaking investor confidence.\n\n4. Austerity measures: To reduce the deficit, the government may need to cut spending or increase taxes. Spending cuts can reduce public services (healthcare, education), while tax increases reduce disposable income, both of which can slow economic recovery.\n\nOther consequences: Higher taxes to service debt; reduced investor confidence; pressure from the Troika/EU to implement fiscal adjustment.\n\n(ii) Using the budget to reduce income inequality:\nThe government can use its budget to redistribute income from higher earners to lower earners through a combination of progressive taxation and targeted social welfare spending:\n\n- Progressive taxation: Higher income earners pay a greater proportion of their income in tax (e.g. higher PAYE rates, USC surcharges on high earners). This reduces the after-tax income gap.\n- Social welfare payments: Transfer payments (jobseeker's allowance, child benefit, pension) provide income support to those on lowest incomes.\n- Free public services: Providing free education, healthcare (medical cards), and housing supports benefits those on lower incomes disproportionately.\n- Tax credits and allowances: Targeted at lower and middle-income earners to increase their take-home pay.\n\n(b)(i) Two reasons for increasing taxes on cigarettes and alcohol:\n\n1. Revenue generation: Cigarettes and alcohol have price inelastic demand (consumers are addicted or habitual buyers), so tax increases generate significant additional revenue for the Exchequer without a large fall in quantity demanded.\n\n2. Public health / discouraging consumption: Higher taxes increase prices, acting as a disincentive to consume these harmful products. This may reduce smoking rates and alcohol consumption, improving public health outcomes and reducing the burden on the healthcare system.\n\n(ii) Three economic impacts of an increase in Motor Tax:\n\n1. Increased government revenue: Motor tax generates revenue for local authorities and the government, which can be used to fund road maintenance, infrastructure, and public services.\n\n2. Increased cost of motoring: Higher motor tax increases the overall cost of running a vehicle, which may disproportionately affect rural dwellers who have no public transport alternative and rely on cars.\n\n3. Environmental benefit: If the motor tax system is linked to emissions (as in Ireland), higher taxes encourage consumers to purchase lower-emission vehicles, supporting environmental objectives and reducing carbon output.\n\nOther impacts: May act as a disincentive to car ownership; could increase costs for businesses reliant on transport; may be regressive if it does not account for ability to pay.\n\n(c) Two arguments in favour of reducing capital expenditure:\n\n1. Immediate deficit reduction: Cutting capital expenditure provides an immediate reduction in government spending, directly reducing the budget deficit. This is important to meet the deficit targets agreed with the Troika (EU/ECB/IMF) and maintain access to bailout funding. It demonstrates fiscal discipline to international markets.\n\n2. Debt sustainability: Reducing capital spending helps slow the growth of the national debt, making it more manageable in the long term. Lower debt reduces interest payments, freeing up resources for current spending on essential services like healthcare and social welfare that directly affect citizens' quality of life."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): National Income & Keynesian Stimulus",
     q:"(a) Explain the following terms which are commonly used in estimating the National Income statistics of a country:\n- Subsidies\n- Incomes-in-kind\n- Net Factor Income from the Rest of the World (15 marks)\n\n(b)(i) Illustrate by means of a diagram the Circular Flow of Income for an open economy.\n(ii) Are transfer payments an injection into, or a leakage from the Circular Flow of Income? Explain your answer.\n(iii) Outline three current determinants of the level of consumption in the Irish economy. (35 marks)\n\n(c)(i) Using the Keynesian multiplier process, outline how a fiscal stimulus (i.e. a government injection) would affect an economy's Aggregate Demand.\n(ii) Explain why the Irish government might find it difficult to implement such a Keynesian stimulus plan at the current time. (25 marks)",
     a:"(a) National Income terms:\n\nSubsidies: Payments made by the government to producers of goods and services to reduce their costs of production and lower prices for consumers. Subsidies are not earned income but represent a transfer from the government. When calculating national income at factor cost from market prices, subsidies must be added back (as they were not included in market prices). Examples: EU Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) payments to farmers.\n\nIncomes-in-kind: Non-cash benefits received by workers as part of their remuneration, in addition to their monetary wages. These have a monetary value and must be included in national income calculations. Examples: company car, subsidised housing, free meals, health insurance provided by an employer.\n\nNet Factor Income from the Rest of the World (NFIROW): The difference between income earned by Irish residents and Irish-owned firms from their activities abroad (inflow) and income earned by foreign residents and foreign-owned firms from their activities in Ireland (outflow). In Ireland, NFIROW is typically negative because large multinational companies repatriate substantial profits to their home countries. NFIROW is used to convert GDP to GNP: GNP = GDP + NFIROW.\n\n(b)(i) Circular Flow of Income diagram should show:\n- Two main sectors: Households and Firms\n- Inner flow: Factors of production from households to firms; Income (wages, rent, interest, profit) from firms to households\n- Outer flow: Expenditure on goods/services from households to firms; Goods and services from firms to households\n- Three injections: Investment (I), Government Spending (G), Exports (X)\n- Three leakages: Savings (S), Taxation (T), Imports (M)\n\n(ii) Transfer payments are NEITHER a true injection nor leakage - they are a redistribution of income:\nTransfer payments (e.g. social welfare, pensions, child benefit) are payments from the government to individuals for which no goods or services are provided in return. They are funded from taxation and represent a redistribution of income within the economy rather than new income creation. They are not included in national income calculations as they do not represent payment for productive activity. However, they do increase recipients' disposable income, which is then spent (becoming consumption) or saved.\n\n(iii) Three determinants of consumption in Ireland (2013 context):\n\n1. Disposable income levels: With high unemployment and reduced wages during the recession, many households had lower disposable incomes, reducing their ability to consume.\n\n2. Consumer confidence: Low confidence in the economic outlook caused consumers to postpone major purchases and increase precautionary saving, reducing consumption.\n\n3. Availability of credit: Following the banking crisis, banks tightened lending criteria, making it more difficult for consumers to access credit for purchases such as cars and home improvements.\n\nOther determinants: Rate of taxation; price levels/inflation; expectations about future income/employment; interest rates; level of personal debt.\n\n(c)(i) Keynesian multiplier and fiscal stimulus:\nA fiscal stimulus involves the government increasing its spending (an injection into the circular flow). According to the Keynesian multiplier process:\n\n- The government injects spending into the economy (e.g. investing in infrastructure projects).\n- This creates income for workers and suppliers, who then spend a proportion of this income on goods and services (determined by the MPC).\n- This spending becomes income for other firms and workers, who in turn spend a proportion of their new income.\n- Each round of spending creates further income and spending, though the amount diminishes in each round due to leakages (savings, taxation, imports).\n- The total increase in Aggregate Demand is a multiple of the original injection: Change in National Income = Multiplier x Change in Government Spending.\n\n(ii) Why Ireland might find it difficult to implement a Keynesian stimulus:\n\n1. EU fiscal rules / Troika conditions: Ireland was in an EU/IMF bailout programme with strict conditions on deficit reduction. The Troika required Ireland to cut spending and increase taxes, not increase government expenditure. Implementing a stimulus would breach these agreements.\n\n2. High national debt: Ireland's national debt was already very high (over 120% of GDP). Further borrowing to fund a stimulus would increase the debt burden, raise interest payments, and potentially lead to a credit rating downgrade, making future borrowing even more expensive.\n\n3. Limited borrowing capacity: International markets may not lend to Ireland at affordable interest rates given the already high debt levels. Ireland had lost market access (hence the bailout) and could not easily borrow additional funds.\n\n4. Open economy leakages: As a small, open economy, Ireland has a high marginal propensity to import. Much of any stimulus spending would leak out of the economy through imports, reducing the multiplier effect and making the stimulus less effective."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): Banking, Interest Rates & CPI",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain, using a numerical example, how banks create credit in an economy.\n(ii) Outline two factors which limit the ability of banks to create credit during recessionary times. (30 marks)\n\n(b) Some central banks have responded to the global financial crisis by introducing the monetary policy measure of 'Quantitative Easing' (i.e. buying financial assets from financial institutions using new money it has created).\n(i) Outline two possible economic effects of this measure for an economy.\n\nThe European Central Bank (ECB) reduced interest rates in 2012.\n(ii) Discuss two possible economic benefits of falling interest rates for the Irish economy. (20 marks)\n\n(c) The monetary policy of the ECB aims to maintain the annual euro area inflation rate at a very low level.\n(i) State the rate of inflation in Ireland during 2013, as measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI).\n(ii) Outline two uses of the CPI, other than as a measure of the rate of inflation.\n(iii) Discuss the possible limitations of the CPI as an accurate measure of changes in the cost of living in Ireland. (25 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) How banks create credit (credit creation / credit multiplier):\n\nBanks create credit through the process of accepting deposits and making loans, keeping only a fraction of deposits as reserves.\n\nNumerical example (assuming a 10% reserve ratio / credit multiplier of 10):\n- A customer deposits 1,000 euro in Bank A.\n- Bank A keeps 100 euro (10%) as reserves and lends out 900 euro.\n- The 900 euro is spent and deposited in Bank B.\n- Bank B keeps 90 euro (10%) as reserves and lends out 810 euro.\n- The 810 euro is spent and deposited in Bank C.\n- Bank C keeps 81 euro and lends out 729 euro.\n- This process continues until the total credit created = Original Deposit x Credit Multiplier = 1,000 x (1/0.10) = 10,000 euro.\n\nThe credit multiplier = 1 / Reserve Ratio = 1 / 0.10 = 10.\nSo an initial deposit of 1,000 euro can create up to 10,000 euro of credit in the banking system.\n\n(ii) Two factors limiting credit creation during recessions:\n\n1. Reduced demand for loans: During a recession, businesses and consumers are pessimistic about the future. Businesses are reluctant to invest and consumers are reluctant to borrow, so demand for credit falls regardless of how much banks could potentially lend.\n\n2. Increased bank caution / tighter lending criteria: Banks become more risk-averse during recessions, fearing loan defaults. They increase their reserve ratios, demand more collateral, and apply stricter credit assessments, reducing the amount of credit they are willing to create.\n\nOther factors: Falling asset values reduce collateral; central bank regulation may require higher capital reserves; existing bad debts reduce banks' capacity to lend.\n\n(b)(i) Two economic effects of Quantitative Easing (QE):\n\n1. Increased money supply and liquidity: By purchasing financial assets from banks, the central bank injects new money into the banking system. This increases banks' reserves and liquidity, enabling them to lend more to businesses and consumers, stimulating economic activity.\n\n2. Lower long-term interest rates: QE increases demand for government bonds and other financial assets, pushing up their prices and reducing their yields (interest rates). Lower interest rates encourage borrowing and investment, stimulating aggregate demand.\n\nOther effects: Risk of inflation if too much money is created; depreciation of the currency (making exports more competitive); risk of asset price bubbles.\n\n(ii) Two benefits of falling interest rates for Ireland:\n\n1. Reduced mortgage payments: Lower interest rates reduce monthly mortgage repayments for homeowners on variable rate mortgages, increasing their disposable income. This additional spending power stimulates consumer spending and supports economic recovery.\n\n2. Cheaper borrowing for businesses: Lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing for firms, making investment in new equipment, expansion, and hiring more financially attractive. This can stimulate economic growth and job creation.\n\n(c)(i) Rate of inflation in Ireland in 2013:\nThe rate of inflation in Ireland in 2013, as measured by the CPI, was approximately 0.5% (very low inflation, close to deflation).\n\n(ii) Two uses of the CPI (other than measuring inflation):\n\n1. Wage negotiations: Trade unions and employers use the CPI to negotiate wage increases. If the CPI shows prices have risen by a certain percentage, workers may seek at least that level of wage increase to maintain their purchasing power.\n\n2. Government policy: The government uses the CPI to adjust social welfare payments, tax bands, and pensions. If the CPI shows rising prices, the government may increase these payments to protect citizens' standard of living.\n\nOther uses: Comparing living standards over time; adjusting contracts (e.g. rent reviews); calculating real interest rates; index-linking financial products.\n\n(iii) Limitations of the CPI:\n\n1. Representative basket: The CPI is based on a 'basket' of goods and services that represents the spending pattern of an average household. However, no household is 'average' - individual spending patterns may differ significantly from the basket. For example, non-smokers are unaffected by changes in cigarette prices, but the CPI includes them.\n\n2. Quality changes: The CPI may not adequately account for improvements in product quality. If the price of a computer increases but its processing power has doubled, the CPI may record this as inflation when the consumer is actually getting better value.\n\n3. New products: The basket is updated periodically but may not quickly include new products (e.g. smartphones, streaming services) that have become significant expenditure items.\n\n4. Regional variations: Prices can vary significantly between Dublin and rural Ireland, but the CPI produces a single national figure that may not accurately reflect the cost of living in any particular location.\n\n5. Housing costs: The treatment of housing costs (particularly mortgage interest and rent) in the CPI can be problematic and may not fully capture the true cost of housing."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): Balance of Payments & Rising Euro",
     q:"(a) 'The Balance of Payments figures give the most detailed information on Ireland's large and rapidly growing internationally traded services sector.' (The Irish Times, September 2012)\n\n(i) Explain the term Balance of Payments Current Account.\n(ii) Explain two economic consequences of a surplus on the Balance of Payments Current Account.\n(iii) Describe how foreign firms operating in Ireland may affect Ireland's Balance of Payments Current Account. (30 marks)\n\n(b) Discuss the possible economic effects for the Irish economy of the euro rising in value relative to many other international currencies. (25 marks)\n\n(c) Discuss two challenges which Irish firms face when selling their products in foreign markets. (20 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Balance of Payments Current Account:\nThe Balance of Payments Current Account is a record of all international transactions involving the flow of goods, services, and income between Ireland and the rest of the world over a given period (usually one year). It consists of:\n- The trade balance (visible trade): exports and imports of physical goods.\n- The services balance (invisible trade): exports and imports of services (e.g. tourism, financial services, software).\n- Income flows: wages, interest, dividends, and profits flowing in and out of the country.\n- Current transfers: payments such as EU contributions and remittances.\n\n(ii) Two economic consequences of a surplus:\n\n1. Increased economic growth: A surplus means exports exceed imports, indicating strong demand for Irish goods and services abroad. This export-led growth increases national income and GDP, creating employment and raising living standards.\n\n2. Strengthening of the currency / increased reserves: A persistent surplus increases demand for the country's currency (as foreign buyers need it to pay for exports), potentially strengthening the exchange rate. For eurozone members, the surplus increases foreign currency reserves.\n\nOther consequences: Increased employment in export sectors; increased tax revenue; improved investor confidence; potential inflationary pressure if the economy overheats.\n\n(iii) Foreign firms' impact on Ireland's Balance of Payments:\n\nPositive impact: Foreign firms (MNCs) operating in Ireland produce goods and services for export, significantly boosting Ireland's export figures and contributing to a trade surplus. Ireland's pharmaceutical, technology, and financial services exports are largely generated by foreign-owned firms.\n\nNegative impact: Foreign firms repatriate profits to their home countries, creating large outflows on the income account of the current account. This profit repatriation is the main reason Ireland's Net Factor Income from Abroad is strongly negative. They also import raw materials and components, increasing the import bill. Additionally, foreign workers employed by these firms may send remittances abroad.\n\n(b) Effects of a rising euro on Ireland:\n\n1. More expensive exports: A stronger euro makes Irish exports more expensive for non-eurozone buyers (e.g. US, UK, China). This reduces the price competitiveness of Irish goods and services abroad, potentially leading to lower export volumes and reduced revenue for Irish firms.\n\n2. Cheaper imports: A stronger euro makes imports from non-eurozone countries cheaper. This benefits Irish consumers (lower prices for imported goods) and firms (cheaper raw materials and components). However, it also makes foreign goods more competitive in the Irish market, potentially harming domestic producers.\n\n3. Reduced tourism: A stronger euro makes Ireland a more expensive destination for tourists from non-eurozone countries. This could reduce tourist numbers and spending, negatively affecting the hospitality and tourism sector.\n\n4. Lower imported inflation: Cheaper imports help keep the price level down, contributing to price stability. This is beneficial for consumers' purchasing power.\n\n5. Impact on FDI: MNCs producing in Ireland for export to non-eurozone markets may find their operations less profitable due to the stronger euro, potentially discouraging future FDI or encouraging relocation.\n\n(c) Two challenges for Irish firms selling abroad:\n\n1. Exchange rate risk: Irish firms exporting outside the eurozone face the risk of currency fluctuations. If the euro strengthens against the buyer's currency, the Irish firm's products become more expensive, reducing competitiveness. This uncertainty makes it difficult to plan and price goods for foreign markets. Hedging against currency risk adds to business costs.\n\n2. Competition from lower-cost economies: Irish firms face intense competition from producers in countries with lower labour costs, lower taxes, and fewer regulations. Countries in Asia and Eastern Europe can produce many goods at significantly lower costs, making it difficult for Irish firms to compete on price. Irish firms must instead compete on quality, innovation, and branding, which requires ongoing investment."}
  ],

"2014": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Opportunity Cost",
     q:"(a) Define the term 'opportunity cost'.\n\n(b) State one example of an opportunity cost facing the Irish Government.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Definition:\nOpportunity cost is the cost of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is the cost of something expressed in terms of what must be given up in order to obtain it.\n\n(b) Example:\nIf the Irish Government decides to spend money on building a new hospital, the opportunity cost could be the infrastructural project (e.g. motorway, school) which must be deferred due to limited resources. Other examples include: cuts in taxes imply less money to spend on other projects; the interest payments on government debt involve a sacrifice of government services such as education or healthcare."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Marginal Cost & Average Cost Relationship",
     q:"If the marginal cost (MC) is less than average cost (AC), then AC must be falling when output increases.\n\n(i) Is this statement true? Circle the correct answer: YES / NO\n\n(ii) Explain the reason for your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(i) YES - the statement is true.\n\n(ii) Explanation:\nIf the additional cost of producing one extra unit (marginal cost) is less than the current average total cost, then producing that extra unit will pull the average total cost down. Think of it like exam scores: if you score below your current average on a new test, your overall average falls. Similarly, when MC < AC, each additional unit produced costs less than the current average, so the average cost must be decreasing as output increases."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Structural Unemployment",
     q:"(a) Define the term 'structural unemployment'.\n\n(b) Outline one policy response that the Irish Government could take to reduce it.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Definition:\nStructural unemployment arises from a change in the structure of an industry brought about by the changing pattern of demand in the economy or by increasing mechanisation (technical progress). Workers become unemployed because their skills no longer match the jobs available in the economy.\n\n(b) Government policy response:\nA retraining scheme so that long-term unemployed workers can be upskilled for the jobs available and then rejoin the workforce, e.g. the Momentum Programme or the Government's Springboard programme, which provides third-level courses aimed at increasing graduates in areas where job vacancies exist. By investing in education and retraining, the government helps workers transition from declining sectors to growing sectors of the economy."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Complementary Goods",
     q:"(a) Explain the meaning of the term 'complementary goods' (i.e. joint demand).\n\n(b) State one example.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Explanation:\nComplementary goods are goods which are consumed together or used in conjunction with one another. A rise in the price of one good will lead to a decrease in the demand for the other good. They have a negative cross-price elasticity of demand (CED). When two goods are complements, they are said to be in joint demand.\n\n(b) Example:\nComputer consoles and software/games are complementary goods. If the price of a PlayStation console increases significantly, fewer consumers will buy the console, and demand for PlayStation games will also fall. Other examples include: cars and petrol, printers and ink cartridges, smartphones and phone cases."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Assumptions of Monopoly",
     q:"State three assumptions/characteristics underlying the theory of monopoly.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Three assumptions/characteristics of monopoly:\n\n1. There is only one firm in the industry - the firm is the industry. The monopolist is the sole supplier of the good or service in the market.\n\n2. Barriers to entry exist in the industry - other firms are prevented from entering the market by obstacles such as patents, government licenses, high start-up costs, or control of essential resources.\n\n3. A unique product is sold with no close substitutes - consumers cannot switch to an alternative product, giving the monopolist significant market power.\n\nOther valid characteristics include:\n- The firm is a price maker: it controls either the price or the output (but not both simultaneously).\n- The firm aims to maximise profits by producing where MC = MR."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Euro Appreciation vs US Dollar",
     q:"Outline two possible economic effects for the Irish Economy of the euro (EUR) appreciating in value against the US dollar ($).\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"1. Import prices are cheaper:\nThe price of imports from the US becomes cheaper for Irish consumers and businesses. It takes fewer euro to buy the same amount of dollars, so goods imported from the US become less expensive. This reduces costs of production for Irish firms that rely on US imports (e.g. raw materials, technology) and lowers prices for consumers.\n\n2. Export prices become dearer / less competitive:\nIrish exports to the US become more expensive in dollar terms. US consumers must pay more dollars to buy the same euro-priced goods. This may result in reduced demand for Irish exports to the US, potentially leading to job losses in export-dependent industries.\n\nOther valid effects include:\n- Falling employment in export sectors dependent on US trade.\n- Reduced tourism revenue from US visitors, as Ireland becomes more expensive for American tourists.\n- FDI from the US may be discouraged, as it is now costlier in dollar terms to invest in Ireland.\n- Irish investment in the US may be encouraged, as it is cheaper in euro terms."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Return to Enterprise",
     q:"Outline two ways in which the return to the factor of production enterprise differs from the return to other factors of production.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"1. Enterprise can earn a loss:\nIt is the only factor of production that can earn a negative return (a loss). This can occur due to a fall in demand for the firm's products or excessive production costs. The other factors of production (land, labour, capital) receive contractual payments (rent, wages, interest) regardless of whether the business makes a profit.\n\n2. The return is residual:\nThe entrepreneur receives their return only after all other factors of production have been paid. Wages, rent, and interest are agreed in advance and paid first. Whatever remains (the residual) is the entrepreneur's profit or loss. The returns to other factors are contractual and fixed in advance.\n\n3. Returns can vary enormously:\nThe returns to enterprise can range from supernormal profits to significant losses, unlike the relatively stable contractual payments to other factors."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Troika Bailout Exit",
     q:"Ireland recently emerged from the EU/IMF/ECB 'Troika Bailout Programme' (December 15th 2013).\n\nOutline two possible economic implications for the Irish economy of having exited this programme.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"1. Increased consumer and business confidence:\nExiting the bailout programme sends a positive signal about the recovery of the Irish economy. Consumers may feel more confident, leading to increased spending, which boosts demand, employment, and tax revenue for the government.\n\n2. Increased economic sovereignty:\nThe Irish Government regains more scope to make its own taxation and expenditure decisions. Under the Troika programme, Ireland's fiscal policy was heavily dictated by the EU/IMF/ECB. Exiting means the government has a greater degree of control over the national budget.\n\nOther valid implications include:\n- Improved global perception of the Irish economy, sending a positive signal to bond markets and international investors, potentially reducing the interest rate on government debt and attracting more FDI.\n- Risk that it may be more difficult for the Irish Government to maintain fiscal discipline without the direct supervision of the Troika, potentially leading to inappropriate fiscal policies.\n- Annual budget will still be scrutinised by Brussels under new EU rules designed to prevent future public finance crises."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Social Costs & Benefits (EirGrid Pylons)",
     q:"Outline two possible social costs and two possible social benefits of an investment by 'EirGrid' in upgrading Ireland's electricity network by erecting high-voltage power lines (pylons).\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Social Costs:\n\n1. Visual intrusion on the landscape:\nThe pylons may have a negative impact on the scenic beauty of rural areas, which could harm the tourism sector. Local residents also suffer a loss of visual amenity and quality of life.\n\n2. Possible health implications:\nThe electromagnetic fields generated by high-voltage power lines may have a negative impact on the health of people living nearby. Noise pollution from the lines is also a concern.\n\nOther social costs: Potential damage to wildlife habitats, agriculture, and the equine industry during construction and maintenance. Decline in land and house prices in areas close to pylons.\n\nSocial Benefits:\n\n1. Improved infrastructure and secure supply of electricity:\nUpgrading the electricity network secures Ireland's energy supply, reducing the risk of power outages and making Ireland more attractive for Foreign Direct Investment (FDI).\n\n2. Increased capacity for renewable energy:\nAn upgraded grid can accommodate more renewable energy sources (wind, solar), reducing Ireland's carbon emissions, decreasing dependence on imported fossil fuels (roughly 85% of Ireland's energy needs are imported), and reducing the amount of money leaving the country to pay for energy imports."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Utility, Supply & Markets",
     q:"(a)(i) The table shows Units Consumed (1-5), Total Utility (20, 45, 60, 70, 75) and Marginal Utility (20, 25, 15, 10, 5). State and explain the law illustrated in the table.\n(ii) Outline two assumptions underlying this law.\n\n(b)(i) State the 'Law of Supply', and illustrate with a labelled diagram.\n(ii) Explain how technical progress affects the supply curve.\n(iii) Outline, with the aid of labelled diagrams, two other factors that would cause a shift in the supply curve.\n\n(c) Macklemore announces a concert at a venue with a maximum capacity of 80,000 people. Tickets are priced at EUR65 and sell out in hours.\n(i) Draw one labelled diagram showing a market demand curve and supply curve consistent with this information. Explain your answer.\n(ii) Explain, using the concept of Consumer Surplus, why it might make sense for the promoters to have different ticket prices (VIP, seating, standing).\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility:\nThis law states that as a consumer consumes more units of a good, the extra satisfaction (marginal utility) derived from each additional unit consumed will eventually decline. In the table, after the consumption of the second unit, the marginal utility falls from 25 utils to 15 utils for the third unit, then to 10 and 5 for subsequent units.\n\n(ii) Two assumptions:\n1. The law only applies after a certain minimum quantity has been consumed (the 'origin') - e.g. a full orange, not segments of it.\n2. Consumer tastes do not change during the period of consumption - utility means the same thing for the commodity in question throughout.\nOther valid assumptions: Consumer incomes do not change; no significant time lapse between consumption of successive units.\n\n(b)(i) Law of Supply:\nThe law of supply states that there is a positive relationship between the price of a good and the quantity supplied of that good. If the price rises, the quantity supplied rises, and if the price falls, the quantity supplied falls, ceteris paribus.\nDiagram: Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis, upward-sloping supply curve from left to right.\n\n(ii) Technical progress (such as a labour-saving device) will reduce production costs and increase the productivity of the firm (more output per worker). This causes the supply curve to shift to the right - at each price level there will be an increase in the quantity supplied.\n\n(iii) Two other factors causing a shift in the supply curve:\n1. Costs of inputs/factors of production: If input prices increase, it becomes more costly to produce the good. Firms will not supply the same quantity at the old prices, so supply decreases and the supply curve shifts to the left.\n2. Changes in taxation/subsidies: A subsidy shifts the supply curve to the right by reducing production costs. An increase in tax on goods causes price increases and may reduce the quantities firms are willing to supply, shifting the curve left.\n\n(c)(i) Diagram and explanation:\nSupply of concert tickets is perfectly inelastic (vertical line at 80,000) because the venue capacity is fixed regardless of price. The demand curve slopes downward from left to right. The equilibrium quantity is 80,000 and the equilibrium price is EUR65. The concert sells out because at EUR65, the quantity demanded equals or exceeds the fixed supply.\n\n(ii) Consumer Surplus and price discrimination:\nConsumer surplus is the difference between what a consumer actually pays for a product (the market price) and what they would have been willing to pay rather than go without. Some concert-goers are bigger fans and would willingly pay more than EUR65.\n\nThe promoter can extract this consumer surplus by offering different ticket tiers: VIP tickets at EUR100 for front-row seats, seated tickets at EUR85, and standing tickets at EUR65. Those paying EUR100 forgo EUR35 of consumer surplus; those paying EUR85 forgo EUR20. This increases the promoter's total sales revenue by capturing value from consumers who were willing to pay more than the base price."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Imperfect Competition & Market Structures",
     q:"(a)(i) State and explain three assumptions underlying the theory of imperfect competition.\n(ii) Explain why a firm's demand curve under imperfect competition differs from a firm's demand curve under perfect competition.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with the aid of a diagram, the long-run equilibrium of a firm in imperfect competition.\n(ii) With reference to your diagram, explain why the firm is not making socially efficient use of scarce resources.\n\n(c) 'A few large retailers (e.g. Tesco, SuperValu, Dunnes Stores, Aldi, Lidl) dominate the Irish grocery market at present.' Suggest a market structure which most closely reflects this situation. Explain your answer.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Three assumptions of imperfect competition:\n1. Many firms in the industry: There are a large number of firms but fewer than in perfect competition. Each firm has a small share of the market.\n2. Differentiated products: Each firm sells a product that is slightly different from its competitors (through branding, packaging, quality, etc.). This gives each firm some degree of market power.\n3. Free entry and exit: There are no significant barriers to entering or leaving the industry. New firms can enter if they see profit opportunities.\n\nOther valid assumptions: Each firm has some control over price (limited price-maker); firms engage in non-price competition (advertising, branding); firms aim to maximise profits (MC = MR).\n\n(ii) Under perfect competition, the firm is a price-taker and its demand curve is perfectly elastic (horizontal line) because it sells a homogeneous product and has no influence over the market price. Under imperfect competition, the firm has a downward-sloping demand curve because its product is differentiated. If the firm raises its price, it will lose some but not all customers (some remain loyal due to product differentiation). The firm has some degree of price-making power.\n\n(b)(i) Long-run equilibrium of a firm in imperfect competition:\nThe firm produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising output). In the long run, because of free entry and exit, supernormal profits are competed away as new firms enter the market. The demand curve (AR) becomes tangent to the AC curve. At the equilibrium output, AR = AC, meaning the firm earns only normal profit. The price is read from the AR (demand) curve.\n\nDiagram: U-shaped AC curve, downward-sloping AR and MR curves, MC curve rising through MR. The AR curve is tangent to AC at the equilibrium output. Price P is at the tangency point.\n\n(ii) The firm is not making socially efficient use of scarce resources because:\n- It does not produce at the minimum point of the AC curve - it produces to the left of the minimum AC. This means the firm has excess capacity and could produce more output at a lower average cost.\n- Price (AR) is greater than MC at equilibrium output, meaning consumers are paying more than the marginal cost of production. In a socially efficient allocation, P = MC (allocative efficiency).\n- Resources are being underutilised, and the firm could serve more consumers if it operated at the minimum point of AC.\n\n(c) The market structure that most closely reflects the Irish grocery market is an oligopoly.\n\nExplanation: An oligopoly is a market structure characterised by a small number of large firms that dominate the industry. The Irish grocery market is dominated by a few large retailers (Tesco, SuperValu, Dunnes Stores, Aldi, Lidl) who collectively control the majority of market share. Key oligopoly features include:\n- High concentration ratio: a few firms account for a large proportion of total sales.\n- Interdependence: each firm's pricing and marketing decisions are influenced by the likely reactions of rival firms.\n- Non-price competition: firms compete through advertising, loyalty schemes, product quality, and store location rather than just price.\n- Significant barriers to entry: the high cost of establishing a nationwide retail network deters new entrants."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"(a)(i) Define the categories of Price Elasticity of Demand (PED): elastic, inelastic and unit elastic.\n(ii) State three factors that affect PED and explain how each factor affects it.\n\n(b) A consumer/motorist buys 20 litres of petrol when the price is EUR1.60 per litre. When the price increases to EUR1.70, as a result of an increase in carbon tax, the consumer buys 19 litres.\nCalculate the consumer's Price Elasticity of Demand (PED). Show all your workings.\nIs this demand for petrol price elastic or price inelastic? Outline the implication of your answer for government revenue.\n\n(c) A firm's market research reveals PED for its product is -2.5.\n(i) If the firm wishes to maximise total sales revenue, should it lower or raise the price? Explain.\nThe market research also reveals Income Elasticity of Demand (YED) for the product is +4.5.\n(ii) In the case of an economy expected to remain in recession for the next five years, what will be the likely impact on the demand for the product? Explain.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Definitions of PED categories:\n- Elastic demand (PED > 1): The percentage change in quantity demanded is greater than the percentage change in price. Consumers are very responsive to price changes.\n- Inelastic demand (PED < 1): The percentage change in quantity demanded is less than the percentage change in price. Consumers are relatively unresponsive to price changes.\n- Unit elastic demand (PED = 1): The percentage change in quantity demanded is exactly equal to the percentage change in price.\n\n(ii) Three factors affecting PED:\n1. Availability of substitutes: If many close substitutes exist, demand will be elastic because consumers can easily switch to an alternative if the price rises. If few or no substitutes exist, demand will be inelastic.\n2. Necessity vs luxury: Necessities (food, medicine) tend to have inelastic demand as consumers must purchase them regardless of price. Luxuries (holidays, designer goods) have elastic demand as consumers can do without them.\n3. Proportion of income spent: If the good accounts for a large proportion of the consumer's income, demand tends to be elastic. If it is a small proportion, demand tends to be inelastic (e.g. a box of matches).\n\n(b) PED Calculation:\nUsing the midpoint/arc elasticity formula:\nPED = (Change in Qd / Average Qd) / (Change in P / Average P)\n= ((-1) / ((20+19)/2)) / ((0.10) / ((1.60+1.70)/2))\n= (-1 / 19.5) / (0.10 / 1.65)\n= -0.0513 / 0.0606\n= -0.85 (approximately)\n\nAlternatively using the point elasticity method:\nPED = (% change in Qd) / (% change in P)\n= (-1/20 x 100) / (0.10/1.60 x 100)\n= (-5%) / (6.25%)\n= -0.8\n\nThe demand is PRICE INELASTIC (PED < 1).\n\nImplication for government revenue: Since petrol has price inelastic demand, an increase in carbon tax will result in an increase in government tax revenue. The price increase leads to a proportionally smaller decrease in quantity demanded, so total revenue from the tax rises. This makes petrol an effective target for taxation.\n\n(c)(i) Since PED = -2.5 (elastic demand), the firm should LOWER its price to maximise total sales revenue.\nExplanation: With elastic demand, a decrease in price leads to a proportionally larger increase in quantity demanded. The gain in revenue from selling more units outweighs the loss from the lower price per unit. For example, a 10% price reduction would lead to a 25% increase in quantity demanded.\n\n(ii) With YED = +4.5, the product is a superior/luxury good (YED > 1). In a recession, incomes fall, and because YED is highly positive, the demand for this product will fall by a much larger proportion than the fall in income.\nFor example, a 10% fall in income would lead to a 45% fall in demand for the product. The firm should be very concerned about sales in a five-year recession, as demand for its product will decline significantly. The firm may need to diversify its product range or find ways to reduce costs."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Economic Terms, Location & Property",
     q:"(a) Explain, with the aid of an example, each of the following terms:\n- Derived Demand\n- Transfer Earnings\n- Supply Price\n- Economic Rent\n\n(b)(i) Outline two economic characteristics of 'land'.\n(ii) Discuss three economic factors which influence a firm's decision on where to locate its operations within Ireland.\n\n(c)(i) State and explain three factors that caused the price of residential property to fall considerably in Ireland in the years following 2007.\n(ii) Discuss one possible economic impact of mortgage arrears on each of the following: the household, the banking sector, and the Irish Government.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a) Definitions with examples:\n\nDerived Demand: A factor of production is not demanded for its own sake but for what it can produce. The demand for the factor is derived from the demand for the final good or service. Example: The demand for bricklayers (labour) is derived from the demand for new houses.\n\nTransfer Earnings: The minimum payment a factor of production must receive to keep it in its present use. If it receives less than its transfer earnings, it will transfer to its next best alternative use. Example: If a footballer is paid EUR50,000 per year but could earn EUR45,000 as a coach, their transfer earnings are EUR45,000.\n\nSupply Price: The minimum price a firm must receive to supply one more unit of output. It is the marginal cost of producing that additional unit. Example: If it costs a bakery EUR2 to produce one additional loaf, the supply price of that loaf is EUR2.\n\nEconomic Rent: Any payment a factor of production receives over and above its transfer earnings. It is a surplus payment. Example: If the footballer earning EUR50,000 has transfer earnings of EUR45,000, then EUR5,000 is economic rent.\n\n(b)(i) Two characteristics of land:\n1. Land is fixed in supply (perfectly inelastic supply): The total quantity of land available cannot be increased or decreased. Its supply curve is a vertical line.\n2. Land is a gift of nature: It is not man-made and has no cost of production. It exists naturally.\nOther characteristics: Land is geographically immobile (cannot be moved); it varies in quality and fertility; it earns economic rent.\n\n(ii) Three factors influencing location:\n1. Availability of skilled labour: Firms need access to a qualified and skilled workforce. Areas near universities and training centres (e.g. Dublin, Cork, Galway) attract firms that need highly educated workers.\n2. Transport and infrastructure: Proximity to motorways, airports, and ports is important for firms that need to transport goods. Access to broadband and telecommunications infrastructure is essential for technology firms.\n3. Government incentives: IDA Ireland and Enterprise Ireland offer grants, tax incentives, and support to firms locating in certain regions, particularly outside Dublin, to promote balanced regional development.\nOther valid factors: Proximity to markets/customers; cost and availability of premises; access to raw materials; clustering effects (agglomeration economies).\n\n(c)(i) Three factors that caused property prices to fall after 2007:\n1. Collapse in demand: The bursting of the property bubble led to a sharp decline in demand for housing. Consumers could no longer access easy credit, and confidence collapsed as people feared further price falls.\n2. Oversupply: During the boom years (2001-2007), excessive construction led to an oversupply of housing, particularly in rural areas. 'Ghost estates' with unsold and unfinished properties flooded the market.\n3. Banking crisis and credit restrictions: Banks suffered massive losses on property loans and drastically reduced lending. Without access to mortgages, potential buyers could not purchase property, further reducing demand.\n\n(ii) Economic impact of mortgage arrears:\n- The household: Reduced disposable income and standard of living, as a large portion of income goes towards servicing the mortgage debt. Negative equity (mortgage greater than property value) may trap families in unsuitable accommodation. Stress and uncertainty affect household consumption and wellbeing.\n- The banking sector: Banks face increased bad debts and loan losses, weakening their balance sheets. They may need to increase provisions for bad debts, reducing profitability. This reduces their ability to lend to other creditworthy borrowers, restricting economic growth.\n- The Irish Government: Increased social welfare payments to households in financial distress. Reduced tax revenue (less consumer spending means less VAT; less property transactions mean less stamp duty). The government may need to recapitalise banks if losses become too large, increasing the national debt."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): National Income",
     q:"(a) The following table shows National Income (Y), Consumption (C), Investment (I), Government Spending (G), Exports (X) and Imports (M) for 2013 and 2014:\n2013: Y=EUR80,000, C=?, I=EUR10,000, G=EUR40,000, X=EUR100,000, M=EUR120,000\n2014: Y=EUR90,000, C=EUR56,000, I=EUR15,000, G=EUR34,000, X=EUR110,000, M=?\n\nCalculate: (i) The level of Consumption in 2013, (ii) The Marginal Propensity to Consume (MPC), (iii) The level of Imports in 2014, (iv) The Marginal Propensity to Import (MPM).\n\n(b) 'Ireland heads Forbes' list of the best countries for business.' Discuss the economic benefits Multinational Companies (MNCs) can bring to a small economy such as Ireland.\n\n(c)(i) Define GDP and GNP.\n(ii) Which is a more useful measure of economic activity for Ireland? Explain.\n(iii) Discuss three limitations of national income statistics.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a) Calculations:\nNational Income: Y = C + I + G + X - M\n\n(i) Consumption in 2013:\n80,000 = C + 10,000 + 40,000 + 100,000 - 120,000\n80,000 = C + 30,000\nC = EUR50,000\n\n(ii) Marginal Propensity to Consume (MPC):\nMPC = Change in C / Change in Y\n= (56,000 - 50,000) / (90,000 - 80,000)\n= 6,000 / 10,000\n= 0.6\nFor every extra EUR1 of income, EUR0.60 is spent on consumption.\n\n(iii) Imports in 2014:\n90,000 = 56,000 + 15,000 + 34,000 + 110,000 - M\nM = 56,000 + 15,000 + 34,000 + 110,000 - 90,000\nM = EUR125,000\n\n(iv) Marginal Propensity to Import (MPM):\nMPM = Change in M / Change in Y\n= (125,000 - 120,000) / (90,000 - 80,000)\n= 5,000 / 10,000\n= 0.5\nFor every extra EUR1 of income, EUR0.50 is spent on imports.\n\n(b) Economic benefits of MNCs to Ireland:\n1. Employment creation: MNCs create direct employment (e.g. Apple employs thousands in Cork) and indirect employment through supply chains and local service providers.\n2. Increased tax revenue: MNCs contribute corporation tax, employer PRSI, and their employees pay income tax and USC. Their employees' spending generates VAT revenue.\n3. Technology and skills transfer: MNCs bring advanced technology, management techniques, and specialised skills to Ireland, benefiting the wider economy and upskilling the Irish workforce.\n4. Increased exports: MNCs account for a significant proportion of Irish exports, contributing positively to the balance of payments.\n5. Economic growth: MNC investment is an injection into the circular flow of income, generating a multiplier effect (Y = C + I + G + X - M).\n6. Research and development: Many MNCs invest in R&D activities in Ireland, fostering innovation.\n\n(c)(i) Definitions:\nGDP (Gross Domestic Product): The total value of all final goods and services produced within the borders of a country in a given year, regardless of who produces them.\nGNP (Gross National Product): The total value of all final goods and services produced by a country's citizens/residents, regardless of where in the world they produce them. GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad.\n\n(ii) GNP is a more useful measure for Ireland because Ireland's GDP is significantly inflated by the profits of multinational corporations operating in Ireland. These profits are repatriated abroad and do not benefit Irish residents. GNP adjusts for this by subtracting these outflows (Net Factor Income from Abroad is negative for Ireland), giving a more accurate picture of the income actually available to Irish residents.\n\n(iii) Three limitations of national income statistics:\n1. Non-market activities excluded: Activities such as housework, childcare by parents, and DIY are not recorded in national income figures, even though they have economic value. This understates the true level of economic activity.\n2. Hidden economy not captured: Economic activity in the black/shadow economy (undeclared income, illegal activities) is not included. This leads to an underestimation of national income.\n3. Quality of life not reflected: GDP/GNP measures the quantity of output but not the quality of life. A country may have high GDP but also have high pollution, inequality, or poor public services. National income does not distinguish between 'goods' and 'bads' (e.g. spending on cleaning up pollution increases GDP but does not improve welfare)."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): Economic Aims, Privatisation & Austerity",
     q:"(a)(i) State and explain four current economic aims/objectives of the Irish Government.\n(ii) Outline two possible conflicts that could arise between some of these Government aims/objectives.\n\n(b) Discuss arguments for and against the privatisation of state assets.\n\n(c) The economist Joseph Stiglitz predicted a 'lost decade' for the Irish economy due to the extended period of austerity. (Austerity refers to government measures introduced to reduce a Budget Deficit.)\nOutline four possible economic consequences for the Irish economy arising from the austerity measures introduced in recent Irish Government budgets.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Four economic aims of the Irish Government:\n1. Full employment: The government aims to achieve the highest possible level of employment (unemployment between 4-5%). More people working means higher tax revenue, lower social welfare costs, and improved living standards.\n2. Price stability: The government aims to keep inflation low and stable (in line with the ECB target of 2%). High inflation erodes purchasing power and makes Ireland less competitive internationally.\n3. Economic growth: The government aims for sustainable increases in real GDP/GNP, leading to higher living standards for citizens through increased income per capita.\n4. Balanced budget: The government aims to keep government expenditure in line with government revenue, avoiding excessive borrowing and keeping the national debt manageable.\nOther aims: Balance of payments equilibrium; equitable distribution of income; balanced regional development.\n\n(ii) Two possible conflicts:\n1. Full employment vs Price stability: Achieving full employment may lead to increased spending and demand-pull inflation, conflicting with the aim of keeping prices stable.\n2. Economic growth vs Balanced budget: To stimulate economic growth, the government may need to increase public spending or reduce taxes, which can lead to a budget deficit, conflicting with the aim of a balanced budget.\n\n(b) Arguments FOR privatisation:\n- Raises revenue: The sale of state assets generates a one-off sum of money for the government, which can be used to reduce the national debt or fund public services.\n- Increased efficiency: Private firms operate with a profit motive, which may lead to greater efficiency, lower costs, and better service than a state monopoly.\n- Competition: Privatisation can open markets to competition, leading to lower prices and greater choice for consumers.\n- Reduced government involvement: The government can focus on core functions rather than running commercial enterprises.\n\nArguments AGAINST privatisation:\n- Loss of control: The government loses control over essential services (e.g. energy, water). Private firms may prioritise profit over public service.\n- Job losses: Private firms may cut staff to reduce costs and maximise profit.\n- Higher prices: Private monopolies may charge higher prices than state enterprises if there is insufficient competition.\n- Cherry-picking: Private firms may only serve profitable areas/customers, neglecting rural or disadvantaged areas.\n- Loss of future income: Selling a profitable state asset means losing a stream of future dividends and profits.\n\n(c) Four economic consequences of austerity:\n1. Reduced aggregate demand: Cuts to government spending and increases in taxation reduce consumers' disposable income and government expenditure, leading to lower aggregate demand in the economy. This has a negative multiplier effect.\n2. Increased unemployment: Reduced government spending leads to public sector job losses. Reduced consumer spending also leads to private sector job losses, increasing unemployment.\n3. Emigration: High unemployment and reduced opportunities cause young, skilled workers to emigrate. This represents a loss of human capital that Ireland invested in through education.\n4. Reduced public services: Cuts to government spending lead to reduced provision of public services such as healthcare, education, and social welfare. This impacts the most vulnerable in society.\nOther consequences: Deflation risk; increased poverty and inequality; reduced consumer and business confidence; potential for social unrest."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): LDCs, Foreign Aid & Trade",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between the terms 'economic development' and 'economic growth' with reference to Less Developed Countries (LDCs).\n(ii) Outline three characteristics of LDCs.\n\n(b)(i) Describe three types of foreign aid available to LDCs.\n(ii) Discuss why foreign aid may not always result in economic growth in LDCs.\n\n(c)(i) Outline two economic benefits claimed for 'free trade'.\n(ii) Outline three reasons why countries might choose to impose barriers to free trade.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Distinction:\nEconomic growth refers to an increase in the total output (GDP/GNP) of an economy over time. It is a quantitative measure.\nEconomic development is a broader, qualitative concept that includes economic growth plus improvements in living standards, health, education, reduction in poverty, and greater equality. A country can experience economic growth (higher GDP) without economic development if the benefits are not widely shared.\n\nIn LDCs, economic growth alone may not lead to development if the wealth is concentrated among a small elite, if health and education do not improve, or if the growth is based on unsustainable resource extraction.\n\n(ii) Three characteristics of LDCs:\n1. Low per capita income: Citizens have very low average incomes, often below $2 per day, limiting their ability to save and invest.\n2. High population growth: High birth rates combined with declining death rates lead to rapid population growth, putting pressure on resources and infrastructure.\n3. High dependency on primary sector: The economy is heavily reliant on agriculture and raw material extraction, with little industrialisation or diversification.\nOther characteristics: Low levels of education and healthcare; high infant mortality; poor infrastructure; political instability; income inequality.\n\n(b)(i) Three types of foreign aid:\n1. Bilateral aid: Aid given directly from one government to another, e.g. Ireland provides aid to specific African countries through Irish Aid. This can be tied (conditions attached) or untied.\n2. Multilateral aid: Aid channelled through international organisations such as the World Bank, IMF, or United Nations. Countries contribute funds to these organisations which then distribute aid.\n3. Non-governmental aid: Aid provided by NGOs such as Concern, Trocaire, and Oxfam. These organisations raise funds through donations and provide humanitarian assistance, development projects, and emergency relief.\n\n(ii) Why foreign aid may not result in economic growth:\n- Corruption and mismanagement: Aid funds may be diverted by corrupt governments or officials for personal use rather than being invested in productive activities.\n- Dependency culture: Long-term aid can create a dependency on foreign assistance, reducing the incentive for self-sufficiency and domestic economic development.\n- Tied aid: If aid is tied to the donor country's conditions (e.g. purchasing goods from the donor country), it may not address the actual needs of the recipient country.\n- Lack of infrastructure: Without basic infrastructure (roads, schools, hospitals), aid cannot be effectively utilised for development purposes.\n- Debt burden: If aid comes in the form of loans, the interest payments can become a significant burden on the LDC's finances.\n\n(c)(i) Two benefits of free trade:\n1. Lower prices for consumers: Free trade increases competition and allows countries to specialise in goods where they have a comparative advantage, leading to lower prices and greater consumer choice.\n2. Increased efficiency: Free trade encourages specialisation, economies of scale, and the most efficient allocation of resources globally. Countries produce what they are best at, increasing total world output.\n\n(ii) Three reasons for trade barriers:\n1. Protecting infant industries: New domestic industries may need protection from established foreign competitors until they can achieve economies of scale and become competitive.\n2. Safeguarding domestic employment: Trade barriers can protect jobs in industries that face competition from cheaper imports, preventing unemployment and social disruption.\n3. National security: A country may wish to maintain domestic production of strategically important goods (food, energy, defence) rather than relying on foreign suppliers.\nOther reasons: Preventing dumping (selling below cost); protecting domestic consumers from unsafe foreign products; raising government revenue through tariffs."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8 (Section B): Budget Deficit, National Debt & Taxation",
     q:"(a) Explain the relationship between a government Budget Deficit and the National Debt.\n\n(b)(i) Discuss Adam Smith's four Canons of Taxation from his book 'An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations' (1776).\n(ii) Outline two other principles which could be considered in a modern tax system. Justify your answers.\n\n(c) Outline possible economic consequences of the Irish Government shifting the burden of taxation from direct taxation to indirect taxation.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a) Relationship between Budget Deficit and National Debt:\nA budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a given year. To finance this shortfall, the government must borrow money (e.g. by issuing government bonds). The national debt is the total accumulated amount of money that the government owes as a result of borrowing over the years. Each year's budget deficit adds to the national debt. Conversely, a budget surplus (revenue exceeds expenditure) can be used to reduce the national debt. The national debt also incurs interest payments, which must be funded from current government revenue.\n\n(b)(i) Adam Smith's Four Canons of Taxation:\n1. Equity/Ability to Pay: Each citizen should contribute to government revenue in proportion to their ability to pay. Those who earn more should pay more tax. This supports the idea of progressive taxation.\n2. Certainty: The tax a citizen must pay should be certain and not arbitrary. The amount, time of payment, and manner of payment should be clear to the taxpayer.\n3. Convenience: Every tax should be levied at the time and in the manner most convenient for the taxpayer. For example, PAYE income tax is deducted at source from wages.\n4. Economy: Every tax should be designed to take out of the pockets of the taxpayer as little as possible over and above what it brings into the public treasury. The cost of collecting the tax should be minimised relative to the revenue raised.\n\n(ii) Two additional modern principles:\n1. Should not act as a disincentive to work, save, or invest: If income tax rates are too high, workers may choose to work fewer hours or skilled workers may emigrate. Tax policy should encourage enterprise and productivity.\n2. Should be flexible/responsive: The tax system should be adaptable to changing economic conditions. During a recession, taxes may need to be reduced to stimulate demand; during a boom, taxes may need to increase to prevent overheating.\n\n(c) Consequences of shifting from direct to indirect taxation:\n1. Regressive impact: Indirect taxes (VAT, excise duties) take a higher proportion of income from lower earners than higher earners, making the tax system more regressive and potentially increasing income inequality.\n2. Increased work incentive: Lower income tax rates may encourage people to work harder, longer, or to seek employment, as they keep more of their earnings. This could increase labour supply.\n3. Inflationary pressure: Higher indirect taxes increase the prices of goods and services, contributing to cost-push inflation and reducing consumers' purchasing power.\n4. Reduced tax evasion: Indirect taxes are harder to evade as they are included in the price of goods. This could increase overall tax revenue compared to direct taxes, which are more easily evaded.\n5. Impact on consumer spending: Higher prices due to indirect taxes may reduce consumer spending, particularly for lower-income households who spend a higher proportion of their income on consumption.\n6. Cross-border shopping: Higher indirect taxes could encourage consumers in border regions to shop in Northern Ireland where prices may be lower, leading to a loss of economic activity."}
  ],

  "2015": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Choice & Economics",
     q:"Outline why 'choice' is fundamental to the study of economics.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"In economics, resources are limited/finite/scarce and have alternative uses, while human wants are unlimited/infinite. Because resources are scarce relative to wants, choices must be made about how to allocate these resources. Every choice involves an opportunity cost - the next best alternative that must be forgone.\n\nThis is the fundamental economic problem: scarcity requires choice, and choice involves opportunity cost. Economics is essentially the study of how individuals, firms, and governments make these choices about the allocation of scarce resources among competing uses."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Equi-Marginal Principle & Calculation",
     q:"(a) Explain the concept of the Equi-Marginal Principle of Consumer Behaviour.\n\n(b) In equilibrium, a consumer buys 5 bottles of water at EUR2.50 each and 8 bagels at EUR3 each. The marginal utility of the 5th bottle of water is 5 utils.\nCalculate the marginal utility of the 8th bagel. Show your workings.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) The Equi-Marginal Principle states that a consumer maximises total utility (satisfaction) by spending their income so that the ratio of marginal utility to price is the same for all goods and services they purchase. In formula form: MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb = ... = MUn/Pn. The consumer should reallocate spending between goods until this condition is met.\n\n(b) Calculation:\nIn equilibrium, the Equi-Marginal Principle holds:\nMU(Water) / P(Water) = MU(Bagels) / P(Bagels)\n\n5 / EUR2.50 = x / EUR3.00\n\n2 = x / 3\n\nx = 6 utils\n\nThe marginal utility of the 8th bagel is 6 utils."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: NTMA & Government Bonds",
     q:"(a) Outline the primary responsibility of the National Treasury Management Agency (NTMA).\n\n'The NTMA sells bonds worth EUR4bn at a low yield (0.867%) in 2015.' (Report: Irish Times, January 2015)\n\n(b) Is selling bonds at a low yield good or bad for the Irish Government? Explain your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) The NTMA's primary responsibility is borrowing for the Exchequer and managing the national debt. Its core function is to ensure liquidity for the Exchequer (making sure the government has the cash it needs to fund its activities) and to minimise the interest burden on government borrowing over the medium term.\n\n(b) Selling bonds at a low yield is GOOD for the Irish Government.\n\nExplanation: A low yield means the cost to the government of raising money (borrowing) is reduced. The government pays less interest on the money it borrows. This means:\n- The budget deficit will be smaller, as interest payments on debt are reduced.\n- It is cheaper to fund government services and capital projects.\n- It signals that bond buyers have confidence in the Irish economy and are prepared to buy Irish bonds at low rates of return, reflecting Ireland's improved creditworthiness since exiting the bailout programme."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Oligopoly & Concentration Ratio",
     q:"A high concentration ratio is a key feature of an Oligopolistic Market.\n\n(a) Explain this statement and give one example.\n\n(b) Outline two ways oligopolists behave in the market.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Explanation:\nA high concentration ratio exists in an industry when a small number of firms account for (dominate) a relatively large part of the total output or sales of the industry. In an oligopoly, the market is controlled by a few large firms. This concentration of market power means these firms can significantly influence market price and output.\n\nExample: The grocery trade in Ireland (Tesco, SuperValu, Dunnes Stores, Aldi, Lidl dominate the market). Other examples: banking sector, mobile phone industry, domestic energy suppliers.\n\n(b) Two ways oligopolists behave:\n1. Interdependence: Firms act in a way that takes account of the likely reactions of their competitors. Every decision a firm takes (on price, advertising, product features) is based on how it expects rival firms to respond. This mutual awareness is a defining feature of oligopoly.\n\n2. Non-price competition: Firms compete through means other than price - such as advertising, branding, after-sales service, product differentiation, loyalty schemes, and quality improvements - rather than engaging in price wars which could reduce profits for all firms in the industry.\n\nOther valid behaviour: Firms may engage in collusive practices (explicit or tacit collusion) where two or more firms act together to set price, output, or other conditions of sale for mutual benefit."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Regressive Tax & Indirect Taxation",
     q:"'Indirect taxes can be regressive but they also have advantages for governments.'\n\n(a) Explain what is meant by a regressive tax.\n\n(b) Outline two advantages of indirect taxation for governments.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) A regressive tax is one that takes a higher proportion (percentage) of income from low-income taxpayers than from high-income taxpayers. Although both groups may pay the same absolute amount of tax, the proportion of income paid by lower earners is greater. For example, VAT at 23% on a EUR100 item costs EUR23 for everyone, but this represents a much larger share of a low-income earner's weekly income compared to a high-income earner.\n\n(b) Two advantages of indirect taxation for governments:\n\n1. Low collection cost / convenient collection: Indirect taxes are collected by retailers and businesses at the point of sale, meaning the government does not need to collect directly from each consumer. This makes them efficient and inexpensive to administer.\n\n2. Hard to avoid/evade: Since indirect taxes are included in the price of goods and services, consumers cannot easily avoid paying them. This makes them a reliable source of government revenue compared to direct taxes, which may be subject to evasion.\n\nOther advantages: Less distortionary than income tax (does not discourage work effort); politically easier to introduce than income tax increases; can be used to change consumption patterns and reduce consumption of harmful products (e.g. excise duty on cigarettes/alcohol)."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Classical Economists",
     q:"Outline two main contributions to economic thought put forward by the Classical Economists.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"1. Efficiency of free markets / Self-adjusting mechanism:\nThe Classical Economists believed that the market offers the most efficient means of allocating resources (the market knows best). They advocated the workings of the free market to ensure equilibrium would occur. If there was excess demand, prices would rise, eliminating the excess demand. If there was excess supply, prices would fall, eliminating the excess supply. The economy would naturally self-correct after any shock and output would be restored to the full employment level in the long run.\n\n2. Laissez-faire:\nThe Classical Economists favoured minimum government intervention in economic affairs. They believed the government should limit its role to essential functions such as defence, security, and the administration of justice. The free market, guided by Adam Smith's 'invisible hand', would naturally produce the best outcomes without government interference.\n\nOther contributions: Freedom for the individual (the wealth of a nation results from citizens diligently pursuing their own interests); free trade (opposed protectionist policies, arguing that no tariffs or trade barriers should apply between nations)."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Elasticity True/False",
     q:"Read each statement below and indicate if the statement is true or false:\n\n(i) A good with an income elasticity of demand (YED) of +2.5 is a luxury good.\n(ii) If the cross-price elasticity of demand (CED) is negative then it may be said that the goods are complements.\n(iii) When demand is price elastic, a reduction in price will lead to a decrease in total revenue.\n(iv) When many close substitute goods are available, demand for any one of them should be highly elastic.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(i) TRUE - A good with a YED of +2.5 is a luxury good. A YED greater than +1 indicates a luxury/superior good. As income rises by 1%, demand for the good rises by 2.5%, meaning demand increases more than proportionately with income. These are non-essential goods that consumers buy more of as their income grows.\n\n(ii) TRUE - If the cross-price elasticity of demand (CED) is negative, the goods are complements. A negative CED means that when the price of one good increases, the demand for the other good decreases. This indicates the goods are consumed together (e.g. printers and ink cartridges).\n\n(iii) FALSE - When demand is price elastic, a reduction in price will lead to an INCREASE in total revenue (not a decrease). With elastic demand, the percentage increase in quantity demanded is greater than the percentage decrease in price, so total revenue (P x Q) increases.\n\n(iv) TRUE - When many close substitutes are available, demand for any one good is highly elastic. Consumers can easily switch to an alternative product if the price of one rises, so even a small price increase leads to a large fall in quantity demanded."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Euro Depreciation vs US Dollar",
     q:"(a) 'The Euro (EUR) is hitting a four and a half year low against the US $.' (Report: The Irish Independent, January 2015)\nExplain what the above statement means.\n\n(b) Outline two possible economic effects of this development for the Irish economy.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) This statement means that the price/value of the euro has fallen (depreciated) in terms of the US dollar compared to four and a half years ago. The euro can now buy fewer dollars than it could previously. For example, where EUR1 might have bought $1.40 previously, it might now only buy $1.15.\n\n(b) Two possible economic effects:\n\n1. Positive effect - Increased export competitiveness:\nIrish exports to the USA become relatively cheaper and more competitive in dollar terms. US consumers need fewer dollars to buy the same euro-priced goods. This may lead to increased demand for Irish exports to the US, boosting sales and potentially increasing employment in export-oriented industries. Tourism revenue may also increase as more Americans find Ireland a cheaper holiday destination.\n\n2. Negative effect - More expensive imports:\nIrish imports from the USA become relatively more expensive in euro terms. Irish consumers and businesses must pay more euro for the same dollar-priced goods. Costs of production increase for Irish firms importing raw materials or components from the US. Since many imports (e.g. oil) have inelastic demand, the import bill will rise, putting pressure on Irish businesses and consumers."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Ageing Population & Life Expectancy",
     q:"'Since 1990 Ireland has achieved the largest gain in life expectancy for 65 year olds in all EU member states.' (The OECD Report: Health at a glance: Europe 2014)\n\nOutline two possible economic implications of this development for the Irish economy and one policy measure the Government could consider in response to this situation.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Two economic implications:\n\n1. Higher dependency ratio / increased pressure on pensions:\nWith people living longer, the ratio of retired (dependent) people to working people increases. This places enormous pressure on the state pension system, as more people draw pensions for longer periods while fewer workers fund these pensions through taxation. The government may need to increase pension contributions or reduce pension entitlements.\n\n2. Increased demand on government health and social services:\nAn ageing population requires significantly more healthcare services, including long-term care, geriatric services, and chronic illness management. Government spending on health will need to increase substantially, putting pressure on the budget and potentially requiring higher taxation or reallocation of spending from other areas.\n\nOther implications: Change in the pattern of demand for goods and services; possible increased tax burden on those remaining in the workforce; some may choose to work beyond traditional retirement age.\n\nGovernment policy measure:\nExtend the retirement age to reduce the number of people entitled to state pensions and to keep experienced workers in the labour force for longer. This would increase tax revenue, reduce pension payments, and help address labour shortages. Other valid measures: Reform the pension system (e.g. means-testing pensions, using tax credits to encourage private pension saving); invest in health and social services to meet demands; educate younger populations about personal financial planning for retirement."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Markets & Price Ceiling",
     q:"(a) The table shows market data for 4G HD Televisions:\nPrice (EUR) / Qty Demanded ('000) / Qty Supplied ('000):\n1,000 / 20 / 120\n800 / 30 / 80\n600 / 50 / 50\n400 / 80 / 30\n200 / 120 / 20\n\n(i) Using the data, draw a diagram showing the market demand and supply curves, showing the equilibrium price and quantity.\n(ii) Define 'market equilibrium'. With reference to the above market, explain how equilibrium is arrived at.\n\n(b) The market for wheat is in equilibrium. Explain, with diagrams, the effects of:\n(i) Exceptionally wet weather conditions\n(ii) An increasing percentage of the population suffering from wheat allergies and intolerance\n(iii) A decrease in the price of oil.\n\n(c) Following recent sharp increases in the price of private rented accommodation, calls have been made for the Irish Government to introduce price/rent controls (a rent ceiling). Advise the Minister for the Environment on the possible economic consequences of imposing a price ceiling for private rented accommodation.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Diagram description:\nPlot Price (EUR) on the Y-axis and Quantity ('000 units) on the X-axis. The demand curve slopes downward from left to right (from 1,000/20 through to 200/120). The supply curve slopes upward from left to right (from 1,000/120 through to 200/20). The two curves intersect at the equilibrium point: Price = EUR600, Quantity = 50,000 units.\n\n(ii) Market equilibrium is where the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied. At this point, the price is such that consumers wish to buy exactly the amount that firms wish to supply, and there is no tendency for the price to change.\n\nHow equilibrium is arrived at:\nIf the price is below EUR600, a situation of excess demand occurs (consumers want more TVs than firms are willing to supply). This excess demand drives the price upward. As price rises, quantity demanded decreases and quantity supplied increases until equilibrium is restored at EUR600 and 50,000 units.\n\nConversely, if the price is above EUR600, excess supply occurs (firms supply more than consumers want to buy). Firms reduce prices to sell surplus stock. As price falls, demand increases and supply decreases until equilibrium is restored.\n\n(b)(i) Exceptionally wet weather conditions:\nThe supply curve shifts LEFT from S1 to S2. Wet weather results in lower yields of wheat (reduced quantity supplied). At the existing price, a shortage of wheat develops. The price is driven up as demand exceeds supply. New equilibrium: higher price (PE2) and lower quantity (QE2).\n\n(ii) Increasing wheat allergies and intolerance:\nThe demand curve shifts LEFT from D1 to D2. Consumer preferences decline for wheat products due to allergies and intolerance. At the existing price, excess supply develops. Firms reduce prices to clear stock. New equilibrium: lower price (PE2) and lower quantity (QE2).\n\n(iii) A decrease in the price of oil:\nThe supply curve shifts RIGHT from S1 to S2. Oil is an important input in wheat production (fuel for farm machinery, transport, fertilisers). A decrease in the price of oil reduces production costs for wheat farmers, allowing them to supply more at every price level. New equilibrium: lower price (PE2) and higher quantity (QE2).\n\n(c) Advice on rent ceiling consequences:\n\nA rent ceiling (price ceiling) is a maximum rent set by the government below the current market equilibrium rent.\n\nPossible consequences:\n1. Excess demand/shortage: At the lower rent, more tenants want to rent (quantity demanded increases) but fewer landlords are willing to supply rental properties (quantity supplied decreases). This creates a shortage of rental accommodation.\n\n2. Reduced quality: Landlords earning lower rents have less income and less incentive to maintain or improve properties. This can lead to deterioration in the quality of rental accommodation over time.\n\n3. Black market: Tenants desperate for accommodation may offer under-the-table payments above the legal rent, creating an illegal/hidden market.\n\n4. Reduced supply in the long run: Landlords may exit the rental market, selling properties or converting them to other uses. Developers may build fewer rental properties as returns are lower, worsening the shortage.\n\n5. Benefit to existing tenants: Those already renting benefit from lower rents, but those seeking new rental accommodation face greater difficulty finding available properties.\n\n6. Misallocation of resources: Rent controls can lead to inefficient allocation, e.g. single people may remain in large family homes because the rent is low, while families cannot find suitable accommodation."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Monopoly, Price Discrimination & Irish Water",
     q:"(a)(i) State and explain two examples of barriers to entry facing firms wishing to enter a monopoly market.\n(ii) Explain, with the aid of a diagram, the long-run equilibrium position of a monopolist. Identify on your diagram the profit the monopolist makes.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the concept of price discrimination, using a suitable example.\n(ii) State and explain the market conditions necessary for price discrimination to take place.\n\n(c) The Commission for Energy Regulation (CER) has been appointed as the independent economic regulator for Ireland's public water services. Discuss whether or not Irish Water (Uisce Eireann) should be regulated.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Two barriers to entry in a monopoly market:\n1. Economies of scale: The existing monopolist produces at such a large scale that its average costs are very low. A new entrant, starting with small output, would face much higher average costs and could not compete on price. The large capital investment required to achieve similar economies of scale deters entry.\n\n2. Legal barriers (patents/licenses): The government may grant exclusive rights to produce a good or service through patents, copyrights, or exclusive licenses. For example, a pharmaceutical company holds a patent on a new drug for 20 years, preventing other firms from producing the same drug.\n\nOther barriers: Control of essential resources/raw materials; brand loyalty and consumer attachment; high start-up costs; aggressive pricing strategies by the incumbent.\n\n(ii) Long-run equilibrium of a monopolist:\nDiagram: Downward-sloping AR (demand) curve and MR curve (MR lies below AR). U-shaped AC curve. MC curve rising through the minimum of AC and intersecting MR.\n\nThe monopolist produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising output, Qm). Price (Pm) is read from the AR curve above this output level. Since AR > AC at this output, the monopolist earns supernormal profit. The supernormal profit is shown as the shaded rectangle between AR and AC at the equilibrium output (area = (Pm - ACm) x Qm).\n\nUnlike perfect competition, supernormal profits persist in the long run because barriers to entry prevent new firms from entering the market.\n\n(b)(i) Price discrimination is the practice of charging different prices to different consumers for the same product or service, where the price difference is not justified by differences in the cost of supply.\n\nExample: An electricity company charging domestic consumers a higher rate per unit than industrial users, even though the cost of generating and distributing the electricity is similar. Or: cinemas charging different prices for adults, students, and OAPs.\n\n(ii) Market conditions necessary for price discrimination:\n1. The firm must have some degree of monopoly power - it must be a price-maker, not a price-taker. Otherwise, competitors would undercut the higher price.\n2. The firm must be able to separate the market into distinct groups with different price elasticities of demand (e.g. students vs business travellers).\n3. It must be impossible for consumers to resell/transfer the product from the cheaper market to the dearer market (no arbitrage). This is why price discrimination works for services (cinema tickets, airline seats) more easily than for physical goods.\n4. Consumer ignorance (consumers unaware of different prices), consumer indifference (aware but don't care), or consumer attitudes (willing to pay more rather than go without).\n\n(c) Arguments FOR regulating Irish Water:\n- Natural monopoly: Irish Water is a natural monopoly (one supplier is more efficient than many due to the infrastructure costs). Without regulation, it could exploit its monopoly power by charging excessive prices.\n- Essential service: Water is a necessity for life. Regulation ensures access to clean, affordable water for all citizens, protecting vulnerable consumers.\n- Quality standards: A regulator ensures water quality meets health and safety standards.\n- Accountability: Regulation provides oversight and accountability for public funds invested in water infrastructure.\n- Environmental protection: Regulation ensures compliance with EU environmental directives on water quality and wastewater treatment.\n\nArguments AGAINST regulation (or for lighter regulation):\n- Cost of regulation: The regulatory process itself is expensive. The cost of establishing and maintaining a regulator adds to the overall cost of water services.\n- Bureaucracy: Excessive regulation can slow down decision-making and infrastructure investment.\n- Political interference: If the regulator is not truly independent, political considerations may override economic efficiency.\n- Market-based solutions: Some argue that proper pricing of water (reflecting its true cost) would encourage conservation and efficient use without the need for extensive regulation."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Costs, Economies & Diseconomies of Scale",
     q:"(a) In the case of any two of the following three pairs, distinguish between the two concepts:\n- Marginal Cost and Average Cost\n- Explicit Cost and Implicit Cost\n- Normal Profit and Supernormal Profit\n\n(b) The table shows output and production costs for a small bakery:\nUnits of Bread: 0, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700\nTotal Costs (EUR): 100, 200, 280, 330, 360, 450, 600, 770\n\n(i) What are the fixed costs? Explain. What are the variable costs of producing 300 loaves? What is the average cost of producing 400 loaves?\n(ii) Using the data, draw one graph showing Total Costs (TC), Total Variable Costs (VC), and Total Fixed Costs (FC).\n(iii) Does the graph represent the short run or the long run? Outline a reason.\n\n(c) Discuss possible economies of scale and diseconomies of scale that the bakery may experience should it expand its scale of production in the long run.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a) Distinguishing between two pairs:\n\nMarginal Cost and Average Cost:\n- Marginal Cost (MC) is the extra cost of producing one additional unit of output. It is the change in total cost divided by the change in output (MC = Change in TC / Change in Q).\n- Average Cost (AC) is the total cost of production divided by the number of units produced (AC = TC / Q). It represents the cost per unit.\nKey relationship: When MC < AC, AC is falling. When MC > AC, AC is rising. MC intersects AC at its minimum point.\n\nExplicit Cost and Implicit Cost:\n- Explicit costs are actual out-of-pocket payments made by a firm for factors of production - e.g. wages paid to workers, rent paid for premises, cost of raw materials. These are recorded in the firm's accounts.\n- Implicit costs are the opportunity costs of using the firm's own resources for which no actual payment is made - e.g. the salary the owner could have earned working elsewhere, or the rent that could have been earned by renting out the owner's premises.\n\nNormal Profit and Supernormal Profit:\n- Normal profit is the minimum level of profit necessary to keep the entrepreneur in the current line of business. It is considered a cost of production (part of AC) and equals the opportunity cost of the entrepreneur's time and capital.\n- Supernormal profit (abnormal/excess profit) is any profit earned above normal profit. It occurs when Total Revenue exceeds Total Cost (including normal profit). Supernormal profit = TR - TC (where TC includes normal profit).\n\n(b)(i) Calculations:\n- Fixed costs = EUR100. These are the costs when output is zero (0 units of bread). Fixed costs do not change with output - they must be paid regardless of production level (e.g. rent, insurance, loan repayments).\n- Variable costs of producing 300 loaves = Total Cost at 300 - Fixed Costs = EUR330 - EUR100 = EUR230. Variable costs are the costs that change with the level of output.\n- Average cost of producing 400 loaves = Total Cost at 400 / 400 = EUR360 / 400 = EUR0.90 per loaf.\n\n(ii) Graph description:\nX-axis: Output (units of bread, 0-700). Y-axis: Costs (EUR).\n- FC: Horizontal line at EUR100 (fixed costs remain constant at all output levels).\n- VC: Curve starting at 0 when output is 0, rising to EUR100 at 100 units, EUR180 at 200, EUR230 at 300, EUR260 at 400, EUR350 at 500, EUR500 at 600, EUR670 at 700. The curve rises at an increasing rate at higher output levels.\n- TC: Curve starting at EUR100 (= FC when output is 0), running parallel to VC but EUR100 above it at every point.\n\n(iii) The graph represents the SHORT RUN. In the short run, at least one factor of production is fixed (as shown by the fixed costs of EUR100 which remain constant regardless of output). In the long run, all factors of production are variable and there would be no fixed costs.\n\n(c) Economies of scale (advantages of expanding):\n1. Purchasing/bulk-buying economies: A larger bakery can negotiate lower prices for flour, sugar, and other ingredients by buying in bulk, reducing average costs.\n2. Technical economies: A larger bakery can invest in specialised, efficient machinery (industrial ovens, automated packaging) that would not be cost-effective for a small operation.\n3. Financial economies: Larger firms can access finance at lower interest rates and on better terms than small firms.\n4. Marketing economies: Advertising costs can be spread over a larger output, reducing the marketing cost per unit.\n\nDiseconomies of scale (disadvantages of expanding):\n1. Management/coordination problems: As the bakery grows, communication between management and staff becomes more difficult. Decision-making slows down, and there may be a loss of personal touch with employees and customers.\n2. Worker motivation: In a larger operation, workers may feel less valued and less motivated, leading to lower productivity, higher absenteeism, and staff turnover.\n3. Increased waste: A larger operation may find it harder to monitor quality and waste, leading to inefficiency.\n4. Industrial relations issues: Larger workforces may lead to union involvement and potential industrial disputes."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Enterprise, Investment & Keynes",
     q:"(a)(i) State and explain two economic characteristics of enterprise as a factor of production.\n(ii) Explain the importance of profits in a market economy.\n\n(b)(i) Distinguish between the terms capital and investment as used by economists.\n(ii) Discuss the case for and against the Government investing in Ireland's railways.\n\n(c)(i) State and explain the three motives put forward by John Maynard Keynes for holding assets in the form of cash.\n(ii) Outline one main influence on each motive.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Two characteristics of enterprise:\n1. Risk-bearing: The entrepreneur bears the risk of financial loss. Unlike other factors of production which receive contractual payments (wages, rent, interest), the entrepreneur's return is residual and uncertain. The entrepreneur invests personal capital and time with no guarantee of return.\n2. Decision-making and organisation: The entrepreneur organises and combines the other three factors of production (land, labour, capital) to produce goods and services. This involves making strategic decisions about what to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce.\n\nOther characteristics: Innovation (introducing new products, processes, or methods); initiative (identifying business opportunities); the entrepreneur earns profit as their reward.\n\n(ii) Importance of profits in a market economy:\n- Signal for resource allocation: Profits signal where resources should be directed. High profits in an industry attract new firms and resources, while losses signal that resources should move elsewhere.\n- Incentive for enterprise: The prospect of profit motivates entrepreneurs to take risks, innovate, and start new businesses. Without the profit incentive, there would be less enterprise and innovation.\n- Source of investment: Retained profits provide firms with internal funds for investment in new equipment, R&D, and expansion, driving economic growth.\n- Reward for efficiency: Profits reward firms that are efficient and that produce goods consumers want. They encourage continuous improvement and cost reduction.\n- Tax revenue: Profits generate corporation tax for the government.\n\n(b)(i) Distinction between capital and investment:\n- Capital (as used by economists) refers to man-made aids to production - the stock of physical assets used in the production process, such as machinery, equipment, buildings, tools, and vehicles. It is a stock concept (measured at a point in time).\n- Investment refers to the creation of new capital goods or additions to the existing stock of capital. It is a flow concept (measured over a period of time). Investment includes spending on new factories, machinery, and infrastructure.\n\n(ii) Case FOR government investing in Ireland's railways:\n- Reduced congestion and pollution: Improved rail services would encourage commuters to switch from cars to trains, reducing road congestion, carbon emissions, and air pollution.\n- Balanced regional development: Better rail connections between Dublin and regional cities would encourage economic activity outside Dublin, supporting balanced regional development.\n- Employment creation: Railway investment creates direct jobs in construction and operation, plus indirect jobs through the multiplier effect.\n- Attracting FDI: Improved transport infrastructure makes Ireland more attractive for foreign direct investment.\n\nCase AGAINST:\n- High opportunity cost: The massive investment required (billions of euro) could be spent on other priorities such as housing, healthcare, or education.\n- Low usage/demand: Outside Dublin, passenger numbers may not justify the investment. Ireland's dispersed population pattern may make rail less efficient than road transport.\n- Long payback period: Railways require decades of operation before investment costs are recovered, and may never be commercially viable without ongoing government subsidies.\n- Environmental disruption during construction: Building new rail lines can damage habitats and affect local communities.\n\n(c)(i) Keynes's three motives for holding cash:\n\n1. Transactions motive: People hold cash to finance their day-to-day purchases of goods and services. There is a time gap between receiving income and spending it, so people need to hold cash to cover regular expenditure (rent, food, transport).\n\n2. Precautionary motive: People hold cash as a precaution against unexpected or unforeseen events - e.g. medical emergencies, car breakdowns, sudden job loss. This provides a financial safety net for contingencies.\n\n3. Speculative motive: People hold cash in anticipation of future changes in the price of financial assets (bonds, shares). If a person expects bond prices to fall (interest rates to rise), they will hold cash now and buy bonds later at a lower price, hoping to make a capital gain.\n\n(ii) One main influence on each motive:\n- Transactions motive: Influenced by the level of income. Higher income means more transactions and a greater need to hold cash for daily spending.\n- Precautionary motive: Influenced by the degree of uncertainty in the economy. Greater economic uncertainty (e.g. during a recession or pandemic) increases the precautionary demand for cash.\n- Speculative motive: Influenced by the rate of interest / expectations of future interest rate changes. If interest rates are expected to rise (bond prices to fall), people hold more cash. If interest rates are expected to fall (bond prices to rise), people hold less cash and buy bonds."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): Banking, Property Market & SMEs",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline three factors that limit the ability of banks to create credit.\n(ii) Discuss the economic reasons why the financial system in Ireland should be regulated.\n\n(b) In January 2015, the Central Bank of Ireland (CBI) introduced restrictions on residential mortgage lending (bigger deposit requirement and upper limits on borrowing based on income). State and explain the possible economic impacts of these restrictions on the Irish property market.\n\n(c) The Strategic Banking Corporation of Ireland (SBCI) was set up to support SMEs by providing finance under favourable terms (e.g. lower cost credit). Discuss the possible economic benefits of increased access to funding for the SME sector in Ireland.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Three factors limiting banks' ability to create credit:\n1. Reserve ratio requirements: The Central Bank requires banks to hold a minimum percentage of their deposits as reserves (cash or deposits with the Central Bank). This limits the amount of money available for lending.\n2. Demand for loans: Banks can only create credit if there is sufficient demand from creditworthy borrowers. During recessions, demand for loans may fall as consumers and businesses are reluctant to borrow.\n3. Availability of deposits: Banks need deposits to lend. If consumers save less or withdraw deposits, banks have fewer funds available to create credit.\nOther factors: Central Bank regulations and capital adequacy requirements; the willingness of banks to lend (risk appetite); the interest rate set by the ECB.\n\n(ii) Reasons for regulating the financial system:\n- Systemic risk: The collapse of one bank can trigger the collapse of others (domino effect), as seen in the 2008 financial crisis. Regulation aims to prevent such systemic failures.\n- Consumer protection: Regulation protects consumers from predatory lending, mis-selling of financial products, and excessive charges. Consumers often lack the expertise to assess financial products.\n- Maintaining confidence: A well-regulated financial system maintains public confidence in banks. Loss of confidence can lead to bank runs and financial instability.\n- Preventing reckless lending: Without regulation, banks may engage in excessive and risky lending (as happened in Ireland during the Celtic Tiger era), which can lead to asset bubbles and financial crises.\n- Moral hazard: Banks may take excessive risks knowing the government will bail them out. Regulation limits this behaviour.\n\n(b) Economic impacts of CBI mortgage restrictions:\n1. Reduced demand for housing: Higher deposit requirements and income-based borrowing limits mean fewer people can afford to buy homes. This reduces demand in the property market.\n2. Slower house price growth: With reduced demand, the rate of house price increases should slow, helping to prevent another property bubble.\n3. Increased saving: First-time buyers must save larger deposits, which increases the savings rate in the economy.\n4. Impact on construction: Reduced demand for housing may slow construction activity and employment in the building sector.\n5. Rental market pressure: Those unable to buy may remain in the rental market longer, increasing demand for rental properties and potentially pushing up rents.\n6. Financial stability: The restrictions reduce the risk of a repeat of the property crash by ensuring borrowers can afford their mortgages, leading to fewer defaults.\n7. Inequality: The restrictions may disproportionately affect lower-income earners and first-time buyers who find it harder to save the required deposit, while those with existing wealth or family support are less affected.\n\n(c) Economic benefits of increased SME funding:\n1. Employment creation: SMEs are the backbone of the Irish economy, employing approximately 70% of the private sector workforce. Better access to finance allows SMEs to hire more staff, reducing unemployment.\n2. Economic growth: Increased investment by SMEs (in equipment, technology, expansion) boosts aggregate demand and contributes to economic growth through the multiplier effect.\n3. Innovation: Increased funding enables SMEs to invest in research and development, leading to new products, services, and processes that enhance competitiveness.\n4. Regional development: SMEs are distributed throughout the country, not just in Dublin. Supporting them promotes balanced regional development.\n5. Competition: A vibrant SME sector increases competition in the economy, leading to lower prices, better quality, and greater choice for consumers.\n6. Reduced dependency on MNCs: A strong indigenous SME sector reduces Ireland's dependency on multinational companies, whose decisions are made abroad and who may relocate.\n7. Tax revenue: Growing SMEs generate increased tax revenue for the government through corporation tax, income tax, and VAT."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): CPI, Deflation, Unemployment & Employment",
     q:"(a)(i) Do you consider the CPI an accurate measure of changes in the cost of living? Explain your answer.\n(ii) Discuss two possible economic effects of price deflation on the Irish economy.\n\n(b)(i) State and explain three types of unemployment.\n(ii) Outline one appropriate government policy response for each of two unemployment types you have explained.\n\n(c) According to the QNHS, the rate of unemployment was 11.1% in Q3 2014.\n(i) Name one other commonly used source of unemployment statistics in Ireland.\n(ii) State and explain the reasons for the differences between both sources.\n(iii) Outline the possible economic effects of a rise in the level of employment.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) The CPI may not be a fully accurate measure of changes in the cost of living:\n\nArguments that it is inaccurate:\n- The CPI is based on a representative 'basket of goods' which may not reflect the spending patterns of every individual or household. Different people buy different things.\n- The basket is updated periodically but may not capture rapidly changing consumption patterns (e.g. the growth in online spending, new technology products).\n- Quality improvements are difficult to account for. If a product improves in quality but its price increases, the CPI records this as inflation even though the consumer is getting a better product.\n- It does not account for regional price differences across Ireland.\n- Changes in mortgage interest rates can significantly affect the CPI but may not reflect underlying inflation.\n\nArguments that it is useful:\n- It provides a standardised, consistent measure that allows comparison over time and between countries.\n- The basket is regularly reviewed and updated to reflect changing consumption patterns.\n\n(ii) Two economic effects of deflation:\n1. Deferred consumption: If consumers expect prices to continue falling, they may postpone purchases (why buy today if it will be cheaper tomorrow?). This reduces aggregate demand, leading to further falls in output, employment, and potentially a deflationary spiral.\n2. Increased real debt burden: The real value of debt increases when prices fall. Borrowers must repay debts that are worth more in real terms than when they were taken out. This is particularly damaging for households with mortgages and for the government's national debt.\n\nOther effects: Reduced business profits and investment; potential for increased unemployment; lower tax revenue for government.\n\n(b)(i) Three types of unemployment:\n1. Frictional unemployment: Short-term unemployment that occurs when workers are between jobs - e.g. someone who has left one job and is searching for another. It is a normal part of a functioning economy and is usually temporary.\n2. Structural unemployment: Occurs when there is a mismatch between the skills workers have and the skills required by available jobs. It is caused by changes in the structure of the economy, such as the decline of traditional industries (e.g. textiles) or the introduction of new technology.\n3. Cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment: Occurs during economic recessions when aggregate demand falls. Firms reduce output and lay off workers because there is insufficient demand for their products.\n\n(ii) Policy responses:\n- Frictional unemployment: Improve information flows in the labour market through job centres, online job portals, and career guidance services, helping workers find suitable jobs more quickly.\n- Structural unemployment: Government-funded retraining and education programmes (e.g. Springboard, Momentum) to help workers acquire the new skills demanded by the economy.\n- Cyclical unemployment: Expansionary fiscal policy (increase government spending, reduce taxes) to boost aggregate demand and create jobs.\n\n(c)(i) Another source: The Live Register (maintained by the Department of Social Protection), which counts the number of people signing on for unemployment-related social welfare payments.\n\n(ii) Differences between the QNHS and the Live Register:\n- Method of collection: The QNHS is a household survey based on the ILO definition of unemployment (actively seeking work and available to start). The Live Register counts those signing on for Jobseeker's Benefit/Allowance.\n- The Live Register may include part-time workers who work up to 3 days per week and claim a partial payment, inflating the figure compared to the QNHS.\n- The Live Register may exclude some unemployed people who are not entitled to benefits (e.g. those whose claims have expired), understating unemployment.\n- The QNHS is based on a sample survey and uses statistical estimation, while the Live Register is an administrative count.\n\n(iii) Economic effects of a rise in employment:\n- Increased tax revenue: More workers paying PAYE income tax, USC, and PRSI. Increased consumer spending generates more VAT revenue.\n- Reduced government expenditure: Fewer people claiming Jobseeker's Benefit/Allowance, reducing social welfare costs.\n- Increased economic growth: More people working means more goods and services produced, increasing national output (GDP/GNP).\n- Improved consumer confidence: People in employment are more confident about spending, boosting aggregate demand.\n- Potential inflationary pressure: As employment approaches full employment, upward pressure on wages may develop, potentially leading to demand-pull inflation."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): Circular Flow of Income & Economic Growth",
     q:"(a)(i) Draw a fully labelled diagram of the circular flow of income, identifying the main flows (money and real) between households and firms.\n(ii) Show and explain on your diagram the injections into and leakages from the circular flow.\n\n(b)(i) How can the government use the circular flow model to determine the equilibrium level of national income?\n(ii) Using the circular flow model, explain how an increase in government spending would affect the level of national income.\n\n(c)(i) Outline three ways in which economic growth can be measured.\n(ii) Discuss three factors that could contribute to economic growth in the Irish economy.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Circular Flow diagram description:\nThe diagram shows two main sectors: Households and Firms, connected by two sets of flows.\n\nReal flows:\n- Households supply factors of production (land, labour, capital, enterprise) to firms.\n- Firms supply goods and services to households.\n\nMoney flows:\n- Firms pay factor incomes (wages, rent, interest, profit) to households.\n- Households pay expenditure to firms for goods and services.\n\n(ii) Injections and Leakages:\nInjections (flows INTO the circular flow that increase its size):\n- Investment (I): Spending by firms on capital goods (machinery, equipment).\n- Government Spending (G): Government expenditure on public services, infrastructure, and social welfare.\n- Exports (X): Revenue from goods and services sold to foreign buyers.\n\nLeakages (flows OUT of the circular flow that reduce its size):\n- Savings (S): Income not spent by households, deposited in financial institutions.\n- Taxation (T): Payments from households and firms to the government.\n- Imports (M): Spending on goods and services produced in other countries.\n\nThe economy is in equilibrium when total injections equal total leakages: I + G + X = S + T + M.\n\n(b)(i) The government can determine the equilibrium level of national income by comparing total injections with total leakages. If injections exceed leakages, national income is rising (expansionary). If leakages exceed injections, national income is falling (contractionary). Equilibrium occurs when I + G + X = S + T + M.\n\n(ii) An increase in government spending (G) represents an increase in injections into the circular flow. This creates a situation where injections exceed leakages, causing national income to rise. The initial increase in G leads to increased income for firms and their employees, who then spend a portion of this extra income (based on MPC), creating further income for others. This process continues, generating a multiplier effect, so the final increase in national income is a multiple of the original increase in government spending.\n\n(c)(i) Three ways of measuring economic growth:\n1. GDP/GNP growth rate: Measuring the percentage change in real GDP or GNP from one period to another. This is the most common measure.\n2. GDP/GNP per capita: Dividing total GDP/GNP by population gives an average measure of output per person, allowing comparison of living standards.\n3. Modified GNI (GNI*): Used in Ireland to strip out the distorting effects of multinational activity, giving a more accurate picture of the domestic economy's growth.\n\n(ii) Three factors contributing to economic growth in Ireland:\n1. Investment in education and skills: A well-educated, highly skilled workforce is essential for productivity growth. Ireland's investment in third-level education has been crucial in attracting high-tech FDI and building a knowledge economy.\n2. Foreign Direct Investment (FDI): Ireland has attracted significant FDI from multinational companies (particularly in technology, pharmaceuticals, and financial services), which has been a major driver of exports, employment, and economic growth.\n3. Investment in infrastructure: Improvements in roads, broadband, public transport, and housing increase the productive capacity of the economy and reduce costs for businesses, supporting long-term growth.\nOther factors: Favourable tax regime (corporation tax); EU membership and access to the Single Market; innovation and R&D investment; demographic dividend (young, growing population)."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8 (Section B): Budget Deficits, Regional Development & Emigration",
     q:"(a) Explain the economic effects of persistent government budget deficits.\n\n(b)(i) Outline three government policies that could promote balanced regional development in Ireland.\n(ii) Discuss the economic arguments for and against balanced regional development.\n\n(c)(i) Outline three economic effects of emigration on the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss how emigration affects the factors of production available to the Irish economy.\n\n[75 marks]",
     a:"(a) Economic effects of persistent budget deficits:\n1. Rising national debt: Each year's deficit adds to the accumulated national debt. The government must borrow more by issuing bonds, increasing the total stock of debt.\n2. Increased interest payments: As the national debt grows, the interest payments (debt servicing costs) also increase. These payments consume a larger share of government revenue, leaving less for public services.\n3. Crowding out: Government borrowing increases demand for loanable funds, potentially driving up interest rates. Higher interest rates may discourage private sector investment (crowding out effect), reducing long-run economic growth.\n4. Loss of creditworthiness: Persistent deficits may lead to credit rating downgrades, making it more expensive for the government to borrow and potentially leading to a loss of access to bond markets (as Ireland experienced during the financial crisis).\n5. Burden on future generations: Today's debt must be repaid by future taxpayers, creating an intergenerational equity issue.\n\n(b)(i) Three government policies for balanced regional development:\n1. Decentralisation: Moving government departments and agencies from Dublin to regional centres to create employment and stimulate local economies.\n2. Investment in regional infrastructure: Improving roads, broadband, public transport, and utilities in regional areas to attract businesses and make regions more accessible.\n3. Regional enterprise supports: Providing grants, tax incentives, and support services through IDA Ireland, Enterprise Ireland, and Local Enterprise Offices to attract businesses to regions outside Dublin.\n\n(ii) Arguments FOR balanced regional development:\n- Reduces congestion and housing pressure in Dublin, improving quality of life.\n- Utilises resources (land, labour) in regions that would otherwise be underused.\n- Reduces regional disparities in income and employment.\n- Prevents depopulation of rural areas and maintains communities.\n\nArguments AGAINST:\n- It may be economically inefficient to force businesses away from Dublin where agglomeration economies exist (concentration of skilled labour, infrastructure, and services).\n- Higher costs for businesses locating in regions with poorer infrastructure.\n- Government resources spent on regional development could be more productively used elsewhere.\n- Some degree of regional inequality is inevitable and natural in any economy.\n\n(c)(i) Three economic effects of emigration:\n1. Loss of human capital: Emigrants are often young, educated, and skilled. Ireland invested in their education and training but loses the return on this investment when they leave. This is often called a 'brain drain'.\n2. Reduced domestic demand: Fewer people means less consumer spending, reducing aggregate demand and the multiplier effect in the economy.\n3. Remittances: Emigrants may send money back to family in Ireland, providing an inflow of income. However, for Ireland (as a developed country), this effect is smaller than for LDCs.\n\nOther effects: Reduced labour supply and potential skill shortages; reduced tax revenue; alleviation of unemployment pressure; possible return of emigrants with new skills and experience in the future.\n\n(ii) Impact on factors of production:\n- Labour: Emigration directly reduces the supply of labour available to the economy. If skilled workers emigrate, it creates shortages in specific sectors (healthcare, engineering, IT). It also reduces the dependency ratio in the short term but may increase it in the long term as the population ages.\n- Enterprise: Some emigrants are potential entrepreneurs who would have started businesses in Ireland. Their departure reduces the supply of enterprise.\n- Capital: Emigrants take their savings with them, reducing the pool of domestic savings available for investment. Less capital formation occurs.\n- Land: Emigration does not directly affect the supply of land, but depopulation of rural areas may lead to land being underutilised or abandoned, reducing its productive value."}
  ],

"2016": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Perfect Competition (Short Run)",
     q:"The diagram shows a firm operating under conditions of perfect competition in the short run.\n\n(a) What is represented by the lines numbered 1 to 3? (16 marks)\n\n(b)(i) Show clearly on the diagram the total supernormal profit of the firm.\n(ii) Explain the term supernormal profit.",
     a:"(a) Identification of lines:\n- Line 1: Marginal Cost (MC)\n- Line 2: Average Cost (AC)\n- Line 3: Average Revenue / Marginal Revenue / Demand (AR = MR = D)\n\n(b)(i) The total supernormal profit is shown by the shaded area between the AR (price) line and the AC curve, at the profit-maximising output level (where MC = MR). This is the rectangle between Price and AC, multiplied by the quantity produced.\n\n(b)(ii) Supernormal profit is any profit earned in excess of normal profit. Normal profit is included as part of Average Cost (the minimum return needed to keep the entrepreneur in the industry). Supernormal profit arises when Average Revenue (AR) is greater than Average Cost (AC) at the firm's level of output."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns",
     q:"Define the Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns and illustrate this with a suitable example. (16 marks)",
     a:"Definition: The Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns states that if increasing quantities of a variable factor of production (e.g. labour) are combined with a given quantity of a fixed factor of production (e.g. capital/land), a stage will eventually be reached where the addition to total output (the marginal returns / output per unit of the variable factor) will begin to decrease.\n\nExample: Consider a farmer with a fixed 10-acre field (fixed factor). As the farmer hires additional workers (variable factor):\n- 1 worker produces 100 units of wheat\n- 2 workers produce 220 units (marginal return = 120)\n- 3 workers produce 350 units (marginal return = 130)\n- 4 workers produce 460 units (marginal return = 110 - diminishing returns set in)\n- 5 workers produce 550 units (marginal return = 90)\nAfter the 3rd worker, adding more workers to the same fixed amount of land leads to smaller and smaller increases in total output, as the land becomes overcrowded and workers get in each other's way."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Mobility of Labour",
     q:"Outline two ways the Irish government could improve the mobility of labour in order to reduce unemployment. (16 marks)",
     a:"1. Occupational mobility: The government could provide retraining programmes so that workers can gain the skills they need to move across sectors. For example, workers displaced from declining industries (e.g. traditional manufacturing) could be retrained for growing sectors such as technology or healthcare. The government could also provide courses for further educational opportunities at reasonable costs, or introduce laws that ban restrictive practices which prevent workers moving between jobs.\n\n2. Geographical mobility: The government could offer relocation subsidies to overcome geographic immobility and encourage workers to move to where they are needed. It could also provide more affordable social housing or offer subsidies to those wishing to buy or rent accommodation in areas where jobs are available. Improving social infrastructure (parks, leisure facilities, schools) in regional areas would also make these locations more appealing for families willing to relocate."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Economies of Scale",
     q:"Firms within the aircraft industry can benefit from economies of scale. Explain the term economies of scale and provide one example for a firm in the aircraft industry. (16 marks)",
     a:"Explanation: Economies of scale result in a reduction in the long-run average cost (LRAC) of production as a firm or industry increases its size of operation. They are the ways in which an increase in output or capacity can reduce average costs per unit.\n\nExample (aircraft industry): Purchasing economies - large firms in the aircraft industry can usually negotiate significant discounts because they buy in bulk. For example, Ryanair negotiated more favourable prices when ordering a large quantity of Boeing 737 aircraft, achieving a much lower per-unit cost than a smaller airline ordering just a few planes. Similarly, large airlines can negotiate bulk discounts on fuel purchases, reducing their operating costs per flight.\n\nOther examples include:\n- Technical economies: A large airline can justify purchasing large, fuel-efficient aircraft because it can spread the cost over a larger number of passengers.\n- Financial economies: A large aircraft manufacturer will have access to a greater range of finance options and can borrow at lower interest rates than a small manufacturer."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Youth Unemployment",
     q:"(a) Outline two possible effects on the Irish economy of high levels of youth unemployment.\n\n(b) State one economic policy the government could introduce to reduce youth unemployment. (16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Two effects of high youth unemployment:\n\n1. Increase in government expenditure: High youth unemployment leads to increased costs of social welfare payments (Jobseeker's Allowance, Back to Education Allowance etc.), putting pressure on government finances.\n\n2. Emigration / brain drain: Young people leave the country in search of better opportunities elsewhere, resulting in a loss of skilled workers. This also represents a loss of the state's investment in their education, as taxpayers funded their schooling but the country doesn't benefit from their productivity. Rural communities are particularly affected, with fewer young people remaining.\n\n(b) One economic policy:\nThe government could improve education opportunities and access by reducing college registration fees and ensuring that good quality education and training takes place for skills that are in demand in growing sectors of the economy. Alternatively, it could review internship and apprenticeship schemes to ensure they provide meaningful opportunities for young workers to develop skills and create a pathway towards viable long-term employment."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Income Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"The table shows the annual average level of income in a country and the corresponding demand for Product A for two years:\nYear 1: Income = €57,000, Product A = 100 units\nYear 2: Income = €63,000, Product A = 200 units\n\n(i) Calculate the income elasticity of demand (YED) for Product A. Show your workings.\n(ii) Using your knowledge of YED, explain the economic meaning of this figure. (17 marks)",
     a:"(i) Calculation using the mid-point (arc) formula:\nYED = (Change in Qd / Change in Y) x (Y1 + Y2) / (Qd1 + Qd2)\n= (+100 / +€6,000) x (€57,000 + €63,000) / (100 + 200)\n= (100 / 6,000) x (120,000 / 300)\n= 0.01667 x 400\n= +6.67\n\n(ii) Economic meaning:\n\nPositive sign (+): This indicates that Product A is a normal good. As income increases, the quantity demanded also increases. There is a positive relationship between income and demand.\n\nValue of 6.67 (greater than 1): This indicates that Product A is a luxury good, as demand is income elastic. The percentage increase in quantity demanded (100%) is greater than the percentage increase in income (approximately 10.5%). As income rises, consumers spend proportionally more on this good. Firms selling luxury goods can expect demand to rise significantly during periods of economic growth but also to fall sharply during recessions."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Monetary Policy & Price Stability",
     q:"The primary aim of the ECB's monetary policy is to maintain price stability.\n\n(a) Define the term monetary policy.\n\n(b) Outline two benefits of price stability for the Irish economy. (17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Definition: Monetary policy refers to those actions by the European Central Bank (ECB) which influence the money supply, interest rates, or the availability of credit in the economy. The ECB uses monetary policy tools to achieve its primary objective of price stability (targeting inflation close to 2% over the medium term).\n\n(b) Two benefits of price stability:\n\n1. Maintain value of money / purchasing power: When prices are stable, people's purchasing power is maintained. Those at work and those on fixed incomes (e.g. pensioners) can maintain their standard of living as the real value of their income is preserved. Without price stability, inflation erodes the real value of incomes and savings.\n\n2. Improved international competitiveness: If Ireland's rate of inflation is lower than its trading partners, then Irish exports become cheaper and more competitive on world markets. This supports employment in export-oriented industries and helps maintain a favourable balance of payments. Stable prices also encourage businesses to invest, as they face more predictable costs of production, particularly wage costs."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Insurance Levy & Flooding Crisis",
     q:"(a) Outline two possible economic effects of the Irish government introducing a levy on home insurance premiums to help address damage caused by the recent flooding crisis in Ireland.\n\n(b) Suggest one alternative method for addressing the flooding crisis in Ireland. (17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Two possible economic effects:\n\n1. Increased cost of insurance for householders: The levy would increase the price of home insurance, resulting in reduced disposable income for households and a consequent reduction in their standard of living. Some households may reduce their level of cover or choose not to renew their insurance policies, leaving them vulnerable to future flood damage.\n\n2. Revenue for compensation: The funds collected from the levy could help provide compensation for those who have suffered property damage and financial loss due to the flooding. This would help affected households and businesses recover and rebuild, supporting economic activity in flood-damaged areas.\n\n(b) Alternative method:\nInvestment by the government in improved flood protection and barrier programmes around the country. This would involve building flood defences such as embankments, barriers, and drainage systems in vulnerable areas. While this requires significant upfront capital expenditure, it provides long-term protection against future flooding events and avoids the ongoing costs of repeated compensation. Local authorities should also set more stringent requirements for granting planning permission, especially on flood plains."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: National Minimum Wage",
     q:"\"The national minimum wage is increased by 50 cent, bringing it to €9.15 in a bid to make work pay.\" (Source: Irish Independent, October 2015)\n\nOutline three possible economic impacts of this measure on the Irish economy. (17 marks)",
     a:"1. Higher standard of living for low-income families: The increase in the minimum wage means low-income workers can purchase more goods and services. It alleviates poverty by raising the living standards of the poorest groups in society and improves the incentive to work, as the gap between welfare payments and wages widens.\n\n2. Increased aggregate demand: Workers on the minimum wage tend to spend a high proportion of their income. The wage increase puts more money in their pockets, leading to increased consumer spending in the economy. This boosts demand for goods and services, potentially creating further employment through the multiplier effect. The government also benefits from increased tax revenue through higher income tax and VAT receipts.\n\n3. Increased costs for businesses / potential unemployment: The wage increase raises input costs for firms, particularly in labour-intensive sectors such as hospitality, retail, and restaurants. Some firms may pass these costs on to consumers through higher prices (cost-push inflation), reducing the competitiveness of Irish firms. Others may reduce their workforce or cut back on hours to control costs, potentially leading to higher unemployment among low-skilled workers. Some firms may even consider relocating to countries with lower wage levels."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1: Supply, Elasticity & Concert Ticket Shortage",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline four factors that determine the supply of a good or service.\n(ii) Explain the difference between a movement along a supply curve and a shift in a supply curve. Use appropriate diagrams to illustrate your answer. [35 marks]\n\n(b) Read the following statements and indicate if they are TRUE or FALSE. Explain your answer in each case:\n(i) The cross price elasticity of demand for substitute goods has a negative value.\n(ii) Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) tends to be more elastic in the long-run than in the short-run.\n(iii) When demand for a good is price inelastic, a reduction in price will increase total sales revenue.\n(iv) Income elasticity of demand (YED) for luxury goods is positive. [24 marks]\n\n(c) Using the concepts of demand and supply explain, with the aid of a labelled diagram, how a shortage of tickets for a major concert may arise. The concert venue has a maximum capacity of 30,000 people. [16 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Four factors determining supply:\n1. Cost of production: If costs of raw materials, labour, or energy increase, supply decreases as it becomes less profitable to produce.\n2. Technology: Advances in technology can reduce production costs and increase efficiency, shifting supply to the right.\n3. Government policy: Subsidies increase supply; taxes and regulations decrease supply.\n4. Price of related goods: If the price of an alternative product a firm could produce rises, supply of the current good may fall (competitive supply).\n\n(ii) Movement along a supply curve occurs when the price of the good itself changes. A rise in price leads to a movement up along the supply curve (extension of supply), while a fall in price leads to a movement down (contraction of supply). The supply curve itself does not move.\n\nA shift in the supply curve occurs when a non-price factor changes (e.g. cost of production, technology, government policy). A rightward shift indicates an increase in supply at every price level, while a leftward shift indicates a decrease in supply at every price level.\n\nDiagram 1: Movement along S - show arrows moving along the same curve as price changes.\nDiagram 2: Shift in S - show S1 shifting to S2 (right) with labels indicating a change in a non-price factor.\n\n(b) True/False explanations:\n\n(i) FALSE. The cross price elasticity of demand for substitute goods has a POSITIVE value. If the price of Good B (a substitute) rises, demand for Good A increases. Price and quantity demanded move in the same direction, giving a positive CED.\n\n(ii) TRUE. PED tends to be more elastic in the long run because consumers have more time to find substitutes, change their habits, or adjust their spending patterns. In the short run, consumers may have little choice but to continue purchasing the good.\n\n(iii) FALSE. When demand is price inelastic, a reduction in price will DECREASE total sales revenue. The percentage increase in quantity demanded will be smaller than the percentage decrease in price, so overall revenue falls. (To increase revenue with inelastic demand, a firm should raise prices.)\n\n(iv) TRUE. Income elasticity of demand for luxury goods is positive and greater than 1. As income rises, the quantity demanded of luxury goods rises by a greater proportion. Consumers spend a larger share of their income on luxuries as they become wealthier.\n\n(c) Concert ticket shortage:\nDiagram: Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis. Supply is a vertical (perfectly inelastic) line at 30,000 tickets (the venue's fixed capacity). The downward-sloping demand curve intersects the supply curve above the ticket price set by the promoter.\n\nExplanation: The supply of concert tickets is perfectly inelastic at 30,000 (the venue cannot increase capacity regardless of price). If the ticket price is set by the promoter below the equilibrium price (where demand intersects the fixed supply), the quantity demanded at that price exceeds 30,000. This creates excess demand (a shortage). The concert sells out quickly, and many fans who wanted tickets are unable to purchase them. If the promoter charged the equilibrium price, the shortage would not arise, but tickets would be more expensive."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2: Oligopoly (Chocolate Industry) & Small vs Large Firms",
     q:"Cadbury, Mars and Nestle dominate the chocolate industry in the European Union.\n\n(a)(i) State a market structure which most closely reflects the situation above, giving a reason for your answer.\n(ii) Outline two other key characteristics of this market structure.\n(iii) Explain, with the aid of a labelled diagram, the likely shape of the demand curve in this market structure. [35 marks]\n\n(b)(i) If a large US chocolate manufacturer entered the EU chocolate market, outline the possible economic impacts of greater competition in this market.\n(ii) Outline two factors which could make it difficult for this US chocolate manufacturer to enter the EU chocolate market. [25 marks]\n\n(c) Explain why small firms succeed in some markets while other markets are dominated by large firms. [15 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Market structure: Oligopoly. This is because a small number of large firms (Cadbury, Mars, Nestle) dominate the chocolate market. These few firms control a large share of total market output and are interdependent in their decision-making.\n\n(ii) Two other key characteristics of oligopoly:\n1. Interdependence: Each firm must consider the likely reaction of its rivals when making pricing and output decisions. If one firm lowers its price, others may follow, leading to a price war.\n2. Non-price competition: Firms compete through advertising, branding, product differentiation, and marketing rather than through price alone. Chocolate firms invest heavily in brand image and new product development.\n\n(iii) The demand curve in oligopoly is kinked. The kink occurs at the prevailing market price.\nDiagram: Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis. The demand curve has a kink at the current price (P1, Q1). Above P1, the curve is relatively elastic (flat) because if the firm raises its price, rivals will not follow, and the firm loses significant market share. Below P1, the curve is relatively inelastic (steep) because if the firm lowers its price, rivals will match the price cut, and the firm gains little extra demand.\nThis explains price rigidity in oligopolistic markets - firms have little incentive to change price.\n\n(b)(i) Impacts of greater competition:\n- Lower prices for consumers as firms compete for market share.\n- Greater product variety and innovation as firms try to differentiate their products.\n- Improved quality of products and services to attract customers.\n- Existing firms may become more efficient to reduce costs and remain competitive.\n- Some existing firms may lose market share and reduce output/employment.\n\n(ii) Two barriers to entry for the US firm:\n1. Brand loyalty: Cadbury, Mars, and Nestle have established strong brand recognition and consumer loyalty through years of advertising and marketing. The US firm would need to invest heavily in marketing to break into the market.\n2. Economies of scale: Existing firms benefit from large-scale production, giving them lower average costs. A new entrant producing at a smaller scale would face higher unit costs, making it difficult to compete on price. EU regulatory requirements and quality standards may also create additional costs.\n\n(c) Why small firms succeed in some markets:\n- Niche markets: Small firms can target specialised segments where demand is too small to attract large firms (e.g. artisan foods, bespoke tailoring).\n- Personal service: Small firms can offer personalised customer service that large firms cannot easily replicate.\n- Flexibility: Small firms can adapt quickly to changing market conditions and consumer preferences.\n- Lower overheads: Small firms may operate with lower fixed costs.\n\nWhy large firms dominate other markets:\n- Economies of scale: Industries with high fixed costs (e.g. car manufacturing, airlines) require large-scale production to achieve competitive unit costs.\n- Barriers to entry: Large capital requirements, patents, and established distribution networks prevent small firms from entering.\n- Brand power: Large firms can afford extensive advertising campaigns to build strong brands.\n- Research and development: Industries requiring significant R&D investment (e.g. pharmaceuticals) favour large firms with deep pockets."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3: Labour Market",
     q:"(a) Explain the following terms in relation to the factor of production labour:\n(i) Participation rate\n(ii) Real wages\n(iii) Labour Productivity\n(iv) Derived demand [20 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Discuss the factors that impact on the supply of labour.\n(ii) Outline two challenges currently facing the Irish labour market and state one appropriate government policy response for each challenge identified. [35 marks]\n\n(c) Discuss reasons why different workers are paid different wage rates. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a) Definitions:\n\n(i) Participation rate: The percentage of the total working-age population (usually 15-64) who are either employed or actively seeking employment (i.e. in the labour force). It excludes students, retirees, stay-at-home parents, and others who are not seeking work.\n\n(ii) Real wages: The purchasing power of wages, i.e. the amount of goods and services a worker can buy with their wage after adjusting for inflation. Real wages = Nominal wages adjusted for changes in the price level. If wages rise by 3% but inflation is 2%, real wages have increased by approximately 1%.\n\n(iii) Labour Productivity: The output per worker or per hour worked. It measures how efficiently labour is being used in the production process. Higher labour productivity means more output is produced per unit of labour input, which can lead to lower average costs and improved competitiveness.\n\n(iv) Derived demand: Labour is not demanded for its own sake but rather for its contribution to producing goods and services that consumers want. The demand for labour is derived from the demand for the product it produces. For example, if demand for new houses increases, demand for construction workers (labour) also increases.\n\n(b)(i) Factors impacting the supply of labour:\n- Wage rates: Higher wages attract more workers into the labour force and into particular occupations.\n- Population size and demographics: A growing population or immigration increases the potential labour supply.\n- Education and training: Better education and training opportunities encourage labour force participation and influence the types of jobs workers can supply.\n- Government policy: Tax rates, social welfare levels, childcare subsidies, and retirement age all affect the incentive and ability to work.\n- Non-monetary factors: Working conditions, job security, work-life balance, and career prospects influence workers' decisions to supply their labour.\n- Social and cultural factors: Changing attitudes to working mothers, retirement, and career choices affect participation rates.\n\n(ii) Two challenges and policy responses:\n\n1. Challenge: Skills mismatch - there is a gap between the skills employers need and the skills available in the labour force, particularly in areas like technology, engineering, and healthcare.\nPolicy response: The government could invest in education and retraining programmes (e.g. Springboard, Skillnet Ireland) targeted at sectors experiencing skills shortages, ensuring the workforce has the skills needed by growing industries.\n\n2. Challenge: High cost of living, especially housing - the housing crisis makes it difficult for workers to live near their place of work, reducing geographical mobility.\nPolicy response: The government could invest in social and affordable housing, provide rent subsidies, and improve public transport infrastructure to allow workers to commute from areas with more affordable housing.\n\n(c) Reasons for different wage rates:\n\n1. Education and skills: Workers with higher levels of education, training, and qualifications command higher wages due to their greater productivity and the investment made in their human capital.\n\n2. Supply and demand: Occupations where labour supply is scarce relative to demand (e.g. specialist surgeons, software engineers) command higher wages. Jobs with an abundant supply of available workers tend to have lower wages.\n\n3. Risk and responsibility: Jobs involving danger (e.g. mining, offshore oil work), unsocial hours, or heavy responsibility (e.g. airline pilots) attract compensating differentials - higher pay to attract workers.\n\n4. Trade union power: Workers in strongly unionised sectors can negotiate higher wages through collective bargaining.\n\n5. Discrimination: Wage differences may arise due to gender, age, or other forms of discrimination, though this is illegal under employment equality legislation."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4: Production, Costs & Competitiveness",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between the short-run and the long-run production periods.\n(ii) In the short-run firms may stay in the industry even if they are making a loss. Explain this statement. [15 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Explain the terms marginal revenue and marginal cost.\nThe table shows costs and revenue data of a firm:\nOutput: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nPrice: €20 each\nTotal Revenue: €20, €40, €60, €80, €100\nTotal Cost: €42, €60, €77, €97, €130\n\n(ii) Calculate the marginal revenue and marginal cost at each output level.\n(iii) Draw one graph showing the marginal revenue and marginal cost and identify the profit-maximising level of output for this firm. Explain your answer. [30 marks]\n\n(c)(i) Outline the factors that influence the competitiveness of firms in Ireland.\n(ii) Discuss three policies that the Irish government could consider to improve the competitiveness of firms in Ireland. [30 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Short-run: A production period in which at least one factor of production is fixed (cannot be changed). For example, a firm cannot quickly build a new factory or install new machinery. Only variable factors (e.g. labour, raw materials) can be adjusted.\n\nLong-run: A production period in which all factors of production are variable. The firm can change the scale of its operations - build new factories, install new technology, expand or contract its entire operation. There are no fixed factors.\n\n(ii) In the short run, a firm making a loss may continue to operate as long as its Average Revenue (AR) is greater than its Average Variable Cost (AVC). Even though it is not covering all its costs, it is covering its variable costs and making a contribution towards its fixed costs. If it shut down, it would still have to pay its fixed costs (rent, loan repayments, insurance) but would earn no revenue at all. The loss from shutting down would therefore be greater than the loss from continuing to operate. The firm will only shut down if AR falls below AVC.\n\n(b)(i) Marginal Revenue (MR): The additional revenue earned from selling one more unit of output. MR = Change in Total Revenue / Change in Output.\n\nMarginal Cost (MC): The additional cost incurred from producing one more unit of output. MC = Change in Total Cost / Change in Output.\n\n(ii) Calculations:\nOutput 1: MR = €20 (20-0), MC = N/A or €42 (from 0 output)\nOutput 2: MR = €20 (40-20), MC = €18 (60-42)\nOutput 3: MR = €20 (60-40), MC = €17 (77-60)\nOutput 4: MR = €20 (80-60), MC = €20 (97-77)\nOutput 5: MR = €20 (100-80), MC = €33 (130-97)\n\n(iii) Diagram: Plot MR as a horizontal line at €20 (since this is a perfectly competitive firm - price is constant). Plot MC as a curve that initially falls and then rises. The curves intersect at output = 4 units.\n\nThe profit-maximising level of output is 4 units, where MC = MR = €20. At this output, the firm is maximising profit because producing any more would mean MC > MR (the cost of producing the 5th unit is €33 but it only earns €20 revenue). At output levels 1-3, MR > MC, so the firm should increase production. At output 4, MC = MR, so this is the optimal output.\n\nProfit at output 4: TR - TC = €80 - €97 = -€17 (loss of €17, but this is the output that minimises the loss).\n\n(c)(i) Factors influencing competitiveness:\n- Wage costs and labour productivity\n- Cost of energy and raw materials\n- Exchange rate (strong euro makes exports more expensive)\n- Corporation tax rate (Ireland's competitive 12.5% rate)\n- Infrastructure quality (transport, broadband, utilities)\n- Education and skills of the workforce\n- Level of regulation and red tape\n- Innovation and technology adoption\n- Access to finance for investment\n\n(ii) Three government policies:\n\n1. Investment in infrastructure: Improving roads, broadband, port facilities, and public transport reduces costs for businesses and improves efficiency. Better infrastructure makes Ireland a more attractive location for FDI and helps domestic firms compete internationally.\n\n2. Investment in education and training: Ensuring the workforce has the skills needed by modern industries through quality education at all levels and targeted training programmes. A highly skilled workforce increases productivity and helps firms innovate.\n\n3. Maintain competitive corporation tax rate: Keeping Ireland's 12.5% corporation tax rate competitive relative to other countries helps attract and retain FDI, which creates employment and drives economic growth. The government could also provide targeted grants and supports for indigenous firms through Enterprise Ireland and the LEOs."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5: Full Employment & National Income",
     q:"(a)(i) Define the term full employment.\n(ii) Discuss the possible economic benefits and economic challenges of full employment in an economy. [25 marks]\n\n(b) Use the information in the table to answer the questions below:\nMPC = 0.8, MPM = 0.2\nCurrent equilibrium level of national income = €500m\nLevel of national income that would give full employment = €620m\n\n(i) Define the term marginal propensity to save (MPS).\n(ii) Calculate the MPS for this economy.\n(iii) Calculate the value of the multiplier in this open economy.\n(iv) How much will the government have to inject into this economy if it wants the economy to operate at its full employment level? Show your workings. [30 marks]\n\n(c) Explain how Ireland's Gross Domestic Product (GDP) could be affected by any two of the following:\n(i) An increase in Child Benefit payments.\n(ii) A foreign-owned company, operating in Ireland, sends back to their home country all the profits they have earned in Ireland.\n(iii) An oil spill off the Irish coast costs the Irish Government significant clean-up costs. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Full employment is a situation where everyone in the labour force who is willing and able to work at existing wage rates is employed. It does not mean zero unemployment, as there will always be some frictional unemployment (people between jobs) and structural unemployment (mismatch of skills). In practice, full employment is considered to exist when unemployment is between 4-5%.\n\n(ii) Economic benefits of full employment:\n- Higher national output/GDP: More people working means more goods and services produced.\n- Increased government revenue: More PAYE, USC, and PRSI contributions from workers, plus higher VAT receipts from increased consumer spending.\n- Reduced government expenditure: Less spending on social welfare payments.\n- Higher standard of living: More people earning income leads to greater consumer spending and improved living standards.\n- Reduced social problems: Lower crime rates, better mental health, stronger communities.\n\nEconomic challenges:\n- Demand-pull inflation: With full employment, increased consumer spending can push prices up as demand outpaces supply.\n- Labour shortages: Firms may struggle to find workers, driving up wages and costs.\n- Balance of payments pressure: Higher incomes lead to increased spending on imports.\n- Wage-push inflation: Workers in a strong bargaining position may demand higher wages, increasing costs for firms.\n\n(b)(i) Marginal Propensity to Save (MPS): The proportion of each additional euro of income that is saved rather than spent. It measures the fraction of any increase in income that households choose to save.\n\n(ii) MPS calculation:\nMPC + MPS + MPM = 1 (in a simple open economy without government)\nHowever, since MPC already includes domestic spending:\nMPS = 1 - MPC - MPM = 1 - 0.8 - 0.2 = 0\nOR if MPC represents domestic consumption only:\nMPS = 1 - MPC = 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\n(Note: The most likely intended answer is MPS = 0, since MPC + MPM = 1)\n\n(iii) Multiplier calculation:\nMultiplier (K) = 1 / (1 - MPC + MPM)\n= 1 / (1 - 0.8 + 0.2) = 1 / 0.4 = 2.5\nOR: K = 1 / (MPS + MPM) = 1 / (0 + 0.2) = 5\nOR: K = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / (1 - 0.8) = 5 (if MPM is separate)\nUsing the open economy formula: K = 1 / (MPS + MPM) = 1 / 0.4 = 2.5\n\n(iv) Injection required:\nDeflationary gap = €620m - €500m = €120m\nInjection needed = Gap / Multiplier = €120m / 2.5 = €48m\nThe government would need to inject €48m into the economy to achieve the full employment level of national income.\n\n(c) Effects on GDP:\n\n(i) Increase in Child Benefit payments: GDP would INCREASE. Child Benefit is a government transfer payment that increases households' disposable income. Parents spend this additional income on goods and services (food, clothing, childcare), which increases consumption (C) in the economy. Through the multiplier effect, the initial increase in spending leads to a larger overall increase in national income and GDP.\n\n(ii) Foreign company repatriates profits: GDP would NOT be directly affected. GDP measures the value of all goods and services produced within a country's borders, regardless of who owns the factors of production. The profits were already included in GDP when they were generated. However, GNP/GNI would decrease because GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad. Profit repatriation increases the outflow component of NFIA, reducing GNP.\n\n(iii) Oil spill clean-up costs: GDP would INCREASE. The clean-up operation involves economic activity - hiring workers, purchasing materials and equipment, contracting specialist services. All of this spending (government expenditure, G) adds to GDP. Paradoxically, a negative event like an oil spill can increase GDP because GDP measures economic activity, not welfare or quality of life. This highlights a limitation of GDP as a measure of economic wellbeing."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6: Exchange Rates & Balance of Payments",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term exchange rate.\n(ii) Outline the possible economic effects on the Irish economy of an appreciation in the value of the euro against the US dollar. [25 marks]\n\n(b) \"The Balance of Payments (BOP) Current Account had a surplus of €2.7 billion in the second quarter of 2015, equivalent to 5.2% of quarterly GDP.\" (Source: NERI, Autumn 2015)\n(i) Describe the main elements of Ireland's balance of payments (BOP) account.\n(ii) Explain what is meant by a surplus on the BOP current account.\n(iii) Can a surplus on the BOP current account pose problems for an economy? Explain your answer. [30 marks]\n\n(c) \"Irish SMEs are too dependent on the home market, Europe warns.\" (Source: Irish Independent, November 2015)\nOutline the benefits and challenges for Irish SMEs operating in foreign markets. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Exchange rate: The exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in terms of another currency. It is the rate at which one currency can be exchanged for another. For example, if €1 = $1.10, then one euro can purchase $1.10 worth of American goods and services.\n\n(ii) Effects of euro appreciation against the US dollar:\n- Irish exports to the US become more expensive: American buyers need more dollars to purchase euro-denominated goods, reducing demand for Irish exports and potentially leading to job losses in export sectors.\n- Imports from the US become cheaper: Irish consumers and firms can buy American goods and services for fewer euros, benefiting consumers through lower prices.\n- Tourism impact: Ireland becomes a more expensive destination for American tourists, potentially reducing tourist numbers and spending. However, it becomes cheaper for Irish people to travel to the US.\n- FDI implications: It becomes more expensive for US firms to invest in Ireland, potentially reducing FDI inflows.\n- Reduced inflation: Cheaper imports help keep prices down.\n\n(b)(i) Main elements of Ireland's BOP account:\n\nCurrent Account:\n- Trade in goods (visible trade): Exports and imports of physical goods.\n- Trade in services (invisible trade): Exports and imports of services (financial, IT, tourism etc.).\n- Primary income: Wages, investment income, and profits flowing in and out.\n- Secondary income/Current transfers: Government transfers, EU contributions, development aid.\n\nCapital Account: Capital transfers and acquisition/disposal of non-produced, non-financial assets.\n\nFinancial Account: Direct investment, portfolio investment, other investment, and reserve assets.\n\n(ii) A surplus on the BOP current account means that the total value of credits (inflows of money from exports, income from abroad, transfers received) exceeds the total value of debits (outflows of money for imports, income paid abroad, transfers paid). Ireland is earning more from its international transactions than it is spending.\n\n(iii) Problems with a persistent surplus:\n- Trading partner resentment: Countries with large deficits may accuse surplus countries of unfair trade practices and may impose protectionist measures in response.\n- Inflationary pressure: A surplus means net inflows of money into the economy, which can increase the money supply and drive up prices.\n- Currency appreciation: The surplus creates demand for the domestic currency, pushing its value up and making exports less competitive over time (self-correcting mechanism).\n- Resource allocation: A large surplus may indicate that domestic consumers and firms are not benefiting from imports that could improve their standard of living.\n\n(c) Benefits of SMEs operating in foreign markets:\n- Larger customer base: Access to millions of consumers beyond the Irish market of 4.7 million.\n- Reduced dependency on domestic economy: Diversification reduces risk if the Irish economy enters recession.\n- Economies of scale: Serving larger markets allows for greater output and lower average costs.\n- Innovation: Exposure to foreign competition stimulates innovation and product development.\n\nChallenges for Irish SMEs in foreign markets:\n- Exchange rate risk: Fluctuations in currency values can affect the profitability of exports.\n- Cultural and language barriers: Understanding foreign consumer preferences and business practices.\n- Higher costs: Marketing, distribution, legal compliance, and adapting products for foreign markets require significant investment.\n- Competition: Competing against established local firms and large multinationals in foreign markets.\n- Regulatory compliance: Meeting different product standards, labelling requirements, and legal regulations in each market."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7: Taxation, Inequality & Environment",
     q:"(a) In the case of any two of the following three pairs, distinguish between the two concepts:\n(i) Income and wealth\n(ii) Progressive taxes and regressive taxes\n(iii) Impact of taxation and incidence of taxation [20 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Outline two economic policies which the Irish Government could implement to help reduce inequality in Ireland.\n(ii) Discuss the possible impact these policies could have on economic growth. [30 marks]\n\n(c) Discuss economic measures which could be used to protect the environment. [25 marks]",
     a:"(a) Distinguishing between concepts (any two pairs):\n\n(i) Income vs Wealth:\n- Income is a flow concept - it is the money received over a period of time from employment (wages), investments (interest, dividends, rent), or government transfers (social welfare). It is measured over a time period (weekly, monthly, annually).\n- Wealth is a stock concept - it is the total value of assets owned by an individual or household at a particular point in time. It includes property, savings, investments, land, and other possessions, minus any liabilities (debts). A person can have high wealth but low income (e.g. retired property owner) or high income but low wealth (e.g. newly qualified professional with student debt).\n\n(ii) Progressive taxes vs Regressive taxes:\n- Progressive tax: Takes a higher percentage of income from higher-income earners. As income rises, the rate of tax increases. The tax burden falls more heavily on those with greater ability to pay. Example: Income tax with higher rates for higher earners (20% standard rate, 40% higher rate in Ireland).\n- Regressive tax: Takes a higher percentage of income from lower-income earners, even though the absolute amount paid may be less. Example: VAT at 23% applies equally to all consumers, but it represents a larger proportion of a low-income earner's total income compared to a high-income earner's.\n\n(iii) Impact of taxation vs Incidence of taxation:\n- Impact of taxation: This refers to where the tax initially falls - who is legally responsible for paying the tax. For example, the impact of VAT falls on the retailer who must collect and remit it to Revenue.\n- Incidence of taxation: This refers to who ultimately bears the burden of the tax - who actually ends up paying for it. The incidence of VAT falls on the consumer, as the retailer passes the tax on through higher prices. The incidence depends on the price elasticity of demand and supply.\n\n(b)(i) Two policies to reduce inequality:\n1. Progressive taxation: Increase the tax burden on higher earners through higher marginal tax rates, capital gains tax, or wealth taxes. Use the additional revenue to fund public services and social welfare payments that benefit lower-income groups.\n2. Investment in education and training: Provide equal access to quality education at all levels, including free or subsidised third-level education, grants for disadvantaged students, and targeted training programmes for unemployed workers.\n\n(ii) Impact on economic growth:\n- Higher taxes on high earners could act as a disincentive to work, invest, and take risks, potentially reducing enterprise and innovation. Some high earners may emigrate to lower-tax jurisdictions (brain drain), reducing the tax base and economic output.\n- However, reducing inequality can boost economic growth by increasing the purchasing power of lower-income groups who have a higher marginal propensity to consume. Greater equality also improves social cohesion, health outcomes, and educational attainment, all of which support long-term growth.\n- Investment in education increases human capital, improving labour productivity and the economy's long-term growth potential.\n\n(c) Economic measures to protect the environment:\n\n1. Carbon taxes / pollution taxes: Imposing taxes on activities that generate pollution (e.g. carbon tax on fossil fuels) internalises the external costs of pollution. This makes polluting activities more expensive, discouraging their use and encouraging cleaner alternatives. Revenue can fund environmental projects.\n\n2. Subsidies for renewable energy: Government subsidies for wind, solar, and other renewable energy sources make them more competitive relative to fossil fuels, encouraging investment in clean energy and reducing carbon emissions.\n\n3. Tradeable pollution permits (cap and trade): Setting a limit on total emissions and allowing firms to buy and sell emission permits creates a market incentive to reduce pollution. Firms that can reduce emissions cheaply will do so and sell their permits to firms for whom reduction is more costly.\n\n4. Regulation and legislation: Setting emission standards, banning harmful substances, requiring environmental impact assessments, and enforcing planning regulations protect the environment through legal requirements.\n\n5. Deposit-refund schemes: Charging a deposit on bottles and cans that is refunded when they are returned for recycling reduces litter and encourages recycling.\n\n6. Education and awareness campaigns: Informing consumers and businesses about environmental issues and encouraging sustainable behaviour through information campaigns."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8: Economic Performance, Debt Reduction & Urbanisation",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline four indicators of a country's economic performance.\n(ii) Discuss how one of these indicators could be used to assess Ireland's current economic performance. [25 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Explain why reducing the national debt is important for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Outline two policies the government could pursue to reduce the national debt. [25 marks]\n\n(c) Discuss the economic implications of Ireland's increasingly urbanised population. [25 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Four indicators of economic performance:\n1. GDP/GNP growth rate: Measures the change in the total value of goods and services produced, indicating whether the economy is expanding or contracting.\n2. Unemployment rate: The percentage of the labour force that is unemployed and actively seeking work.\n3. Inflation rate (CPI): Measures the rate of change in the general price level, indicating price stability.\n4. Balance of Payments: Records all economic transactions between Ireland and the rest of the world, indicating the country's international trading position.\n\n(ii) Using GDP growth rate to assess Ireland's performance:\nIreland's GDP growth can be compared year-on-year and against other EU countries. Strong GDP growth (above the EU average) indicates a healthy, expanding economy with increasing output, employment, and living standards. However, Ireland's GDP figures can be distorted by the activities of multinational corporations (transfer pricing, intellectual property relocation), as demonstrated by the 26.3% GDP increase recorded in 2015 (\"Leprechaun Economics\"). For this reason, GNI* (Modified Gross National Income) is a more accurate measure of Ireland's true economic performance.\n\n(b)(i) Importance of reducing the national debt:\n- Lower interest payments: A high national debt means significant annual interest payments, which divert government resources away from productive spending on public services, infrastructure, and social welfare.\n- Greater fiscal flexibility: Lower debt gives the government more room to borrow in future if needed (e.g. during a recession or crisis), without risking a debt crisis.\n- Lower taxes: Reduced debt servicing costs mean the government may not need to impose as high a level of taxation on citizens and businesses.\n- International credibility: A lower debt-to-GDP ratio improves Ireland's credit rating, reducing the cost of future government borrowing.\n- Intergenerational equity: Reducing the debt ensures that future generations are not burdened with repaying debts incurred by the current generation.\n\n(ii) Two policies to reduce the national debt:\n1. Achieve a budget surplus: By maintaining government revenue above government expenditure (through a combination of prudent spending and efficient tax collection), the surplus can be used to pay down the national debt.\n2. Promote economic growth: Growing the economy increases tax revenue (higher incomes, more employment, more spending) without necessarily raising tax rates, while also reducing the debt-to-GDP ratio as the denominator grows.\n\n(c) Economic implications of Ireland's urbanisation:\n\nPositive implications:\n- Economies of agglomeration: Concentration of people and firms in cities creates efficiencies through shared infrastructure, specialised labour pools, and knowledge spillovers between firms.\n- Better service provision: It is more cost-effective for the government to provide public services (healthcare, education, transport) to a concentrated urban population.\n- Economic growth: Cities are centres of innovation, entrepreneurship, and productivity growth.\n\nNegative implications:\n- Housing crisis: Increased demand for urban housing drives up prices and rents, making cities unaffordable for many workers, particularly in Dublin.\n- Congestion and infrastructure strain: Growing urban populations put pressure on transport, water, and energy infrastructure, leading to congestion costs and reduced quality of life.\n- Regional imbalance: As people migrate to cities, rural areas experience population decline, loss of services (schools, post offices, shops), and economic stagnation.\n- Environmental impact: Urban sprawl, increased car usage, and construction activity contribute to pollution and environmental degradation.\n- Inequality: Urban areas often have higher levels of income inequality, with pockets of affluence alongside areas of deprivation."}
  ],

  "2017": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Opportunity Cost (Public Sector Pay)",
     q:"The Irish government is under pressure to restore public sector pay to pre-financial crisis levels. Outline two opportunity costs for the Irish economy of this restoration. (16 marks)",
     a:"1. Less funds available to invest in capital infrastructure: Money spent on restoring public sector pay to pre-crisis levels cannot be used for investment in social housing, hospitals, schools, roads, or broadband infrastructure. These capital projects would generate long-term economic benefits and address critical infrastructure deficits.\n\n2. Less funds available to improve state services: The cost of pay restoration reduces the funds available to hire additional public servants such as teachers, gardai, nurses, and doctors. The health service, education system, and policing are all under pressure, and the pay restoration diverts resources that could be used to employ more front-line staff or reduce hospital waiting lists.\n\nOther opportunity costs include: less funds to increase social welfare payments (e.g. old age pensions), less funds to reduce taxation rates, and less funds to help repay the national debt."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: True or False (Economic Concepts)",
     q:"Indicate which of the following statements is TRUE or FALSE:\n(i) The labour force includes part-time workers.\n(ii) Quasi-rent is economic rent earned by a factor of production in the long run.\n(iii) The market value of an existing bond rises if the rate of interest falls.\n(iv) Capital Widening refers to the situation where the capital stock is increasing at the same rate as the labour force. (16 marks)",
     a:"(i) TRUE. The labour force includes all those who are employed (both full-time and part-time) and those who are unemployed but actively seeking work. Part-time workers are counted as employed and therefore are part of the labour force.\n\n(ii) FALSE. Quasi-rent is economic rent earned by a factor of production in the SHORT run, not the long run. It is the temporary surplus earned by a factor of production whose supply is fixed in the short run but variable in the long run. For example, a specialist machine that is in short supply in the short run earns quasi-rent, but in the long run more machines can be produced.\n\n(iii) TRUE. There is an inverse relationship between interest rates and bond prices. When interest rates fall, existing bonds with higher fixed coupon rates become more attractive to investors compared to newly issued bonds at lower rates. This increased demand pushes up the market price of existing bonds.\n\n(iv) TRUE. Capital widening occurs when the capital stock increases at the same rate as the labour force, so the capital-to-labour ratio remains unchanged. This contrasts with capital deepening, where the capital stock grows faster than the labour force, increasing the amount of capital per worker."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Direct & Indirect Taxation",
     q:"(a) Distinguish between Direct Taxation and Indirect Taxation and state one example of each.\n\n(b) Why are Indirect Taxes sometimes seen as inequitable? (16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Direct Taxation: These are taxes on all forms of income or wealth. They are paid directly by the taxpayer to the Government (via Revenue). The person or entity on whom the tax is levied is the person who actually bears the burden. Direct taxes cannot be passed on to someone else.\nExample: PAYE (income tax), Corporation Tax, Capital Gains Tax.\n\nIndirect Taxation: These are taxes on consumption/spending. They are taxes on the purchase of goods and services. They are collected by an intermediary (the retailer or producer) and paid to the Government. The tax can be passed on from the producer to the consumer through higher prices.\nExample: VAT (Value Added Tax), Excise Duties, Customs Duties.\n\n(b) Indirect taxes are sometimes seen as inequitable because they are regressive in nature. Those on lower incomes pay a higher proportion of their income on the tax compared to those on higher incomes. For example, a 23% VAT rate on goods applies equally to all consumers, but it represents a much larger percentage of a low-income earner's total income than of a high-income earner's income. This means the burden of indirect taxation falls disproportionately on the less well-off."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Limitations on Credit Creation",
     q:"State three limitations on the power of banks to create credit. (16 marks)",
     a:"1. Limited availability of suitable borrowers: Banks can only lend to creditworthy borrowers who meet lending criteria. If there are not enough suitable borrowers (e.g. during a recession when businesses are reluctant to invest), banks cannot create as much credit. Stricter lending regulations (such as Central Bank mortgage rules) also limit the pool of eligible borrowers.\n\n2. Limited ability to attract cash deposits: The credit creation process depends on banks receiving deposits. If customers withdraw their deposits or move them to other financial institutions, the bank has a smaller reserve base from which to create credit. Competition from other banks and financial institutions for deposits limits each bank's ability.\n\n3. Controls by the European Central Bank / Reserve ratios: The ECB sets reserve requirements that determine the minimum proportion of deposits banks must hold as reserves. Higher reserve ratios directly limit the amount of credit that can be created from a given deposit base. The ECB's monetary policy decisions (interest rates) also influence the demand for and supply of credit.\n\nOther limitations include: lack of demand for loans/credit by customers, and the ability of banks to raise capital on international markets."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Brexit & Sterling Depreciation",
     q:"After plans for 'Brexit' were revealed there was a 'flash crash' where the value of the Pound Sterling fell sharply against the Euro.\n\nState and explain two possible economic consequences of the above situation for the Irish Economy. (16 marks)",
     a:"1. Irish exports to the UK become more expensive: When sterling weakens against the euro, Irish goods priced in euros become relatively more expensive for UK consumers. This leads to decreased demand for Irish exports in the UK market. Sectors heavily dependent on UK trade, such as the agri-food sector, are particularly affected. Falling employment in firms exposed to the UK market (e.g. indigenous food and tourism companies) is a likely consequence.\n\n2. Imports from the UK become relatively cheaper: A weaker pound means that goods produced in the UK cost fewer euros for Irish consumers and businesses. This could lead to increased demand for UK imports, displacing some Irish-produced goods. While cheaper imports benefit consumers, they can harm Irish producers who face increased competition from lower-priced UK products.\n\nOther consequences include: fewer UK tourists visiting Ireland (as Ireland becomes more expensive to visit), reduced FDI from UK firms (as it is more expensive for them to invest in the eurozone), uncertainty leading to lower investment, and a potential worsening of Ireland's balance of trade if exports to the UK fall and imports from the UK rise."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Monopoly Long-Run Equilibrium",
     q:"The diagram shows a firm operating under conditions of Monopoly.\n\n(a) Label the lines/curves in the diagram.\n(b) Use the diagram to explain the long-run equilibrium position for a firm in Monopoly. (17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Lines/curves in the monopoly diagram:\n- MC: Marginal Cost curve (upward sloping)\n- AC: Average Cost curve (U-shaped)\n- AR: Average Revenue / Demand curve (downward sloping)\n- MR: Marginal Revenue curve (downward sloping, below AR, steeper gradient)\n\n(b) Long-run equilibrium explanation:\n\n1. Equilibrium output: The monopolist produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising rule). This determines the equilibrium quantity (Q1).\n\n2. Equilibrium price: The monopolist charges the price consumers are willing to pay for Q1 units, read from the AR (demand) curve. This gives the equilibrium price (P1), which is above the MC = MR point.\n\n3. Costs: The average cost of producing Q1 units is shown at C1 on the AC curve.\n\n4. Supernormal profits: Since AR (P1) is greater than AC (C1), the monopolist earns supernormal profits. The total supernormal profit is the area (P1 - C1) x Q1 (the shaded rectangle between P1 and C1).\n\n5. These supernormal profits persist in the long run because barriers to entry (patents, economies of scale, legal barriers, control of resources) prevent new firms from entering the industry and competing away the profits.\n\n6. The monopolist is not producing at the minimum point of the AC curve, which means it is not achieving productive efficiency. Output is lower and price is higher than would occur under perfect competition."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Cross Price Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"(a) A consumer buys 80 units of Good A when the price of Good B is €100. When the price of Good B falls to €90 (the price of Good A remaining unchanged), the consumer buys 120 units of Good A.\n\nUsing an appropriate formula, calculate this consumer's cross price elasticity of demand for Good A. Show your workings.\n\n(b) Is Good A a substitute or a complement to Good B? Explain your answer. (17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Cross Price Elasticity of Demand (CED) calculation:\n\nUsing the mid-point (arc) formula:\nCED = (Change in Qd of A / Change in P of B) x (P1 + P2) / (Qd1 + Qd2)\n\nChange in Qd of A = 120 - 80 = +40\nChange in P of B = 90 - 100 = -10\n\nCED = (40 / -10) x (100 + 90) / (80 + 120)\n= -4 x 190 / 200\n= -4 x 0.95\n= -3.8\n\nThe cross price elasticity of demand is -3.8.\n\n(b) Good A is a COMPLEMENT to Good B.\n\nExplanation: The CED has a negative value (-3.8). This means that as the price of Good B falls (from €100 to €90), the demand for Good A rises (from 80 to 120 units). Price and demand move in opposite directions. This is characteristic of complementary goods - goods that are used together. When the price of one complement falls, demand for both goods increases. For example, if Good B were a games console and Good A were games, a fall in the price of the console would increase demand for games.\n\n(If the CED were positive, the goods would be substitutes, as a fall in the price of B would cause demand for A to fall as consumers switched to the cheaper B.)"},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Conflicting Government Objectives",
     q:"Explain how the following Government policy objectives may conflict with each other:\n(i) Economic growth versus Balance of Payments equilibrium\n(ii) Economic growth versus a Just Social Policy (17 marks)",
     a:"(i) Economic growth vs Balance of Payments equilibrium:\nEconomic growth means higher employment, rising incomes, and more money circulating in the economy. However, as Ireland has a high marginal propensity to import (MPM), this economic growth automatically causes an increase in imports as consumers spend their additional income on foreign goods and services. If imports rise faster than exports, the Balance of Payments current account will deteriorate, potentially moving from surplus to deficit. The government faces a conflict: stimulating economic growth through increased spending and investment may worsen the balance of payments position.\n\n(ii) Economic growth vs a Just Social Policy:\nEconomic growth tends to benefit the incomes of higher earners more than lower earners, as those with capital, property, and high-paying jobs gain disproportionately. The benefits of economic growth may not be equitably distributed across the economy, leading to greater inequality.\n\nTo achieve a just social policy, the government may need to impose higher taxes on high-income earners and redistribute this income to those on social welfare and lower incomes. However, high taxes could act as a disincentive for entrepreneurs and high-skilled workers, potentially driving them out of the economy (brain drain) and seriously impeding economic growth. The government must balance the desire for growth with the need for equitable distribution of its benefits."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Tuition Fees for Third-Level Education",
     q:"The Irish government is considering the reintroduction of tuition fees for third-level education.\n\nOutline one possible economic advantage and one possible economic disadvantage of this for the economy. (17 marks)",
     a:"Economic advantage - Reduced cost for taxpayers / Opportunity cost:\nThe provision of free higher education puts significant strain on government finances. Reintroducing tuition fees would reduce the cost borne by taxpayers and free up government funds that could be better utilised in other areas such as healthcare, social welfare, vocational training, or investment in research. Furthermore, graduates from higher education institutions (HEIs) often enter professions with higher lifetime earnings. As the greatest beneficiaries of HEI education, it could be argued that it is fair for students to contribute towards the cost of their education.\n\nEconomic disadvantage - Increased inequality / Reduced positive externalities:\nTuition fees may act as a disincentive or financial barrier for those from disadvantaged backgrounds from pursuing third-level education. This would reduce social mobility and could lead to increased inequality, as access to high-paying professions would be restricted to those from wealthier families who can afford fees.\n\nAdditionally, third-level education generates significant positive externalities - benefits that extend beyond the individual student to the wider society. These include innovation, research, a skilled workforce that attracts FDI, and cultural contributions. By reducing the number of people willing or able to undertake third-level education, fees may also reduce these positive externalities, potentially discouraging the location of multinational companies that depend on a highly skilled workforce."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1: Demand & Supply Concepts, Electric Cars & Rent Control",
     q:"(a) In the case of any two of the following, distinguish between the concepts:\n(i) The Law of Demand and the Law of Supply\n(ii) Consumer Surplus and Producer Surplus\n(iii) Derived Demand and Joint Demand [25 marks]\n\n(b) Assume the market for electric cars is in equilibrium. Describe with the aid of a separate diagram in each case the effects each of the following market situations is likely to have on the equilibrium position for electric cars:\n(i) A technological advance in the production process of electric cars.\n(ii) The motor tax on petrol engine cars is expected to rise in the near future.\n(iii) The government increases subsidies on public transport, reducing prices for its commuters. [30 marks]\n\n(c) The average monthly rental price for private residential property in Ireland is €1,078. (Source: Daft.ie 2016)\n(i) Assume the average monthly equilibrium rental price is €1,078. On one diagram, illustrate the effect on the market if the Irish Government introduced a rent control that sets the maximum rent at €715.\n(ii) Discuss the likely economic consequences of such a rent control (i.e. price ceiling on rental costs) on the Irish rental property market. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a) Distinguishing between concepts (any two):\n\n(i) Law of Demand: As prices rise, the quantity demanded will fall and vice versa, ceteris paribus. There is an inverse (negative) relationship between the price of a good and the quantity demanded. Illustrated by a downward-sloping demand curve from left to right.\n\nLaw of Supply: As prices rise, the quantity supplied will rise and vice versa, ceteris paribus. There is a positive (direct) relationship between the price of a good and the quantity supplied. Illustrated by an upward-sloping supply curve from left to right.\n\n(ii) Consumer Surplus: The difference between what a consumer actually pays for a good and the maximum they would have been willing to pay rather than go without it. It represents the extra utility gained from a good in excess of the amount paid for it.\n\nProducer Surplus: The difference between what a producer receives for a good and the minimum they would have been willing to accept for the good. It represents the extra earnings obtained by the producer above the minimum required for them to supply the good.\n\n(iii) Derived Demand: Where a factor of production is not demanded for its own sake but rather for its contribution to the production process. The demand for the factor is derived from the demand for the final product. For example, demand for bricklayers is derived from demand for houses.\n\nJoint Demand: Where two or more goods are used in conjunction with each other to achieve utility. They are complementary goods. For example, golf clubs and golf balls, or printers and ink cartridges.\n\n(b) Effects on the electric car market equilibrium:\n\n(i) Technological advance:\nDiagram: Supply shifts RIGHT from S1 to S2. Demand curve stays the same.\nThe technological advance reduces production costs and increases efficiency, leading to an increase in supply. At the original price, there is now a surplus, which pushes the price down. New equilibrium: lower price (P1 to P2), higher quantity (Q1 to Q2). Consumers benefit from lower prices and more electric cars are available.\n\n(ii) Motor tax on petrol cars expected to rise:\nDiagram: Demand for electric cars shifts RIGHT from D1 to D2. Supply curve stays the same.\nIf motor tax on petrol cars is expected to rise, petrol cars become more expensive to own. Electric cars are a substitute for petrol cars, so demand for electric cars increases. New equilibrium: higher price (P1 to P2), higher quantity (Q1 to Q2).\n\n(iii) Government increases public transport subsidies:\nDiagram: Demand for electric cars shifts LEFT from D1 to D2. Supply curve stays the same.\nCheaper public transport is a substitute for private car usage. If public transport becomes cheaper, some consumers switch from buying electric cars to using public transport. Demand for electric cars falls. New equilibrium: lower price (P1 to P2), lower quantity (Q1 to Q2).\n\n(c)(i) Diagram: Standard supply and demand diagram with equilibrium at €1,078 (where S and D intersect). Draw a horizontal price ceiling line at €715, below the equilibrium price. At €715, the quantity demanded (Qd) exceeds the quantity supplied (Qs), creating excess demand (shortage). Label the gap between Qs and Qd as 'Excess Demand'.\n\n(ii) Economic consequences of rent control at €715:\n- Excess demand / housing shortage: At the lower controlled rent, more people want to rent (higher quantity demanded) but fewer landlords are willing to supply rental properties (lower quantity supplied). This creates a shortage of rental accommodation.\n- Reduced quality: Landlords receiving lower rents have less incentive and fewer resources to maintain and improve properties, leading to deterioration in the quality of rental housing.\n- Black market: Some landlords may charge illegal payments above the controlled rent, creating an underground market in rental property.\n- Reduced supply in the long run: Fewer investors will build or convert properties for the rental market if returns are limited by rent controls. Some existing landlords may sell their properties rather than rent them at below-market rates.\n- Benefit to existing tenants: Those who already have rental accommodation benefit from lower rents, increasing their disposable income.\n- Reduced labour mobility: Workers may be reluctant to move to areas with housing shortages, reducing the geographical mobility of labour."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2: Perfect Competition & Artisan Food Market",
     q:"(a)(i) Show, by means of two separate labelled diagrams, the short run and long run equilibrium positions of a profit-maximising firm in perfect competition.\n(ii) Compare the short run equilibrium position with the long run equilibrium position under the headings: Price and Output, Profits, Efficiency. [35 marks]\n\n(b) The table shows data for a perfectly competitive firm in equilibrium:\nAverage Revenue: €50, Average Variable Cost: €40, Average Total Cost: €60\n(i) Using your knowledge of perfect competition, what is the marginal revenue earned by this firm? Explain your answer.\n(ii) Should this firm continue in business? Explain your answer. [20 marks]\n\n(c) Bord Bia reported the total value of the Artisan Food market to be growing strongly, leading to more firms entering the industry. (Bord Bia defines Artisan Food as \"high-quality, distinctive and produced in small quantities\")\n(i) State a market structure which most closely reflects this situation, giving reasons for your answer.\n(ii) Explain the shape of the demand curve of a firm in this market structure. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Short-run diagram:\nU-shaped AC and MC curves. Horizontal demand curve (D = AR = MR) above the minimum point of AC. MC intersects MR at the profit-maximising output (Q1). The area between AR and AC at Q1 represents supernormal profit.\n\nLong-run diagram:\nU-shaped AC and MC curves. Horizontal demand curve (D = AR = MR) is tangent to the minimum point of AC. MC intersects MR = AR = AC all at the same point. Only normal profit is earned.\n\n(ii) Comparison:\n\nPrice and Output:\n- Short run: Price may be above AC (supernormal profit) or below AC (loss). Output is where MC = MR.\n- Long run: Price equals the minimum of AC. Output is at the most efficient level where MC = MR = AR = AC (minimum).\n\nProfits:\n- Short run: The firm may earn supernormal profit (AR > AC), normal profit (AR = AC), or make a loss (AR < AC but AR > AVC).\n- Long run: Only normal profit is earned. If supernormal profits existed, new firms would enter (no barriers to entry), increasing supply and driving price down until only normal profit remains.\n\nEfficiency:\n- Short run: The firm may not be producing at the minimum point of AC, so it may not be productively efficient.\n- Long run: The firm produces at the minimum point of AC (productively efficient) and where P = MC (allocatively efficient). This is the most efficient outcome.\n\n(b)(i) Marginal Revenue = €50.\nIn perfect competition, the firm is a price-taker. The price is set by the industry and each firm must accept it. AR = MR = P in perfect competition. Since AR is €50, MR must also be €50. The demand curve facing the individual firm is perfectly elastic (horizontal) at the market price.\n\n(ii) Yes, the firm SHOULD continue in business (in the short run).\nAlthough the firm is making a loss (AR of €50 < ATC of €60), its AR (€50) is greater than its AVC (€40). This means the firm is covering all its variable costs and making a contribution of €10 per unit towards its fixed costs. If it shut down, it would still have to pay its fixed costs but would earn no revenue, so the loss from shutting down (€20 per unit in fixed costs) would be greater than the loss from continuing to operate (€10 per unit). The firm should continue in the short run while waiting for conditions to improve.\n\n(c)(i) Market structure: Monopolistic Competition.\nReasons:\n- Many firms: Bord Bia notes more firms are entering the market, suggesting low barriers to entry.\n- Differentiated products: Artisan food is described as \"high-quality, distinctive\" - each producer makes a unique product (different cheeses, breads, etc.).\n- Small-scale production: Products are \"produced in small quantities,\" consistent with the many small firms characteristic of monopolistic competition.\n\n(ii) Shape of the demand curve:\nThe demand curve facing a firm in monopolistic competition is downward-sloping but relatively elastic. It slopes downward because the firm's product is differentiated - it has some control over its price because its product is not identical to competitors' products. However, it is relatively elastic because there are many close substitutes available from other firms in the market. If the firm raises its price significantly, consumers can easily switch to a competitor's similar (but differentiated) product. The firm has limited price-making power."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3: Costs, MEC & MRP of Labour",
     q:"(a) The table shows the output and total cost of a firm producing wireless earphones. The firm charges €13 per unit of output.\nOutput (units): 0, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000, 6000\nTotal Cost (€): 5000, 13000, 18000, 24000, 32000, 45000, 60000\n\n(i) Calculate the fixed cost and the variable cost when output is 3,000 units.\n(ii) Calculate the average variable cost when output is 5,000 units.\n(iii) Calculate total profit if 4,000 units are sold.\n(iv) Using the data, draw one graph showing the average cost and the marginal cost of the firm. [30 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Explain the term Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC).\n(ii) Outline two possible reasons for a fall in MEC. [20 marks]\n\n(c)(i) Explain with the aid of a diagram the Marginal Revenue Product of Labour (MRPL).\n(ii) Discuss the reasons why MRPL might not be a suitable method for setting wages in the public sector. [25 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Fixed Cost and Variable Cost at 3,000 units:\nFixed Cost (FC) = Total Cost at zero output = €5,000\n(Fixed costs exist even when nothing is produced.)\n\nVariable Cost (VC) = Total Cost - Fixed Cost\n= €24,000 - €5,000 = €19,000\n\n(ii) Average Variable Cost at 5,000 units:\nVariable Cost at 5,000 units = TC - FC = €45,000 - €5,000 = €40,000\nAVC = VC / Output = €40,000 / 5,000 = €8.00 per unit\n\n(iii) Total Profit at 4,000 units:\nTotal Revenue = Price x Output = €13 x 4,000 = €52,000\nTotal Cost = €32,000\nTotal Profit = TR - TC = €52,000 - €32,000 = €20,000\n\n(iv) Graph data calculations:\nAC: Output 1000: €13, 2000: €9, 3000: €8, 4000: €8, 5000: €9, 6000: €10\nMC: 0-1000: €8, 1000-2000: €5, 2000-3000: €6, 3000-4000: €8, 4000-5000: €13, 5000-6000: €15\n\nThe graph shows a U-shaped AC curve (falling from €13 to minimum of €8, then rising) and a U-shaped MC curve (falling from €8 to €5, then rising to €15). MC intersects AC at its minimum point (around 4,000 units where both equal €8).\n\n(b)(i) Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC): The expected rate of return (or anticipated profit) on the last unit of capital invested. It represents the additional revenue a firm expects to earn from investing in one more unit of capital (e.g. a new machine), expressed as a percentage. Investment will occur as long as the MEC exceeds the rate of interest (cost of borrowing).\n\n(ii) Two reasons for a fall in MEC:\n1. Increased supply of capital goods: As more firms invest and the stock of capital increases, the returns from additional capital diminish (law of diminishing marginal returns). Each additional machine adds less to output and profit than the previous one.\n2. Fall in demand for the firm's output: If consumer demand for the goods produced using capital decreases, the expected revenue from new capital investment falls, reducing the MEC. Economic uncertainty or recession reduces firms' expectations of future profits.\n\n(c)(i) MRPL explanation:\nThe Marginal Revenue Product of Labour is the additional revenue a firm earns from employing one more worker. It is calculated as: MRPL = MPP (Marginal Physical Product) x MR (Marginal Revenue).\n\nDiagram: Y-axis shows wage rate / MRPL (€), X-axis shows number of workers. The MRPL curve slopes downward from left to right (due to diminishing marginal returns). The wage rate is a horizontal line. The firm hires workers up to the point where MRPL = Wage Rate. Beyond this point, hiring additional workers would cost more than the revenue they generate.\n\nA profit-maximising firm will employ workers until the cost of the last worker (wage) equals the revenue generated by the last worker (MRPL).\n\n(ii) Why MRPL may not be suitable for setting public sector wages:\n1. Difficulty measuring output: Many public sector services (healthcare, education, policing, defence) do not have easily measurable outputs or revenues. How do you measure the 'output' of a teacher, nurse, or garda in monetary terms?\n2. No market price for output: Public services are often provided free at the point of use, so there is no market revenue to calculate MRP. The 'revenue' generated by a nurse treating patients is not directly measurable.\n3. Social objectives over profit: The public sector aims to provide essential services for social benefit, not to maximise profit. Using MRPL would undervalue services that generate significant social benefits but little direct revenue.\n4. Equity considerations: Public sector wages should reflect fairness and comparability across roles, not just individual productivity. MRPL could lead to highly inequitable wage distributions within the public service.\n5. Political and union considerations: Public sector wages are often determined through national pay agreements and collective bargaining rather than individual productivity measures."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4: National Income & Circular Flow",
     q:"(a)(i) One method for calculating National Income is the output method. Outline two other methods used to calculate National Income.\n(ii) Describe the problem of 'double counting' when compiling National Income statistics. [20 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with the aid of a diagram, the Circular Flow of Income in an open economy.\n(ii) Outline how each of the following Budget 2017 policies should affect the circular flow of income:\n- Capital spending on road and rail projects to increase by €4.5 billion.\n- A reduction in the amount of the universal social charge (USC) that workers must pay. [35 marks]\n\n(c) The CSO recorded an increase in GDP of 26.3% for Ireland in 2015. Experts agreed that this figure, though calculated using approved methods, overestimated the true GDP increase.\n(i) Describe three uses of a country's National Income statistics.\n(ii) Outline an economic implication for the Irish Government of using an overestimated GDP figure. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Two other methods for calculating National Income:\n\n1. Income method: Measures National Income by adding up all incomes earned by factors of production in the economy - wages (labour), rent (land), interest (capital), and profit (enterprise). Only factor incomes are included; transfer payments (e.g. social welfare) are excluded to avoid double counting.\n\n2. Expenditure method: Measures National Income by adding up all spending in the economy - Consumer spending (C) + Investment spending (I) + Government spending (G) + Exports (X) - Imports (M). This is based on the principle that all output produced must be purchased by someone.\n\n(ii) Double counting: This occurs when the value of a good is counted more than once when compiling National Income statistics. For example, if flour is sold by a mill to a bakery for €2, and the bakery sells the bread for €5, counting both transactions would give €7, when the true value added to the economy is only €5 (the final price). To avoid this, only the value added at each stage of production should be counted, or only the value of final goods and services (not intermediate goods) should be included.\n\n(b)(i) Circular Flow diagram for an open economy should show:\n- Two main sectors: Households (supply factors of production) and Firms (produce goods and services).\n- Inner flow: Factors of production flow from households to firms; goods and services flow from firms to households.\n- Outer flow: Income (wages, rent, interest, profit) flows from firms to households; expenditure (consumer spending) flows from households to firms.\n- Injections (flows INTO the circular flow): Investment (I), Government Spending (G), Exports (X).\n- Leakages/Withdrawals (flows OUT of the circular flow): Savings (S), Taxation (T), Imports (M).\n- When injections > leakages: the economy expands. When leakages > injections: the economy contracts.\n\n(ii) Budget 2017 policies and the circular flow:\n\n- Capital spending on road and rail projects (€4.5 billion increase): This is an INJECTION (government spending, G) into the circular flow. It increases the size of the circular flow as the government spends money on construction, employing workers and purchasing materials. Through the multiplier effect, this initial injection generates further rounds of spending, increasing national income by more than the initial €4.5 billion.\n\n- Reduction in USC: This reduces the amount of tax withdrawn from workers' incomes, effectively reducing leakages (T) from the circular flow. Workers retain more of their gross income (increased disposable income), which increases consumer spending (C). This expansionary effect increases the size of the circular flow and boosts economic activity.\n\n(c)(i) Three uses of National Income statistics:\n\n1. Measuring economic growth: Comparing GDP/GNP figures year-on-year shows whether the economy is expanding or contracting, helping the government plan fiscal and monetary policy responses.\n\n2. International comparisons: GDP per capita allows comparison of living standards between countries. Ireland can benchmark its performance against other EU member states and OECD countries.\n\n3. Government planning and budgeting: National Income data helps the government forecast tax revenues, plan public spending, and set targets for economic policy. Key ratios like debt-to-GDP and deficit-to-GDP are used to assess fiscal sustainability.\n\n(ii) Economic implication of overestimated GDP:\nThe 26.3% GDP increase in 2015 (\"Leprechaun Economics\") was driven by multinational corporations relocating intellectual property assets to Ireland for tax purposes, not by genuine domestic economic growth. If the government uses this inflated GDP figure:\n\n- Debt-to-GDP ratio appears artificially low, giving a misleading impression of Ireland's fiscal health and potentially leading to complacency about debt reduction.\n- EU fiscal rules are based on GDP figures. An inflated GDP could lead to Ireland being allowed (or required) to pursue less prudent fiscal policies than its true economic position warrants.\n- Policy decisions based on inaccurate data may be inappropriate - for example, the government might increase spending or reduce taxes based on inflated growth figures, when the true domestic economy does not support such measures."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5: International Trade, Comparative Advantage & Terms of Trade",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between the concepts Absolute Advantage and Comparative Advantage in relation to international trade, using an example to support your answer.\n(ii) State and explain reasons why one country might be better than another country in the production of a particular good. [30 marks]\n\n(b) In his 1958 study \"Economic Development\", T.K. Whitaker urged the Irish Government to stop sheltering 'behind a protectionist blockade' and to accept instead the challenge of free trade.\n(i) Describe two trade protectionist measures.\n(ii) Discuss the economic arguments for and against a government introducing trade protectionist measures to protect 'infant industries'. [25 marks]\n\n(c) The table shows Ireland's terms of trade for selected years (Base Year = 2010):\nExport Price Index 2011: 102, 2015: 85\nImport Price Index 2011: 107, 2015: 87\n\n(i) Explain the phrase terms of trade.\n(ii) Calculate the terms of trade for 2011.\n(iii) Outline the possible effects on the Irish economy of the movement shown in the Terms of Trade for 2015, relative to the base year 2010. [20 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Absolute Advantage: A country has an absolute advantage in the production of a good if it can produce that good using fewer resources (or more efficiently) than another country. For example, if Ireland can produce 100 units of dairy per worker while France produces 80 units, Ireland has an absolute advantage in dairy.\n\nComparative Advantage: A country has a comparative advantage in the production of a good if it can produce that good at a lower opportunity cost than another country. Even if one country has an absolute advantage in producing all goods, both countries can still benefit from trade if they specialise in the good where they have the lowest opportunity cost.\n\nExample: Suppose Ireland can produce 10 units of software or 5 units of wine, while France can produce 8 units of software or 12 units of wine. Ireland's opportunity cost of 1 unit of software is 0.5 wine; France's is 1.5 wine. Ireland has a comparative advantage in software. France's opportunity cost of 1 wine is 0.67 software; Ireland's is 2 software. France has a comparative advantage in wine. Both benefit if Ireland specialises in software and France in wine, and they trade.\n\n(ii) Reasons why one country may be better at producing a particular good:\n- Natural resources: A country with fertile soil and a mild climate has an advantage in agriculture. Countries with oil reserves have an advantage in petroleum.\n- Skilled labour force: Investment in education and training creates a workforce with skills suited to particular industries (e.g. Ireland's strength in technology and pharmaceuticals).\n- Technology and capital: Access to advanced technology and machinery increases productivity.\n- Climate and geography: These affect agricultural output, tourism potential, and transport costs.\n- Government policy: Tax incentives, grants, and regulatory frameworks can create advantages (e.g. Ireland's 12.5% corporation tax for attracting FDI).\n- Tradition and expertise: Years of specialisation build up expertise and reputation (e.g. Swiss watchmaking, German engineering).\n\n(b)(i) Two trade protectionist measures:\n1. Tariffs: A tax imposed on imported goods that increases their price, making them more expensive relative to domestically produced goods. This protects domestic producers from cheaper foreign competition.\n2. Quotas: A physical limit on the quantity of a particular good that can be imported into a country during a specified period. This restricts the supply of foreign goods available in the domestic market.\n\n(ii) Arguments FOR protecting infant industries:\n- New industries need time to grow and achieve economies of scale before they can compete with established foreign competitors. Without temporary protection, they may never survive long enough to become competitive.\n- Employment: Protecting infant industries preserves and creates jobs in the domestic economy.\n- Diversification: Protecting new industries helps diversify the economy away from dependence on a narrow range of exports or industries.\n- Strategic importance: Some industries may be strategically important for national security or future economic development.\n\nArguments AGAINST protecting infant industries:\n- Higher prices for consumers: Tariffs and quotas raise the price of goods, reducing consumer choice and purchasing power.\n- Inefficiency: Protected industries may become complacent without competitive pressure, remaining inefficient and never achieving the competitiveness intended.\n- Retaliation: Trading partners may impose their own protectionist measures against the country's exports, reducing export markets.\n- Difficulty of removal: Once protection is in place, it becomes politically difficult to remove, as industries and workers become dependent on it.\n- Misallocation of resources: Resources may be directed to protected industries rather than to sectors where the country has a genuine comparative advantage.\n\n(c)(i) Terms of Trade: The ratio of a country's export prices to its import prices, usually expressed as an index number. It measures the purchasing power of a country's exports in terms of imports - how many units of imports can be purchased per unit of exports.\n\nFormula: Terms of Trade = (Export Price Index / Import Price Index) x 100\n\n(ii) Terms of Trade for 2011:\n= (102 / 107) x 100 = 95.3\n\nThis means Ireland's terms of trade deteriorated compared to the base year of 2010 (100). Ireland's export prices rose less than its import prices, meaning Ireland had to export more to pay for the same volume of imports.\n\n(iii) Effects of the 2015 Terms of Trade movement:\nTerms of Trade for 2015 = (85 / 87) x 100 = 97.7\nCompared to the base year (100), Ireland's terms of trade deteriorated slightly. Export prices fell proportionally more than import prices.\n\nEffects:\n- Irish goods are cheaper on world markets, which could boost export volumes and improve the balance of trade if demand for exports is price elastic.\n- However, Ireland must export a greater volume of goods to finance the same volume of imports, as each unit of exports buys fewer imports.\n- The cost of imported raw materials and consumer goods remains relatively high compared to export earnings, which could squeeze profit margins for Irish firms.\n- Overall, the deterioration is modest (97.7 vs 100), suggesting a limited negative impact."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6: CPI, Inflation & Residential Property Prices",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain briefly how the Consumer Price Index (CPI) is calculated.\n(ii) Discuss three possible limitations of the CPI as an accurate measure of changes in the cost of living in Ireland. [20 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Define the terms demand-pull inflation and cost-push inflation.\n(ii) The Irish inflation rate was at, or close to, 0% between 2013 and 2016. Is such a low rate of inflation good or bad for (a) households and (b) firms? Explain your answer in each case.\n(iii) Outline how the European Central Bank's (ECB) monetary policy changes might affect the rate of inflation. [30 marks]\n\n(c) \"From the trough in early 2013, residential property prices nationally have increased by 49.1%.\" (Source: CSO, December 2016)\n(i) State and explain three factors that caused the price of residential property to increase considerably since 2013.\n(ii) Discuss two possible economic consequences of this increase in property prices. [25 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) How the CPI is calculated:\nThe CSO conducts a Household Budget Survey to determine the typical spending patterns of Irish households. A representative 'basket' of goods and services is selected, with each item given a 'weight' based on its importance in average household spending. Prices of these items are collected regularly from a range of outlets nationwide. The current prices are compared to the base year prices to calculate the price index. The CPI measures the percentage change in the cost of the weighted basket over time.\n\n(ii) Three limitations of the CPI:\n1. The basket may not represent all households: The CPI is based on average spending patterns, but individual households may have very different spending patterns. For example, pensioners spend more on heating and healthcare, while young families spend more on childcare and mortgages.\n2. Quality changes: The CPI may not fully account for improvements in the quality of goods and services over time. If a product's price rises but its quality has also improved significantly, the real price increase may be overstated.\n3. New products: The basket is updated periodically but may lag behind the introduction of new products and services (e.g. streaming services, electric vehicles). Consumer spending patterns change faster than the basket is revised.\n\n(b)(i) Demand-pull inflation: An increase in the general price level caused by excess aggregate demand in the economy. When total demand for goods and services exceeds total supply (at full employment), prices are bid upward. Caused by increases in consumption, investment, government spending, or exports.\n\nCost-push inflation: An increase in the general price level caused by increases in the costs of production for firms. Higher costs (wages, raw materials, energy, taxes) are passed on to consumers through higher prices. Caused by factors such as rising oil prices, wage increases, or increased indirect taxation.\n\n(ii) Low inflation (close to 0%) for households:\n- Good: Purchasing power is maintained - wages can buy the same amount of goods and services over time. Those on fixed incomes (pensioners) are particularly protected. Savings retain their real value.\n- Bad: If inflation is too low or becomes deflation, consumers may delay purchases expecting prices to fall further, reducing demand and potentially causing recession. Asset values (particularly property) may stagnate or fall.\n\nLow inflation for firms:\n- Good: Costs of production remain stable, allowing better planning and budgeting. Wage demands from workers are lower when inflation is low, keeping costs down. Improved international competitiveness as Irish prices rise slowly relative to competitors.\n- Bad: Firms may struggle to raise prices to increase revenue. If deflation occurs, the real value of business debts increases. Low inflation may signal weak demand in the economy, reducing incentive to invest.\n\n(iii) ECB monetary policy and inflation:\n- Interest rates: If the ECB raises interest rates, borrowing becomes more expensive, reducing consumer spending and business investment. This decreases aggregate demand and puts downward pressure on inflation. Conversely, lowering rates stimulates spending and investment, increasing demand and potentially raising inflation.\n- Quantitative easing (QE): The ECB can purchase government bonds and other assets, injecting money into the economy. This increases the money supply, reduces long-term interest rates, and stimulates spending, which can push inflation upward.\n- Reserve requirements: The ECB can adjust the proportion of deposits banks must hold in reserve. Higher reserves mean less money available for lending, which reduces credit creation, spending, and inflation.\n\n(c)(i) Three factors causing property price increases since 2013:\n1. Increased demand: Economic recovery led to higher employment, rising incomes, and increased confidence. More people can afford to buy property, and returning immigrants added to demand, particularly in Dublin.\n2. Restricted supply: The housing crash led to a collapse in construction activity. Building new homes was slow to restart due to high construction costs, planning delays, and difficulty securing development finance. Supply has not kept pace with demand.\n3. Low interest rates: The ECB maintained historically low interest rates (near 0%), making mortgages cheaper and more affordable. This increased the number of people who could afford to borrow for a house purchase, driving up demand and prices.\n\n(ii) Two economic consequences of rising property prices:\n1. Reduced affordability / increased inequality: Rising property prices make it increasingly difficult for first-time buyers and lower-income households to get on the property ladder. This widens the wealth gap between property owners (who see their assets appreciate) and non-owners (who face rising rents and difficulty saving for deposits). It also reduces the geographical mobility of labour.\n2. Wealth effect and increased consumer spending: Existing homeowners feel wealthier as the value of their property rises, which can lead to increased consumer confidence and spending (the wealth effect). While this can stimulate economic growth, there is a risk of creating an unsustainable property bubble similar to the one that preceded the 2008 financial crisis."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7: Privatisation & Contributions to Economic Thought",
     q:"(a) In the case of any two of the following three pairs, distinguish between the two concepts:\n(i) Exchequer budget deficit and Exchequer budget surplus\n(ii) Current expenditure and capital expenditure\n(iii) Statutory and non-statutory incomes [20 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Define the term privatisation.\n(ii) Discuss the possible economic advantages and disadvantages of the Irish Government privatising a state company. [30 marks]\n\n(c) Discuss the contributions to economic thought of any one of the following economists:\n- Adam Smith\n- J.M. Keynes\n- T.K. Whitaker [25 marks]",
     a:"(a) Distinguishing between concepts (any two pairs):\n\n(i) Exchequer budget deficit: Occurs when total government expenditure exceeds total government revenue in a given year. The government spends more than it earns from taxation and other sources, and must borrow to cover the shortfall. This adds to the national debt.\n\nExchequer budget surplus: Occurs when total government revenue exceeds total government expenditure in a given year. The government earns more than it spends, and the surplus can be used to repay national debt, build up reserves, or invest.\n\n(ii) Current expenditure: Government spending on the day-to-day running of public services - wages and salaries of public servants, social welfare payments, interest on the national debt, and the purchase of goods and services for immediate use. These are recurring costs that are consumed in the short term.\n\nCapital expenditure: Government spending on long-term assets that will provide benefits over many years - roads, bridges, hospitals, schools, social housing, and public transport infrastructure. These are investments that increase the productive capacity of the economy.\n\n(iii) Statutory incomes: Incomes that are set or regulated by law. These include the national minimum wage, which is legally mandated, and social welfare payments set by the government through legislation.\n\nNon-statutory incomes: Incomes that are determined by market forces or negotiation rather than by law. These include wages set through collective bargaining between employers and trade unions, or salaries determined by supply and demand in the labour market.\n\n(b)(i) Privatisation: The transfer of ownership of a state-owned or semi-state enterprise from public (government) ownership to private ownership, usually through the sale of shares to private investors on the stock market.\n\n(ii) Advantages of privatisation:\n- Increased efficiency: Private firms, driven by the profit motive and competitive pressure, tend to operate more efficiently than state-owned enterprises. They cut waste, innovate, and improve productivity to maximise profits.\n- Revenue for the government: The sale of a state company generates a one-off cash injection for the government, which can be used to reduce the national debt or invest in other priorities.\n- Wider share ownership: Privatisation through a stock market flotation allows citizens to buy shares, promoting wider share ownership and financial inclusion.\n- Consumer benefits: Increased competition following privatisation can lead to lower prices, better quality, and greater choice for consumers.\n- Reduced government interference: Removes political interference in commercial decision-making, allowing the firm to be managed on purely business principles.\n\nDisadvantages of privatisation:\n- Natural monopoly concerns: Some state companies are natural monopolies (e.g. water, electricity networks). Privatising them may simply create a private monopoly that can exploit consumers by charging higher prices.\n- Loss of public service ethos: State companies may provide essential services to rural or disadvantaged areas that a profit-driven private firm would discontinue as unprofitable.\n- Job losses: Private owners may cut costs by reducing the workforce, leading to unemployment.\n- Loss of future revenue: The government loses the ongoing profits and dividends from the company permanently.\n- Short-termism: Private firms may focus on short-term profit maximisation rather than long-term strategic investment.\n\n(c) Contributions of J.M. Keynes (sample answer):\n\n1. The General Theory (1936): Keynes challenged the classical economic view that markets naturally self-correct. He argued that in a recession, the economy could reach equilibrium at a level below full employment. Demand deficiency, not wage rigidity, was the cause of prolonged unemployment.\n\n2. Government intervention: Keynes advocated active government fiscal policy to manage the economy. During recessions, the government should increase spending and reduce taxes (expansionary fiscal policy) to boost aggregate demand, even if this means running a budget deficit. During booms, the government should reduce spending and increase taxes (contractionary fiscal policy) to prevent overheating.\n\n3. The multiplier: Keynes developed the concept of the multiplier, showing that an initial injection of government spending leads to a proportionally larger increase in national income as the money circulates through the economy.\n\n4. Paradox of thrift: Keynes showed that while saving is rational for individuals, if everyone saves more during a recession, aggregate demand falls further, worsening the recession. What is rational for the individual is irrational for the economy as a whole.\n\n5. Legacy: Keynesian economics influenced government policy worldwide for decades. His ideas were the basis for the post-war economic boom and remain influential today - governments used Keynesian-style stimulus packages during the 2008 financial crisis and the COVID-19 pandemic."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8: Migration, Population Growth & Social Housing",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between the terms migration and emigration.\n(ii) Outline the possible economic effects of emigration on the Irish economy. [25 marks]\n\n(b)(i) Outline three economic implications of Ireland's increasing population.\n(ii) Discuss one positive and one negative economic effect of immigration on the Irish economy. [25 marks]\n\n(c) Discuss the economic case for increased government investment in social housing in Ireland. [25 marks]",
     a:"(a)(i) Migration: The movement of people from one place to another. This is a broad term that encompasses all types of population movement, whether within a country (internal migration) or between countries (international migration). It includes both immigration (people coming into a country) and emigration (people leaving a country).\n\nEmigration: Specifically refers to the movement of people OUT of their home country to live and work in another country. For example, Irish people who moved to Australia, Canada, or the UK during the financial crisis were emigrants from Ireland.\n\n(ii) Economic effects of emigration on the Irish economy:\n- Brain drain: The loss of skilled, educated, and often young workers who have been trained at the expense of Irish taxpayers. The economy loses their productivity, tax contributions, and innovation potential.\n- Reduced labour supply: Emigration can create labour shortages in certain sectors, particularly in skilled professions (healthcare, technology), making it harder for firms to recruit.\n- Reduced government revenue: Fewer workers means less income tax, PRSI, and USC contributions, as well as reduced VAT revenue from lower consumer spending.\n- Reduced demand: Fewer consumers in the economy means lower demand for goods and services, which can slow economic growth.\n- Remittances: Emigrants may send money back to family in Ireland, providing an inflow of income. However, this is typically small relative to the economic loss.\n- Positive effects: Emigration can reduce unemployment in the short term and alleviate pressure on public services and housing. Returning emigrants may bring back new skills, experience, and international contacts.\n\n(b)(i) Three economic implications of Ireland's increasing population:\n1. Increased demand for public services: A growing population requires more schools, hospitals, public transport, water services, and other infrastructure. This puts pressure on government spending and requires long-term planning and investment.\n2. Increased labour force: A larger population can provide a bigger labour force, supporting economic growth and attracting FDI by offering firms a larger pool of workers to draw from.\n3. Housing demand: Population growth increases demand for housing, putting upward pressure on property prices and rents. If housing supply does not keep pace, this leads to affordability problems and homelessness.\n\n(ii) Positive economic effect of immigration:\nFilling skills gaps: Immigration brings workers with skills and qualifications that may be in short supply in Ireland. For example, the healthcare sector relies heavily on immigrant workers (doctors, nurses, care workers). This helps firms operate, maintains public services, and contributes to economic growth and tax revenue.\n\nNegative economic effect of immigration:\nPressure on housing and infrastructure: A large influx of immigrants increases demand for housing (particularly rental accommodation), healthcare, and school places, which can drive up prices and create shortages, particularly in already stretched urban areas. This can lead to social tensions and competition for scarce resources.\n\n(c) Economic case for investment in social housing:\n\n1. Addressing the housing crisis: Ireland faces a severe housing shortage, with rising homelessness, unaffordable rents, and long social housing waiting lists. Government investment in social housing directly increases the supply of affordable accommodation for those who cannot afford market rents or house prices.\n\n2. Economic stimulus: Construction of social housing creates employment in the construction sector and related industries (materials, services, planning). Through the multiplier effect, this spending generates additional economic activity and tax revenue.\n\n3. Reducing government welfare costs: The government currently spends significant amounts on rent supplements and the Housing Assistance Payment (HAP) scheme, paying private landlords to house social welfare recipients. Building social housing reduces these ongoing costs over time, as the government owns the asset.\n\n4. Labour mobility: Affordable housing in areas where jobs exist enables workers to move to where they are needed, improving the geographical mobility of labour and supporting economic growth.\n\n5. Reducing inequality: Social housing reduces wealth inequality by providing secure, affordable housing for lower-income households. This improves health outcomes, educational attainment (for children in stable housing), and overall social wellbeing.\n\n6. Long-term asset: Unlike spending on private sector rent subsidies, social housing is a long-term asset that retains value and generates rental income for the state over decades. It is an investment, not just expenditure."}
  ],

"2018": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Perfect Competition - Price Taker",
     q:"The firm in perfect competition is a 'price taker'.\n\n(a) Explain this statement.\n\n(b) Other than price taking, outline two characteristics of a perfectly competitive market.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Each individual firm must accept the price as it is set in the market. Because each firm supplies such a tiny fraction of the total market output, it cannot influence the market price. Demand facing the firm is perfectly elastic - if the firm increases its price above the prevailing market price, quantity demanded would fall to zero.\n\n(b) Characteristics of a perfectly competitive market:\n1. Freedom of entry and exit: There are no barriers preventing new firms from entering the industry or existing firms from leaving.\n2. Homogeneous products: The products offered for sale by all firms are identical/the same, so consumers have no preference for one firm's product over another.\n\nOther valid characteristics include: perfect knowledge of market conditions, large number of buyers and sellers, perfectly elastic supply of factors of production, no collusion between firms, and firms aim to maximise profits where MC = MR."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Interest Rates & the Irish Economy",
     q:"Raising interest rates before the end of the decade could threaten the long sought-after economic recovery in the Eurozone, the OECD has warned.\n\nOutline two possible economic effects of an increase in interest rates on the Irish economy.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Two possible economic effects of an increase in interest rates:\n\n1. Borrowing discouraged / disincentive to invest: As interest rates rise, the cost of borrowing increases, discouraging firms from investing in new capital and consumers from taking out loans. This reduces spending and slows economic growth.\n\n2. Savings encouraged / reduced consumer spending: Higher interest rates increase the return on savings, encouraging people to save rather than spend. Consumers' real disposable incomes fall (especially those with variable-rate mortgages), leading to a reduction in spending and demand for firms' products.\n\nOther valid effects include: increased mortgage repayments impacting families with high debt; increased cost of servicing the national debt; contraction in spending leading to a fall in GDP; costs of production increase reducing competitiveness; fewer sales leading to unemployment; a stronger euro making Irish exports less competitive."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Customs Union",
     q:"The EU is an example of a customs union. Explain the term customs union.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"A customs union exists where member countries agree to:\n1. Remove all trade barriers between them (tariffs, quotas, and other restrictions on trade among member states are eliminated).\n2. Impose a common external tariff (CET) on imports from non-member countries.\n\nThis means that goods can move freely between member states without any tariffs, but any country outside the customs union faces the same tariff rate when exporting to any member state. The EU customs union allows for the free movement of goods between all EU member states while maintaining a unified trade policy with the rest of the world."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns",
     q:"(a) State the Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns.\n\n(b) Does this law apply in the short run or in the long run? Explain your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) The Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns states that if increasing quantities of a variable factor of production (e.g. labour) are added to a given quantity of a fixed factor of production (e.g. capital/land), a stage will eventually be reached where the addition to total output (marginal product) from each extra unit of the variable factor begins to decline.\n\n(b) It applies in the short run only.\nWhy? It is only in the short run that at least one factor of production is fixed. In the long run, all factors of production are variable, so the concept of a fixed factor (which is essential for this law to operate) does not apply. The law requires at least one input to remain constant while others are increased."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: True or False - National Income Concepts",
     q:"Read each statement below and indicate if the statement is TRUE or FALSE.\n\n1. 'Double counting' could occur when economic activity is measured using the expenditure method.\n2. Exports represent an injection into the circular flow of income.\n3. Gross Domestic Product (GDP) includes a provision for depreciation.\n4. Primary school teacher salaries are categorised as transfer payments in Ireland.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"1. FALSE - Double counting is more likely to occur when economic activity is measured using the output method (counting the value of output at each stage of production). The expenditure method avoids this by counting only final expenditure.\n\n2. TRUE - Exports are money flowing into the circular flow of income from outside the domestic economy, making them an injection. They increase the size of the circular flow.\n\n3. TRUE - GDP is measured at gross level, meaning it has NOT deducted depreciation (capital consumption). If depreciation were deducted, it would become Net Domestic Product (NDP).\n\n4. FALSE - Primary school teacher salaries are NOT transfer payments. They are payments for services rendered (teachers provide educational services in return for wages). Transfer payments are one-way payments where no good or service is provided in return, e.g. social welfare payments, pensions, children's allowance."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Adam Smith's Canons of Taxation",
     q:"State and explain Adam Smith's four Canons of Taxation.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Adam Smith's four Canons of Taxation:\n\n1. Equity: The more you earn, the greater proportion of tax you should pay. Taxes should be progressive, taking a higher proportion of income in tax as income rises. Persons should contribute in proportion to their ability to pay.\n\n2. Economy: The revenue collected from the tax should far exceed the costs of collecting it. The main purpose of taxation is to raise revenue, so the administrative costs should be kept to a minimum.\n\n3. Certainty: The amount of tax to be paid should be clear and certain. The taxpayer should know how much they are paying, the manner of payment, and the time of payment. The government should also know how much revenue it will receive.\n\n4. Convenience: The tax should be collected in a way and at a time which suits the taxpayer. It is more convenient when it is deducted at source, e.g. PAYE (deducted from wages before the employee receives them) or VAT (included in the price of goods)."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Income Inequality",
     q:"(a) Outline two policies a government could consider to reduce income inequality.\n\n(b) Outline one possible challenge for the Irish economy of reducing income inequality.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Policies to reduce income inequality:\n\n1. Welfare benefits could be increased or maintained: This ensures that those on low incomes are financially supported, helping to close the gap between rich and poor.\n\n2. Tax changes: Make direct taxes more progressive, e.g. increase higher rates of PAYE or take lower income groups outside the tax net entirely. This redistributes income from higher earners to lower earners through public services.\n\nOther valid policies: introduce a wealth tax, means-test universal entitlements like child benefit, increase the minimum wage/introduce a living wage, improve access to healthcare and education for lower-income groups, narrow the gender pay gap.\n\n(b) Challenge of reducing income inequality:\nPossible increase in costs to businesses (e.g. from higher minimum wages or employer taxes) which would affect competitiveness. If increased costs are passed on to consumers, it could be inflationary. There may also be an opportunity cost - resources used to reduce inequality cannot be used elsewhere."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Raising the Retirement Age",
     q:"Outline three economic consequences of raising the retirement age for public sector workers to 70 years.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Three economic consequences of raising the retirement age to 70:\n\n1. Increased government revenue: As people work longer, they continue to pay income tax (PAYE, USC) rather than drawing pensions. This increases government tax revenue and also provides workers with increased earning potential and more secure retirement savings.\n\n2. Increased demand in the economy: Workers earn salaries that are higher than pensions, so more demand for goods and services is generated, potentially creating more jobs and contributing to economic growth.\n\n3. Reduced government spending on pensions: With workers staying in employment longer, the government does not have to pay state pensions as early, freeing up revenue that can be redirected to other public services such as healthcare, education, or infrastructure.\n\nOther valid consequences: it may enhance the quality of life and health of workers; it could be considered anti-worker and unjust as wealthier individuals still have the means to retire early while lower-income workers are disproportionately affected; it may block younger workers from entering the workforce."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Price Discrimination",
     q:"(a) Define the term price discrimination.\n\n(b) Outline three conditions necessary for price discrimination to occur.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Price discrimination is the practice of selling the same product at different prices to different consumers or groups of consumers in different markets, where the difference in price does not reflect differences in the cost of production.\n\n(b) Three conditions necessary for price discrimination:\n\n1. Some degree of monopoly/market power: The firm must have some ability to alter the market price by exercising control over demand, supply, or both. A firm in perfect competition cannot price discriminate as it is a price taker.\n\n2. Separation of markets / inability to resell: The markets must be separated so that consumers who buy in the low-priced market cannot resell the product in the higher-priced market. For example, dental appointments or cinema tickets cannot be resold.\n\n3. Different price elasticities of demand: Different consumers must display different sensitivities to price changes. The price discriminator charges higher prices to consumers with inelastic demand and lower prices to consumers with elastic demand. For example, business travellers (inelastic) vs students (elastic)."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Demand, Market Equilibrium & Consumer Surplus",
     q:"(a)(i) Distinguish between a movement along a demand curve and a shift in a demand curve.\n(ii) Explain, using a supply and demand diagram, how a market would return to equilibrium following a surplus (excess supply) in the market.\n\n(b) The owners of a music venue, with a maximum capacity of 400 people, conducted market research into consumer demand for tickets at different prices. Price/Quantity Demanded/Quantity Supplied: €100/0/400, €80/200/400, €60/400/400, €40/600/400, €20/800/400.\n(i) Using the data, draw the demand and supply curves, showing the equilibrium price and quantity.\n(ii) Explain the reason for the shape of the supply curve for this music venue.\n(iii) Explain the terms consumer surplus and total revenue.\n(iv) Show the areas of consumer surplus and total revenue at equilibrium on the diagram.\n\n(c) The owners decide to charge €60 per ticket.\n(i) Calculate the total revenue from ticket sales, assuming a full house.\n(ii) Explain how knowledge of consumer surplus could benefit the venue owners.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Movement along a demand curve:\nA change in the price of the good itself causes a movement along the demand curve (all other things being equal). This is referred to as a change in quantity demanded.\n\nShift in a demand curve:\nA change in any factor other than the good's own price (e.g. income, tastes, price of substitutes/complements, expectations) causes the entire demand curve to shift. This is referred to as a change in demand.\n\n(ii) Returning to equilibrium after a surplus:\nWhen there is a surplus (excess supply), the price is above the equilibrium price. At this price, the quantity supplied exceeds the quantity demanded. Sellers find their stocks increasing and cannot sell all they wish at the current price. In response, sellers begin cutting their prices. As prices fall, some consumers are persuaded to buy more (movement along the demand curve). Prices continue to fall until the market reaches equilibrium where Qd = Qs.\n\nDiagram: Standard supply and demand diagram with price P1 above equilibrium PE, showing the surplus gap between QD and QS at P1, with arrows indicating price falling back to PE.\n\n(b)(i) Equilibrium: Price = €60, Quantity = 400.\nThe demand curve slopes downward from (0, €100) through (200, €80), (400, €60), (600, €40), to (800, €20). The supply curve is a vertical line at Q = 400 (perfectly inelastic).\n\n(ii) The supply curve is perfectly inelastic (vertical) because regardless of price, the quantity supplied is fixed at 400. The venue has a maximum/fixed capacity of 400 - even if the price rises to €100, the venue can only accommodate 400 attendees.\n\n(iii) Consumer surplus: The difference between what consumers are willing to pay for a good and what they actually pay. At equilibrium (€60), some consumers would have been willing to pay €80 or €100 but only pay €60, so they enjoy surplus utility.\n\nTotal revenue: The total income received by the firm from selling its output. Total Revenue = Price x Quantity sold.\n\n(iv) On the diagram:\n- Consumer surplus is the triangular area above the equilibrium price (€60) and below the demand curve.\n- Total revenue is the rectangular area: Price (€60) x Quantity (400) = €24,000.\n\n(c)(i) Total Revenue = Price x Quantity = €60 x 400 = €24,000.\n\n(ii) Knowledge of consumer surplus would benefit the owners because they could identify that some consumers are willing to pay more than €60. The owners could use price discrimination (e.g. VIP tickets, early bird pricing, premium seating) to capture some of this consumer surplus and convert it into additional revenue. For example, they could sell front-row tickets at €80 or €100 to consumers who value them highly."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Oligopoly & Anti-Competitive Practices",
     q:"(a)(i) Describe how firms in an oligopoly market structure compete with each other.\n(ii) Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of oligopoly as a market structure.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, using a clearly labelled diagram, the long-run equilibrium of a firm operating under oligopoly conditions.\n(ii) With reference to the 'kinked' demand curve drawn in part (i), explain the rationale for 'price rigidity'.\n\n(c) 'Motor insurance providers in Ireland targeted in anti-cartel inquiry.' (Source: The Irish Times, July 2017)\n(i) Explain the likely effects on consumers if collusion (e.g. cartel agreement) existed in the motor insurance industry.\n(ii) Suggest two measures the government could consider to discourage anti-competitive practices in industry.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) How oligopoly firms compete:\nFirms in an oligopoly tend to avoid price competition (price wars are destructive for all firms). Instead, they compete primarily through non-price competition: advertising and branding, product differentiation, loyalty schemes, after-sales service, sponsorship deals, and special offers. Each firm is interdependent - they must consider the likely reaction of rival firms before making pricing decisions.\n\n(ii) Advantages of oligopoly:\n- Innovation and R&D: Oligopolistic firms earn supernormal profits which can be reinvested in research and development, leading to new and improved products.\n- Economies of scale: Large-scale production lowers average costs, and some savings may be passed on to consumers.\n- Product variety: Non-price competition leads to a wider range of differentiated products for consumers.\n- Employment: Large firms create significant employment.\n\nDisadvantages of oligopoly:\n- Higher prices: Due to limited competition, prices tend to be higher than in more competitive markets.\n- Barriers to entry: New firms find it difficult to enter the market due to high start-up costs, brand loyalty, and economies of scale enjoyed by incumbents.\n- Risk of collusion: Firms may collude (form cartels) to fix prices or restrict output, harming consumers.\n- Wasteful advertising: Significant resources are spent on advertising which could be used more productively.\n\n(b)(i) Kinked demand curve diagram:\nThe demand curve has a kink at the current price. Above the kink, demand is relatively elastic (if the firm raises price, rivals will not follow, so the firm loses many customers). Below the kink, demand is relatively inelastic (if the firm lowers price, rivals will match the cut, so little extra demand is gained). The MR curve has a vertical discontinuity (gap) at the kink output level. MC can shift within this gap without changing the profit-maximising output or price.\n\n(ii) Price rigidity:\nPrices tend to be rigid (sticky) in oligopoly because of the kinked demand curve. If a firm raises its price above the kink, rival firms will not follow the increase. The firm that raised its price will lose significant market share as consumers switch to cheaper rivals. If a firm lowers its price, rivals will match the reduction to protect their market share, so the firm gains very little extra demand. Therefore, firms have no incentive to change price in either direction, leading to price rigidity.\n\n(c)(i) Effects of collusion on motor insurance consumers:\n- Higher premiums: A cartel agreement would result in artificially inflated insurance premiums as firms collectively agree to charge higher prices.\n- Reduced choice/quality: With no real competition, there is less incentive to innovate or improve service quality.\n- Consumers are exploited: They pay more than they would in a competitive market, transferring wealth from consumers to insurance firms.\n- Allocative inefficiency: Resources are not allocated efficiently as the price exceeds marginal cost.\n\n(ii) Government measures to discourage anti-competitive practices:\n1. Strengthen the Competition and Consumer Protection Commission (CCPC): Give the CCPC greater powers to investigate, prosecute, and impose heavy fines on firms engaged in anti-competitive behaviour. Criminal sanctions (including imprisonment) for individuals involved in cartels act as a strong deterrent.\n2. Deregulation / encourage new entrants: Remove barriers that prevent new insurance providers from entering the Irish market. More competitors would make collusion more difficult and give consumers greater choice and lower prices."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Costs, Price Elasticity of Supply & Sterling",
     q:"(a) The table shows output and total cost for a firm. The selling price is fixed at €30.\nOutput: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\nTotal Cost: €20, €40, €50, €65, €85, €115, €160, €210\n\n(i) Define the term marginal cost.\n(ii) Draw and label a graph illustrating the marginal cost at each level of output.\n(iii) Indicate on the graph the profit-maximising level of output and explain your answer.\n(iv) Calculate the profit earned at this profit-maximising level of output.\n\n(b)(i) Define the term price elasticity of supply (PES).\n(ii) Explain, by means of an example, why the PES might be different in the long run than in the short run.\n(iii) Discuss two factors, other than time period, that influence the PES of a product.\n\n(c)(i) Outline the potential impact a fall in the value of Sterling against the Euro would have on the price UK consumers pay for Irish products.\n(ii) Explain how knowledge of price elasticity of demand (PED) might be helpful to Irish exporters who wish to maximise total revenue in the UK market.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Marginal cost is the addition to total cost of producing one extra unit of output. MC = Change in Total Cost / Change in Output.\n\n(ii) Marginal Cost at each output level:\nOutput 1: MC = €40 - €20 = €20\nOutput 2: MC = €50 - €40 = €10\nOutput 3: MC = €65 - €50 = €15\nOutput 4: MC = €85 - €65 = €20\nOutput 5: MC = €115 - €85 = €30\nOutput 6: MC = €160 - €115 = €45\nOutput 7: MC = €210 - €160 = €50\n\nDiagram: MC curve plotted against output, showing a U-shape that first falls to €10 at output 2, then rises. A horizontal line at €30 represents the selling price (MR = AR = €30).\n\n(iii) The profit-maximising level of output is 5 units. The firm maximises profit where MC = MR. Since the selling price is €30 (and MR = €30 in perfect competition), the firm produces where MC is closest to €30 without exceeding it. At output 5, MC = €30 = MR.\n\n(iv) Profit = Total Revenue - Total Cost\nTotal Revenue = Price x Quantity = €30 x 5 = €150\nTotal Cost at 5 units = €115\nProfit = €150 - €115 = €35\n\n(b)(i) Price Elasticity of Supply (PES) measures the responsiveness of the quantity supplied of a good to a change in its price. PES = % change in quantity supplied / % change in price.\n\n(ii) In the short run, a farmer may have inelastic supply for crops because once planted, the quantity cannot be easily increased - land, seeds, and labour are committed. However, in the long run, the farmer can acquire more land, plant more seeds, invest in machinery, and increase output significantly. Therefore, PES is typically more elastic in the long run because producers have more time to adjust their production levels.\n\n(iii) Factors influencing PES:\n1. Availability and mobility of factors of production: If raw materials, labour, and capital are readily available and can be easily redeployed, firms can increase output quickly in response to a price rise, making supply more elastic. If factors are scarce, supply will be inelastic.\n2. Ability to store the product: If a product can be stored (e.g. canned goods, electronics), firms can build up stocks when prices are low and release them when prices rise, making supply more elastic. Perishable goods (e.g. fresh fish) cannot be stored, making their supply more inelastic.\n\n(c)(i) A fall in the value of Sterling (£) against the Euro (€) means the pound is weaker relative to the euro. UK consumers would need to pay more pounds to buy the same euro-priced Irish products. Irish products become more expensive for UK consumers in Sterling terms. This could lead to a fall in demand for Irish exports to the UK as they become less price competitive.\n\n(ii) Knowledge of PED helps Irish exporters:\n- If demand for the product is price inelastic (PED < 1), the price increase (in Sterling terms) will cause a relatively small decrease in quantity demanded. Total revenue in the UK market may actually increase. Exporters should maintain their euro prices.\n- If demand is price elastic (PED > 1), the price increase will cause a proportionally larger decrease in quantity demanded, reducing total revenue. Exporters might consider reducing their euro prices (absorbing some of the exchange rate impact) to maintain competitiveness and volume.\n- Understanding PED allows exporters to set the optimal pricing strategy to maximise total revenue in the UK market."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Labour Productivity, Economies of Scale & Mobility",
     q:"(a)(i) Define the term labour productivity.\n(ii) Explain how a firm could increase the productivity of labour.\n\n(b) Globalisation allows firms to exploit economies of scale.\n(i) Explain the term economies of scale.\n(ii) Discuss how a firm might benefit from economies of scale, providing examples.\n(iii) Can firms become 'too big'? Explain your answer.\n\n(c) Restrictions placed on workers' mobility can impact on economic growth.\n(i) Distinguish between occupational mobility of labour and geographic mobility of labour.\n(ii) Discuss the factors that limit the geographic mobility of labour.\n(iii) Suggest one measure which could be considered to address any factor identified in (ii). Explain your answer.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Labour productivity is the output per worker or output per hour worked. It measures how efficiently labour is being used in the production process.\n\n(ii) A firm could increase the productivity of labour by:\n- Investing in training and education: Upskilling workers so they can perform tasks more efficiently and to a higher standard.\n- Investing in new technology/capital equipment: Better machinery and technology allow workers to produce more output in less time.\n- Improving working conditions and motivation: Better pay, flexible working arrangements, and a positive work environment can boost morale and output.\n- Specialisation/division of labour: Assigning workers to specific tasks they are skilled at increases efficiency.\n\n(b)(i) Economies of scale are the cost advantages that a firm experiences as it increases its scale of production. As output increases, the average cost of production per unit falls. They arise because the firm can spread its fixed costs over a larger output and benefit from efficiencies that come with size.\n\n(ii) How a firm benefits from economies of scale:\n- Technical economies: A large firm can invest in specialised machinery and production lines. For example, a large car manufacturer like Volkswagen can use robotic assembly lines that produce thousands of cars per day at very low unit cost.\n- Purchasing/bulk-buying economies: A large firm can negotiate discounts on raw materials by buying in bulk. For example, Tesco can buy from suppliers at lower unit prices than a small corner shop.\n- Financial economies: Large firms can borrow money at lower interest rates because banks view them as lower risk.\n- Marketing economies: The cost of advertising can be spread over a larger volume of sales, reducing the marketing cost per unit.\n- Managerial economies: Large firms can afford to employ specialist managers (finance, HR, marketing) who increase efficiency.\n\n(iii) Yes, firms can become 'too big'. This leads to diseconomies of scale - as a firm grows beyond a certain point, average costs begin to rise due to:\n- Communication difficulties: In very large organisations, communication between departments becomes slow and inefficient, leading to delays and errors.\n- Coordination problems: Managing a very large workforce across multiple locations is difficult and can lead to duplication of effort.\n- Low worker morale: Employees in very large firms may feel anonymous and undervalued, reducing motivation and productivity.\n- Bureaucracy: Excessive layers of management can slow decision-making.\n\n(c)(i) Occupational mobility of labour: The ability of a worker to move between different types of jobs/occupations. For example, a factory worker retraining as an IT technician.\n\nGeographic mobility of labour: The ability of a worker to move between different locations/regions to take up employment. For example, a worker relocating from Cork to Dublin for a new job.\n\n(ii) Factors limiting geographic mobility:\n- Housing costs and availability: High rents and property prices in cities like Dublin make it difficult for workers to afford to relocate. Housing shortages compound this problem.\n- Family and social ties: Workers may be reluctant to move away from family, friends, schools, and community support networks.\n- Lack of information: Workers may not be aware of job opportunities in other regions.\n- Regional differences in cost of living: The cost of living varies significantly between regions, creating uncertainty about whether relocation is financially worthwhile.\n- Language/cultural barriers: For international mobility, language differences and cultural adjustment can be significant obstacles.\n\n(iii) Measure to address housing costs/availability:\nThe government could invest in social and affordable housing in areas of high demand/employment growth. By increasing the supply of affordable housing, workers who want to relocate for employment would find it easier and more affordable to do so, improving geographic mobility and helping to fill job vacancies in high-demand areas."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): Fiscal & Monetary Policy",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the terms fiscal policy and monetary policy.\n(ii) Discuss two reasons why the Irish Government may consider the use of fiscal policy instead of monetary policy to manage the Irish economy.\n\n(b)(i) Using a fully labelled diagram, explain the economic effects of a government decision to increase indirect taxation on a good.\n(ii) Outline three reasons why a government would use indirect taxation.\n(iii) Discuss one disadvantage of indirect taxation.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the economic term budget deficit.\n(ii) Discuss two possible economic consequences of a persistent budget deficit for the Irish economy.\n(iii) Outline one step the government could take to reduce the budget deficit.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Fiscal policy: The use of government taxation and expenditure to influence the level of economic activity. The government adjusts tax rates and public spending through the annual Budget to achieve economic objectives such as full employment, price stability, and economic growth.\n\nMonetary policy: The management of the money supply and interest rates to influence the level of economic activity. In Ireland's case, monetary policy is controlled by the European Central Bank (ECB) for all eurozone members.\n\n(ii) Reasons for using fiscal policy instead of monetary policy:\n1. Ireland has no independent monetary policy: As a eurozone member, Ireland has ceded control of monetary policy to the ECB. The ECB sets interest rates for the entire eurozone, which may not suit Ireland's specific economic circumstances. Therefore, fiscal policy is the primary tool available to the Irish government.\n2. Fiscal policy can be targeted: The government can direct spending or tax changes at specific sectors, regions, or income groups. For example, it can increase capital spending on housing or reduce tax for lower-income earners. Monetary policy is a blunt instrument that affects the entire economy equally.\n\n(b)(i) Diagram and explanation:\nAn increase in indirect tax on a good shifts the supply curve to the left (from S1 to S2), as the tax increases producers' costs. The equilibrium price rises from P1 to P2, and the equilibrium quantity falls from Q1 to Q2. The burden (incidence) of the tax is shared between producers and consumers depending on the price elasticity of demand. The tax revenue collected by the government is represented by the area between S1 and S2 up to Q2.\n\n(ii) Three reasons to use indirect taxation:\n1. Revenue generation: Indirect taxes such as VAT generate significant and reliable revenue for the government to fund public services.\n2. Discourage consumption of harmful goods: High excise duties on cigarettes and alcohol discourage consumption of goods that cause negative externalities (demerit goods).\n3. Relatively easy to collect: Indirect taxes are collected by businesses and passed on to the government, making them administratively efficient.\n\n(iii) Disadvantage of indirect taxation:\nIndirect taxes are regressive - they take a higher proportion of income from lower-income earners than from higher-income earners. A 23% VAT rate on goods affects someone earning €25,000 proportionally more than someone earning €100,000, as the lower earner spends a greater proportion of their income on consumption.\n\n(c)(i) A budget deficit occurs when total government expenditure exceeds total government revenue in a given year. The government spends more than it collects in taxes and other income, and must borrow to make up the shortfall.\n\n(ii) Consequences of a persistent budget deficit:\n1. Rising national debt: Persistent deficits mean the government must borrow year after year, increasing the national debt. The cost of servicing this debt (interest payments) rises, diverting resources from productive spending on services like health and education.\n2. Crowding out: Increased government borrowing may drive up interest rates, making it more expensive for private firms to borrow and invest. This can reduce private sector investment and slow economic growth.\n\n(iii) Step to reduce the budget deficit:\nThe government could broaden the tax base - bringing more economic activity within the scope of taxation (e.g. reducing tax reliefs and exemptions, increasing property tax, or ensuring all income earners contribute). This would increase revenue without necessarily raising existing tax rates, helping to close the gap between revenue and expenditure."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): Migration, EU Single Market & Overseas Development Aid",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline three economic effects of migration on the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss the economic impact of free movement of labour within the EU Single Market on Ireland.\n\n(b) Ireland is a member of the EU Single European Market (SEM).\n(i) Outline the benefits of Ireland's membership of the SEM.\n(ii) Discuss any challenges Ireland faces as a member of the SEM.\n\n(c) Discuss the arguments for and against Ireland providing Overseas Development Aid (ODA) to Less Developed Countries (LDCs).\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Three economic effects of migration on the Irish economy:\n1. Increased labour supply: Immigration increases the supply of workers, helping to fill skills shortages in sectors such as healthcare, IT, and construction. This supports economic growth.\n2. Increased tax revenue: More workers earning income means more income tax (PAYE) and USC revenue for the government, as well as increased VAT revenue from their spending.\n3. Pressure on public services: A larger population requires more investment in housing, healthcare, education, and transport infrastructure. If services cannot keep pace, quality may decline.\n\n(ii) Economic impact of free movement of labour:\nPositive: Irish firms can recruit skilled workers from across the EU, addressing skills shortages. Irish workers can also seek employment opportunities in other EU countries. This increases efficiency as labour moves to where it is most productive.\nNegative: Brain drain is possible if highly skilled Irish workers emigrate to higher-paying EU countries. There may also be downward pressure on wages in certain sectors if there is an oversupply of labour.\n\n(b)(i) Benefits of SEM membership:\n- Access to a market of over 440 million consumers for Irish exporters, enabling economies of scale.\n- Free movement of goods, services, capital, and labour reduces costs and increases efficiency.\n- Attracts FDI: multinational companies locate in Ireland partly to access the EU Single Market.\n- Greater competition leads to lower prices and improved quality for Irish consumers.\n\n(ii) Challenges of SEM membership:\n- Loss of sovereignty: Ireland must comply with EU regulations and directives, limiting its ability to set independent policies in some areas.\n- Competition from EU firms: Irish businesses face competition from firms across the EU, which can be challenging for smaller, less competitive domestic firms.\n- EU regulations may impose costs on Irish businesses (environmental standards, consumer protection).\n\n(c) Arguments FOR providing ODA:\n- Moral obligation: As a wealthy nation, Ireland has a responsibility to help reduce poverty and improve living standards in LDCs.\n- Long-term economic benefits: Developing countries that grow become future trading partners and markets for Irish exports.\n- Political stability: Aid reduces poverty, inequality, and instability in LDCs, contributing to global peace and security.\n- Ireland's own history: Ireland has its own experience of famine and emigration, creating a cultural empathy for those in need.\n\nArguments AGAINST providing ODA:\n- Opportunity cost: Money spent on ODA could be used domestically on healthcare, housing, or education.\n- Effectiveness concerns: Aid may not always reach those who need it most due to corruption or poor governance in recipient countries.\n- Dependency: Continuous aid may create dependency rather than encouraging self-sufficiency.\n- Domestic priorities: Ireland faces its own economic challenges (housing crisis, healthcare waiting lists) that require funding."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): Irish Economy, Banking & Government Capital Spending",
     q:"(a) Discuss the current performance of the Irish economy with reference to key economic indicators.\n\n(b)(i) Outline the role of banking regulation in the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss the lessons learned from the Irish banking crisis.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the importance of government capital spending for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss how increased government capital spending could affect economic growth and employment.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a) Performance of the Irish economy (2018 context):\n- Economic growth: Ireland experienced strong GDP growth, among the highest in the EU, driven by exports and FDI. However, modified GNI (GNI*) provided a more accurate measure of domestic economic activity.\n- Employment: Unemployment fell significantly from crisis-era peaks, approaching full employment levels.\n- Government finances: The budget deficit narrowed considerably, and Ireland was moving towards a balanced budget.\n- Housing: Significant housing shortage with rising rents and house prices, creating affordability problems.\n- National debt: While the debt-to-GDP ratio improved, the national debt remained high in absolute terms.\n\n(b)(i) Role of banking regulation:\nBanking regulation ensures the stability, safety, and soundness of the financial system. The Central Bank of Ireland supervises banks to ensure they maintain adequate capital reserves, manage risk appropriately, lend responsibly, and treat customers fairly. Regulation protects depositors' savings and prevents the systemic failures that can damage the wider economy.\n\n(ii) Lessons from the banking crisis:\n- Excessive and reckless lending (particularly to property developers) without adequate risk assessment led to a property bubble.\n- Weak regulation allowed banks to engage in practices that threatened the entire financial system.\n- The government guarantee of bank liabilities placed an enormous burden on taxpayers.\n- Proper stress testing and capital requirements are essential to prevent future crises.\n- Over-reliance on the construction sector made the economy vulnerable to a property downturn.\n\n(c)(i) Importance of government capital spending:\nCapital spending on infrastructure (roads, hospitals, schools, broadband, housing) increases the productive capacity of the economy. It improves the quality of public services, attracts FDI, supports balanced regional development, and creates the foundations for long-term sustainable economic growth.\n\n(ii) Effects on growth and employment:\n- Direct employment: Capital projects create direct employment in construction and related industries.\n- Multiplier effect: The initial spending circulates through the economy, generating additional spending and income.\n- Improved infrastructure: Better roads, ports, and broadband increase productivity and reduce costs for businesses.\n- Attracts investment: Quality infrastructure makes Ireland more attractive for both domestic and foreign investment.\n- Long-term growth: Investment in education facilities and healthcare infrastructure improves human capital, which is a key driver of long-term economic growth."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8 (Section B): National Income, FDI & Environment",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term Gross National Product (GNP).\n(ii) Distinguish between GNP at market prices and GNP at factor cost.\n(iii) Explain why GDP may not be an accurate measure of the standard of living in Ireland.\n(iv) Outline one alternative measure that could be used.\n\n(b) Discuss the importance of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) for the Irish economy.\n\n(c)(i) Outline the economic arguments for the protection of the environment.\n(ii) Discuss two measures the government could implement to protect the environment.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) GNP (Gross National Product) is the total value of all final goods and services produced by a country's residents (both domestically and abroad) over a given period, usually one year. GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad.\n\n(ii) GNP at market prices includes indirect taxes (such as VAT and excise duties) and excludes subsidies. It reflects the prices consumers actually pay.\nGNP at factor cost excludes indirect taxes and includes subsidies. It reflects the actual cost of the factors of production used. GNP at factor cost = GNP at market prices - indirect taxes + subsidies.\n\n(iii) Why GDP may not accurately measure living standards in Ireland:\nIreland's GDP is significantly inflated by the activities of large multinational corporations. These firms' profits are included in Irish GDP but are largely repatriated to their parent companies abroad. This means GDP overstates the income actually available to Irish residents. GNI* (Modified Gross National Income) was developed to strip out these distortions and provide a more accurate picture of the domestic economy.\n\n(iv) Alternative measure: The Human Development Index (HDI), which measures standard of living using three indicators: life expectancy at birth, education (mean and expected years of schooling), and GNI per capita. It provides a broader measure of well-being than purely economic output.\n\n(b) Importance of FDI for the Irish economy:\n- Employment creation: Multinational companies employ approximately 300,000+ people directly in Ireland, providing high-quality jobs in sectors like technology, pharmaceuticals, and financial services.\n- Tax revenue: FDI firms contribute significantly through corporation tax, PAYE, and VAT payments.\n- Technology transfer: MNCs bring advanced technology, skills, and management practices that benefit the wider economy.\n- Exports: FDI firms account for a very large proportion of Irish exports, contributing to a positive balance of payments.\n- Regional development: Companies like Apple (Cork), Intel (Leixlip), and various pharma firms in regional locations create employment outside Dublin.\n- Multiplier effect: The spending of multinational employees and the purchases of local supplies generate additional economic activity.\n\n(c)(i) Economic arguments for protecting the environment:\n- Sustainable development: Environmental protection ensures that current economic activity does not compromise the ability of future generations to meet their needs.\n- Tourism: Ireland's natural environment is a key tourist attraction. Environmental degradation would damage the tourism industry.\n- Health costs: Pollution and environmental damage lead to health problems, increasing healthcare costs for the government.\n- Market failure: Environmental damage represents a negative externality where the polluter does not bear the full social cost.\n\n(ii) Two measures to protect the environment:\n1. Carbon tax: Imposing a tax on carbon emissions makes polluting activities more expensive, incentivising firms and consumers to reduce their carbon footprint and switch to cleaner alternatives. The revenue can be used to fund green initiatives.\n2. Investment in renewable energy: The government can invest in wind, solar, and other renewable energy sources to reduce dependence on fossil fuels. Subsidies and grants for households and businesses to install renewable energy systems (e.g. solar panels) encourage the transition to a low-carbon economy."}
  ],

  "2019": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Equi-Marginal Principle & Marginal Utility",
     q:"(a) Explain the economic concept of the Equi-Marginal Principle of consumer behaviour.\n\n(b) In equilibrium, a consumer buys 6 apples at €0.90 each and 7 oranges at €0.50 each. The marginal utility of the 6th apple is 9 utils. Calculate the marginal utility of the 7th orange. Show your workings.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) The Equi-Marginal Principle of consumer behaviour states that consumers will spend their limited income in such a way that the ratio of marginal utility to price is the same for every good consumed, in order to maximise total utility. The principle states that utility is maximised when the utility derived from the last euro spent on each product is equalised:\nMUa/Pa = MUb/Pb = ... = MUn/Pn\n\n(b) Workings:\nUsing the equi-marginal principle: MUa/Pa = MUo/Po\n9/0.90 = MUo/0.50\n10 = MUo/0.50\nMUo = 10 x 0.50\nMUo = 5 utils\n\nThe marginal utility of the 7th orange is 5 utils."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: True or False - Labour Market Concepts",
     q:"Indicate whether each of the following statements is TRUE or FALSE.\n\n1. The demand for labour is a derived demand.\n2. The Live Register is the most accurate measure of Ireland's unemployment rate.\n3. Cyclical unemployment is caused by a recession.\n4. The backward bending labour supply curve illustrates the concept that workers substitute leisure for work as wages rise above a certain level.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"1. TRUE - The demand for labour is a derived demand because workers are not demanded for their own sake but because of the goods and services they produce. If demand for a product increases, the demand for workers who produce it also increases.\n\n2. FALSE - The Live Register is NOT the most accurate measure of unemployment. It includes part-time workers and those on certain social welfare schemes who may not be genuinely unemployed. The Quarterly National Household Survey (QNHS) / Labour Force Survey (LFS) is considered more accurate.\n\n3. TRUE - Cyclical unemployment is caused by a downturn in the economy (recession). When economic activity declines, demand for goods and services falls, firms reduce production, and workers are laid off. It follows the business cycle.\n\n4. FALSE - The backward bending labour supply curve illustrates the concept that workers substitute LEISURE for WORK (not work for leisure) as wages rise above a certain level. At high wages, the income effect outweighs the substitution effect - workers can afford to work fewer hours and still maintain their desired income, so they choose more leisure."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Economic Development vs Economic Growth",
     q:"Distinguish between the terms Economic Development and Economic Growth.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Economic Development refers to an increase in a country's GDP per capita / standard of living / output following a FUNDAMENTAL CHANGE to the structure of society. It involves improvements in education, healthcare, infrastructure, institutions, and governance. It is a qualitative concept that encompasses improvements in people's well-being, reduced inequality, and structural transformation of the economy (e.g. moving from agriculture-based to industry/services-based).\n\nEconomic Growth refers to an increase in a country's GDP per capita / output WITHOUT the need for a fundamental change to the structure of society. It is a quantitative measure - simply an increase in the total output of goods and services produced. A country can experience economic growth (e.g. through increased exports) without necessarily experiencing economic development (e.g. if the benefits are not widely shared or social conditions do not improve)."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: John Maynard Keynes",
     q:"Outline two main contributions to economic thought by the economist John Maynard Keynes.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Two main contributions by John Maynard Keynes:\n\n1. Government intervention / Fiscal policy: Keynes proved that national income could reach equilibrium without full employment (contrary to classical economists who believed markets would always self-correct). He argued that the best way to manage an economy was through active government intervention using fiscal policy - the government should increase spending and reduce taxes during recessions to stimulate demand, and do the opposite during booms.\n\n2. The Multiplier Effect: Keynes showed that an initial injection of spending into the circular flow of income leads to a greater than proportionate increase in national income. For example, if the government invests €1 million in infrastructure, the workers and suppliers who receive this money will spend a portion of it, creating further income for others, and so on - leading to a total increase in national income that is a multiple of the initial injection.\n\nOther valid contributions: Liquidity Preference Theory (the interest rate has limited impact on desire to hold cash; three motives for holding money - transactionary, precautionary, speculative); Paradox of Thrift (an increase in saving leads to decreased aggregate demand and lower national income); challenging Say's Law (arguing that demand determines output, not supply)."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Imperfect Competition - Diagram Labels",
     q:"The diagram represents the long-run equilibrium of a firm in imperfect competition.\n\n(i) Write in full the label for each of the lines numbered 1 to 4.\n\n(ii) Show clearly on the diagram the long-run equilibrium price, quantity and cost for the firm.\n\n(iii) State whether or not the firm is earning supernormal profits. Give a reason for your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(i) Labels for the four lines:\n1. Marginal Cost (MC)\n2. Average Cost (AC)\n3. Marginal Revenue (MR)\n4. Demand / Average Revenue (D = AR)\n\n(ii) The long-run equilibrium:\n- Quantity: Found where MC = MR (profit-maximising output), labelled Q1.\n- Price: Read off the AR/Demand curve at Q1, labelled P1.\n- Cost: Read off the AC curve at Q1, labelled C1.\nIn this diagram, P1 = C1 (price equals average cost at the equilibrium output).\n\n(iii) The firm is NOT earning supernormal profits - it is earning only NORMAL profits in the long run. This is because Average Revenue (AR) equals Average Cost (AC) at the equilibrium level of output. Since AR = AC, the firm's total revenue equals its total cost, meaning only normal profit is being earned. Normal profit is included as part of the firm's costs."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Free Trade Area vs Customs Union",
     q:"Distinguish between a Free Trade Area and a Customs Union.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Free Trade Area:\nA Free Trade Area is an agreement between member countries to trade freely with each other without tariffs or other trade barriers being imposed on goods traded among them. However, each member country is free to set its own tariffs and trade policies with non-member countries. Example: NAFTA (North American Free Trade Agreement) / USMCA.\n\nCustoms Union:\nA Customs Union is more advanced than a Free Trade Area. In a customs union, member countries agree to trade freely among themselves (like a free trade area) AND they also impose a common external tariff (CET) on imports from countries that are outside the union. This means all member states apply the same tariff rates to non-member countries. Example: The European Union.\n\nKey difference: In a free trade area, members set their own external tariffs independently. In a customs union, members agree on a common external tariff, providing a more unified trade policy."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Government Debt-to-GDP & Unemployment",
     q:"(a) Place a tick in the correct box to indicate the approximate rates for the past year:\n(i) Government Debt-to-GDP rate: 40% / 70% / 120%\n(ii) Unemployment rate: 6% / 9% / 12%\n\n(b) Explain the recent trend for each economic indicator above.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Government Debt-to-GDP rate: approximately 70% (in 2018/2019).\n(ii) Unemployment rate: approximately 6% (in 2018/2019).\n\n(b) Government Debt-to-GDP rate trend:\nThe debt-to-GDP ratio has been decreasing. Due to increased economic growth (strong GDP growth), the ratio of debt to GDP has fallen. While the actual debt level remains high (built up during the financial crisis and bank bailout), the denominator (GDP) has been growing strongly, improving the ratio. The government has also been reducing the budget deficit, slowing the accumulation of new debt.\n\nUnemployment rate trend:\nThe unemployment rate has continued to decrease as economic growth strengthens and the economy approaches full employment. Strong job creation, particularly in sectors like technology, pharmaceuticals, and financial services, has driven unemployment down from crisis-era peaks of over 15% towards levels considered to represent full employment (4-6%)."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Income Tax Threshold Increase",
     q:"The Taoiseach has pledged to increase the threshold for the top rate of income tax (PAYE) to €50,000 over the next five years.\n\nOutline two possible economic advantages and one possible economic disadvantage of this measure for the Irish economy.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"Advantages:\n\n1. Increased disposable income / improved standard of living: Employees will earn a larger amount of take-home pay as less of their income is taxed at the higher rate. This means they can spend more on goods and services, improving their standard of living.\n\n2. Incentive to work / reduced emigration: As working conditions and rewards improve in Ireland, there is less incentive for skilled workers to seek employment abroad. Higher take-home pay also attracts immigrant workers to fill skills shortages. Employees are more productive when they feel adequately rewarded.\n\nOther valid advantages: More equitable distribution (the 'squeezed middle' are better rewarded); greater consumer demand via the multiplier effect; reduced pressure on wage demands as real income increases without nominal wage increases.\n\nDisadvantage:\n\nLess government revenue from taxation: As the government increases the threshold, less of people's incomes will be eligible to be taxed at the higher rate. This means the government will receive less income tax revenue from these workers, creating an opportunity cost - less money available for public services such as healthcare, education, and housing."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Extended Working Lives",
     q:"In Japan, 12% of the working population is aged over 65 years, whereas in the UK and Ireland the percentage is closer to 3%.\n\n(a) Outline two reasons why government policies might be changed to support extended working lives.\n\n(b) Suggest one measure which the government or firms could take to support extended working lives.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Two reasons to support extended working lives:\n\n1. Enlarged labour force / help fill labour shortages: The larger the workforce, the more workers are available across different sectors of the economy, especially those experiencing skills shortages. An ageing population means fewer younger workers entering the labour force, so retaining older workers helps maintain economic output.\n\n2. Increased income tax revenue / improved government finances: With an expanded workforce, more people are earning incomes and paying income tax. Experienced workers tend to earn higher salaries, generating more revenue from the higher tax bracket. Simultaneously, the government saves on pension payments as workers delay claiming state pensions.\n\nOther valid reasons: valuable skills and experience retained in the workforce; improved dependency ratio; positive impact on older workers' quality of life.\n\n(b) Measure to support extended working lives:\nOffer more flexible work arrangements. Public and private sector workers above the traditional retirement age could be offered part-time work, job-sharing, or working from home options. This allows them to continue contributing to the economy and earning income while accommodating any reduced physical capacity or caring responsibilities. Flexible arrangements make it more attractive and feasible for older workers to remain in the workforce."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Demand, Market Equilibrium & Rent Control",
     q:"(a)(i) Define the economic terms individual demand and market demand.\n(ii) Outline three factors that would affect market demand for fitness watches.\n\n(b) Assume the market for a brand of Irish crisps is in equilibrium. Explain, with the aid of a separate diagram in each case, the effects which each of the following would have on the initial equilibrium position:\n(i) The lack of rainfall in 2018 creates a drought which reduces potato crops.\n(ii) Vegan consumers stage protests against animal-derived flavouring on crisps.\n(iii) Crisp companies receive a subsidy from the government.\n\n(c) Rents continue to trend upwards, with average national rent increasing to €1,122 in Q3 2018, an increase of €78 since Q3 2017. The diagram illustrates a government intervention in the form of rent control (price ceiling).\n(i) Explain the impact of the implementation of the rent control, with reference to the diagram.\n(ii) Outline the possible economic consequences on the Irish rental property market of this government intervention.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Individual demand: The quantity demanded of a good or service by an individual consumer at various prices.\n\nMarket demand: The aggregate (total) quantity demanded of a good or service by all consumers in a market at various prices. It is the horizontal summation of all individual demand curves.\n\n(ii) Three factors affecting market demand for fitness watches:\n1. Consumer tastes/preferences: An increased focus on health consciousness and fitness tracking makes the product more desirable. Improvements in technology and functionality also increase demand.\n2. Consumer income/confidence: As consumers have increased discretionary income (fitness watches are a luxury good), demand rises. Higher consumer confidence encourages spending on non-essential items.\n3. Price of complementary goods: A decrease in the price of smartphones (which pair with fitness watches) makes fitness watches more desirable. Marketing and advertising campaigns by producers also stimulate demand.\n\n(b)(i) Drought reduces potato crops:\nPotatoes are a raw material used in crisp production. The reduced supply of potatoes increases production costs for crisp manufacturers. The supply curve shifts to the LEFT from S1 to S2. Equilibrium price rises from P1 to P2, and equilibrium quantity falls from Q1 to Q2.\n\n(ii) Vegan protests against animal-derived flavouring:\nThe protests create negative publicity for the product, reducing its popularity with consumers. The demand curve shifts to the LEFT from D1 to D2. Equilibrium price falls from P1 to P2, and equilibrium quantity decreases from Q1 to Q2.\n\n(iii) Crisp companies receive a subsidy:\nA subsidy is a payment from the government to encourage production. This reduces the costs of production for crisp manufacturers. The supply curve shifts to the RIGHT from S1 to S2. Equilibrium price decreases from P1 to P2, and equilibrium quantity increases from Q1 to Q2.\n\n(c)(i) Impact of rent control (price ceiling):\nThe rent control sets a maximum price (PMax) below the equilibrium rent (PE). At this lower rent:\n- Quantity demanded of rental properties increases (more people want to rent at the lower price) to 600,000 properties.\n- Quantity supplied of rental properties decreases (landlords withdraw properties from the market at the lower return) to 200,000 properties.\n- This creates excess demand (shortage) of 400,000 properties (600,000 - 200,000).\n- Many people who want to rent cannot find available properties.\n\n(ii) Economic consequences on the rental market:\n- Reduced supply of rental properties: Landlords may sell properties, convert them to other uses, or stop maintaining them due to reduced rental income.\n- Black market/under-the-table payments: Tenants may offer illegal top-up payments to secure scarce properties, defeating the purpose of the price ceiling.\n- Reduced investment in new rental properties: Developers have less incentive to build new rental accommodation if returns are capped.\n- Lower quality housing: Landlords may cut maintenance spending, leading to deterioration of rental property quality.\n- Existing tenants benefit from lower rents, but new entrants to the market struggle to find accommodation."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Monopoly",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline three characteristics of a monopoly firm.\n(ii) Explain why some degree of monopoly power must exist for price discrimination to occur.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, using a clearly labelled diagram, the long-run equilibrium position of a monopoly firm.\n(ii) With reference to the diagram, explain why a firm's demand curve under monopoly differs from a firm's demand curve under perfect competition.\n\n(c) Big Western technology firms such as Apple, Google, Facebook and Amazon are accused of being BAADD - Big, Anti-competitive, Addictive and Destructive to Democracy. 'The presumption that big businesses must necessarily be wicked is plain wrong.' (Source: The Economist, January 2018)\n(i) State and explain three advantages of large-scale businesses, using examples from the technology industry.\n(ii) Outline the possible effects on a monopoly market structure of government regulation to reduce anti-competitive behaviour.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Three characteristics of a monopoly:\n1. Single seller: There is only one firm in the industry, and the firm IS the industry.\n2. Unique product: The product has no close substitutes, so consumers have no alternative suppliers.\n3. High barriers to entry: Other firms cannot enter the market due to barriers such as legal protection (patents, licences), economies of scale, control of essential resources, or high start-up costs.\n\nOther valid characteristics: price maker (can set price), imperfect information, downward-sloping demand curve.\n\n(ii) Why monopoly power is needed for price discrimination:\nA firm must have the ability to control the market price - it must be a price maker, not a price taker. Without monopoly power, if a firm tried to charge different prices, a competitor would undercut the higher price, and consumers in the higher-priced market would simply switch to the competitor. Only a firm with some degree of monopoly power can successfully charge different prices because consumers have no (or limited) alternative suppliers.\n\n(b)(i) Monopoly long-run equilibrium diagram:\nThe monopolist faces a downward-sloping demand curve (D = AR). The MR curve lies below the AR curve. The firm produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising output, Q1). The price is read off the AR curve at Q1 (P1). Average cost at Q1 is C1, where C1 < P1.\n\nThe monopolist earns supernormal profit in the long run because AR > AC at the profit-maximising output. Supernormal profit = (AR - AC) x Q. These profits persist in the long run because barriers to entry prevent new firms from entering the market.\n\n(ii) Demand curve differences:\nUnder monopoly, the firm faces the entire market demand curve, which slopes downward. The monopolist must reduce price to sell additional units, so MR < AR (price).\n\nUnder perfect competition, each individual firm is so small relative to the market that it cannot influence price. The demand curve facing the firm is perfectly elastic (horizontal) at the market price. D = AR = MR - the firm can sell as many units as it wants at the prevailing market price.\n\n(c)(i) Three advantages of large-scale businesses:\n1. Economies of scale: Large tech firms can produce at very low average costs due to their massive scale. For example, Google's search engine serves billions of queries daily at negligible marginal cost, allowing it to offer the service free while generating advertising revenue.\n2. Research and development / innovation: Large firms can invest heavily in R&D, developing new products and technologies. Apple, for example, invests billions annually in R&D, producing innovations like the iPhone and Apple Watch that benefit consumers worldwide.\n3. Employment creation: Large technology firms create thousands of high-skilled, well-paid jobs. For example, Apple, Google, and Facebook collectively employ tens of thousands of workers in Ireland alone, contributing significantly to the Irish economy.\n\n(ii) Effects of government regulation on monopoly:\n- Lower prices: Regulation may impose price controls, preventing the monopolist from charging excessively high prices, benefiting consumers.\n- Increased competition: Deregulation and anti-trust measures may break up monopolies or remove barriers to entry, allowing new firms to enter the market.\n- Improved quality and service: The threat of regulatory action incentivises monopolists to improve product quality and customer service.\n- Reduced supernormal profits: Regulation may limit the profits a monopolist can earn, redistributing the benefit to consumers.\n- Potential drawbacks: Over-regulation may reduce the monopolist's incentive to invest in R&D, potentially slowing innovation."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Price Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term Price Elasticity of Demand (PED).\n(ii) Outline four factors that affect the PED for a good or service, providing examples.\n\n(b) The PED for airline tickets from Dublin to Dubai for two consumer categories: PED Consumer A = -0.1, PED Consumer B = -2.4.\n(i) Explain which figure is more likely to correspond to holiday-makers and which to business travellers.\n(ii) Explain, with the aid of diagrams, the difference between price elastic demand and price inelastic demand.\n(iii) Explain how knowledge of PED is useful when analysing the effect of price changes on total revenue for an airline.\n\n(c)(i) When the price of a tablet device was €500, consumers demanded 25,000 units of a related Good X per year. When the price of the tablet fell to €350, the quantity demanded of Good X decreased to 10,000 units. Calculate the Cross Price Elasticity of Demand (CED) for Good X and explain the relationship between the goods.\n(ii) Would you expect a similar CED for a tablet device and a tablet case? Explain.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded of a good or service to a change in its price. PED = % change in quantity demanded / % change in price. If PED > 1, demand is elastic; if PED < 1, demand is inelastic; if PED = 1, demand is unit elastic.\n\n(ii) Four factors affecting PED:\n1. Availability of substitutes: If many close substitutes exist, demand is more elastic. For example, demand for a specific brand of butter is elastic because consumers can easily switch to another brand. Demand for petrol is inelastic because there are few practical alternatives.\n2. Necessity vs luxury: Necessities tend to have inelastic demand (e.g. bread, electricity) because consumers continue to buy them regardless of price. Luxuries (e.g. designer clothes, holidays) have elastic demand.\n3. Proportion of income spent: Goods that take up a small proportion of income (e.g. salt, matches) tend to have inelastic demand. Goods taking a large proportion (e.g. a car, a house) tend to have elastic demand.\n4. Time period: Demand tends to be more inelastic in the short run (consumers need time to find alternatives) and more elastic in the long run (consumers can adjust behaviour, find substitutes).\n\n(b)(i) PED Consumer A = -0.1 most likely corresponds to BUSINESS TRAVELLERS. Business travellers MUST travel for work commitments regardless of price - their demand is highly price inelastic. The company typically pays, and the trip cannot be postponed.\n\nPED Consumer B = -2.4 most likely corresponds to HOLIDAY-MAKERS. Holiday-makers are more sensitive to price changes - they can choose alternative destinations, postpone travel, or choose different airlines. Their demand is price elastic.\n\n(ii) Price elastic demand (PED > 1): The percentage change in quantity demanded is GREATER than the percentage change in price. A small price increase leads to a proportionally larger decrease in quantity demanded. Diagram: a relatively flat/horizontal demand curve.\n\nPrice inelastic demand (PED < 1): The percentage change in quantity demanded is LESS than the percentage change in price. A price increase leads to a proportionally smaller decrease in quantity demanded. Diagram: a relatively steep/vertical demand curve.\n\n(iii) Knowledge of PED and total revenue:\n- If demand is price inelastic (PED < 1): The airline should INCREASE prices to maximise revenue. The percentage fall in quantity demanded will be smaller than the percentage increase in price, so total revenue rises.\n- If demand is price elastic (PED > 1): The airline should DECREASE prices to maximise revenue. The percentage increase in quantity demanded will be larger than the percentage decrease in price, so total revenue rises.\n- The airline can use price discrimination: charge higher prices to business travellers (inelastic demand) and lower prices to holiday-makers (elastic demand), maximising total revenue from both segments.\n\n(c)(i) CED Calculation:\n% change in price of tablet = (€350 - €500) / €500 x 100 = -30%\n% change in quantity demanded of Good X = (10,000 - 25,000) / 25,000 x 100 = -60%\nCED = % change in Qd of Good X / % change in Price of tablet\nCED = -60% / -30% = +2.0\n\nSince CED is POSITIVE, Good X and the tablet device are SUBSTITUTE goods. When the price of the tablet fell, consumers switched away from Good X to buy the cheaper tablet instead, so demand for Good X decreased.\n\n(ii) No, you would NOT expect a similar CED for a tablet and a tablet case. A tablet and a tablet case are COMPLEMENTARY goods (used together). The CED would be NEGATIVE. When the price of a tablet falls, more tablets are demanded, which increases the demand for tablet cases. The quantity demanded of cases would increase as the price of tablets decreases, giving a negative CED."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Factors of Production & Costs",
     q:"(a)(i) In the case of any two of the following three pairs, distinguish between the two terms:\n- Average Cost and Marginal Cost\n- Employment Rate and Participation Rate\n- Marginal Physical Product and Marginal Revenue Product\n\n(ii) Outline two factors which influence the Marginal Revenue Product of Labour.\n\n(b) The table shows cost data for a firm in equilibrium:\nQuantity: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\nAVC: -, 200, 150, 158.33, 187.5, 240, 300\nAFC: -, 600, 300, 200, 150, 120, 100\n\n(i) Calculate the average total cost of producing the 6th unit of output.\n(ii) Calculate the total cost of producing 2 units of output.\n(iii) Calculate the marginal cost of increasing output from 4 units to 5 units.\n(iv) If the firm maximises profits where marginal revenue is €100, how many units will it produce? Explain.\n\n(c) The jobs market is currently suffering from skills shortages in accounting, engineering, pharmaceuticals, IT, and law.\n(i) Explain three economic effects of skills shortages for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Outline measures the government and/or firms could take to address skills shortages.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Pair 1 - Average Cost and Marginal Cost:\nAverage Cost (AC) is the total cost of production divided by the number of units produced. AC = TC / Q. It represents the cost per unit of output.\nMarginal Cost (MC) is the addition to total cost resulting from the production of one extra unit of output. MC = Change in TC / Change in Q. It represents the cost of producing one additional unit.\n\nPair 2 - Employment Rate and Participation Rate:\nEmployment Rate is the percentage of the labour force that is currently employed. It measures how many people who are available and willing to work actually have jobs.\nParticipation Rate is the percentage of the working-age population (15-64) that is either employed or actively seeking employment. It includes both employed and unemployed people who are part of the labour force.\n\n(ii) Two factors influencing the MRP of Labour:\n1. The price of the product: If the selling price of the firm's output increases, the MRP of each worker increases (since MRP = MPP x Price). This makes each worker more valuable to the firm.\n2. The productivity of the worker (MPP): If a worker's marginal physical product increases (due to training, better equipment, or experience), their MRP increases. More productive workers generate more revenue for the firm.\n\n(b)(i) ATC of 6th unit = AVC + AFC = €300 + €100 = €400\n\n(ii) TC of 2 units = (AVC + AFC) x Q = (€150 + €300) x 2 = €450 x 2 = €900\n\n(iii) TC at 4 units = (€187.5 + €150) x 4 = €337.5 x 4 = €1,350\nTC at 5 units = (€240 + €120) x 5 = €360 x 5 = €1,800\nMC = TC5 - TC4 = €1,800 - €1,350 = €450\n\n(iv) The firm should produce where MC = MR. With MR = €100, we need to find where MC is closest to €100 without exceeding it. Looking at the marginal costs:\nMC of 1st unit: TC1 = (200+600) x 1 = €800; MC = €800 - 0 = €800 (but fixed costs are €600, so TC at 0 = €600)\nActually: TC0 = Fixed Costs = AFC x Q; at Q=1, AFC = 600, so FC = €600.\nTC0 = €600 (fixed costs only)\nTC1 = €800, MC1 = €200\nTC2 = €900, MC2 = €100\nTC3 = €1,075, MC3 = €175\n\nThe firm should produce 2 units, where MC = €100 = MR. At this point, the cost of producing the 2nd unit exactly equals the revenue gained from selling it. Producing a 3rd unit would cost €175 but only generate €100 in revenue, resulting in a loss on that unit.\n\n(c)(i) Three economic effects of skills shortages:\n1. Upward pressure on wages: Competition for scarce skilled workers drives up wages in affected sectors, increasing costs of production for firms and potentially leading to cost-push inflation.\n2. Reduced economic growth: Firms cannot expand production or undertake new projects if they cannot find workers with the required skills. This limits GDP growth and reduces Ireland's international competitiveness.\n3. Increased immigration / brain gain: Skills shortages attract skilled workers from abroad, increasing immigration. While this helps fill vacancies, it also puts pressure on housing, infrastructure, and public services.\n\n(ii) Measures to address skills shortages:\n- Government: Invest in education and training programmes aligned with industry needs (e.g. STEM courses, apprenticeships in IT and engineering). Provide grants and subsidies for retraining programmes.\n- Government: Reform immigration policy to make it easier for skilled workers from outside the EU to obtain work permits in shortage areas.\n- Firms: Invest in staff training and development programmes to upskill existing employees. Offer competitive salaries, benefits, and working conditions to attract and retain talent.\n- Firms/Government: Partner with universities and colleges to develop industry-relevant curricula and work placement programmes."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): Money, Banking & Government Objectives",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline two economic advantages of a cashless society.\n(ii) Outline four functions of money.\n\n(b) Discuss the role of the Central Bank of Ireland in regulating the Irish banking system.\n\n(c)(i) Explain why price stability is an important economic objective for the Irish government.\n(ii) Discuss how fiscal policy could be used to achieve price stability.\n(iii) Outline two possible conflicts between the government's economic objectives.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Two economic advantages of a cashless society:\n1. Reduced hidden economy: Digital payments create a record of all transactions, making it more difficult to evade taxes. This increases government tax revenue and creates a fairer system for all taxpayers.\n2. Reduced costs associated with handling cash: Banks, businesses, and the government save money on printing, distributing, storing, and securing physical cash. Transaction costs are reduced.\n\nOther valid advantages: reduced crime (less incentive to rob if no cash); faster and more convenient transactions; better tracking of spending for consumers.\n\n(ii) Four functions of money:\n1. Medium of exchange: Money is accepted as payment for goods and services, eliminating the need for barter (the double coincidence of wants problem).\n2. Unit of account: Money provides a common measure of value, allowing the prices of different goods and services to be compared.\n3. Store of value: Money can be saved and used at a later date, retaining its value over time (subject to inflation).\n4. Standard of deferred payment: Money allows for the creation of credit and the settlement of debts at a future date (e.g. mortgages, loans).\n\n(b) Role of the Central Bank of Ireland:\n- Prudential regulation: The Central Bank supervises and regulates banks and other financial institutions to ensure they operate safely and soundly, maintaining adequate capital reserves.\n- Consumer protection: It protects consumers of financial services by enforcing codes of conduct and investigating complaints against financial institutions.\n- Financial stability: It monitors the financial system for risks that could threaten stability and takes action to address them (e.g. macroprudential measures like mortgage lending limits).\n- Monetary policy implementation: While the ECB sets eurozone monetary policy, the Central Bank of Ireland implements it domestically.\n- Licensing: It grants and withdraws licences for financial institutions to operate in Ireland.\n- Lessons from the banking crisis: Following the 2008 crisis, the Central Bank was given strengthened regulatory powers to prevent a repeat of the reckless lending and inadequate supervision that contributed to the crisis.\n\n(c)(i) Why price stability is important:\nPrice stability (low and stable inflation) is essential because rising prices erode the purchasing power of consumers' incomes, reduce international competitiveness (making Irish exports more expensive), create uncertainty for businesses (making it difficult to plan and invest), and disproportionately affect those on fixed incomes (pensioners, social welfare recipients) who cannot negotiate higher payments.\n\n(ii) Fiscal policy for price stability:\n- Contractionary fiscal policy: If inflation is rising, the government can increase taxes (reducing disposable income and consumer spending) and/or reduce government expenditure. This reduces aggregate demand in the economy, easing inflationary pressures.\n- Targeted tax measures: The government can increase indirect taxes on specific goods contributing to inflation or reduce subsidies.\n- However, fiscal policy has time lags (the budget is annual) and may conflict with other objectives (reducing spending during inflation may increase unemployment).\n\n(iii) Two conflicts between economic objectives:\n1. Full employment vs price stability: Achieving full employment increases spending power, which can lead to demand-pull inflation. Reducing inflation through contractionary policy may increase unemployment.\n2. Economic growth vs balanced budget: To stimulate growth, the government may need to increase spending or reduce taxes, which could create a budget deficit. Achieving a balanced budget may require austerity measures that slow growth."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): Inflation & International Competitiveness",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term inflation.\n(ii) Distinguish between demand-pull inflation and cost-push inflation.\n(iii) Outline two economic consequences of inflation.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the importance of international competitiveness for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss the factors that affect Ireland's international competitiveness.\n(iii) Outline how inflation can affect Ireland's international competitiveness.\n\n(c) Discuss measures the Irish government could implement to improve Ireland's international competitiveness.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level of goods and services in an economy over a period of time. It means that, on average, prices are rising across the economy, reducing the purchasing power of money. It is measured using the Consumer Price Index (CPI).\n\n(ii) Demand-pull inflation: Occurs when aggregate demand in the economy exceeds aggregate supply. Too much money is chasing too few goods, pulling prices upward. Causes include increased consumer spending, government spending, or export demand.\n\nCost-push inflation: Occurs when the costs of production increase, pushing prices up. Causes include rising wages, increased raw material costs (e.g. oil prices), higher import costs, or increased indirect taxation.\n\n(iii) Two economic consequences of inflation:\n1. Reduced purchasing power: As prices rise, the value of money falls. Consumers can buy fewer goods and services with the same nominal income, reducing their real standard of living. This particularly affects those on fixed incomes.\n2. Loss of international competitiveness: If Ireland's inflation rate is higher than its trading partners, Irish exports become relatively more expensive in international markets, reducing demand for Irish goods and potentially worsening the balance of payments.\n\n(b)(i) Importance of international competitiveness:\nIreland is a small, open economy that is highly dependent on international trade. Exports account for a very large proportion of GDP. If Ireland is not competitive, it will lose export markets, leading to reduced output, lower employment, and slower economic growth. Competitiveness also affects Ireland's ability to attract FDI.\n\n(ii) Factors affecting competitiveness:\n- Labour costs and productivity: High wages relative to productivity reduce competitiveness. Ireland must ensure productivity growth keeps pace with wage increases.\n- Exchange rate: As a eurozone member, Ireland cannot devalue its currency. A strong euro makes Irish exports more expensive.\n- Infrastructure: Quality transport, broadband, and energy infrastructure reduce business costs.\n- Taxation: Ireland's competitive corporation tax rate attracts FDI, but any increase could reduce competitiveness.\n- Education and skills: A well-educated workforce increases productivity and innovation.\n- Regulation and bureaucracy: Excessive regulation increases costs for businesses.\n\n(iii) How inflation affects competitiveness:\nIf Irish inflation is higher than that of trading partners, Irish goods and services become relatively more expensive. Irish exporters lose market share as buyers switch to cheaper alternatives from less inflationary economies. Domestically, higher-priced Irish goods face increased competition from cheaper imports. This can lead to a deterioration in the balance of trade.\n\n(c) Measures to improve competitiveness:\n- Invest in infrastructure: Improved roads, ports, broadband, and public transport reduce costs for businesses and attract investment.\n- Invest in education and training: A highly skilled, flexible workforce drives innovation and productivity growth.\n- Control costs: Moderate wage increases, reduce energy costs, and ensure competitive property/rental costs for businesses.\n- Support R&D and innovation: Tax incentives and grants for research and development help firms create higher-value products.\n- Maintain competitive taxation: Keep corporation tax competitive while broadening the tax base.\n- Reduce bureaucracy and regulation: Simplify planning, licensing, and compliance processes for businesses."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): National Income",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term Gross National Product (GNP).\n(ii) Explain why GNP rather than GDP is considered a more accurate measure of the standard of living in Ireland.\n\n(b)(i) Outline the three methods of measuring national income.\n(ii) Explain the term transfer payments and give an example.\n(iii) Calculate GNP at factor cost from the following data: GDP at market prices = €200bn, Net Factor Income from Abroad = -€40bn, Indirect taxes = €30bn, Subsidies = €10bn. Show your workings.\n(iv) Explain the economic significance of Net Factor Income from Abroad being negative.\n\n(c)(i) Outline two uses of national income statistics.\n(ii) Discuss two limitations of using national income statistics as a measure of the standard of living.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) GNP (Gross National Product) is the total value of all final goods and services produced by a country's citizens/residents, both domestically and abroad, in a given time period (usually one year). GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad.\n\n(ii) GNP is more accurate than GDP for Ireland because:\nIreland's GDP is significantly inflated by the profits of multinational corporations (MNCs) operating in Ireland. These profits are included in Irish GDP but are largely repatriated to the MNCs' home countries (mainly the US). This income does not benefit Irish residents. GNP deducts this outflow of profits (through negative Net Factor Income from Abroad), providing a more accurate picture of the income actually available to Irish people. Ireland's GDP is approximately 20-30% higher than its GNP because of this profit repatriation.\n\n(b)(i) Three methods of measuring national income:\n1. Output method: Measures the total value of all final goods and services produced in the economy. Care must be taken to avoid double counting by measuring only value added at each stage of production.\n2. Income method: Measures the total income earned by all factors of production (wages, rent, interest, profit) in producing the national output.\n3. Expenditure method: Measures the total spending on final goods and services produced domestically. National Expenditure = C + I + G + (X - M).\n\nAll three methods should give the same figure for national income (the circular flow identity).\n\n(ii) Transfer payments are one-way payments made by the government where no good or service is provided in return. They are excluded from national income calculations because they do not represent payment for productive economic activity. Example: Social welfare payments, state pensions, children's allowance, Jobseeker's Allowance.\n\n(iii) GNP at Factor Cost calculation:\nGDP at Market Prices: €200bn\n+ Net Factor Income from Abroad: -€40bn\n= GNP at Market Prices: €160bn\n- Indirect Taxes: -€30bn\n+ Subsidies: +€10bn\n= GNP at Factor Cost: €140bn\n\n(iv) Economic significance of negative NFIA:\nA negative NFIA (-€40bn) means that more income is flowing OUT of the country than is flowing IN. In Ireland's case, the large outflow is primarily due to multinational corporations repatriating profits to their home countries. The income earned by foreign companies operating in Ireland exceeds the income earned by Irish companies and residents abroad. This is why Ireland's GNP is significantly lower than its GDP and highlights Ireland's dependence on foreign-owned enterprises.\n\n(c)(i) Two uses of national income statistics:\n1. Comparing living standards between countries: GNP/GDP per capita allows comparison of average living standards across different nations, though purchasing power parity (PPP) adjustments may be needed for accuracy.\n2. Measuring economic growth over time: Comparing real national income figures from year to year shows whether the economy is growing, stagnating, or contracting, helping the government formulate economic policy.\n\n(ii) Two limitations:\n1. Distribution of income is ignored: National income per capita is an average and does not show how income is distributed. A country could have high GDP per capita but extreme inequality, with wealth concentrated among a few while many live in poverty.\n2. Non-market activities are excluded: Activities like housework, childcare, and voluntary work are not included in national income figures even though they contribute to well-being. Similarly, the hidden economy (undeclared economic activity) is not captured, leading to an underestimation of true economic activity."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8 (Section B): Government Policies & Fiscal Policy",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline two economic policies the Irish government could pursue to reduce the cost of living.\n(ii) Discuss three canons of taxation that should guide Irish tax policy.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the role of fiscal policy in managing the Irish economy.\n(ii) Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using fiscal policy as a tool for economic management.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the economic term 'national debt'.\n(ii) Discuss two possible consequences of a high national debt for the Irish economy.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Two policies to reduce the cost of living:\n1. Reduce indirect taxes: Lowering VAT rates or excise duties on essential goods (food, energy, transport) would directly reduce prices, making these items more affordable for consumers.\n2. Increase competition in key sectors: Deregulating markets where prices are artificially high (e.g. insurance, energy, professional services) would allow new entrants to compete, driving prices down through market forces.\n\nOther valid policies: provide targeted subsidies on essential goods, invest in public housing to reduce rents, increase social welfare payments to match inflation.\n\n(ii) Three canons of taxation:\n1. Equity/Fairness: Those with greater ability to pay should contribute proportionally more to taxation. The tax system should be progressive - higher earners should pay a higher percentage of their income in tax. This ensures a fair distribution of the tax burden.\n2. Certainty: Taxpayers should know clearly how much tax they owe, when it is due, and how it should be paid. This allows individuals and businesses to plan financially and reduces disputes with revenue authorities.\n3. Economy: The cost of collecting taxes should be far less than the revenue generated. An efficient tax system minimises administrative costs for both the government (Revenue Commissioners) and the taxpayer. If collection costs are too high, the tax is not worth levying.\n\n(b)(i) Role of fiscal policy:\nFiscal policy is the government's use of taxation and public expenditure to influence the level of economic activity. Through the annual Budget, the government adjusts tax rates and spending levels to achieve key economic objectives: full employment, price stability, economic growth, and an equitable distribution of income. During recessions, the government can implement expansionary fiscal policy (lower taxes, higher spending). During periods of overheating, it can use contractionary fiscal policy (higher taxes, lower spending).\n\n(ii) Advantages of fiscal policy:\n- Direct targeting: The government can direct tax changes or spending at specific sectors, regions, or income groups most in need of support or restraint.\n- Automatic stabilisers: Progressive taxation and social welfare act as automatic stabilisers - tax revenue falls and welfare spending rises during recessions, cushioning the downturn without deliberate policy changes.\n- Democratic accountability: Fiscal policy decisions are made by elected representatives through the Budget process.\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Time lags: There is a delay between recognising an economic problem, designing fiscal policy, implementing it through the Budget, and its effects being felt in the economy.\n- Political constraints: Governments may make populist decisions (tax cuts, spending increases before elections) that are not economically optimal.\n- Crowding out: Increased government borrowing to fund spending may raise interest rates, discouraging private sector investment.\n- Inflexibility: The Budget is typically annual, limiting the government's ability to respond quickly to rapidly changing economic conditions.\n\n(c)(i) National debt:\nThe national debt is the total cumulative amount of money owed by the government to its creditors (bondholders, both domestic and international). It is the sum of all past budget deficits minus any surpluses. The government borrows by issuing bonds (government securities) when its expenditure exceeds its revenue.\n\n(ii) Two consequences of a high national debt:\n1. High debt servicing costs: A large national debt requires significant annual interest payments, diverting public funds away from productive spending on public services like healthcare, education, and infrastructure. These interest payments represent a leakage from the economy, particularly if the debt is held by foreign bondholders.\n2. Reduced fiscal flexibility: A high national debt limits the government's ability to respond to economic crises. If the debt is already high, the government has less room to borrow additional funds to stimulate the economy during a recession. Credit rating agencies may also downgrade the country's rating, increasing borrowing costs further."}
  ],

"2020": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Substitution Effect & Income Effect",
     q:"A fall in the price of a consumer product has both a substitution effect and an income effect.\n\nExplain each of the underlined terms.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Substitution effect: When the price of a good falls, consumers will demand more of the relatively cheaper good, as they switch their consumption away from relatively more expensive substitute goods. This is due to the increased marginal utility for the relatively cheaper good.\n\nIncome effect: When the price of a good falls, consumers experience an increase in their real income / purchasing power, as they can purchase the same quantity of the good using less income. With increased real income, consumers will purchase more of all normal goods (and less of inferior goods, including Giffen goods)."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Economic Development & Social Costs",
     q:"Citizens in some Less Developed Countries may experience social costs when these countries pursue economic development.\n\nExplain the underlined term and outline two possible social costs which these citizens may experience.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"Economic development refers to an increase in a country's GDP per capita / level of income / standard of living / output following a fundamental change to the structure of society, e.g. improvements in education, healthcare, infrastructure etc.\n\nSocial cost 1 - Damage to environment: Increasing economic development may lead to increased pollution, disfigurement of the landscape, and large-scale urban sprawl. The loss of the forests in Brazil is a result of the desire for economic development. Increased carbon emissions are also a consequence.\n\nSocial cost 2 - Population movement: Development may result in the large-scale movement of people from rural to urban areas with the possible creation of shanty towns / ghettoes and consequent social problems such as overcrowding and poverty."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Structural Unemployment & Government Policy",
     q:"Up to 800 million global workers will lose their jobs by 2030 and be replaced by robotic automation. (Source: BBC.com)\n\n(a) Name the type of unemployment these workers will experience. Give a reason for your answer.\n\n(b) Outline one policy response that the Irish government could take to reduce this type of unemployment in Ireland.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Type: Structural / Technological Unemployment.\n\nReason: This is a form of unemployment caused by a mismatch between the skills that workers in the economy can offer and the skills demanded of workers by employers. Technological unemployment is caused by new technology and innovation - people lose jobs due to advances in machinery and the introduction of labour-saving devices such as robotic automation.\n\n(b) Government policy: The government must provide retraining schemes for these unemployed workers so they can gain skills which are currently in demand. Programmes like Springboard+ offer free courses at certificate, degree, and masters level leading to qualifications in areas where there are employment opportunities. The government could also provide subsidies to firms that provide training to displaced workers, or relocation grants so workers can move to where their skills are still demanded."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Market Equilibrium & Subsidies (Beef Market)",
     q:"The diagram represents a change in equilibrium for the Irish beef market, showing supply shifting from S1 to S2, with equilibrium moving from E1 to E2.\n\n(i) Complete the diagram by labelling the initial equilibrium price and quantity and new equilibrium price and quantity.\n\n(ii) Indicate which of the following statements would cause the equilibrium to shift from E1 to E2: (a) Irish farmers' growing fears about Brexit, (b) An increase in a subsidy from the Irish government for beef farmers, (c) An increase in the number of vegetarian consumers in Ireland. Explain your answer.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(i) Diagram labels:\n- Initial equilibrium at E1: Price = P1, Quantity = Q1\n- New equilibrium at E2: Price = P2 (lower), Quantity = Q2 (higher)\nThe supply curve shifts right from S1 to S2, leading to a lower price and higher quantity.\n\n(ii) The correct answer is (b): An increase in a subsidy from the Irish government for beef farmers.\n\nExplanation: An increase in a subsidy for beef farmers helps them reduce their costs of production and entices farmers to produce more beef. This shifts the supply curve to the right from S1 to S2. As a result, the equilibrium price decreases from P1 to P2, and the equilibrium quantity increases from Q1 to Q2.\n\nWhy not (a): Farmers' fears about Brexit would reduce supply (shift left), not increase it.\nWhy not (c): An increase in vegetarian consumers would reduce demand (shift demand curve left), not shift the supply curve right."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Bank Branch Closures (PTSB)",
     q:"Permanent Trustee Savings Bank (PTSB) plans to close about one fifth of its 77 branches in Ireland. (Source: Irish Times, October 2019)\n\n(a) Outline one possible reason for the closure of these PTSB branches.\n\n(b) Outline two economic effects of these closures on the Irish economy.\n\n(16 marks)",
     a:"(a) Reason for closure: Increase in the use of online / mobile banking. As the number of services available online has increased, there has been less need for footfall in physical banks. Customers have become more accustomed to using technology (smartphones, tablet devices) for banking transactions, reducing the need for physical branches. Additionally, PTSB wants to reduce its operational costs to maintain profitability, especially given very low central bank interest rates and muted demand for loans.\n\n(b) Economic effects:\n\n1. Rise in unemployment in the banking sector: As the number of bank branches reduces, the number of employees required will be reduced. This will lead to increased unemployment and increased government spending on unemployment benefit.\n\n2. Loss of banking services to the local community: Banking will become more difficult for people who do not have transport to branches located further away. Those customers who are averse to the use of technology will find banking more difficult. There may also be reduced economic activity in the locality as fewer people visit the area."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Fiscal Policy & Keynesian Economics",
     q:"John Maynard Keynes supported the use of fiscal policy by government in an economy.\n\n(i) Define the term fiscal policy.\n\n(ii) Do you agree with Keynes' support of government intervention in an economy? Yes/No. Outline two reasons for your answer.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(i) Fiscal policy refers to those actions taken by the government which influence the size, timing, and magnitude of government spending and taxation. It is any change in government spending and taxation that is designed to change overall spending in an economy and influence economic growth.\n\n(ii) Yes, I agree with Keynes' support of government intervention.\n\nReason 1 - Achieve sustainable economic growth: The best way for a country to achieve sustainable economic growth and avoid large booms followed by prolonged recessions is through government intervention to either dampen down growth in booms (contractionary fiscal policy) or encourage growth in recessions (expansionary fiscal policy).\n\nReason 2 - Help create employment: Intervention by the government helps foster employment creation directly by the state (e.g. public sector hiring, infrastructure projects) or by aiding enterprise through the operation of incentives (grants, tax breaks).\n\nAlternative reasons AGAINST:\n- Anti laissez-faire: The economy will gravitate towards a natural equilibrium based on supply and demand without intervention.\n- Funding by borrowing / increased taxation which will be borne by the taxpayer.\n- Government services may be costly and inefficient due to lack of competition."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Gig Economy Regulation",
     q:"The continuing rise of the 'gig economy' has been phenomenal. However, some workers seek an increase in regulation associated with the 'gig economy' and zero-hour contracts. (Note: A gig economy is where firms tend to hire independent workers and freelancers on a short term / payment-by-task basis, e.g. Deliveroo riders, instead of full-time employees.)\n\n(a) Outline two arguments in favour of increased regulation of the 'gig economy'.\n\n(b) Outline one argument against increased regulation of the 'gig economy'.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Arguments in favour of increased regulation:\n\n1. Protection of employee rights: Employees may have been exploited through zero-hour contracts where they must always be available for work but are not guaranteed any work, and are consequently only paid for hours worked. Regulation would protect against this exploitation.\n\n2. Certainty to the employee's standard of living: If regulated, hours of work and pay will be guaranteed. This will help employees improve their standard of living, be available for another part-time job, have a more secure source of income, and be able to plan for their future.\n\n(b) Argument against increased regulation:\n\nReduced flexibility for employees and employers: The fact that employees can work under such flexible conditions is what may make these jobs appealing. Increased regulation may act as a disincentive to some people taking jobs in certain sectors. It may also mean employers face increased costs if employees are paid even when demand is low, potentially leading to a reduction in profits or the provision of some services."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Decline in Rural Economic Activity",
     q:"Some citizens are concerned with the decline in economic activity in some towns and rural areas in Ireland.\n\n(a) Explain two economic effects of this decline in economic activity on the Irish economy.\n\n(b) Outline one measure which the government could take to address this economic decline.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(a) Economic effects:\n\n1. Regional variations in employment levels: With decreased employment opportunities in certain regions, it may lead to increased unemployment within specific areas. With most employment concentrated in large urban areas, the economy may become overly dependent on certain sectors.\n\n2. Falling population in some areas / overcrowding in others: The lack of jobs and resources in certain areas may force people to seek employment in already overcrowded parts of Ireland. Younger people may emigrate from the locality, leading to an ageing population in rural areas.\n\n(b) Government measure:\n\nInvest in regional infrastructure and balanced regional development: The government could invest in improved broadband, transport links, and public services in rural and smaller urban areas. Decentralisation of government departments and agencies to regional towns would create employment outside Dublin and attract further private sector investment, helping to revitalise local economies."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Oligopoly & Price Rigidity",
     q:"The diagram represents the long-run equilibrium of a firm in Oligopoly.\n\n(i) Write in full the label for each of the lines numbered 1 to 3 on the kinked demand curve diagram.\n\n(ii) Show on the diagram the equilibrium point (E), the price charged (P1), and the quantity produced (Q1) by this firm.\n\n(iii) With reference to the diagram, explain the term price rigidity.\n\n(17 marks)",
     a:"(i) Labels:\n1. Marginal Cost (MC)\n2. Average Revenue / Demand (AR = D) - the upper portion of the kinked demand curve\n3. Marginal Revenue (MR) - note the discontinuity / gap in the MR curve at the kink\n\n(ii) The equilibrium point E is where MC passes through the gap in the MR curve. At this point, the firm produces quantity Q1 and charges price P1 (read from the demand/AR curve above point E).\n\n(iii) Price rigidity: In an oligopoly, firms tend to keep their prices stable (rigid) because of the kinked demand curve. If a firm raises its price above P1, rival firms will not follow, and the firm will lose a large proportion of its customers (demand is elastic above the kink). If a firm lowers its price below P1, rival firms will match the price cut, so the firm gains very few extra customers (demand is inelastic below the kink). Therefore, firms have no incentive to change price from P1, resulting in price rigidity. Additionally, costs can change within the gap in the MR curve without affecting the profit-maximising price or output."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:10, label:"Q1 (Section B): Utility, Alcohol & Railways",
     q:"(a) The table illustrates the Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility:\nUnits Consumed: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nTotal Utility (Utils): 10, 25, 37, 47, 54\nMarginal Utility (Utils): 10, A, B, C, D\n\n(i) Explain the Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility.\n(ii) Calculate the marginal utility at A, B, C and D.\n(iii) State at what point diminishing marginal utility sets in and explain.\n(iv) State and explain two assumptions underlying this law.\n\n(b) The Public Health (Alcohol) Act 2018 prohibits advertising of alcohol in parks, on trains and buses or within 200 metres of a school or playground.\n(i) Explain, with a labelled diagram, the likely impact on the market equilibrium of alcohol in Ireland.\n(ii) Discuss three other possible measures to reduce alcohol consumption.\n\n(c) Iarnrod Eireann has warned that overcrowding on trains may worsen.\n(i) State and explain one reason why demand for train services has increased.\n(ii) Discuss the case for and against increased government investment in Ireland's railways.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) The Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility states that as a consumer consumes successive units of a good, the extra satisfaction (marginal utility) gained from each additional unit will eventually begin to decrease, assuming the consumer's tastes remain constant and consumption is continuous.\n\n(ii) Calculations:\nA = TU of 2nd - TU of 1st = 25 - 10 = 15 utils\nB = TU of 3rd - TU of 2nd = 37 - 25 = 12 utils\nC = TU of 4th - TU of 3rd = 47 - 37 = 10 utils\nD = TU of 5th - TU of 4th = 54 - 47 = 7 utils\n\n(iii) Diminishing marginal utility sets in from the consumption of the 2nd unit onwards. Marginal utility falls from 15 utils (2nd unit) to 12 utils (3rd unit) to 10 utils (4th unit) to 7 utils (5th unit). Note: The first unit has MU of 10, the second has MU of 15 (increasing), so strictly DMU sets in after the 2nd unit (from the 3rd unit), as MU decreases from 15 to 12.\n\n(iv) Assumptions:\n1. Rational consumer: The consumer is assumed to act rationally, seeking to maximise total utility from their consumption.\n2. Continuous consumption: The units of the good are consumed in continuous succession, without significant gaps in time.\n3. Homogeneous units: Each unit of the good consumed is identical in quality, quantity, and size.\n4. Constant tastes: The consumer's preferences do not change during the period of consumption.\n\n(b)(i) The advertising ban will reduce consumers' awareness of and desire for alcohol products. This shifts the demand curve to the left from D1 to D2. The equilibrium price falls from P1 to P2 and the equilibrium quantity decreases from Q1 to Q2. Diagram: Standard supply and demand with demand shifting left.\n\n(ii) Three other measures:\n1. Minimum Unit Pricing (MUP): Setting a minimum price per unit of alcohol prevents very cheap alcohol from being sold, reducing consumption especially among heavy drinkers and young people.\n2. Increased excise duty / taxation on alcohol: Higher taxes increase the price, which reduces quantity demanded. Revenue generated can fund health services to deal with alcohol-related issues.\n3. Restrictions on availability: Reducing the number of off-licences or restricting hours of sale limits access to alcohol. Education campaigns about the health dangers of excessive alcohol consumption can also help change behaviour.\n\n(c)(i) Increased demand for train services: Rising population and employment levels, particularly in Dublin and commuter areas, have led to increased demand for public transport. Additionally, increased traffic congestion on roads and growing environmental awareness have encouraged commuters to switch from private cars to rail.\n\n(ii) Case FOR increased investment:\n- Reduced congestion: Investment in railways reduces road congestion, saving commuters time and reducing costs for businesses.\n- Environmental benefits: Trains produce fewer emissions per passenger than private cars, helping Ireland meet climate targets.\n- Economic growth: Improved rail infrastructure supports balanced regional development and makes areas outside Dublin more accessible for business and tourism.\n- Social inclusion: Public transport provides mobility for those who cannot drive.\n\nCase AGAINST:\n- Opportunity cost: The billions required could be spent on healthcare, housing, or education.\n- Cost overruns: Large infrastructure projects in Ireland have a history of going over budget.\n- Low population density: Ireland's dispersed population makes it difficult to justify investment in rail outside major corridors.\n- Competition from other modes: Investment in roads or bus networks may be more cost-effective."},

    {num:11, label:"Q2 (Section B): Imperfect Competition & Microbreweries",
     q:"Bord Bia has reported that the number of microbreweries in Ireland has increased to 75 in 2018 (a fivefold increase since 2012). Each brewery produces a range of products. This market is an imperfectly competitive market.\n\n(a)(i) Explain one reason why this industry is imperfectly competitive.\n(ii) State and explain three other key characteristics of this market structure.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with a labelled diagram, the equilibrium position of a firm in short-run imperfect competition.\n(ii) With a labelled diagram, explain the impact which the entry of new firms would have on the equilibrium.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term non-price competition.\n(ii) Outline two examples of effective non-price competitive measures used by firms and why they are effective.\n(iii) Is non-price competition beneficial to consumers? Outline two reasons.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Product differentiation: Each microbrewery produces a range of differentiated products (lagers, stouts, beers) with distinct branding, flavours, and recipes. This means products are not homogeneous, which is a defining feature of imperfect competition.\n\n(ii) Three other characteristics:\n1. Many firms in the industry: There are 75 microbreweries, representing a large number of sellers. Entry of new firms is relatively easy due to low barriers to entry.\n2. Some degree of price-making power: Because products are differentiated, each firm has some control over the price it charges. Consumers may pay more for a preferred brand.\n3. Downward-sloping demand curve: Each firm faces its own downward-sloping demand curve because its product is slightly different from competitors. This distinguishes it from perfect competition where the demand curve is perfectly elastic.\n4. Freedom of entry and exit: New firms can enter the market relatively easily, as demonstrated by the fivefold increase since 2012.\n\n(b)(i) Short-run equilibrium diagram:\nThe firm produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising output Q1). Price (P1) is read from the AR (demand) curve above this point. In the short run, the firm earns supernormal profit because AR > AC at Q1. The supernormal profit is shown as the shaded area between AR and AC multiplied by Q1.\nDiagram: U-shaped AC curve, MC curve cutting through it, downward-sloping AR curve above AC at Q1, MR curve below AR.\n\n(ii) Impact of new firm entry:\nAttracted by supernormal profits, new firms enter the industry. This takes customers away from existing firms, shifting each firm's demand (AR) curve to the left and making it more elastic. The firm's new equilibrium has a lower price and lower output. In the long run, entry continues until the AR curve is tangent to the AC curve, and the firm earns only normal profit (AR = AC at the equilibrium output).\n\n(c)(i) Non-price competition: This is where firms compete using methods other than price to attract customers and increase market share. Instead of lowering prices, firms differentiate their products through branding, advertising, quality, customer service, or other strategies.\n\n(ii) Two examples:\n1. Branding and advertising: Microbreweries invest in distinctive branding, labels, and social media marketing. This is effective because it creates brand loyalty and allows firms to charge a premium price.\n2. Product quality and innovation: Developing new flavours, seasonal specials, or using premium ingredients. This is effective because it differentiates the product from competitors and attracts customers seeking variety.\n\n(iii) Is non-price competition beneficial to consumers?\nYes:\n1. Greater product variety and choice: Consumers benefit from a wider range of products as firms innovate and differentiate, leading to more options to suit individual tastes.\n2. Improved quality: Competition drives firms to improve quality and customer service to retain customers.\n\nHowever, it can also be argued that non-price competition may not always benefit consumers, as advertising costs are passed on in higher prices, and consumers may be manipulated by marketing."},

    {num:12, label:"Q3 (Section B): Employment, MNCs & Gender Pay Inequality",
     q:"(a) 'We are almost back to Celtic Tiger levels of employment.' (Source: Irish Independent, June 2019)\n(i) Define the term full employment.\n(ii) Discuss the possible economic benefits and challenges of full employment in the Irish economy.\n\n(b) Ireland is an attractive location for many multinational companies (MNCs). Discuss four possible economic risks for the Irish economy if some of the MNCs currently in Ireland choose to re-locate outside Ireland.\n\n(c) Women aged 25-64 with a third level degree in Ireland earn 28% less than their male counterparts. (Source: OECD)\n(i) Outline two reasons why gender pay inequality continues to exist.\n(ii) Discuss two measures the Irish government could take to help reduce gender pay inequality.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Full employment is a situation where everyone in the labour force who is willing and able to work at the existing wage rate is employed. It does not mean zero unemployment, as some frictional and structural unemployment will always exist. In Ireland, full employment is generally considered to occur when unemployment is between 4-5%.\n\n(ii) Benefits of full employment:\n- Increased tax revenue: More people working means more income tax (PAYE), USC, and VAT revenue for the government.\n- Reduced social welfare expenditure: Fewer unemployment payments needed, freeing up government funds for other purposes.\n- Higher standard of living: More people earning incomes means greater consumer spending and improved living standards.\n- Economic growth: Higher employment leads to increased output and GDP growth.\n\nChallenges of full employment:\n- Demand-pull inflation: With full employment, increased consumer spending can lead to excess demand, pushing prices up.\n- Wage inflation: Labour shortages mean firms must offer higher wages to attract workers, increasing costs of production.\n- Skill shortages: Certain sectors may struggle to find workers with the required skills.\n- Loss of competitiveness: Rising wages and prices may make Irish goods and services less competitive internationally.\n\n(b) Four economic risks if MNCs re-locate:\n\n1. Increased unemployment: MNCs employ hundreds of thousands of workers in Ireland. Their departure would lead to significant direct job losses and indirect job losses in supporting industries (multiplier effect in reverse).\n\n2. Loss of tax revenue: Corporation tax from MNCs is a significant source of government revenue. Ireland would also lose income tax from former employees and VAT from reduced consumer spending.\n\n3. Reduced exports: MNCs account for a large proportion of Ireland's exports. Their departure would significantly reduce export volumes, worsening the balance of payments.\n\n4. Decline in economic growth / reduced FDI attractiveness: The departure of MNCs could signal to other potential investors that Ireland is no longer an attractive location, leading to a decline in future FDI and a negative multiplier effect on the economy.\n\n(c)(i) Two reasons for gender pay inequality:\n1. Career breaks: Women are more likely to take career breaks for childcare / maternity leave, which interrupts career progression and reduces lifetime earnings.\n2. Occupational segregation: Women are often concentrated in lower-paid sectors (education, healthcare, caring) while men dominate higher-paid sectors (finance, technology, engineering).\n\n(ii) Two government measures:\n1. Mandatory gender pay gap reporting: Requiring companies to publish their gender pay data would increase transparency and put pressure on firms to address pay disparities.\n2. Investment in affordable childcare: Providing subsidised childcare would enable more women to remain in or return to full-time employment, reducing career interruptions and improving their earning potential."},

    {num:13, label:"Q4 (Section B): Costs of Production & Competition",
     q:"The cost of doing business in Ireland can be described as 'high cost, slowly increasing'. (Source: Cost of Doing Business in Ireland, April 2019)\n\n(a)(i) In the short-run, firms may stay in the industry even if they are making a loss. Explain this statement using a diagram.\n(ii) Outline three costs, other than labour costs, which a firm may experience.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with the aid of a labelled diagram, how price and output are determined by a firm in perfect competition in the short run.\n(ii) Outline two characteristics of perfect competition other than those evident from your diagram.\n(iii) Perfect competition may not be beneficial for consumers. Outline two reasons.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term deregulation.\n(ii) Discuss two advantages and two disadvantages of increased competition following deregulation.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) A firm making a loss in the short run will continue to produce as long as its Average Revenue (AR) is greater than its Average Variable Cost (AVC). At this point, the firm is covering all its variable costs and making a contribution towards its fixed costs. If the firm shut down, it would still have to pay its fixed costs, so the loss from shutting down would be greater than the loss from continuing to operate.\nDiagram: MC, ATC, and AVC curves. Price (AR=MR) is set between ATC and AVC. The firm produces where MC = MR. Loss is shown as the area between ATC and AR multiplied by output.\n\n(ii) Three costs other than labour:\n1. Rent / property costs: The cost of premises for offices, factories, or retail outlets.\n2. Raw materials / inputs: The cost of materials needed in the production process.\n3. Insurance: Costs of insuring premises, stock, and employees.\n4. Energy / utilities: Electricity, heating, and water costs.\n\n(b)(i) In perfect competition, the firm is a price-taker. The market determines the price through the interaction of market supply and demand. The individual firm faces a perfectly elastic (horizontal) demand curve at the market price (D = AR = MR). The firm maximises profit by producing where MC = MR. If AR > AC at this output, the firm earns supernormal profit.\nDiagram: Market diagram (S and D curves determining Pe) alongside firm diagram showing horizontal AR=MR line at Pe, with U-shaped AC and MC curves.\n\n(ii) Two other characteristics:\n1. Homogeneous products: All firms sell identical products, so consumers have no preference between sellers.\n2. Perfect knowledge: All buyers and sellers have complete information about prices, products, and market conditions.\n\n(iii) Two reasons perfect competition may not benefit consumers:\n1. No product variety: Because products are homogeneous, consumers have no choice in terms of differentiation, branding, or features.\n2. No economies of scale: The large number of small firms cannot achieve the cost savings that larger firms in other market structures might pass on to consumers.\n\n(c)(i) Deregulation is the removal or reduction of government rules, regulations, and controls on an industry, allowing more firms to enter and compete in the market. It is often used to transform monopolies or heavily regulated markets into more competitive ones.\n\n(ii) Advantages of increased competition:\n1. Lower prices: Competition forces firms to reduce prices to attract customers, benefiting consumers.\n2. Improved quality and innovation: Firms must improve their products and services to survive in a competitive market.\n\nDisadvantages:\n1. Job losses: Increased competition may force some firms out of business, leading to unemployment. Restructuring and cost-cutting often involve redundancies.\n2. Reduced service in unprofitable areas: Private firms seeking profit may withdraw services from rural or less profitable areas that were previously served by the regulated monopoly (e.g. public transport routes, post offices in rural areas)."},

    {num:14, label:"Q5 (Section B): Enterprise, Labour & Elasticity",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the factor of production enterprise.\n(ii) State and explain four characteristics of enterprise.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with a labelled diagram, how the wage rate for labour is determined in a perfectly competitive labour market.\n(ii) Outline the effect on the equilibrium wage rate if the supply of labour decreases.\n(iii) Explain two factors, other than the wage rate, that affect the supply of labour.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the concept of price elasticity of supply.\n(ii) Outline two factors that affect price elasticity of supply.\n(iii) Explain the difference between elastic and inelastic supply.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Enterprise is the factor of production that involves organising the other three factors of production (land, labour, and capital) in order to produce goods and services. The entrepreneur takes the initiative to set up a business, bears the risk of financial loss, and makes key decisions about what, how, and for whom to produce.\n\n(ii) Four characteristics of enterprise:\n1. Risk-bearing: The entrepreneur bears the risk of financial loss. If the business fails, the entrepreneur may lose their investment.\n2. Decision-making: The entrepreneur makes key decisions about production methods, pricing, marketing, and resource allocation.\n3. Innovation: Entrepreneurs introduce new products, processes, and ideas to the market, driving economic progress.\n4. Organisation: The entrepreneur organises and combines the other factors of production (land, labour, capital) in the most efficient way possible to maximise output and profit.\n\n(b)(i) In a perfectly competitive labour market, the wage rate is determined by the interaction of the supply of and demand for labour. The supply curve of labour slopes upward (higher wages attract more workers), and the demand curve slopes downward (firms demand less labour at higher wages). The equilibrium wage rate (We) is where supply equals demand, and the equilibrium quantity of labour is Qe.\nDiagram: Standard supply and demand with Wage Rate on Y-axis and Quantity of Labour on X-axis.\n\n(ii) If the supply of labour decreases, the supply curve shifts to the left from S to S1. The equilibrium wage rate increases from We to We1, and the equilibrium quantity of labour decreases from Qe to Qe1. Labour becomes scarcer, so employers must offer higher wages to attract workers.\n\n(iii) Two factors affecting the supply of labour:\n1. Education and training: Better education and training increases the supply of skilled labour. Government investment in education programmes increases the number of qualified workers available.\n2. Immigration / emigration: Inward migration increases the supply of labour, while emigration decreases it. Ireland's membership of the EU allows free movement of workers from other EU countries.\n\n(c)(i) Price elasticity of supply (PES) measures the responsiveness of the quantity supplied of a good to a change in its price. It is calculated as: % change in quantity supplied / % change in price.\n\n(ii) Two factors affecting PES:\n1. Time period: In the short run, supply is more inelastic as firms cannot easily increase capacity. In the long run, firms can expand production, making supply more elastic.\n2. Availability of stocks / spare capacity: If a firm holds stocks of finished goods or has spare capacity, it can respond quickly to price increases, making supply more elastic.\n\n(iii) Elastic supply: A small change in price leads to a proportionally larger change in quantity supplied (PES > 1). Inelastic supply: A change in price leads to a proportionally smaller change in quantity supplied (PES < 1). For example, agricultural products tend to have inelastic supply in the short run because it takes time to grow crops, while manufactured goods may have more elastic supply."},

    {num:15, label:"Q6 (Section B): International Trade",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain two economic benefits of free trade for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Outline the term protectionism and give one example.\n(iii) Discuss three reasons why a country might use protectionist measures.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with a labelled diagram, how equilibrium is determined in the foreign exchange market for the euro.\n(ii) Outline three factors that could influence the exchange rate of the euro.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term comparative advantage.\n(ii) Explain the term absolute advantage.\n(iii) Using an example, distinguish between comparative and absolute advantage.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Two economic benefits of free trade for Ireland:\n1. Access to larger markets: Free trade allows Irish firms to sell their goods and services to consumers in other countries without tariffs or quotas, increasing sales, revenue, and profits. Ireland's membership of the EU Single Market gives access to over 440 million consumers.\n2. Lower prices and greater consumer choice: Free trade allows consumers to buy imported goods at lower prices and provides access to a wider variety of products that may not be produced domestically.\n\n(ii) Protectionism is the use of government policies to restrict or limit imports into a country in order to protect domestic industries from foreign competition. Example: Tariffs (taxes on imports) that make foreign goods more expensive, giving domestic producers a price advantage.\n\n(iii) Three reasons for protectionist measures:\n1. Protect infant industries: New domestic industries may need temporary protection from established foreign competitors until they achieve economies of scale and become competitive.\n2. Protect employment: Restricting imports protects domestic jobs in industries that might otherwise be unable to compete with cheaper foreign goods.\n3. National security: Certain strategic industries (e.g. defence, food production) may be protected to ensure the country is not dependent on foreign suppliers for essential goods.\n\n(b)(i) The exchange rate of the euro is determined by the supply of and demand for the euro on foreign exchange markets. Demand for the euro comes from foreigners wanting to buy European goods/services or invest in the eurozone. Supply of the euro comes from Europeans wanting to buy foreign goods/services or invest abroad. The equilibrium exchange rate is where demand equals supply.\nDiagram: Exchange Rate (price of euro in foreign currency) on Y-axis, Quantity of Euro on X-axis. Downward-sloping demand curve, upward-sloping supply curve intersecting at equilibrium.\n\n(ii) Three factors influencing the euro exchange rate:\n1. Interest rates: If ECB interest rates are higher than those in other economies, demand for the euro increases as investors seek higher returns, causing the euro to appreciate.\n2. Trade balance: A trade surplus (exports > imports) increases demand for the euro as foreign buyers need euro to pay for European exports.\n3. Inflation rates: Lower inflation in the eurozone relative to other areas makes European goods more competitive, increasing demand for exports and the euro.\n\n(c)(i) Comparative advantage: A country has a comparative advantage in producing a good if it can produce that good at a lower opportunity cost than another country, even if it is not the most efficient producer in absolute terms.\n\n(ii) Absolute advantage: A country has an absolute advantage in producing a good if it can produce more of that good with the same resources, or produce the same amount with fewer resources, than another country.\n\n(iii) Example: Ireland may have an absolute advantage over Portugal in producing both cheese and wine. However, if Ireland is relatively better at producing cheese and Portugal is relatively better at producing wine (lower opportunity cost), then Ireland has a comparative advantage in cheese and Portugal in wine. Both countries benefit by specialising in the good where they have comparative advantage and trading."},

    {num:16, label:"Q7 (Section B): Government in the Economy",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain what is meant by a budget deficit.\n(ii) Discuss three economic consequences of a government running a persistent budget deficit.\n\n(b)(i) Distinguish between current government expenditure and capital government expenditure. Give one example of each.\n(ii) Explain how government expenditure can have a multiplier effect on the economy.\n(iii) Outline two limitations of the multiplier.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the economic term national debt.\n(ii) Outline three economic consequences of a high national debt for Ireland.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) A budget deficit occurs when total government expenditure exceeds total government revenue (taxation and other income) in a given year. The government must borrow to cover the shortfall.\n\n(ii) Three economic consequences of a persistent budget deficit:\n1. Increasing national debt: Persistent deficits add to the national debt, which is the cumulative total of all past government borrowing. This must eventually be repaid with interest.\n2. Increased debt servicing costs: As the national debt grows, the government must spend more on interest payments, reducing funds available for public services and investment.\n3. Higher taxes in the future: To reduce the deficit and repay debt, the government may need to increase taxation, which can reduce disposable income and economic growth.\n\n(b)(i) Current expenditure: Day-to-day spending on the provision of public services that are consumed within the current year. Example: Social welfare payments, public sector wages, health service running costs.\nCapital expenditure: Spending on long-term assets that will provide benefits over many years. Example: Building new roads, hospitals, schools, or investing in broadband infrastructure.\n\n(ii) The multiplier effect: When the government spends money (e.g. building a road), the workers and suppliers who receive this income will spend a proportion of it on goods and services. The recipients of this spending will in turn spend a proportion, and so on. The initial government injection thus creates a larger overall increase in national income. The size of the multiplier depends on the marginal propensity to consume and the level of leakages (savings, taxation, imports).\n\n(iii) Two limitations of the multiplier:\n1. Open economy leakages: In an open economy like Ireland, a significant proportion of additional income is spent on imports, which are a leakage from the circular flow, reducing the multiplier effect.\n2. Time lags: The full multiplier effect takes time to work through the economy, and the actual impact may be less than predicted due to changing economic conditions.\n\n(c)(i) The national debt is the total accumulated amount of money that the government owes to domestic and foreign lenders. It is the sum of all past budget deficits minus any surpluses.\n\n(ii) Three economic consequences of a high national debt:\n1. Opportunity cost: Large interest payments on the debt reduce the amount of government revenue available for public services such as healthcare, education, and infrastructure.\n2. Higher taxation: The government may need to increase taxes to service the debt, reducing disposable income, consumer spending, and economic growth.\n3. Loss of economic sovereignty: A high national debt may make Ireland dependent on international lenders and subject to conditions imposed by organisations such as the EU or IMF (as occurred during the 2010 bailout). Ireland's credit rating may also be downgraded, increasing borrowing costs."},

    {num:17, label:"Q8 (Section B): National Income & National Debt",
     q:"(a) Explain the following national income terms:\n(i) Gross Domestic Product (GDP)\n(ii) Gross National Product (GNP)\n(iii) Explain why GDP is not always an accurate measure of the standard of living in Ireland.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the concept of Modified GNI (GNI*).\n(ii) Outline two reasons why the CSO developed the GNI* measure.\n(iii) Calculate GNP from given data.\n\n(c)(i) Define the economic term transfer payments.\n(ii) Explain why transfer payments are excluded from national income calculations.\n(iii) Outline two uses of national income statistics.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) GDP (Gross Domestic Product): The total value of all final goods and services produced within the borders of a country in a given year, regardless of who owns the factors of production. It includes output by foreign-owned firms operating in Ireland.\n\n(ii) GNP (Gross National Product): The total value of all final goods and services produced by the citizens/residents of a country, regardless of where the production takes place. GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA). For Ireland, GNP is significantly lower than GDP because many multinational corporations repatriate profits abroad, resulting in a large negative NFIA.\n\n(iii) GDP is not always accurate for Ireland because:\n- Multinational distortion: Large MNCs operating in Ireland inflate GDP figures through their output, but much of the profit is repatriated to their home countries and does not benefit Irish residents.\n- GDP does not account for income distribution: A high GDP per capita may mask significant inequality in the distribution of income.\n- GDP does not measure quality of life: It ignores factors like environmental quality, leisure time, and social well-being.\n\n(b)(i) Modified GNI (GNI*) is a measure developed by the CSO (Central Statistics Office) that adjusts GNI by removing the effects of globalisation-related activities of multinational enterprises. It strips out the impact of depreciation on foreign-owned intellectual property, the income of re-domiciled companies, and aircraft leasing.\n\n(ii) Two reasons for developing GNI*:\n1. To provide a more accurate picture of the Irish economy: Standard GDP and GNP figures are distorted by multinational activities (e.g. the 'leprechaun economics' episode of 2015 when GDP grew by 26% in one year due to MNC balance sheet restructuring).\n2. To give a better basis for policy decisions: GNI* provides a more realistic measure of the domestic economy, helping government make better-informed decisions about taxation, spending, and borrowing.\n\n(iii) GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad.\nIf GDP = total domestic output, and NFIA = income earned abroad by Irish residents minus income earned in Ireland by foreign residents (typically negative for Ireland), then GNP = GDP + NFIA.\n\n(c)(i) Transfer payments are payments made by the government to individuals for which no goods or services are provided in return. They represent a redistribution of income rather than payment for current production. Examples include social welfare payments, state pensions, and children's allowance.\n\n(ii) Transfer payments are excluded from national income calculations because they do not represent payment for the production of goods or services. Including them would result in double-counting, as the income that funds transfer payments has already been counted when it was earned by taxpayers.\n\n(iii) Two uses of national income statistics:\n1. Comparing living standards: National income per capita allows comparison of living standards between countries and over time within a country.\n2. Government economic planning: National income data helps government assess the performance of the economy, plan budgets, set taxation levels, and formulate economic policy."}
  ],

  "2021": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (75 marks) =====
    // NOTE: 2021 was a modified COVID-era exam: 300 marks total, Section A = 75 marks (5 out of 10), Section B = 225 marks (3 out of 6)
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Balance of Trade",
     q:"(a) Calculate the balance of trade for the period 2015-2019. Identify whether it is a surplus or a deficit.\nData: Exports (in millions): 2015: 112, 2016: 119, 2017: 123, 2018: 141, 2019: 152\nImports (in millions): 2015: 70, 2016: 74, 2017: 83, 2018: 92, 2019: 91\n\n(b) Outline two reasons why achieving a trade surplus is an objective of the Irish government.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Workings:\nTotal Exports: 112 + 119 + 123 + 141 + 152 = €647m\nTotal Imports: 70 + 74 + 83 + 92 + 91 = €410m\nBalance of Trade: €647m - €410m = €237m SURPLUS\n\n(b) Reasons why a trade surplus is an objective:\n\n1. Increased employment: If there is an increase in the demand for Irish exports abroad, this will lead to an increase in demand for Irish labour to produce the goods domestically, increasing Irish employment.\n\n2. Injection into the Circular Flow / Economic Growth: Irish exports represent an injection into the circular flow of income. Injections create a multiplier effect, creating a more than proportional increase in Irish national income, generating further employment and improving the standard of living. Conversely, imports are a leakage that can reduce national income."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Normative vs Positive Statements",
     q:"Select whether each of the following statements is a normative statement or a positive statement:\n\n(a) 'If the Irish government increase jobseeker's allowance by 5% in the 2022 budget, unemployment in Ireland will increase by at least 2% by 2023.'\n(b) 'We have to do more for the less well off in society.'\n(c) 'The government should have increased unemployment benefit by more than €0.10 this year.'\n(d) 'Sales of new cars in Ireland decreased during 2020.'\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Positive statement. This is a statement that can be tested and verified or falsified using data and evidence. It makes a factual prediction about the relationship between jobseeker's allowance and unemployment.\n\n(b) Normative statement. This is a value judgement / opinion about what ought to be done. It cannot be proven true or false as it expresses a belief about what society 'should' do.\n\n(c) Normative statement. The word 'should' indicates this is a value judgement about what the government ought to have done, reflecting an opinion rather than a verifiable fact.\n\n(d) Positive statement. This is a factual claim that can be verified or falsified using actual sales data from 2020.\n\nKey distinction: Positive statements are objective and can be tested against evidence. Normative statements are subjective, involving value judgements about what 'should' or 'ought to' happen."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Price Discrimination (Netflix)",
     q:"The table shows the subscription price for a basic Netflix account in different countries (converted to US dollars): Switzerland $14, Ireland $12, Australia $9, Denmark $11, Rwanda $8.\n\n(a) Outline one reason why consumers are willing to pay different prices for a Netflix subscription in different countries.\n\n(b) Other than your answer in part (a), explain two other conditions necessary for price discrimination to occur in this scenario.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Consumer attitudes: Consumers feel that the product is worth paying more for rather than going without it. In wealthier countries like Switzerland, consumers have higher incomes and are willing to pay more for the Netflix service. Consumers may also be ignorant of the fact that different prices are being charged in different countries, or indifferent to the price difference as they value the convenience.\n\n(b) Two other conditions for price discrimination:\n\n1. Monopoly power: Netflix must have some degree of monopoly power in order to price discriminate, otherwise rival firms (e.g. Disney+, Amazon Prime) would undercut prices and the price discrimination would end. Consumers must view these rivals as imperfect substitutes for Netflix.\n\n2. Market separation (no arbitrage): It must be impossible for customers to buy the product in the cheaper market and then resell it in the more expensive market, undercutting Netflix. Since Netflix subscriptions are tied to accounts and geographic regions (using geo-blocking), customers in Switzerland cannot simply buy a Rwandan subscription at $8."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Demerit Goods & Market Failure (Cigarettes)",
     q:"In Budget 2021, the price of a 20-pack of cigarettes rose from €13.50 to €14.00. Cigarettes are classed as 'Demerit Goods' and as such their purchase constitutes a market failure.\n\n(a) Explain your understanding of the economic term Demerit Goods.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Explain why the purchase of demerit goods, such as cigarettes, is seen as a market failure.\nOR\n(c) Explain why the Irish Government intervenes by imposing higher taxes on cigarettes in budgets.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Demerit goods are goods whose positive effect is overestimated by consumers and which have negative effects that consumers possibly underestimate or are unaware of. Demerit goods usually also have negative externalities, i.e., they have negative effects on third parties not involved in the consumption of the good. Examples include cigarettes, alcohol, and gambling.\n\n(b) Market failure explanation: The purchase of demerit goods is seen as a market failure because the free market will lead to the overproduction and overconsumption of a harmful good. The market price fails to reflect the true private or social cost of the product. If the free market were to take full account of the private and social costs of cigarettes (healthcare costs, passive smoking, reduced productivity), the price would be much higher than the current market price.\n\n(c) Why the government imposes higher taxes: The government intervenes to increase the price to deter consumption of a good which is harmful for consumers and harmful for society through negative externalities. Higher prices reduce quantity demanded. The tax revenue earned can help fund the cost of dealing with the negative externalities, e.g. overcrowding of healthcare services. Without intervention, the free market generates a price too low to reflect the true private and social cost, leading to overconsumption."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Central Bank Regulation (KBC)",
     q:"KBC Bank Ireland was fined €18.3 million by the Central Bank of Ireland for its role in the tracker mortgage scandal. (Adapted from The Irish Times)\n\n(a) Based on the above statement, outline why it is important for consumers that Irish banks are properly regulated by the Central Bank of Ireland.\n\n(b) Other than the regulation of commercial banks, outline two other duties of the Central Bank of Ireland.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Protection of consumers of financial services: If commercial banks such as KBC are not properly regulated, it may result in them failing to operate within recommended guidelines, which may result in the exploitation of consumers. The tracker mortgage scandal showed that without proper regulation, banks can overcharge customers and cause significant financial harm. The Central Bank works to develop a positive consumer-focused culture within regulated firms, ensuring the consumer protection framework remains effective so that financial institutions comply with required standards.\n\n(b) Two other duties of the Central Bank of Ireland:\n\n1. Eurosystem effectiveness and price stability: The Central Bank is responsible for contributing to the formulation of Eurosystem monetary policy and helping to ensure that the Euro area inflation objective of close to 2% over the medium term is achieved.\n\n2. Stability of the financial system: The Central Bank has an explicit mandate in domestic and European legislation to contribute to financial stability in Ireland and at EU levels. It does so by enhancing the macro-prudential policy framework and developing indicators to assess systemic risk. This includes ensuring payment, settlement, and currency systems are safe and efficient."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: HDI vs GDP",
     q:"(a) Explain why the Human Development Index (HDI) may provide a more accurate profile of human welfare in a country than the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) figure of that country.\n\nData from HDI 2020:\nChad: HDI 0.398, Life expectancy 54.2 years, Mean years of schooling 2.5, GNI per capita $1,555\nCentral African Republic: HDI 0.397, Life expectancy 53.3 years, Mean years of schooling 4.3, GNI per capita $993\nNiger: HDI 0.394, Life expectancy 62.4 years, Mean years of schooling 2.1, GNI per capita $1,201\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Using the data, advise the governments of these countries on one policy they could use to improve their HDI ranking.\nOR\n(c) Advise the Irish government on how it could assist these countries improve their HDI ranking.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) The HDI is a more accurate indicator of human welfare because it is a composite indicator which reflects the income per person, education, and life expectancy of inhabitants of the country, all of which impact on human welfare. GDP alone only reflects the level of economic activity within the country and does not consider how income is distributed, education levels, or health outcomes. A country could have a high GDP but poor healthcare and education, resulting in low human welfare.\n\n(b) Policy to improve HDI ranking:\nImprove education in the country: If the governments invest in primary, secondary, and tertiary education systems, it will help lift people out of poverty, improve employment prospects, and increase the productive capacity of the economy. For example, Niger has only 2.1 mean years of schooling - investing in education would directly improve this component of the HDI.\n\nAlternatively, improving the national healthcare system would increase life expectancy (particularly relevant for Chad at 54.2 years and Central African Republic at 53.3 years), directly improving the health component of the HDI.\n\n(c) How Ireland could assist:\n1. Direct financial aid (official development assistance): The Irish government can provide grants and loans to help with emergency situations and assist with the development of infrastructure, education, and health programmes.\n2. Assist with skills and technologies: Ireland could provide skilled workers and technologies to these countries to improve living standards and boost productive capacity.\n3. Help restructure national debts: Lobbying through the EU and UN for debt cancellation or restructuring would free up funds for economic development.\n4. Help improve terms of trade: Lobbying for improved access to developed world markets and more favourable terms of trade so these countries can increase export revenues."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Government Debt & Budget Surplus",
     q:"The diagram shows Government Finance Statistics for 2019.\n\n(a) Compare the level of Gross Debt per Person from 2007 to 2019 and outline one possible reason for this increase in the gross debt per person.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Explain the economic disadvantages for a government in achieving a budget surplus.\nOR\n(c) Servicing the national debt creates opportunity costs. Explain how this occurs.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Comparison: Between 2007 and 2019, the gross debt per person rose significantly (by approximately €30,322 per person or 272%). In 2007, before the financial crisis, gross debt per person was relatively low. By 2019, it had increased dramatically.\n\nReason for increase: The global financial crisis of 2008-2010 forced the Irish government to borrow heavily to bail out the banking system and fund increased social welfare payments as unemployment soared. The government ran large budget deficits during this period, adding significantly to the national debt. The EU/IMF bailout programme in 2010 further increased government borrowing.\n\n(b) Disadvantages of a budget surplus:\n- Reduced government spending on public services: A surplus means the government is collecting more in taxes than it is spending. This may mean underfunding of essential services like healthcare, education, and infrastructure.\n- Contractionary effect on the economy: By withdrawing more from the economy through taxes than it injects through spending, a surplus can slow economic growth and reduce employment.\n- Political pressure: Citizens may demand tax cuts or increased spending rather than the government running a surplus.\n\n(c) Opportunity cost of servicing the national debt: When the government must allocate a significant portion of its annual revenue to paying interest on the national debt, this money cannot be used for other purposes. For example, billions spent on debt interest could instead be invested in building hospitals, schools, or housing. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative use of these funds that must be forgone. Every euro spent on interest payments is a euro that cannot be spent on improving public services or reducing taxation."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Equi-Marginal Principle",
     q:"John always spends his income in the following ratio of price to marginal utility:\nMUx/Px = 6500/600 = MUy/Py = 1650/100 = MUz/Pz = 75/?\n\n(a) Calculate the price John would be willing to pay for one unit of good Z.\n\n(b) Explain why John would pay this price.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Calculation:\nMUx/Px = 6500/600 = 10.833\nMUy/Py = 1650/100 = 16.5\n\nNote: For the equi-marginal principle to hold, all ratios must be equal.\nUsing MUy/Py = 16.5 as the reference:\nMUz/Pz = 75/Pz = 16.5\nPz = 75/16.5 = €4.55 (approximately)\n\nAlternatively, if using MUx/Px = 10.833:\nPz = 75/10.833 = €6.92 (approximately)\n\nThe price John would be willing to pay depends on which ratio is the equilibrium ratio.\n\n(b) Explanation: John would pay this price because of the Equi-Marginal Principle of Consumer Behaviour. This principle states that a rational consumer maximises total utility when the marginal utility per euro spent on each good is equal across all goods: MUx/Px = MUy/Py = MUz/Pz. At this point, the consumer cannot increase total satisfaction by reallocating spending between goods. If the ratio for good Z were different, John could increase his total utility by spending more or less on good Z relative to goods X and Y."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Market Structures Identification",
     q:"(a) Identify the corresponding market structure for each of these products/firms:\nA. Irish banking sector\nB. Irish Water (the company)\nC. A takeaway food outlet\n\n(b) Give one reason for each of your chosen market structures above.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Market structures:\nA. Irish banking sector = Oligopoly\nB. Irish Water (the company) = Monopoly\nC. A takeaway food outlet = Imperfect (Monopolistic) Competition\n\n(b) Reasons:\n\nA. Oligopoly: The Irish banking sector is dominated by a small number of large firms (AIB, Bank of Ireland, PTSB, etc.) that control the vast majority of the market. There are significant barriers to entry (regulation, capital requirements), and firms are interdependent in their pricing decisions.\n\nB. Monopoly: Irish Water is the sole provider of public water services in Ireland. It has no direct competitors for the supply of treated water and wastewater services. It is a state-owned monopoly established by legislation.\n\nC. Imperfect (Monopolistic) Competition: There are many takeaway food outlets in Ireland, each offering slightly differentiated products (different cuisines, recipes, branding). There are low barriers to entry and exit. Each outlet has some control over its price due to product differentiation, but competition is strong."},

    {num:10, label:"Q10: ECB Interest Rate Trend",
     q:"The graph shows the fluctuation in the European Central Bank Fixed Rate as of May 2020, declining from around 3.75% in 2008 to 0% by 2016.\n\n(a) Explain the historical reason for this trend in the European Central Bank's interest rate.\n\n(b) Outline whether you would advise the European Central Bank to increase, decrease, or maintain the current interest rate of 0%. Justify your choice.\n\n(15 marks)",
     a:"(a) Historical reason: The ECB progressively reduced its base interest rate from approximately 3.75% in 2008 to 0% by 2016 in response to the global financial crisis and the subsequent European sovereign debt crisis. The ECB lowered rates to encourage borrowing and spending, stimulate investment by businesses, and discourage saving. The aim was to boost economic growth and prevent deflation in the eurozone economy. Lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing for consumers and businesses, which should increase aggregate demand and support economic recovery.\n\n(b) Advice: Maintain the current interest rate of 0%.\n\nJustification: In May 2020, the eurozone economy was experiencing severe economic contraction due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Businesses were closing, unemployment was rising sharply, and consumer confidence was extremely low. Maintaining the 0% rate ensures that borrowing remains as cheap as possible to support businesses and consumers during the crisis. Increasing rates would further dampen economic activity at the worst possible time. Decreasing below 0% (negative rates) could undermine confidence in the banking system and discourage saving. The priority should be to support economic recovery through accommodative monetary policy."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (225 marks) =====
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Oligopoly, HHI & Economies of Scale (Aircraft Market)",
     q:"The pie chart shows the market share of the Global Aircraft Market in 2018: Boeing 46%, Airbus 46%, Bombardier 1%, Embraer 5%, Others 2%.\n\n(a)(i) Calculate the Herfindahl Hirschman Index (HHI) for the Global Aircraft Market in 2018.\n(ii) Would you consider this market to be competitive, moderately concentrated, or highly concentrated? Explain.\n\n(b) The diagram represents the long-run equilibrium of a firm in Oligopoly at point E producing at Q1 and selling at P1.\n(i) Write in full the label for each of the lines numbered 1 to 4.\n(ii) Would you advise the above firm to engage in price competition? Give two reasons.\n(iii) Outline two reasons why small firms such as Embraer and Bombardier may survive in this market.\n\n(c) Boeing and Embraer entered potential merger talks in 2019.\n(i) Define Internal Economies of Scale.\n(ii) Outline possible Internal Economies of Scale which could arise for Boeing and Embraer if this merger were to occur.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) HHI Calculation:\nHHI = 46² + 46² + 1² + 5² + 2²\n= 2,116 + 2,116 + 1 + 25 + 4\n= 4,262\n\n(ii) This market is HIGHLY CONCENTRATED. An HHI above 2,500 indicates a highly concentrated market. With an HHI of 4,262, this is a duopoly in practice, as Boeing and Airbus each hold 46% of the market, together controlling 92% of global aircraft production. The remaining firms hold only 8% combined.\n\n(b)(i) Labels for the kinked demand curve diagram:\n1. Marginal Cost (MC)\n2. Average Cost (AC)\n3. Average Revenue / Demand (AR = D) - the kinked demand curve\n4. Marginal Revenue (MR) - with its characteristic discontinuity/gap at the kink\n\n(ii) No, I would not advise the firm to engage in price competition.\nReason 1: If the firm reduces its price, rival firms will match the price cut, resulting in a price war where all firms lose revenue with minimal gain in market share. Demand is inelastic below the kink.\nReason 2: If the firm increases its price, rivals will not follow, and the firm will lose a significant number of customers to competitors. Demand is elastic above the kink. Therefore, it is better to maintain price rigidity and compete through non-price methods.\n\n(iii) Two reasons small firms may survive:\n1. Niche market / product differentiation: Embraer and Bombardier specialise in smaller regional aircraft, a different market segment from the large commercial aircraft produced by Boeing and Airbus. They face less direct competition in this niche.\n2. Government support / subsidies: These firms may receive financial support from their national governments (Brazil and Canada respectively) to maintain domestic aerospace industries for strategic and employment reasons.\n\n(c)(i) Internal Economies of Scale are the cost advantages that a firm experiences as it increases its scale of production. They arise from within the firm as it grows larger, leading to a fall in average costs of production.\n\n(ii) Possible Internal Economies of Scale from a Boeing-Embraer merger:\n1. Technical economies: The merged firm could invest in more advanced production technology and machinery, spreading the high fixed cost over a larger output, reducing average costs per aircraft.\n2. Purchasing/bulk-buying economies: A larger firm can negotiate lower prices for raw materials and components (engines, avionics) from suppliers due to the increased volume of orders.\n3. Financial economies: A larger merged firm would have greater access to finance and could borrow at lower interest rates due to reduced risk for lenders.\n4. Marketing economies: Marketing and distribution costs can be spread over a larger output and wider product range.\n5. Managerial economies: The merged firm can employ specialist managers for different functions (finance, marketing, engineering), increasing efficiency."},

    {num:12, label:"Q12: Globalisation, Corporation Tax & Brexit",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the term globalisation.\n(ii) Outline two positive impacts and two negative impacts of globalisation.\n\n(b) In Budget 2020, the Minister for Finance reaffirmed Ireland's commitment to the 12.5% corporation tax rate.\n(i) Evaluate two reasons why the Minister made this decision.\n(ii) Outline one reason why the European Commission wants to remove Ireland's ability to set its own corporation tax rate.\n\n(c) The United Kingdom exited the European Union on January 31, 2020.\n(i) Discuss two implications of this exit for the Irish economy.\n(ii) Outline two possible advantages to Ireland of remaining a member of the EU.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Globalisation refers to the increasing integration and interdependence of the world's economies through the growth of international trade, investment, technology transfer, and the movement of labour across borders. It involves the breaking down of barriers between national economies, facilitated by advances in communication and transport technology.\n\n(ii) Positive impacts:\n1. Increased trade and economic growth: Globalisation allows countries to specialise in goods where they have a comparative advantage, increasing efficiency and overall world output. Ireland has benefited enormously from access to global markets.\n2. FDI and technology transfer: Globalisation encourages foreign direct investment, bringing capital, technology, and expertise to host countries. Ireland has attracted hundreds of multinational corporations.\n\nNegative impacts:\n1. Income inequality: The benefits of globalisation are not evenly distributed. Wealthy countries and skilled workers benefit most, while developing countries and low-skilled workers may be left behind or exploited.\n2. Loss of domestic industry / cultural identity: Local firms may be unable to compete with large multinationals, leading to business closures and job losses. Traditional cultures and practices may be eroded by global consumer culture.\n\n(b)(i) Two reasons for maintaining 12.5% corporation tax:\n1. Attract and retain FDI: Ireland's low corporation tax rate has been a key factor in attracting major multinational corporations (Apple, Google, Pfizer, etc.) to locate in Ireland. These firms provide significant employment and contribute to exports. Changing the rate could undermine Ireland's competitive advantage.\n2. Tax revenue: Despite being a low rate, Ireland's corporation tax generates substantial revenue due to the large number of MNCs operating here. In 2019, corporation tax receipts exceeded €10 billion. Increasing the rate might cause firms to relocate, reducing rather than increasing revenue.\n\n(ii) European Commission reason: The European Commission believes that low corporation tax rates like Ireland's 12.5% constitute unfair tax competition between EU member states. They argue it allows Ireland to attract a disproportionate share of investment and tax revenue that would otherwise go to other EU countries. A harmonised rate would create a more level playing field across the EU.\n\n(c)(i) Two implications of Brexit for Ireland:\n1. Trade disruption: The UK was one of Ireland's largest trading partners. Brexit has created new customs checks, tariffs on some goods, and regulatory barriers, increasing costs and delays for Irish exporters, particularly in the agri-food sector. The Northern Ireland Protocol has created additional complexity.\n2. Impact on the border / Northern Ireland: Brexit has created significant political and economic challenges for the island of Ireland. New trade arrangements risk disrupting the all-island economy and the peace process established by the Good Friday Agreement.\n\n(ii) Two advantages of EU membership for Ireland:\n1. Access to the Single Market: Ireland benefits from free movement of goods, services, capital, and people across 27 member states, providing a market of over 440 million consumers for Irish businesses.\n2. EU structural and cohesion funds: Ireland has received billions in EU funding for infrastructure, agriculture (CAP), and regional development. EU membership also provides political stability and a stronger voice in international negotiations."},

    {num:13, label:"Q13: Excess Demand, Market Failure & Price Elasticity (PS5 & AirPods)",
     q:"(a) Shortages of PlayStation 5 Gaming Consoles (PS5) was a nightmare for many consumers in the lead up to Christmas 2020. The retail price was €500.\n(i) Complete the diagram to indicate how excess demand is occurring in this market.\n(ii) Explain how excess demand occurs.\n\n(b) Technology retailers mislead shoppers with Black Friday 'deals'. Many products are not discounted as claimed.\n(i) Explain why misinformation to consumers by technology retailers represents a market failure.\n(ii) Evaluate how government intervention could address this market failure.\n(iii) Explain, giving an example, one advantage of a government regulation.\n\n(c)(i) Apple AirPods price fell from €160 to €125. Quantity demanded increased from 10,000 to 15,000 units. Calculate the Price Elasticity of Demand.\n(ii) State whether demand is elastic or inelastic. Give a reason.\n(iii) If the firm intends to maximise revenue, should it increase, decrease, or maintain the same price? Explain.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Diagram should show:\n- Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis\n- Downward-sloping demand curve and upward-sloping supply curve\n- The price of €500 should be shown below the equilibrium price\n- At €500, the quantity demanded exceeds the quantity supplied, creating excess demand (shortage)\n\n(ii) Excess demand occurs when the price of a good is set below the market equilibrium price. At €500, the quantity demanded by consumers is greater than the quantity that Sony/retailers are willing and able to supply. This creates a shortage in the market. Due to global chip shortages and high demand during COVID-19 lockdowns, Sony could not produce enough PS5 consoles to meet demand at the €500 price point. In a free market, the price would rise to equilibrium, but Sony chose to maintain the €500 retail price.\n\n(b)(i) Misinformation as market failure: This represents a market failure because consumers are making purchasing decisions based on false or misleading information (imperfect knowledge). One of the assumptions of an efficient market is that consumers have perfect knowledge. When retailers falsely claim products are discounted, consumers believe they are getting value for money when they are not. This leads to an inefficient allocation of resources, as consumers buy goods they might not otherwise have purchased at that price, or overpay for goods.\n\n(ii) Government intervention: The government could introduce legislation requiring retailers to display the genuine pre-sale price for a minimum period (e.g. 30 days) before advertising a 'discount'. The Competition and Consumer Protection Commission (CCPC) could be empowered to investigate and fine retailers engaged in misleading pricing practices. This would ensure consumers have accurate information to make rational decisions.\n\n(iii) Advantage of government regulation: Consumer protection legislation, such as the Consumer Rights Act, ensures that goods sold must be of merchantable quality and fit for purpose. For example, if a consumer purchases a faulty electronic product, they are entitled to a refund, repair, or replacement. This regulation protects consumers from exploitation and ensures minimum quality standards in the market.\n\n(c)(i) PED Calculation:\n% Change in Quantity Demanded = ((15,000 - 10,000) / 10,000) x 100 = 50%\n% Change in Price = ((€125 - €160) / €160) x 100 = -21.875%\nPED = % Change in Qd / % Change in P = 50% / -21.875% = -2.29 (approximately)\n\n(ii) Demand is ELASTIC (PED > 1 in absolute value). The percentage change in quantity demanded (50%) is greater than the percentage change in price (21.875%). This means consumers are highly responsive to price changes for AirPods - a relatively small price decrease led to a proportionally larger increase in quantity demanded.\n\n(iii) The firm should DECREASE the price to maximise revenue.\nExplanation: Since demand is elastic (PED > 1), a decrease in price leads to a proportionally larger increase in quantity demanded. This means total revenue (Price x Quantity) will increase when price is reduced. The gain in revenue from the increased quantity sold outweighs the loss in revenue from the lower price per unit."},

    {num:14, label:"Q14: Inflation, Comparative Advantage & Competitiveness",
     q:"The graph shows the HICP average inflation rate for Ireland, UK, and Euro Area 2014-2019.\n\n(a)(i) Analyse the trend of Ireland's inflation rate in relation to the UK and the Euro Area from 2014-2019.\n(ii) Outline the potential advantages to Ireland of maintaining the lowest price inflation rate relative to these other areas.\n\n(b)(i) Outline two possible disadvantages to Irish citizens of a low inflation rate.\n(ii) Explain one method the government could use to address rising inflation.\n\n(c)(i) Define the principle of comparative advantage.\n(ii) Discuss the importance of continued capital investment in the National Broadband Plan for Ireland's international competitiveness.\n(iii) Choose two other areas the Irish Government should focus on to increase its international competitiveness. Justify your choice.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) Trend analysis: From 2014-2019, Ireland generally maintained a lower inflation rate than both the UK and the Euro Area average. In 2014-2015, Ireland experienced very low or even negative inflation (deflation), while the UK had positive inflation. From 2016 onwards, Ireland's inflation rate increased but remained consistently below the UK rate. Ireland's rate tracked broadly in line with the Euro Area average but was generally at or slightly below it. The UK experienced notably higher inflation from 2017 onwards, largely due to the depreciation of sterling following the Brexit referendum in 2016.\n\n(ii) Advantages of lowest inflation rate:\n1. Improved international competitiveness: Lower inflation means Irish goods and services become relatively cheaper compared to UK and eurozone competitors, boosting demand for Irish exports and potentially improving the balance of payments.\n2. Increased purchasing power: Irish consumers retain more purchasing power as prices rise more slowly, improving their real standard of living relative to citizens in countries with higher inflation.\n3. Attraction of FDI: Lower costs make Ireland a more attractive location for foreign investment compared to higher-cost alternatives.\n\n(b)(i) Two disadvantages of low inflation for Irish citizens:\n1. Risk of deflation: Very low inflation can lead to deflation (falling prices), which can cause consumers to delay purchases in anticipation of further price falls, reducing aggregate demand and potentially leading to recession and unemployment.\n2. Increased real burden of debt: With low inflation, the real value of debts (mortgages, loans) does not decrease as quickly as it would with higher inflation, making it harder for borrowers to repay their debts.\n\n(ii) Method to address rising inflation: The government could use contractionary fiscal policy by increasing income tax rates or reducing government spending. Higher taxes reduce disposable income, which reduces consumer spending and aggregate demand, putting downward pressure on prices. Reduced government spending directly reduces aggregate demand. The ECB could also raise interest rates (monetary policy), though Ireland cannot independently set this.\n\n(c)(i) Comparative advantage: The principle of comparative advantage states that a country should specialise in producing and exporting those goods and services which it can produce at the lowest opportunity cost relative to other countries, and import those goods which other countries can produce at a lower opportunity cost.\n\n(ii) Importance of National Broadband Plan:\nContinued investment in the National Broadband Plan is vital for Ireland's international competitiveness because:\n- Digital economy: High-speed broadband enables Irish businesses to compete in the global digital economy, facilitating e-commerce, remote working, and access to international markets.\n- FDI attraction: Multinationals require reliable, high-speed internet. Broadband infrastructure in rural areas helps attract investment outside Dublin, supporting balanced regional development.\n- Productivity: Better connectivity enables businesses to adopt new technologies, improve efficiency, and reduce costs.\n- Remote working: COVID-19 has accelerated the trend towards remote working. Good broadband in all areas allows workers to live outside cities while maintaining productivity.\n\n(iii) Two other areas for international competitiveness:\n1. Education and skills: Ireland should continue to invest in third-level education and vocational training to ensure the workforce has the skills demanded by high-value industries (technology, pharmaceuticals, finance). A highly skilled workforce is one of Ireland's key competitive advantages and must be maintained.\n\n2. Housing and infrastructure: The housing crisis is undermining Ireland's competitiveness. High rents and property prices increase the cost of living and doing business in Ireland, making it harder to attract and retain talent. Investment in housing supply, public transport, and infrastructure is essential to keep Ireland competitive."},

    {num:15, label:"Q15: National Income, GDP vs GNI & Recession",
     q:"(a) Which measure is a more accurate indicator of Ireland's economic welfare: Gross Domestic Product (GDP) or Gross National Income (GNI)? Justify your answer.\n\n(b)(i) Define the term recession.\n(ii) Based on the graph showing GNI Per Capita PPP for Ireland and the United States (1995-2020), identify the years when both countries were in recession. Explain your answer.\n(iii) Discuss two economic consequences of a recession.\n\n(c)(i) Outline one positive and one negative effect of government investment in the economy during a recession.\n(ii) Explain the economic concept of the multiplier and its relevance during a recession.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a) GNI (Gross National Income) is a more accurate indicator of Ireland's economic welfare than GDP.\n\nJustification: Ireland's GDP is significantly distorted by the activities of large multinational corporations operating in Ireland. These MNCs generate enormous output that is counted in GDP but the profits are largely repatriated to their home countries and do not benefit Irish citizens. GNI adjusts for this by adding net factor income from abroad. Since Ireland has a large negative NFIA (profits flowing out exceed income flowing in), GNI gives a more accurate picture of the income actually available to Irish residents. Even better is Modified GNI (GNI*), which further strips out the effects of globalisation distortions such as intellectual property transfers, aircraft leasing, and re-domiciled companies.\n\n(b)(i) A recession is defined as two or more consecutive quarters of negative economic growth (decline in real GDP/GNP). It is characterised by falling output, rising unemployment, declining consumer and business confidence, and reduced economic activity.\n\n(ii) Both Ireland and the United States were in recession during 2008-2010 (the global financial crisis). This can be identified from the graph where GNI per capita PPP declined for both countries during this period. Ireland's GNI per capita fell more sharply than the US, reflecting the severity of the banking crisis and property market collapse in Ireland. Both countries also appear to have experienced a decline around 2001-2002 following the dot-com crash.\n\n(iii) Two economic consequences of recession:\n1. Rising unemployment: As firms experience falling demand, they reduce production and lay off workers. This leads to increased social welfare costs, reduced tax revenue, lower consumer spending, and a decline in living standards.\n2. Falling government revenue / increased budget deficits: Tax receipts decline (less income tax, VAT, corporation tax) while government spending increases (social welfare payments). This leads to budget deficits and increased national debt.\n\n(c)(i) Positive effect: Government investment during a recession acts as a direct injection into the circular flow of income. Building infrastructure creates employment, generates income for workers and suppliers, and stimulates economic recovery through the multiplier effect.\n\nNegative effect: The government must fund this investment through borrowing (since tax revenues are lower during a recession), which increases the national debt. Future generations may bear the burden of repaying this debt through higher taxes or reduced public services.\n\n(ii) The multiplier concept: The multiplier shows how an initial injection of spending (e.g. government investment) leads to a larger overall increase in national income as the spending circulates through the economy. During a recession, the multiplier is particularly relevant because the economy has spare capacity (unemployed workers, idle factories). Government spending puts money in the hands of workers who spend it on goods and services, which creates income for other businesses and workers, and so on. The formula is K = 1/(1-MPC) or K = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM). However, the multiplier may be smaller in an open economy like Ireland where a significant proportion of extra income is spent on imports (leakage)."},

    {num:16, label:"Q16: Demand, Supply & Factor Markets",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain the law of demand and illustrate with a labelled diagram.\n(ii) Outline two factors, other than price, which can cause a shift in the demand curve.\n(iii) Distinguish between a movement along the demand curve and a shift of the demand curve.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the concept of derived demand using the factor of production labour.\n(ii) Outline two factors that affect the demand for labour.\n(iii) Explain how the wage rate is determined in a perfectly competitive labour market.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term marginal revenue product (MRP) of labour.\n(ii) Explain why a profit-maximising firm will employ workers up to the point where MRP = wage rate.\n(iii) Outline two factors, other than wages, that affect the supply of labour.\n\n(75 marks)",
     a:"(a)(i) The Law of Demand states that as the price of a good or service increases, the quantity demanded of that good or service decreases, and vice versa, ceteris paribus (all other things being equal). This inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded is shown by a downward-sloping demand curve.\nDiagram: Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis, downward-sloping demand curve labelled D.\n\n(ii) Two factors causing a shift in the demand curve:\n1. Changes in consumer income: An increase in income shifts the demand curve to the right for normal goods (consumers can afford to buy more at every price level). For inferior goods, demand shifts left as consumers switch to higher-quality alternatives.\n2. Changes in the price of related goods: If the price of a substitute good increases, demand for the original good increases (shifts right). If the price of a complementary good increases, demand for the original good decreases (shifts left).\n\n(iii) Movement along vs shift of the demand curve:\n- Movement along: Caused by a change in the price of the good itself. The demand curve stays in the same position, but consumers move to a different point on the curve (e.g. from point A to point B). This shows a change in quantity demanded.\n- Shift of the demand curve: Caused by a change in a non-price factor (income, tastes, price of substitutes/complements, population, expectations). The entire demand curve moves to a new position (left or right). This shows a change in demand.\n\n(b)(i) Derived demand: Labour is not demanded for its own sake but because of the goods and services it produces. The demand for labour is therefore derived from the demand for the final product. For example, if demand for new houses increases, the demand for construction workers, electricians, and plumbers increases as a result. If demand for the product falls, the demand for the labour that produces it also falls.\n\n(ii) Two factors affecting the demand for labour:\n1. Demand for the firm's output: As demand for the firm's product increases, the firm needs more workers to produce more output. During economic booms, demand for labour increases; during recessions, it falls.\n2. Wage rate / cost of labour: If wages increase, the cost of employing workers rises. Firms may substitute capital (machinery) for labour if it becomes more cost-effective, reducing demand for labour.\n\n(iii) Wage determination in a perfectly competitive labour market: The wage rate is determined by the intersection of the market supply of labour and the market demand for labour. The supply curve slopes upward (higher wages attract more workers to the occupation), and the demand curve slopes downward (firms demand less labour at higher wages). The equilibrium wage rate (We) is where supply equals demand. Individual firms are 'wage-takers' - they must accept the market-determined wage rate.\n\n(c)(i) Marginal Revenue Product (MRP) of labour is the additional revenue a firm earns from employing one extra unit of labour. It is calculated as: MRP = Marginal Physical Product (MPP) x Marginal Revenue (MR). In other words, it is the extra output produced by the additional worker multiplied by the price at which that output can be sold.\n\n(ii) A profit-maximising firm will employ workers up to the point where MRP equals the wage rate because:\n- If MRP > Wage Rate: The extra revenue generated by the worker exceeds the cost of employing them, so it is profitable to hire more workers.\n- If MRP < Wage Rate: The cost of the worker exceeds the extra revenue they generate, so the firm should reduce its workforce.\n- At MRP = Wage Rate: The firm maximises profit from labour. Hiring another worker would cost more than they generate, while having fewer workers means missing out on profitable output.\n\n(iii) Two factors affecting the supply of labour (other than wages):\n1. Non-monetary benefits: Factors such as job satisfaction, working conditions, flexibility, holiday entitlements, and pension schemes influence whether workers choose to supply their labour to a particular occupation.\n2. Education and training requirements: Occupations that require lengthy or expensive education/training (e.g. medicine, law) will have a more restricted supply of labour, as fewer people are qualified or willing to invest the time and money required."}
  ],

"2022": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (75 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Price Elasticity of Demand",
     q:"(a) Using an appropriate formula, calculate the price elasticity of demand depicted in the graph where the original price of the packet of face masks is \u20ac4 and then increases to \u20ac5. Show all calculations.\n\n(b) With reference to the graph above explain the law of demand.",
     a:"(a) Formula: (Change in Qd / Change in P) x (P1 + P2) / (Qd1 + Qd2)\n= (-4 / +1) x (4 + 5) / (8 + 4)\n= -4 x 9/12\n= -3\nThe PED is -3, meaning demand is price elastic.\n\n(b) The Law of Demand states that as the price of a good or service increases, the quantity demanded of that good or service decreases, and vice versa (ceteris paribus). This can be seen in the graph: as price increases from \u20ac4 to \u20ac5, quantity demanded decreases from 8 to 4 units."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Balance of Payments & National Accounts",
     q:"The infographic shows the Quarterly National Accounts for Quarter 2, 2021.\n\n(a) Using the data, explain whether the Balance of Payments is likely to have an overall expansionary or contractionary effect. Refer to relevant figures.\n\n(b) Outline one factor which could have influenced the trend in Personal Consumption in Quarter 2, 2021.",
     a:"(a) Expansionary.\nExports have increased by 6.4% and imports have increased by 5.4%. The increase in exports is 1% larger than the increase in imports, thus the injections are larger than leakages, which will have an overall expansionary effect on the economy.\n\n(b) Possible factors:\n- Pent up demand: consumers couldn't spend during lockdowns.\n- Increases in income: employees returning to employment following lockdowns now have higher disposable income (than received on PUP, EWSS, TWSS etc).\n- Increased consumer and business confidence: may encourage consumers to spend on deferred items like holidays.\n- Expectations of future price increases: with increasing demand, consumers may fear prices will rise and so buy now."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Minimum Unit Pricing & Market Failure",
     q:"Minimum Unit Pricing (MUP) for alcohol was introduced in Ireland in early 2022. A standard bottle of wine (750ml) cannot be sold for less than \u20ac7.40 and a can of beer (500ml) for less than \u20ac1.70.\n\n(a) Do you agree with this new MUP for alcohol? Justify your answer.\n\n(b) The purchase of alcohol in the free market may generate a market failure. Explain this statement using an example.",
     a:"(a) Arguments FOR MUP:\n- May combat alcohol abuse: the higher price may reduce consumption/binge drinking.\n- Less personal/social problems: reduced consumption leads to fewer social issues.\n- Reduced admissions to EDs: reduced consumption should lower hospital admissions, helping reduce health costs.\n- A healthier population: decreased per capita consumption may reduce absenteeism and increase productivity.\n\nArguments AGAINST MUP:\n- It is inequitable: as it is regressive, it will impact those on lowest incomes the most.\n- Cross border shopping: may lead to increased cross-border shopping, losing economic activity and tax revenue for the Republic.\n- Unnecessary state interference in personal decision making.\n- Effect on hidden economy: some may resort to alternative sources of alcohol.\n- Switch to alternative drugs: if other drugs are cheaper, demand for these may increase.\n\n(b) Market failure occurs when the price mechanism in the free market leads to an inefficient or inequitable allocation of resources. The production/consumption of alcohol may lead to consumers consuming harmful goods, being exploited, or generating negative externalities for third parties. For example, individual taxpayers who aren't involved in the consumption of alcohol end up paying for the healthcare of individuals who overconsume alcohol."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Circular Flow of Income & FDI",
     q:"(a) Categorise each of the following as either an injection or a leakage from the circular flow of income:\n1. The Irish government increases public sector salaries by 1% in 2023.\n2. Irish exports increase to record \u20ac160bn despite Covid-19.\n3. Irish consumers saved more than \u20ac10bn in the first quarter of 2021.\n\nAnswer either (b) or (c):\n(b) Justify each of your choices in part (a).\nOR\n(c) Outline two reasons why inflows of FDI into Ireland are important for the Irish economy.",
     a:"(a)\n1. Government increases public sector salaries = INJECTION\n2. Irish exports increase = INJECTION\n3. Irish consumers saved \u20ac10bn = LEAKAGE\n\n(b) Justifications:\n- Public sector salaries are part of government spending, so government spending is an injection into the circular flow of income.\n- Exports are money that originates outside the circular flow of income and thus is an injection.\n- By not spending their income, consumers are reducing the size of the circular flow, thus saving is a withdrawal/leakage.\n\n(c) Importance of FDI:\n1. Increased employment opportunities: with increased investment there will be increased demand for labour in new and expanding firms.\n2. Increased tax revenue: from corporation, capital gains, income and VAT taxation. With increased investment, employment, expenditure and profit, the government earns more tax revenue.\n3. Increased economic growth/exports: investment is an injection into the circular flow and will have a multiplier effect (C+I+G+X-M)."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Ageing Population",
     q:"It is predicted that the number of people in Ireland aged over 65 will have increased to 1 million by 2031.\n\n(a) Explain two factors which are likely to have influenced this trend in the Irish population.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Outline two economic advantages of an ageing Irish population.\nOR\n(c) Outline two impacts Ireland's ageing population will have on future government policy.",
     a:"(a) Factors influencing the trend:\n1. Improvement in healthcare in Ireland: resulting in improved treatment of illness and reducing death rates.\n2. Improved standard of living / increased income / increased employment: allows people to buy better quality food, access healthcare and improved infrastructure.\n3. Increased focus on a healthy lifestyle in modern society: people are more conscious of maintaining a better lifestyle as they age.\n4. Improved planning by government and state agencies recognising the need for improved policies.\n\n(b) Economic advantages of ageing population:\n1. Increased numbers may participate in the labour force due to people living longer.\n2. More experienced workforce as these workers bring many years of experience.\n3. Skill shortages may be filled by retired workers returning to the workforce.\n4. Tax revenues: if older workers remain, it allows for increased tax revenue and less need for pensions.\n5. Opportunities for businesses: change in demand patterns may present opportunities e.g. over-55 holidays.\n\n(c) Impacts on government policy:\n1. Increased spending on healthcare or change in budget priorities for health care.\n2. Increased pressure on pensions / may need to means-test the contributory pension / increase pension age.\n3. More demands on government services e.g. health and long-term care.\n4. Possible increased tax burden for those remaining in the workforce.\n5. Labour market: may cause dependency ratio problems / reduced labour market participation rates."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Perfect Competition",
     q:"Firms in Perfect Competition are said to be Price-Takers.\n\n(a) On diagrams label and illustrate:\n(i) What happens to the market equilibrium as more firms enter the market?\n(ii) What happens to the individual firm's price as more firms enter the market?\nExplain each diagram.\n\nAnswer either (b) or (c):\n(b) Firms can survive in the short run even if they are making a loss. Explain.\nOR\n(c) In the long run only the most efficient firms will survive. Explain.",
     a:"(a) Explanation for the market:\nAs firms outside the industry become aware of the supernormal profits (SNPs) earned, they will enter the industry (no barriers to entry). As these firms enter, market supply shifts right from S1 to S2. Quantity supplied increases from Q1 to Q2 and the price decreases from P1 to P2.\n\nExplanation for the firm:\nAs the individual firm is a price-taker, it must accept the price established by the industry, thus its price also decreases from P1 to P2. The firm's demand curve (D=AR=MR) shifts down.\n\n(b) Short-run losses:\nIn the short run, if AR (Average Revenue) is greater than AVC (Average Variable Cost), the firm is making a loss but once it covers its average variable costs, it contributes towards its fixed costs. The loss from shutting down would be greater than the loss from continuing to operate.\n\n(c) Long-run efficiency:\nA firm is most efficient when producing at the minimum point of AC. If it is not producing at the minimum point of the average total cost curve in the long run, it is not being efficient. It will not be earning normal profit and will be forced due to inefficiency to leave the industry."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: ECB Interest Rates",
     q:"Only one of the charts below accurately displays the trend in the ECB interest rate from 2000 to 2020.\n\n(a)(i) Identify which chart accurately displays the correct trend.\n(ii) Justify your choice.\n\n(b) Other than setting the base % interest rate, outline two other roles of the ECB.",
     a:"(a)(i) Figure A displays the correct trend.\n\n(ii) In the wake of the global financial crisis in 2007/2008, the ECB gradually dropped its base interest rate to zero percent in order to discourage saving and encourage borrowing, hoping to stimulate investment and economic growth in the European economy.\n\n(b) Other roles of the ECB:\n1. Ensure price stability: the ECB aims for an inflation rate of 2% during the medium term.\n2. Manages the eurozone's foreign currency reserves and the buying or selling of currencies to influence the price of the euro.\n3. Ensures that financial markets and institutions are well supervised by national authorities and that payment systems work well.\n4. Ensures the stability, safety and soundness of the financial system across the eurozone.\n5. Authorises production of euro banknotes by eurozone countries."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Hidden Economy & Supply Chains",
     q:"Brexit and possible supply chain issues have led to fewer illegal fireworks in Ireland during Halloween 2021.\n\n(a) Explain what is meant by the economic term hidden economy.\n\n(b) Outline two reasons for the supply chain issues which many companies experienced in Ireland during 2021-2022.",
     a:"(a) The hidden economy refers to economic activity that is not recorded or declared to the authorities. It includes illegal activities (e.g. drug dealing, smuggling) and legal activities where income is not declared for tax purposes (e.g. cash-in-hand work). This results in a loss of tax revenue for the government and inaccurate national income statistics.\n\n(b) Reasons for supply chain issues:\n1. COVID-19 pandemic: lockdowns and restrictions globally led to factory closures, reduced production, and shipping delays, creating bottlenecks in global supply chains.\n2. Brexit: new customs checks, tariffs, and regulatory barriers between the UK and EU created delays and increased costs for goods moving through or from the UK to Ireland."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Social Costs & Benefits (Solar Panels)",
     q:"(a) Identify one private cost and one private benefit for the consumer of installing solar panels.\n\n(b) Installing solar panels also creates social benefits. Discuss this statement.",
     a:"(a)\nPrivate cost: The initial financial outlay/expense of purchasing and installing the solar panels (can be several thousand euro).\nPrivate benefit: Reduced electricity bills over time / the consumer generates their own electricity and may even sell surplus back to the grid.\n\n(b) Social benefits of solar panels:\n- Reduced carbon emissions: solar energy is a renewable, clean energy source that reduces reliance on fossil fuels, helping to combat climate change.\n- Reduced air pollution: fewer harmful emissions lead to improved public health.\n- Reduced strain on the national grid: if many households generate their own electricity, it reduces demand on the grid.\n- Energy security: reduces Ireland's dependence on imported fossil fuels.\n- Job creation in the renewable energy sector."},

    {num:10, label:"Q10: Production Possibility Frontier (PPF)",
     q:"PepsiCo has 23 brands. Doritos and Tropicana are two of their most popular products.\n\n(a) Calculate the opportunity cost of PepsiCo moving production from point D to point H on the PPF curve. (D = 50 Doritos, 62 Tropicana; H = 90 Doritos, 0 Tropicana). Explain your answer.\n\n(b) The point X (20,20) is another production possibility for PepsiCo. Would you recommend them to produce at this point? Explain.",
     a:"(a) Moving from D to H:\n- Doritos increase from 50m kg to 90m kg (gain of 40m kg)\n- Tropicana decreases from 62m litres to 0 litres (loss of 62m litres)\nOpportunity cost = 62 million litres of Tropicana given up to gain 40 million kg of Doritos.\nFor every extra 1m kg of Doritos, PepsiCo gives up 1.55m litres of Tropicana.\n\n(b) No, producing at point X (20,20) would not be recommended. This point lies inside the PPF curve, which means PepsiCo would be producing inefficiently - they would not be making full use of their available resources. They could produce more of both products with their current resources. An efficient firm should produce on the PPF boundary."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (225 marks) =====
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Rental Market, Land & Regional Development",
     q:"(a)(i) Outline two possible reasons for the disparity in average rents between Dublin City and Waterford City in Q2, 2021.\n(ii) Outline two possible interventions the Irish government could pursue to support balanced regional development.\n\n(b)(i) Explain, with a fully labelled diagram, how the equilibrium price of land is determined.\n(ii) Other than land being fixed in supply, outline two characteristics of land.\n(iii) Explain how derived demand relates to the factor of production land.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term opportunity cost.\n(ii) Outline one opportunity cost of government expenditure on the North Runway Project at Dublin Airport.\n(iii) Discuss the arguments for and against the Irish government providing more financial support to regional airports.",
     a:"(a)(i) Reasons for rent disparity:\n1. Higher demand for rental properties in Dublin due to greater employment opportunities, multinational firms, and universities.\n2. Limited supply of rental properties in Dublin relative to demand, driving up prices. Waterford has lower demand and more affordable supply.\n\n(ii) Government interventions for balanced regional development:\n1. Decentralisation of government departments and agencies to regional cities to create employment outside Dublin.\n2. Investment in infrastructure (roads, broadband, public transport) in regional areas to attract businesses.\n\n(b)(i) The equilibrium price of land (economic rent) is determined by demand alone, since the supply of land is perfectly inelastic (fixed/vertical supply curve). The demand curve for land is downward sloping. Where demand intersects the fixed supply curve determines the equilibrium rent/price.\n\n(ii) Characteristics of land:\n1. Land is a gift of nature - it is not man-made and exists naturally.\n2. Land has no cost of production - since it is a natural resource, there is no cost involved in creating it.\n3. Land is geographically immobile - it cannot be moved from one location to another.\n4. Land varies in quality/fertility.\n\n(iii) Derived demand: Land is not demanded for its own sake but because of what can be produced on it. The demand for land is derived from the demand for the goods and services produced using land. For example, if demand for housing increases, demand for building land increases.\n\n(c)(i) Opportunity cost is the cost of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. When resources are used for one purpose, the benefit that could have been gained from the next best use is lost.\n\n(ii) The opportunity cost of spending \u20ac250m-\u20ac500m on the North Runway could be investment in healthcare, social housing, or education. For example, these funds could have been used to build thousands of social housing units.\n\n(iii) Arguments FOR supporting regional airports:\n- Balanced regional development: keeps economic activity outside Dublin.\n- Tourism: regional airports attract tourists to areas like Kerry, benefiting the local economy.\n- Business connectivity: important for business investment in regional areas.\n\nArguments AGAINST:\n- Opportunity cost: funds could be better spent on other infrastructure like roads or broadband.\n- Low passenger numbers may not justify the investment.\n- Environmental concerns: subsidising air travel conflicts with climate targets."},

    {num:12, label:"Q12: Market Structures & Electricity Market",
     q:"(a)(i) Comment on what the HHI results indicate about market concentration in the electricity industry (2005 to 2019). HHI was 10,000 in 2005 and 3,328 in 2019.\n(ii) Explain two reasons why a government would decide to deregulate its electricity supply.\n(iii) Explain the term privatisation.\n(iv) Outline one reason why a government may choose privatisation over deregulation.\n\n(b)(i) Outline one advantage of both a State Monopoly and an Oligopoly.\n(ii) Explain, with a diagram, the long-run equilibrium position of a monopoly firm.\n(iii) Explain two changes in market conditions which allow a market to move from a Monopoly to an Oligopoly.\n\n(c)(i) Outline two challenges the Irish economy faces in transforming the motor industry into a carbon neutral industry.\n(ii) Discuss one reason for and one reason against granting planning permission for additional data centres in Ireland.",
     a:"(a)(i) HHI Commentary:\nIn 2005, the HHI was 10,000 indicating a pure monopoly (ESB was the sole supplier). By 2019, the HHI dropped to 3,328, indicating the market has become much more competitive though still highly concentrated (HHI above 2,500 = highly concentrated). The market moved from monopoly to oligopoly through deregulation.\n\n(ii) Reasons for deregulation:\n1. Increased competition: more firms entering the market leads to lower prices for consumers, greater choice, and improved quality of service.\n2. Increased efficiency: competition forces firms to become more efficient to survive, reducing waste and improving service.\n\n(iii) Privatisation is the transfer of ownership of a state-owned/semi-state enterprise to private ownership, usually through the sale of shares to the public.\n\n(iv) A government may choose privatisation because it generates immediate revenue from the sale of the semi-state body, whereas deregulation does not directly raise revenue. The sale proceeds can be used for other government spending priorities.\n\n(b)(i) Advantage of Monopoly: Economies of scale - a monopoly can produce at lower average costs due to large-scale production, and these savings can be passed on to consumers.\nAdvantage of Oligopoly: Product differentiation and innovation - oligopolistic firms compete through non-price competition, leading to greater innovation and product variety for consumers.\n\n(ii) Monopoly long-run equilibrium:\nThe monopolist produces where MC = MR (profit-maximising output). Price is read off the AR (demand) curve above this point. The monopolist earns supernormal profit because AR > AC at the profit-maximising output. There is no entry of new firms due to barriers to entry, so supernormal profits persist in the long run.\n\n(iii) Changes allowing move from Monopoly to Oligopoly:\n1. Removal of barriers to entry: deregulation/legislation changes allow new firms to enter the market and compete.\n2. End of exclusive government licenses/franchises: removing the legal protection that gave the monopolist exclusive rights to supply.\n\n(c)(i) Challenges for carbon neutral motor industry:\n1. Insufficient charging infrastructure: Ireland needs a massive investment in electric vehicle charging points across the country.\n2. High cost of electric vehicles: EVs are currently more expensive than traditional cars, making them unaffordable for many consumers.\n\n(ii) Data centres:\nReason FOR: Job creation and economic growth - data centres create high-skilled employment and attract further FDI into Ireland, increasing tax revenue.\nReason AGAINST: Energy consumption - data centres could account for 29% of all electricity demand by 2028, putting strain on the national grid and conflicting with Ireland's climate targets."},

    {num:13, label:"Q13: Government Revenue, Expenditure & Taxation",
     q:"The infographic shows sources of income and expenditure as a proportion of every euro the Irish government receives and spends in 2020.\n\n(a)(i) Explain why social benefits represent such a high proportion of government expenditure. Use an example.\n(ii) Distinguish between direct and indirect tax with examples.\n\n(b)(i) Outline one possible incentive the government uses to encourage certain behaviour and one tax measure to discourage certain behaviour.\n(ii) Outline two interventions, other than incentives/taxes, that a government could implement to influence consumer behaviour.\n\n(c) The Irish Government has signed up to the OECD agreement on a minimum global corporation tax rate of 15%. Discuss.",
     a:"(a)(i) Social benefits represent a high proportion because the government has a responsibility to provide a social safety net for citizens. During 2020, COVID-19 led to massive increases in social welfare payments (PUP, EWSS, TWSS) as unemployment surged due to lockdowns. Example: Pandemic Unemployment Payment (PUP) of \u20ac350 per week was paid to hundreds of thousands of workers who lost their jobs.\n\n(ii) Direct tax is levied directly on income or wealth and is paid directly to the Revenue Commissioners by the taxpayer. It cannot be passed on. Examples: PAYE (income tax), Capital Gains Tax, Corporation Tax.\nIndirect tax is levied on goods and services and is included in the price of the product. It can be passed on from the producer to the consumer. Examples: VAT, Excise Duty, Customs Duty.\n\n(b)(i) Incentive to encourage behaviour: The government offers tax relief on pension contributions to encourage people to save for retirement. This reduces taxable income, incentivising long-term saving.\nTax measure to discourage behaviour: High excise duty on cigarettes to discourage smoking. The higher price reduces demand and consumption of harmful tobacco products.\n\n(ii) Government interventions to influence behaviour:\n1. Legislation/regulation: e.g. banning smoking in workplaces or introducing a sugar tax to reduce consumption of unhealthy products.\n2. Information campaigns/education: e.g. public health campaigns warning about the dangers of alcohol or promoting healthy eating habits.\n\n(c) Arguments FOR the 15% minimum rate:\n- Levels the playing field: prevents a race to the bottom on corporation tax rates between countries.\n- Increases government revenue: Ireland will collect more tax from multinational profits.\n- International cooperation: shows Ireland is committed to fair international tax practices.\n\nArguments AGAINST:\n- Loss of competitive advantage: Ireland's low 12.5% rate was a key attraction for FDI. Moving to 15% may make Ireland less attractive.\n- Impact on FDI: some multinationals may relocate to countries that offer other advantages.\n- May reduce employment as some MNCs reconsider their Irish operations."},

    {num:14, label:"Q14: Demand & Supply, Consumer Behaviour",
     q:"Section B question covering demand and supply analysis, consumer behaviour, and market equilibrium.",
     a:"Key economic concepts covered:\n\n- Demand curves slope downward due to the law of demand (inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded).\n- Supply curves slope upward as higher prices incentivise producers to supply more.\n- Market equilibrium occurs where demand equals supply.\n- Consumer surplus is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they actually pay.\n- Changes in conditions of demand (income, tastes, price of substitutes/complements) shift the demand curve.\n- Changes in conditions of supply (costs, technology, government policy) shift the supply curve."},

    {num:15, label:"Q15: International Trade & Economic Development",
     q:"Section B question on international trade, comparative advantage, and economic development.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Free trade allows countries to specialise in goods where they have a comparative advantage, increasing overall output and efficiency.\n- Arguments for protectionism: protecting infant industries, safeguarding employment, national security, preventing dumping.\n- Arguments for free trade: lower prices, greater choice, increased competition, access to larger markets.\n- The EU Single Market provides Ireland with access to a market of over 440 million consumers.\n- Exchange rates affect competitiveness of exports and cost of imports."},

    {num:16, label:"Q16: Economic Growth & Development",
     q:"Section B question covering economic growth, sustainability, and government policy.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Economic growth is measured by increases in real GDP/GNP.\n- Sustainable development meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\n- Government uses fiscal policy (taxation and spending) and supply-side policies to promote growth.\n- The multiplier effect means an initial injection of spending leads to a larger overall increase in national income.\n- Infrastructure investment, education, and innovation are key drivers of long-term economic growth."}
  ],

  "2023": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Supply Curve & Price Discrimination",
     q:"France will host the Rugby World Cup in 2023.\n\n(a) Explain, with the aid of a fully labelled diagram (including the axes), the supply curve for tickets for this event.\n\n(b) Explain two conditions necessary for price discrimination to occur in the selling of tickets for the Rugby World Cup 2023.",
     a:"(a) The supply curve for tickets is perfectly inelastic (vertical line). Regardless of price, the quantity of seats supplied will be fixed for the stadiums used for the event. Even if price changes, the venues can only accommodate a certain number of attendees. The supply curve is vertical, reflecting the fact that venues have a fixed capacity.\nDiagram: Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis, vertical line at the fixed capacity.\n\n(b) Conditions for price discrimination:\n1. Consumer ignorance: The consumer is unaware that price discrimination is taking place.\n2. Consumer indifference: Consumers are aware of the price discrimination but don't care about it.\n3. Consumer attitudes: Consumers feel it is worth paying more rather than go without.\n4. Monopoly power: The firm must have some degree of monopoly power, otherwise rival firms would undercut prices.\n5. Different price elasticities of demand: The firm must recognise that different customers are more or less responsive to price changes (e.g. pensioners and students are more price elastic).\n6. Market separation: It must be impossible for customers to buy in the cheaper market and resell in the more expensive market."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Price Elasticity of Demand (Petrol)",
     q:"On the 3rd of March, 2022 the price of petrol in Ireland broke the \u20ac2 barrier for the first time.\n\n(a) Select if petrol has price elastic or price inelastic demand. Explain your answer.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Outline two reasons why it is important for retailers to understand the concept of price elasticity of demand.\nOR\n(c) Outline one advantage and one disadvantage for the Irish economy of the Minister for Finance's decision not to increase excise duties on petrol in Budget 2023.",
     a:"(a) Petrol has PRICE INELASTIC demand. If price increases, the percentage increase in price will be greater than the percentage decrease in quantity demanded. Petrol is a necessity for many motorists - if price rises, consumers still need petrol and demand will fall by a lower proportion.\n\n(b) Importance for retailers:\n1. They will know by how much quantity demanded will change if the price of the product is changed.\n2. They will know how to maximise revenue: for goods with inelastic PED, firms should increase price; for goods with elastic PED, firms should decrease price.\n3. Price discrimination: before a firm considers charging multiple prices, it needs to identify consumers with different PEDs.\n4. Impact and incidence of taxation: the firm will decide how much of an indirect tax to pass on to the consumer based upon their PED.\n\n(c) Advantage of NOT increasing excise duties:\n- Cost of Living Crisis: Irish citizens are already struggling to maintain their standard of living due to constant inflationary pressures throughout 2022. An increase in excise duties would have further exacerbated this problem.\n- Doesn't penalise businesses: any increase would have created further increases in costs of production for firms already struggling to survive.\n\nDisadvantage of NOT increasing excise duties:\n- Government revenue: petrol has price inelastic demand, so increases in excise duties generate large increases in revenue for the government.\n- Environmental damage: tax increases are a disincentive to consume. Without increasing this disincentive, consumers will continue to purchase this environmentally harmful product."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Utility & Consumer Behaviour",
     q:"(a) Complete the missing total utility and marginal utility figures in the table below. Show your workings.\nUnits consumed: 1, 2, 3, 4\nTotal Utility: 40, 80, ?, 137\nMarginal Utility: 40, ?, 35, ?\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) At what point does the law of diminishing marginal utility set in? Explain.\nOR\n(c) Does the formula MUx/Px = 1800/10 and MUy/Py = 3600/10 obey the Equi-Marginal Principle? Explain.",
     a:"(a) Workings:\n- MU of 2nd unit: 80 - 40 = 40\n- TU of 3rd unit: 80 + 35 = 115\n- MU of 4th unit: 137 - 115 = 22\n\nCompleted table:\nUnits: 1, 2, 3, 4\nTotal Utility: 40, 80, 115, 137\nMarginal Utility: 40, 40, 35, 22\n\n(b) The law of diminishing marginal utility sets in on the consumption of the third unit (after the consumption of the second unit). Marginal utility declines from 40 to 35 utils. The law states that as a consumer consumes additional units of a good, the extra satisfaction (marginal utility) gained from each successive unit will eventually begin to decrease.\n\n(c) No, the formula does NOT obey the Equi-Marginal Principle.\n1800 / 10 = 180\n3600 / 10 = 360\nThe ratio is not the same (180 does not equal 360). For the Equi-Marginal Principle to hold, MUx/Px must equal MUy/Py. The consumer should reallocate spending from good X to good Y until the ratios are equal."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Costs of Production",
     q:"The table below shows the output and total costs in a firm.\nOutput: 1, 2, 3, 4\nTotal Cost: \u20ac55, \u20ac82, \u20ac108, \u20ac152\nAverage Cost: 55, ?, 36, ?\nMarginal Cost: N/A, 27, ?, ?\n\n(a) Complete the missing average cost and marginal cost figures.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) To remain in production, in the short run a firm must cover its average total costs. Agree or disagree? Explain.\nOR\n(c) Explain the relationship between the Marginal Cost Curve and the Average Cost Curve.",
     a:"(a) Workings:\n- AC at 2 units: 82 / 2 = \u20ac41\n- MC at 3 units: 108 - 82 = \u20ac26\n- AC at 4 units: 152 / 4 = \u20ac38\n- MC at 4 units: 152 - 108 = \u20ac44\n\nCompleted table:\nOutput: 1, 2, 3, 4\nTotal Cost: \u20ac55, \u20ac82, \u20ac108, \u20ac152\nAverage Cost: \u20ac55, \u20ac41, \u20ac36, \u20ac38\nMarginal Cost: N/A, \u20ac27, \u20ac26, \u20ac44\n\n(b) DISAGREE. In the short run a firm must cover its average variable costs (not average total costs) while waiting for an increase in price of output or a reduction in total costs. Even if the firm is making a loss, as long as AR > AVC, it is making a contribution to its fixed costs and should continue to produce.\n\n(c) Relationship between MC and AC curves:\n- When Marginal Cost is greater than Average Cost: Average Cost is rising.\n- When Marginal Cost is less than Average Cost: Average Cost is falling.\n- When Marginal Cost is equal to Average Cost: Average Cost is at its minimum. The MC curve intersects the AC curve at its lowest point."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Production Possibility Frontier",
     q:"The diagram shows the Production Possibilities Frontier for a digital manufacturing company that produces both laptops and tablet devices.\n\n(a) Identify from the diagram which option (A, B or C) represents an efficient, an inefficient, and an impossible production point.\n\n(b) Justify each of your choices.",
     a:"(a)\nEfficient Production Point: C (on the PPF boundary)\nInefficient Production Point: A (inside the PPF boundary)\nImpossible Production Point: B (outside the PPF boundary)\n\n(b) Justifications:\n- Efficient (C): An efficient production point is where the firm is making the best possible use of scarce resources. It is producing as much as it can given current resources. This can be seen anywhere on the PPF boundary.\n- Inefficient (A): An inefficient production point is where the firm is wasteful of scarce resources. It is possible to produce more tablets and laptops given current resources. This is any point inside the PPF boundary.\n- Impossible (B): An impossible production point is one which the firm cannot currently produce given their current scarce resources. To produce at this level, they would need to increase their factors of production. This is any point outside the PPF boundary."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Direct & Indirect Taxation",
     q:"(a) In each case, indicate whether the tax is direct or indirect:\n- VAT\n- PAYE\n- Customs and Excise Duty\n\n(b) Outline three canons/principles of taxation which are achieved through the use of direct taxes.",
     a:"(a)\n- VAT: Indirect Tax\n- PAYE: Direct Tax\n- Customs and Excise Duty: Indirect Tax\n\n(b) Canons/principles of taxation:\n1. Certainty: It should be clear and unambiguous to the taxpayer the amount they are required to pay and the reason for doing so.\n2. Convenience: The tax should be levied at a time and in a manner which is most convenient for the taxpayer to pay (e.g. PAYE is deducted at source).\n3. Equity: The tax system should operate fairly. An equitable tax system should take a larger amount from those who are more able to pay than others (progressive taxation).\n4. Economy: The cost of tax collection should be far exceeded by the amount of tax which is collected.\n5. Should not act as a disincentive to save/work/invest: No tax should negatively affect those willing to supply their labour or discourage entrepreneurs from investing.\n6. Evasion should be impossible: Otherwise the tax is not effective in raising revenue."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Housing Market & Vacant Property Tax",
     q:"Landlords are leaving the Irish housing market in their thousands, blaming excessive taxation, rent control and constantly changing laws.\n\n(a) Show on the diagram how this development will alter the rental market equilibrium position. Explain your answer.\n\n(b) Outline two reasons why the Irish government introduced the vacant property tax.",
     a:"(a) As landlords leave the market, the supply of rental properties decreases. The supply curve shifts to the left from S to S1. The equilibrium price increases from Pe to Pe1, and the equilibrium quantity decreases from Qe to Qe1. This means rents will rise and fewer properties are available.\n\n(b) Reasons for vacant property tax:\n1. Increased government revenue to help with the homelessness/housing crisis.\n2. Vacant properties represent a market failure: in a market where some consumers are unable to find a home, vacant properties represent a waste of scarce resources. The tax incentivises owners to bring properties into use.\n3. More equitable: some vacant properties are holiday homes (second households). A progressive tax system should take a higher proportion from owners of greater wealth.\n4. Increased pressure on the Irish housing market year on year, exacerbated further by the war in Ukraine."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: ECB & Monetary Policy",
     q:"Eurozone interest rates increased four times in the last six months of 2022.\n\n(a) Justify why the European Central Bank (ECB) made this decision.\n\n(b) Does being a member of the Eurozone affect Ireland's ability to implement monetary policy in Ireland? Explain.",
     a:"(a) The ECB increased interest rates to combat rising inflation in the eurozone. Following the COVID-19 pandemic and the war in Ukraine, inflation surged due to supply chain disruptions and rising energy prices. Higher interest rates discourage borrowing and encourage saving, which reduces aggregate demand in the economy, helping to bring inflation back down towards the ECB's 2% target.\n\n(b) Yes, being a member of the Eurozone significantly affects Ireland's ability to implement monetary policy. Ireland has given up control of its own monetary policy to the ECB. Ireland cannot independently set interest rates, control money supply, or adjust exchange rates. The ECB sets a single monetary policy for all 20 eurozone members, which may not always suit Ireland's specific economic conditions. For example, interest rates set to cool inflation in Germany may be inappropriate for Ireland's economy."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Diminishing Marginal Returns & Specialisation",
     q:"China is the world's powerhouse in manufacturing goods e.g. clothing. Its share of world exports dwarfs every other nation.\n\n(a) Explain the economic concept of the law of diminishing marginal returns.\n\n(b) Outline two cost advantages China's firms experience through specialisation.",
     a:"(a) The Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns states that as more and more units of a variable factor of production (e.g. labour) are added to a fixed factor (e.g. capital/land), there comes a point where the extra output (marginal product) produced by each additional unit of the variable factor will begin to decrease. This assumes at least one factor is fixed (short run) and technology remains constant.\n\n(b) Cost advantages of specialisation for China:\n1. Lower average costs through economies of scale: by specialising in manufacturing, China's firms produce huge volumes, spreading fixed costs over millions of units and achieving very low average costs per unit.\n2. Increased productivity/expertise: workers who specialise in specific tasks become highly skilled and efficient at those tasks, increasing output per worker and reducing labour costs per unit.\n3. Reduced training costs: when workers focus on specific tasks, less time and money is spent on training.\n4. Bulk buying of raw materials: large-scale specialised production allows firms to negotiate lower prices for inputs."},

    {num:10, label:"Q10: National Income Accounts (GNP, GNI, Modified GNI)",
     q:"Given that GDP at Current Market Prices was \u20ac426bn, use the information to calculate B (Gross National Income at CMP) and C (Modified Gross National Income at CMP).\n\nData: Net Factor Income from Abroad: -\u20ac103bn; EU Subsidies: \u20ac1.7bn; EU Taxes: \u20ac0.5bn; Adjustments (redomiciled companies, depreciation on R&D/IP, aircraft leasing): \u20ac89bn.\n\nA = GNP at CMP = \u20ac323bn (given).",
     a:"A = GNP at CMP:\nGDP at CMP + Net Factor Income from Abroad\n= \u20ac426bn + (-\u20ac103bn) = \u20ac323bn (given)\n\nB = Gross National Income at CMP:\nGNP at CMP + EU Subsidies - EU Taxes\n= \u20ac323bn + \u20ac1.7bn - \u20ac0.5bn\n= \u20ac324.2bn\n\nC = Modified Gross National Income (GNI*) at CMP:\nGNI at CMP - Adjustments\n= \u20ac324.2bn - \u20ac89bn\n= \u20ac235.2bn\n\nWhy this matters: Modified GNI (GNI*) was developed by the CSO to provide a more accurate measure of the size of the Irish economy. Standard GDP and GNP figures are distorted by the activities of large multinationals (profit repatriation, intellectual property, aircraft leasing). GNI* strips out these distortions to give a truer picture of the domestic economy."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Population, Labour & Human Development",
     q:"(a)(i) Calculate the percentage change in the Irish population from 2016 to 2022. (Population 2016: 4,761,865; Population 2022: 5,123,536; Change: 361,671)\n(ii) Explain two factors which influenced this trend.\n(iii) Discuss one negative and one positive effect of population increase on government finances.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the factor of production labour.\n(ii) Explain two factors that influence geographical mobility of labour.\n(iii) Explain two ways the government can increase geographical mobility of labour.\n\n(c)(i) If average life expectancy in Ireland decreased, would it positively or negatively affect Ireland's HDI score? Explain.\n(ii) Despite Ireland's high HDI ranking, inequalities still exist. Outline two economic policies to address inequality.\n(iii) Outline two possible economic disadvantages of implementing these policies.",
     a:"(a)(i) Percentage change = (361,671 / 4,761,865) x 100 = 7.6% increase\n\n(ii) Factors influencing population growth:\n1. Immigration: Ireland's strong economy attracted significant numbers of immigrants, particularly from EU countries and further afield, for employment opportunities.\n2. Natural increase: birth rates exceeded death rates, contributing to population growth. Improved healthcare and living standards contributed to lower death rates.\n\n(iii) Positive effect: Increased tax revenue - more people working means more income tax, VAT, and other tax revenues for the government.\nNegative effect: Increased government expenditure - more people require public services such as healthcare, education, housing, and infrastructure, putting pressure on government spending.\n\n(b)(i) Labour refers to all human effort, both mental and physical, used in the production of goods and services. It includes all workers from manual labourers to highly skilled professionals.\n\n(ii) Factors affecting geographical mobility:\n1. Housing availability and affordability: Workers may be unable to move to areas where jobs are available if housing is too expensive or unavailable (e.g. Dublin's housing crisis).\n2. Family/social ties: Workers may be reluctant to move away from family, friends, and community connections.\n\n(iii) Government measures to increase geographical mobility:\n1. Investment in housing: building more affordable housing in areas of economic growth to allow workers to relocate.\n2. Investment in transport infrastructure: improving roads, rail, and public transport to allow workers to commute rather than relocate, or to make relocation easier.\n\n(c)(i) It would NEGATIVELY affect Ireland's HDI score. Life expectancy at birth is one of the three components of the HDI (along with education and GNI per capita). A decrease in life expectancy would lower the health component, thus reducing Ireland's overall HDI score.\n\n(ii) Policies to address inequality:\n1. Progressive taxation: increasing the tax rate for higher earners so they contribute proportionally more, with revenue redistributed through social welfare and public services.\n2. Investment in education: providing equal access to quality education for all, including grants and subsidies for disadvantaged students.\n\n(iii) Disadvantages of these policies:\n1. Brain drain/emigration: higher taxes on high earners may cause skilled workers to emigrate to countries with lower tax rates.\n2. Increased government debt: funding these policies may require increased government borrowing if tax revenue is insufficient."},

    {num:12, label:"Q12: Market Structures (Perfect Competition, Oligopoly, Windfall Tax)",
     q:"(a)(i) Identify three characteristics of a perfectly competitive market.\n(ii) Identify one reason why the market for Irish milk can no longer be considered perfectly competitive.\n(iii) Explain, with a diagram, the long-run equilibrium of a firm in perfect competition.\n\n(b)(i) Based on the market share figures (Kerry Group 48%, Glanbia 27%, Dairygold 8%, Lakeland 7%, Dale Farm 3%, Aurivo 3%, Arrabawn 2%, Tipperary Co-op 2%), calculate the HHI for Irish Dairy Producers.\n(ii) Is this market competitive, moderately concentrated, or highly concentrated? Explain.\n(iii) Outline one advantage and one disadvantage of a perfectly competitive market.\n\n(c) Budget 2023 proposed a temporary 33% windfall tax on energy companies' profits. Discuss arguments for and against.",
     a:"(a)(i) Characteristics of perfect competition:\n1. Large number of buyers and sellers - no single buyer or seller can influence the market price.\n2. Homogeneous (identical) products - all firms sell the same product.\n3. Perfect knowledge - all buyers and sellers have full information about prices and products.\n4. Free entry and exit - no barriers to entering or leaving the industry.\n5. Price-takers - individual firms must accept the market price.\n\n(ii) The market for Irish milk is no longer perfectly competitive because firms now differentiate their products (low-fat milk, super milk, lactose-free milk, organic milk). Product differentiation means products are no longer homogeneous.\n\n(iii) Long-run equilibrium in perfect competition:\nIn the long run, firms in perfect competition earn only normal profit. The firm produces where MC = MR = AR = AC at the minimum point of the AC curve.\nDiagram: U-shaped AC curve with MC cutting through its minimum point. Price line (D=AR=MR) is horizontal and tangent to the minimum point of AC. Output is at Q where MC = MR.\nExplanation: If supernormal profits existed, new firms would enter (no barriers), increasing supply, pushing down price until only normal profit remains. If losses existed, firms would exit, reducing supply, pushing up price until normal profit is restored.\n\n(b)(i) HHI Calculation:\nHHI = 48\u00b2 + 27\u00b2 + 8\u00b2 + 7\u00b2 + 3\u00b2 + 3\u00b2 + 2\u00b2 + 2\u00b2\n= 2,304 + 729 + 64 + 49 + 9 + 9 + 4 + 4\n= 3,172\n\n(ii) The market is HIGHLY CONCENTRATED. An HHI above 2,500 indicates a highly concentrated market. Kerry Group alone holds 48% market share, and the top two firms (Kerry Group and Glanbia) control 75% of the market. This is an oligopoly.\n\n(iii) Advantage of perfectly competitive market: Allocative efficiency - resources are allocated efficiently as price equals marginal cost (P = MC), ensuring that the right amount of goods is produced.\nDisadvantage: No product variety - since products are homogeneous, consumers have no choice in terms of product differentiation or branding.\n\n(c) Arguments FOR the windfall tax:\n- Fairness/equity: energy companies earned excessive profits due to the energy crisis caused by the war in Ukraine, not through innovation or efficiency. It is fair to tax these windfall gains.\n- Revenue generation: the windfall tax would generate significant revenue that could fund energy credits and support vulnerable households during the cost of living crisis.\n- Consumer protection: excess profits suggest consumers are being overcharged, and the tax redistributes some of this back.\n\nArguments AGAINST:\n- Discourages investment: energy companies may reduce investment in new energy infrastructure and renewable energy if they fear future windfall taxes.\n- Uncertainty: creates uncertainty for businesses and may deter FDI in the energy sector.\n- Difficult to implement: defining what constitutes 'excess' or 'windfall' profit is subjective and could lead to legal challenges."},

    {num:13, label:"Q13: Circular Flow of Income, Multiplier & Fiscal Policy",
     q:"(a)(i) Draw a fully labelled diagram showing the Circular Flow of Income for an open economy.\n(ii) Explain the terms injections and leakages with two examples of each.\n\n(b)(i) Using the multiplier formula, calculate the multiplier. State the relationship used.\n(ii) Explain the economic significance of the multiplier.\n(iii) Outline two limitations of the multiplier concept.\n\n(c)(i) Explain the term fiscal policy.\n(ii) Outline two fiscal policy measures the government could use to stimulate the economy.\n(iii) Discuss one advantage and one disadvantage of using fiscal policy.",
     a:"(a)(i) Circular Flow diagram should show:\n- Households and Firms as the two main sectors\n- Flow of factors of production from households to firms\n- Flow of income (wages, rent, interest, profit) from firms to households\n- Flow of expenditure from households to firms\n- Flow of goods and services from firms to households\n- Injections: Investment (I), Government Spending (G), Exports (X)\n- Leakages: Savings (S), Taxation (T), Imports (M)\n\n(ii) Injections are flows of money INTO the circular flow of income that increase its size.\nExample 1: Investment (I) - spending by firms on capital goods (machinery, equipment).\nExample 2: Government Spending (G) - government expenditure on public services, infrastructure.\n\nLeakages are flows of money OUT of the circular flow that reduce its size.\nExample 1: Savings (S) - income not spent by households that is saved in financial institutions.\nExample 2: Imports (M) - spending on goods and services produced in other countries.\n\n(b)(i) Multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC) or 1 / (MPT + MPS + MPM)\nThe multiplier shows the number of times by which a change in investment/injection will be multiplied to give the final change in national income.\n\n(ii) Economic significance: The multiplier indicates that an initial injection of spending into the economy will have a larger overall effect on national income. For example, if the multiplier is 2, an initial injection of \u20ac1 billion will ultimately increase national income by \u20ac2 billion, as the injection circulates through the economy.\n\n(iii) Limitations of the multiplier:\n1. Time lag: the full multiplier effect takes time to work through the economy and may not materialise as quickly as expected.\n2. Leakages may be larger than estimated: if savings, taxation, or imports are higher than expected, the actual multiplier effect will be smaller.\n\n(c)(i) Fiscal policy refers to the government's use of taxation and public expenditure to influence the level of economic activity in the economy.\n\n(ii) Fiscal policy measures to stimulate the economy:\n1. Reduce income tax: gives consumers more disposable income, increasing consumer spending and aggregate demand.\n2. Increase government capital expenditure: investment in infrastructure (roads, hospitals, schools) directly creates employment and stimulates demand through the multiplier effect.\n\n(iii) Advantage: Direct impact - government can target specific sectors or groups in need of stimulus.\nDisadvantage: Time lags - it takes time to implement fiscal policy changes (budget process) and for the effects to be felt in the economy."},

    {num:14, label:"Q14: Inflation, Exchange Rates & Trade",
     q:"Section B question covering inflation, exchange rates, and international trade issues facing the Irish economy.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level. Measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI).\n- Causes: Demand-pull inflation (excess demand in the economy) and Cost-push inflation (rising costs of production).\n- Effects of inflation: Reduced purchasing power, redistribution of income (hurts fixed-income earners), uncertainty for businesses, loss of international competitiveness.\n- Exchange rates: The price of one currency in terms of another. Affects export competitiveness and import costs.\n- A strong euro makes Irish exports more expensive abroad but imports cheaper.\n- Ireland, as a small open economy, is particularly vulnerable to exchange rate fluctuations.\n- Balance of Payments: records all economic transactions between Ireland and the rest of the world."},

    {num:15, label:"Q15: Market Failure & Government Intervention",
     q:"Section B question covering market failure, externalities, public goods, and the role of government intervention.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Market failure occurs when the free market leads to an inefficient or inequitable allocation of resources.\n- Types: Externalities (positive and negative), public goods, merit goods, demerit goods, monopoly power, information asymmetry.\n- Negative externalities: Costs imposed on third parties not involved in the transaction (e.g. pollution).\n- Positive externalities: Benefits enjoyed by third parties (e.g. education benefits society).\n- Government interventions: Taxation, subsidies, regulation, legislation, provision of public goods.\n- Cost-benefit analysis: Used by governments to assess whether a project's social benefits outweigh its social costs."},

    {num:16, label:"Q16: Enterprise & Economic Development",
     q:"Section B question covering enterprise, factors of production, and economic development in Ireland.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Enterprise is the factor of production that involves taking risks and organising the other factors of production.\n- The entrepreneur bears the risk of loss in return for potential profit.\n- Rewards: normal profit (minimum to keep entrepreneur in the industry) and supernormal profit (above normal profit).\n- Government supports for enterprise: grants (Enterprise Ireland, LEOs), tax incentives, training programmes.\n- FDI has been crucial for Ireland's economic development - IDA Ireland attracts multinational investment.\n- Challenges for Irish enterprise: housing costs, Brexit, global competition, climate change requirements."}
  ],

  "2024": [
    // ===== SECTION A: Short Response Questions (100 marks) =====
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Balance of Payments",
     q:"The infographic displays data relating to Ireland's Balance of Payments on the Current Account for Q2 2023.\n\n(a) From the data, calculate the balance of payments current account balance. State whether it is a surplus or a deficit. Show all workings.\n\n(b) Outline one advantage of having a surplus on the balance of payments.",
     a:"(a) Workings:\nCredits: \u20ac76bn + \u20ac91bn = \u20ac167bn\nDebits: \u20ac38bn + \u20ac83bn = \u20ac121bn\nBalance: \u20ac167bn - \u20ac121bn = \u20ac46bn SURPLUS\n\n(b) Advantages of a surplus:\n- Increased Economic Growth: If exports are greater than imports, more goods and services are being produced, which increases GNP per capita.\n- Increased Employment: If exports have increased, more people are needed to produce these goods and services.\n- Increased Tax Revenue: Exports contribute significant taxation revenue. Employment from exports generates direct tax (PAYE, USC).\n- Injection into the economy: Exports are an injection into the circular flow, increasing the multiplier effect and raising national income.\n- Attracts FDI: MNCs may become aware of Ireland's success on foreign markets and be encouraged to locate in Ireland."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Full Employment",
     q:"Ireland's seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for October 2023 was 4.8%. This may be regarded as full employment.\n\n(a) Explain what you understand by the term full employment.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Explain, giving two reasons, why full employment is an economic aim of the government.\nOR\n(c) Explain how any other two current economic aims of the government may conflict with full employment.",
     a:"(a) Full employment is a situation when everyone in the labour force who are willing and able to work are employed at existing wage rates. In Ireland it usually occurs when the unemployment rate lies between 4% to 5%. It does not mean zero unemployment, as some frictional and structural unemployment will always exist.\n\n(b) Reasons full employment is an economic aim:\n1. Increased direct/indirect tax revenue: When individuals are in employment, they contribute through PAYE and USC (direct tax). With more disposable income, they spend more, generating VAT revenue (indirect tax).\n2. Decreased social welfare payments: If individuals are employed, the government does not have to pay social welfare, reducing current expenditure.\n3. Boosts demand/economic growth: Employed individuals have more disposable income to spend, boosting aggregate demand.\n4. Reduction in poverty: Employment provides income, increasing standard of living and reducing poverty levels.\n\n(c) Conflicts with full employment:\n1. Full employment vs Price stability: With full employment comes increased spending, which can lead to demand-pull inflation.\n2. Full employment vs Balanced budget: Government may increase expenditure to maintain full employment, conflicting with its desire to balance the budget.\n3. Full employment vs Balance of payments equilibrium: More people working means higher incomes, and as Ireland is a small open economy, some increased income will be spent on imports, potentially reducing the surplus.\n4. Full employment vs Housing for all: Full employment increases demand for home ownership, but labour shortages, planning permission difficulties, and supply lags create housing problems."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Price Ceiling & Energy Crisis",
     q:"During 2022, the equilibrium megawatt price per hour for electricity was \u20ac600. The European Commission considered a \u20ac200 per megawatt hour limit.\n\n(a) Illustrate on the diagram how this proposal would alter the market.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Explain your understanding of the economic term price ceiling.\nOR\n(c) In Budget 2024 the government announced energy credits of \u20ac450 for all households. Explain why the government introduced this credit.",
     a:"(a) On the diagram, draw a horizontal line at \u20ac200 (below the equilibrium price of \u20ac600). This creates excess demand as the quantity demanded at \u20ac200 exceeds the quantity supplied. Label the price ceiling line at P1 (\u20ac200) and show the gap between quantity demanded and quantity supplied as excess demand.\n\n(b) A price ceiling is a maximum price, usually set by a government, that can be charged for a good/service by law. This price is set below the equilibrium price set by the market - a firm cannot legally exceed this maximum price. It is a measure put in place to prevent consumers being charged excessive prices. However, it creates excess demand (shortage) as the low price discourages supply but encourages demand.\n\n(c) Reasons for \u20ac450 energy credit:\n- Ease cost of living / improve standard of living: The main aim was to reduce electricity bills for all households as inflation was still very high, negatively impacting citizens' standards of living.\n- Protect fixed income holders: The \u20ac450 intervention helped those on fixed incomes who saw a big drop in living standards as energy costs soared in 2022/2023.\n- Market Failure: This intervention helped protect consumers suffering from a partial market failure (the market produced an unsatisfactory price).\n- War in Ukraine: This \u20ac450 intervention (costing over \u20ac1.2 billion) was part of a broader \u20ac4.1 billion package supporting households and businesses during high energy prices caused by Russia's invasion of Ukraine."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Capital Deepening & Demand for Labour",
     q:"(a) The table shows a firm's capital to labour ratio:\n2022: 5 machines, 5 workers (ratio 1:1)\n2023: 10 machines, 5 workers (ratio 2:1)\nTick whether the firm has engaged in capital widening or capital deepening. Explain.\n\nAnswer (b) or (c):\n(b) Outline two factors which may affect an individual company's demand for labour.\nOR\n(c) Explain the term Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC).",
     a:"(a) The firm has engaged in CAPITAL DEEPENING.\nCapital deepening occurs when an increase in the capital stock leads to more capital per worker. In 2023, the firm is employing 2 machines per worker compared to 2022 when it was only employing 1 machine per worker. The number of workers stayed the same (5) but machines doubled (from 5 to 10).\n\n(Capital widening would mean the capital-to-labour ratio stays the same while both increase proportionally.)\n\n(b) Factors affecting demand for labour:\n1. Wage Rate: The lower the wage rate, the higher the firm's demand for labour. If wage rate increases, cost to employer increases, which may lower demand.\n2. Demand for the firm's output (derived demand): If demand for a firm's output is high, it may hire extra staff. During recessions, demand for labour falls.\n3. Availability of new technology: New technology can lead to a fall in demand for labour (e.g. self-service checkouts, online check-in facilities).\n4. Entrepreneurs' expectations: If optimistic about the future, they may invest in new projects generating demand for labour.\n5. Price of other factors: A firm compares the cost of capital with labour to determine which is more competitive.\n6. Government incentives/subsidies: e.g. reducing employer's PRSI or LEO employment grants.\n7. MRP of labour: Will the worker generate more revenue than his/her wage rate? If so, it is profitable to employ them.\n\n(c) The Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC) is the extra profit earned as a result of employing one extra unit of capital. It measures the expected rate of return on an additional unit of investment."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Excess Demand & Concert Tickets",
     q:"Harry Styles performed a concert at Slane Castle on 10th June 2023 with a maximum capacity of 80,000 people. Ticket prices were \u20ac97.10 each and the concert sold out in hours.\n\n(a) Using the information, illustrate with a fully labelled diagram how a shortage of tickets may arise.\n\n(b) Explain your diagram.",
     a:"(a) Diagram should show:\n- Price on Y-axis, Quantity on X-axis\n- Vertical supply curve (perfectly inelastic) at 80,000 tickets\n- Downward-sloping demand curve\n- Price line at \u20ac97.10 below the equilibrium price\n- Excess demand shown as the gap between quantity demanded and 80,000 at the price of \u20ac97.10\n\n(b) Explanation:\nThe demand for tickets is downward sloping from left to right (law of demand). The supply of tickets is fixed/perfectly inelastic at 80,000 (the venue's maximum capacity cannot be changed regardless of price). If the price of tickets (\u20ac97.10) is set below the equilibrium price, there will be excess demand - more people want to buy tickets at that price than there are tickets available. This is why the concert sold out in hours, with many potential buyers unable to get tickets."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Cost Curves (AC & MC)",
     q:"(a) With the aid of a fully labelled diagram (including the axes), explain the relationship between a firm's short run average cost curve and its marginal cost curve.\n\n(b) Explain the shape of the short run average cost curve (downward sloping part and upward sloping part).",
     a:"(a) Diagram: U-shaped Average Cost (AC) curve and MC curve. MC intersects AC at the minimum point of AC. Y-axis: Cost/Price/\u20ac, X-axis: Quantity/Output.\n\nRelationship:\n- If marginal cost is less than average cost, then average cost is falling.\n- If marginal cost is greater than average cost, then average cost is rising.\n- If marginal cost equals average cost, then average cost is at its minimum point (constant).\n\n(b) Shape of the short run AC curve:\n\nDownward sloping part:\nSpecialisation/division of labour: As production increases, the firm can employ specialists or existing workers can concentrate on fewer tasks. This leads to greater efficiency and lower unit costs.\nOR: Greater spread of fixed costs: As output increases, fixed costs are spread over a larger number of units. Fixed costs don't increase directly with production, so the fixed cost per unit falls.\n\nUpward sloping part:\nThe law of diminishing marginal returns: As more of a variable factor (labour) is added to a fixed factor (capital), at some stage the increase in output caused by the last unit of the variable factor will begin to decline. It takes more workers to produce each additional unit of output, so average costs begin to rise."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Market Failure (Vaping)",
     q:"The number of teenagers in Ireland who vape is on the rise. Vaping poses a number of possible dangers to teenagers.\n\n(a) Explain why the purchase of vape products are a form of market failure.\n\n(b) Outline two ways the Irish government could intervene in the vaping market to overcome this market failure.",
     a:"(a) The purchase of demerit goods such as vape products is regarded as market failure because in a free market, vapes will be overproduced and overconsumed. Their consumption causes negative externalities - effects on third parties not involved in the consumption or production of vape products. Vape products are demerit goods as users may underestimate or be unaware of their harmful effects. Their free-market price fails to reflect their true private and social cost. If the free market took full account of the private and social costs of vapes, their price would be much higher.\n\n(b) Government interventions:\n1. Ban sale of vapes to those under 18 years of age: The government could ban the sale of vapes to consumers under 18 and enforce this law by imposing heavier penalties for sellers who breach the law.\n2. Impose indirect taxes/excise duties on vape products: This would increase the price, reducing demand (especially among price-sensitive teenagers). The tax revenue could fund public health campaigns.\n3. Regulation and advertising restrictions: Ban advertising and marketing of vape products, especially those targeted at young people.\n4. Education/information campaigns: Provide information about the health dangers of vaping to discourage consumption."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Price Discrimination",
     q:"Price discrimination remains a popular pricing strategy used by firms worldwide.\n\n(a) Explain, using an example, what you understand by the term price discrimination.\n\n(b) Outline two characteristics of consumers that permit/allow price discrimination to take place.",
     a:"(a) Price discrimination occurs when a firm charges different prices to different consumers for the same good or service, where the price difference is not due to differences in costs of production. Example: A cinema charging adults \u20ac12 for a ticket but charging students \u20ac8 for the same film, same seat, same screening. The cost to the cinema of providing the service is the same for both consumers.\n\n(b) Consumer characteristics that permit price discrimination:\n1. Consumer ignorance: The consumer is unaware that different prices are being charged to different groups. They don't know they could potentially get the product cheaper.\n2. Consumer indifference: Consumers are aware that price discrimination exists but they don't care enough to seek out the cheaper price. The convenience of purchasing at the higher price outweighs the saving.\n3. Different price elasticities of demand: Different groups of consumers have different sensitivities to price changes. Students and pensioners tend to be more price elastic (responsive to price changes) while business travellers tend to be price inelastic.\n4. Consumer attitudes: Some consumers feel the product is worth paying more for rather than going without it."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Opportunity Cost & Externalities",
     q:"(a) Coillte has pledged to add 100,000 hectares of woodland to the 440,000 hectares it already manages by 2050, costing \u20ac2 billion.\nOutline one opportunity cost of this pledge and one social benefit.\n\n(b) The Atlantic Technological University was launched in April 2022, creating a positive externality for counties Donegal, Sligo, and Mayo.\nExplain what you understand by the term positive externality.",
     a:"(a) Opportunity cost: The \u20ac2 billion spent on woodland expansion could have been used for alternative purposes such as investment in social housing, healthcare infrastructure, or road improvements. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone.\n\nSocial benefit: Increased woodland helps combat climate change by absorbing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere (carbon sequestration). Other social benefits include biodiversity, recreational amenities, flood prevention, and improved air quality for the wider community.\n\n(b) A positive externality is a benefit that is enjoyed by a third party who is not directly involved in the production or consumption of a good or service. The third party did not pay for this benefit. In the case of ATU, the university creates benefits for the wider community beyond just the students and staff - local businesses benefit from increased spending, the region benefits from a more educated workforce, and communities benefit from research and cultural activities the university provides."},

    {num:10, label:"Q10: National Income Accounts (GDP to GNI)",
     q:"Given that GDP at Current Market Prices was \u20ac430bn, use the information to calculate GNI at CMP.\nData: Net Factor Income from Abroad: -\u20ac105bn; EU Subsidies: \u20ac2bn; EU Taxes: \u20ac0.5bn.\n\n(i) GNP at CMP = \u20ac325bn (given)\n(ii) Calculate GNI at CMP.\n\n(b) Explain the term Net Factor Income from Abroad.",
     a:"(i) GNP at CMP = GDP at CMP + Net Factor Income from Abroad\n= \u20ac430bn + (-\u20ac105bn) = \u20ac325bn (given)\n\n(ii) GNI at CMP = GNP at CMP + EU Subsidies - EU Taxes\n= \u20ac325bn + \u20ac2bn - \u20ac0.5bn\n= \u20ac326.5bn\n\n(b) Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) is the difference between income earned by Irish residents/companies from their economic activities abroad, and income earned by foreign residents/companies from their economic activities in Ireland.\n\nIreland's NFIA is negative (-\u20ac105bn) because large amounts of profit earned by multinational corporations operating in Ireland are repatriated to their home countries. The outflow of profits from Ireland exceeds the inflow of income earned by Irish people/companies abroad. This is why Ireland's GNP is significantly lower than its GDP."},

    // ===== SECTION B: Extended Response Questions (300 marks) =====
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Tourism, Multiplier & Market Equilibrium",
     q:"(a)(i) Calculate the percentage increase in foreign tourists in August 2023 (737,600) compared to July 2021 (380,000). Show workings.\n(ii) Outline the effect of this increase in tourism on Ireland's circular flow of income.\n(iii) Outline one possible disadvantage of increasing tourism numbers.\n\n(b)(i) Given MPT = 0.22, MPM = 0.30, MPC = 0.6, calculate the multiplier and explain its economic meaning.\n(ii) Explain how a reduction in income tax rates would affect the multiplier.\n(iii) Outline two uses of national income statistics.\n\n(c) Assume the market for Irish organic ice cream is in equilibrium. Explain, with diagrams, the effects of:\n(i) A prolonged heatwave during summer.\n(ii) An increase in lactose intolerant consumers.\n(iii) Producers receiving a subsidy for organic ice cream production.",
     a:"(a)(i) Percentage increase = ((737,600 - 380,000) / 380,000) x 100\n= (357,600 / 380,000) x 100 = 94.1% increase\n\n(ii) Tourism spending by foreign visitors is an injection into Ireland's circular flow of income (similar to exports). It increases the flow of money in the economy, leading to a multiplier effect as the tourism spending circulates through various sectors (hotels, restaurants, transport, retail).\n\n(iii) Disadvantage of increasing tourism:\n- Increased pressure on infrastructure and housing: tourist hotspots may experience overcrowding, strain on public services, and increased property prices/rents as homes are converted to short-term holiday lets (e.g. Airbnb), reducing housing supply for residents.\n\n(b)(i) Multiplier = 1 / (MPT + MPM + MPS)\nFirst calculate MPS: MPC + MPS + MPT + MPM = 1\nMPS = 1 - 0.6 - 0.22 - 0.30 = -0.12\nOR using: Multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC) if only domestic factors considered\nMore accurately: Multiplier = 1 / (MPT + MPM + MPS)\nSince MPC = 0.6, we can derive MPS = 1 - MPC - MPT - MPM = 1 - 0.6 - 0.22 - 0.30 = -0.12\nUsing the withdrawal method: K = 1 / (1 - MPC + MPT + MPM)\n= 1 / (1 - 0.6 + 0.22 + 0.30) = 1 / (0.4 + 0.22 + 0.30) = 1 / 0.92 = 1.09\n\nOR: K = 1 / (1 - MPC) is simplified to K = 1 / 0.4 = 2.5\n\nEconomic meaning: For every \u20ac1 injected into the economy, national income will ultimately increase by the multiplier value. The initial spending circulates through the economy as one person's spending becomes another's income.\n\n(ii) A reduction in income tax rates would increase the multiplier. Lower taxes mean consumers have more disposable income (higher MPC), so more of each euro earned is respent in the economy rather than being withdrawn as tax. The MPT (marginal propensity to tax) falls, reducing total withdrawals and increasing the multiplier.\n\n(iii) Uses of national income statistics:\n1. Comparing living standards between countries: GDP/GNP per capita allows comparison of average living standards across different nations.\n2. Measuring economic growth: Comparing national income figures over time shows whether the economy is growing or contracting, helping government plan economic policy.\n\n(c)(i) Prolonged heatwave:\nDemand shifts RIGHT from D to D1. A heatwave increases the desire for ice cream, increasing demand at every price level. Equilibrium price rises from Pe to Pe1 and equilibrium quantity increases from Qe to Qe1.\n\n(ii) Increase in lactose intolerant consumers:\nDemand shifts LEFT from D to D1. More lactose intolerant consumers means fewer people willing/able to buy dairy ice cream, decreasing demand. Equilibrium price falls from Pe to Pe1 and equilibrium quantity decreases from Qe to Qe1.\n\n(iii) Producers receiving a subsidy:\nSupply shifts RIGHT from S to S1. A subsidy reduces producers' costs of production, allowing them to supply more at every price level. Equilibrium price falls from Pe to Pe1 and equilibrium quantity increases from Qe to Qe1. Consumers benefit from lower prices and greater availability."},

    {num:12, label:"Q12: Taxation, Inflation & Regulation",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain two canons/principles of taxation (other than certainty).\n(ii) Outline two effects of an increase in hidden economy activities.\n(iii) A new 30% tax rate for middle-income earners has been proposed. Outline one economic consequence for either the taxpayer or the government.\n\n(b)(i) Outline the overall trend of the CPI annual % change from March to October 2023.\n(ii) Calculate the price index for the current year given weights (Food 65%, Transport 20%, Other 15%) and prices.\n(iii) Outline two possible economic effects of high price inflation.\n\n(c)(i) Outline two reasons why the gambling industry should be regulated.\n(ii) Outline one possible effect of demerit good markets.\n(iii) One assumption of perfect competition is perfect knowledge. Explain how imperfect knowledge can lead to market failure.",
     a:"(a)(i) Canons of taxation:\n1. Equity: The tax system should operate fairly. Those with greater ability to pay should contribute proportionally more (progressive taxation). The burden should not fall disproportionately on lower earners.\n2. Economy: The cost of collecting the tax should be far less than the revenue generated. An efficient tax system minimises administrative costs while maximising revenue collection.\n3. Convenience: Tax should be levied at a time and in a manner most convenient for the taxpayer (e.g. PAYE deducted at source from wages).\n4. Should not act as a disincentive: Tax should not discourage people from working, saving, or investing.\n\n(ii) Effects of increased hidden economy:\n1. Loss of tax revenue: Income from hidden economic activities is not declared, reducing the government's tax take and its ability to fund public services.\n2. Inaccurate national income statistics: Hidden economic activity is not recorded in official GDP/GNP figures, making economic data unreliable for policy planning.\n3. Unfair competition: Businesses operating in the hidden economy avoid taxes and regulations, giving them an unfair advantage over legitimate businesses.\n\n(iii) Economic consequence of a new 30% rate:\nFor the taxpayer: Increased disposable income - middle-income earners would pay 30% instead of 40% on a portion of their income, leaving them with more take-home pay to spend or save.\nFor the government: Reduced tax revenue in the short term - the lower rate would mean less income tax collected from middle earners, potentially creating a budget shortfall.\n\n(b)(i) The CPI annual % change shows a downward trend from March to October 2023. Inflation was at its highest around March (approximately 7%) and declined steadily to approximately 3.6% by October. This indicates that while prices are still rising, the rate of increase is slowing (disinflation).\n\n(ii) Price index calculation:\nFood: Simple price index = (66/60) x 100 = 110. Weight result = 110 x 0.65 = 71.5\nTransport: Simple price index = (45/30) x 100 = 150. Weight result = 150 x 0.20 = 30.0\nOther: Simple price index = (96/80) x 100 = 120. Weight result = 120 x 0.15 = 18.0\nPrice Index = 71.5 + 30.0 + 18.0 = 119.5\nThis means the general price level has increased by 19.5% from the base year.\n\n(iii) Economic effects of high inflation:\n1. Reduced purchasing power: As prices rise, the value of money falls, and consumers can buy fewer goods and services with the same income, reducing their standard of living.\n2. Loss of international competitiveness: If Irish inflation is higher than that of trading partners, Irish exports become more expensive, reducing demand and potentially worsening the balance of payments.\n\n(c)(i) Reasons for gambling regulation:\n1. Consumer protection: Gambling can be addictive and lead to financial ruin, mental health problems, and family breakdown. Regulation protects vulnerable consumers from exploitation.\n2. Prevention of money laundering: Unregulated gambling can be used to launder proceeds of crime. Regulation ensures proper oversight and reporting of transactions.\n\n(ii) Effect of demerit good markets:\nDemerit goods (like cigarettes) are overproduced and overconsumed in a free market because consumers underestimate the private and social costs. This leads to negative externalities such as increased healthcare costs (borne by taxpayers), passive smoking affecting third parties, and reduced workforce productivity.\n\n(iii) Imperfect knowledge and market failure:\nPerfect competition assumes all buyers and sellers have perfect knowledge of prices, quality, and market conditions. In reality, imperfect knowledge means consumers may not be aware of all available options, prices, or product quality. This can lead to market failure as consumers make suboptimal choices (e.g. paying too much, buying inferior products, or consuming goods whose true costs they don't understand). Firms may also exploit information asymmetry to charge higher prices or sell inferior products."},

    {num:13, label:"Q13: Market Structures & Competition",
     q:"Section B question covering market structures (perfect competition, monopoly, oligopoly, monopolistic competition), barriers to entry, and competition policy.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Perfect competition: Many buyers and sellers, homogeneous products, perfect knowledge, free entry/exit, price-takers. Long-run equilibrium: normal profit only (P = MC = AC at minimum AC).\n\n- Monopoly: Single seller, unique product, high barriers to entry, price-maker. Can earn supernormal profit in long run. May lead to higher prices and lower output than competitive markets.\n\n- Oligopoly: Few large firms dominate, interdependent decision-making, significant barriers to entry, non-price competition (advertising, branding). Kinked demand curve theory explains price rigidity.\n\n- Monopolistic competition: Many firms, differentiated products, low barriers to entry, some price-making power. Short-run supernormal profits attracted away by new entrants in long run.\n\n- Barriers to entry: Economies of scale, legal barriers (patents, licenses), brand loyalty, high start-up costs, control of key resources.\n\n- Competition policy: Government intervention to prevent anti-competitive behaviour (price fixing, cartels, abuse of dominant position). In Ireland, the CCPC (Competition and Consumer Protection Commission) enforces competition law."},

    {num:14, label:"Q14: International Trade & Globalisation",
     q:"Section B question covering international trade, globalisation, the EU, and Ireland's trading position.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Absolute advantage: A country can produce a good more efficiently (using fewer resources) than another country.\n- Comparative advantage: A country can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country. Even if one country is better at producing everything, trade is still beneficial based on comparative advantage.\n\n- Free trade benefits: Lower prices, greater consumer choice, specialisation, economies of scale, increased competition.\n- Protectionism: Tariffs, quotas, subsidies, embargoes. Arguments for: protecting infant industries, national security, preventing dumping, safeguarding employment.\n\n- Ireland and the EU: Benefits include access to Single Market (free movement of goods, services, capital, labour), EU structural funds, and political stability. Challenges include loss of monetary policy independence and compliance costs.\n\n- Brexit impact on Ireland: Trade disruption with the UK, Northern Ireland Protocol issues, potential loss of UK as a land bridge to EU markets.\n\n- Globalisation: Increased interconnectedness of economies. Benefits include FDI, technology transfer, and cultural exchange. Challenges include inequality, environmental concerns, and loss of national sovereignty."},

    {num:15, label:"Q15: Government Policy & Economic Management",
     q:"Section B question covering fiscal policy, monetary policy, supply-side policies, and government management of the Irish economy.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Fiscal policy: Government use of taxation and spending to influence the economy. Expansionary (lower taxes/higher spending to stimulate growth) vs contractionary (higher taxes/lower spending to cool overheating economy).\n\n- Monetary policy: Controlled by the ECB for all eurozone members. Tools include interest rates, open market operations, and quantitative easing. Ireland cannot independently set interest rates.\n\n- Supply-side policies: Aim to increase the productive capacity of the economy. Include investment in education/training, infrastructure, deregulation, and reducing bureaucracy.\n\n- Budget deficit vs surplus: A deficit occurs when government spending exceeds revenue. A surplus occurs when revenue exceeds spending. The national debt is the cumulative total of all past deficits.\n\n- Automatic stabilisers: Tax revenues and social welfare payments that automatically adjust with the economic cycle, helping to smooth fluctuations.\n\n- The multiplier effect: An initial change in spending leads to a proportionally larger change in national income."},

    {num:16, label:"Q16: Factors of Production & Enterprise",
     q:"Section B question covering the four factors of production (land, labour, capital, enterprise), their rewards, and their role in economic development.",
     a:"Key concepts:\n\n- Land: All natural resources. Reward is rent. Characteristics: gift of nature, no cost of production, fixed in supply (perfectly inelastic), geographically immobile, varies in quality.\n\n- Labour: All human effort (mental and physical) in production. Reward is wages. Affected by education, skills, mobility (geographical and occupational), and population trends.\n\n- Capital: Man-made aids to production (machinery, equipment, buildings). Reward is interest/profit. Capital formation occurs through saving and investment. Types: fixed capital (buildings, machinery) and working capital (raw materials, stocks).\n\n- Enterprise: Organising the other factors, decision-making, risk-bearing, and innovation. Reward is profit (normal and supernormal). The entrepreneur bears the risk of loss.\n\n- Capital widening: Increasing capital stock in proportion to labour (ratio stays same).\n- Capital deepening: Increasing capital stock relative to labour (more capital per worker).\n\n- Marginal Efficiency of Capital (MEC): The expected rate of return on an additional unit of capital investment. Investment continues as long as MEC exceeds the interest rate.\n\n- Derived demand: Factors of production are not demanded for their own sake but because of the goods/services they produce."}
  ]
};
